SUBJECTS.find((s) => s.id === 'oop').pyq = [{"p":0,"q":"1","marks":"4","unit":1,"topic":"Virtual area and polymorphism","qh":"<p>Create a base class Shape containing a virtual function area(). Derive two classes, Rectangle and Circle, from Shape. Override the area() function in both derived classes and demonstrate runtime polymorphism using a base class pointer.</p>","sh":"<p>area() is virtual in Shape, so a call through Shape* selects the function of the object&#x27;s actual class at run time. Making it pure virtual also prevents creation of a meaningless bare Shape.</p><pre>#include &lt;iomanip&gt;\n#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass Shape {\npublic:\n    virtual double area() const = 0;\n    virtual ~Shape() = default;\n};\nclass Rectangle : public Shape {\n    double length, breadth;\npublic:\n    Rectangle(double l, double b) : length(l), breadth(b) {}\n    double area() const override { return length * breadth; }\n};\nclass Circle : public Shape {\n    double radius;\npublic:\n    explicit Circle(double r) : radius(r) {}\n    double area() const override { return 3.14159 * radius * radius; }\n};\nint main() {\n    Rectangle rectangle(3, 4);\n    Circle circle(2);\n    Shape* shape = &amp;rectangle;\n    cout &lt;&lt; fixed &lt;&lt; setprecision(2);\n    cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Rectangle: &quot; &lt;&lt; shape-&gt;area() &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n    shape = &amp;circle;\n    cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Circle: &quot; &lt;&lt; shape-&gt;area() &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Rectangle: 12.00</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Circle: 12.57</p><p>The same pointer expression shape-&gt;area() invokes two different overrides.</p>","exam":"mid","year":2026,"branch":"SE","src":"MIDSEM sept 26(SE).pdf"},{"p":0,"q":"2","marks":"4","unit":1,"topic":"Virtual functions and constructors","qh":"<p>Explain virtual functions and pure virtual functions. Write a C++ program demonstrating a virtual function. Discuss the different types of constructor with examples.</p>","sh":"<p>A virtual function is declared virtual in a base class and can be overridden; calls through a base pointer/reference use dynamic dispatch. A pure virtual function has = 0; its class is abstract and cannot be instantiated. A derived concrete class must provide an override for every inherited pure virtual function. A virtual destructor is appropriate when deleting derived objects through a base pointer.</p><p>Common constructor forms are: default (no arguments), parameterized (accepts values), copy (initializes from another object), move (takes resources from a temporary), and a constructor that allocates dynamic storage. The last is commonly called a dynamic constructor in this syllabus; it is a use of a constructor, not a separate C++ language category. The program below demonstrates a virtual call and the first three constructor forms.</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass Animal {\npublic:\n    virtual void sound() const { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Animal sound\\n&quot;; }\n    virtual ~Animal() = default;\n};\nclass Dog : public Animal {\npublic:\n    void sound() const override { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Bark\\n&quot;; }\n};\nclass Box {\n    int value;\npublic:\n    Box() : value(0) {}                    // default\n    explicit Box(int v) : value(v) {}      // parameterized\n    Box(const Box&amp; other) : value(other.value) {} // copy\n    int get() const { return value; }\n};\nint main() {\n    Dog dog;\n    Animal* animal = &amp;dog;\n    animal-&gt;sound();\n    Box a, b(5), c(b);\n    cout &lt;&lt; a.get() &lt;&lt; &#x27; &#x27; &lt;&lt; b.get() &lt;&lt; &#x27; &#x27; &lt;&lt; c.get() &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Bark</p><p class=\"ln i0\">0 5 5</p><p>For example, Box(Box&amp;&amp; other) is a move constructor, while a constructor containing data = new int[n]; is a dynamic-allocation constructor and must be paired with correct ownership and cleanup.</p>","exam":"mid","year":2026,"branch":"SE","src":"MIDSEM sept 26(SE).pdf"},{"p":0,"q":"3","marks":"4","unit":1,"topic":"Inheritance access and static count","qh":"<p>Explain the different types of inheritance in C++ with suitable diagrams. Discuss the role of access specifiers in inheritance. Write a program to count the number of objects created and destroy using static data member.</p>","sh":"<p>The five common inheritance structures are:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Single:        A → B</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Multilevel:    A → B → C</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Hierarchical:  A → B, A → C</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Multiple:      A + B → C</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Hybrid:        a combination, e.g. A → B, A → C, B + C → D</p><p>For class Derived : access Base, the inheritance access controls how accessible base members appear in the derived class:</p><pre>Base member   public inheritance   protected inheritance   private inheritance\npublic        public               protected               private\nprotected     protected            protected               private\nprivate       inaccessible directly in all three cases</pre><p>The base class&#x27;s private members still exist in the derived object, but derived code must use an accessible base member function to reach them. public inheritance models an is-a relationship; protected and private inheritance restrict the inherited interface.</p><p>Assumption: “destroy” means “destroyed”; count all constructions and destructions during the demonstrated scope. The static members belong to the class, so all instances update the same counters.</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass Tracked {\n    static int created, destroyed;\npublic:\n    Tracked() { ++created; }\n    Tracked(const Tracked&amp;) { ++created; }\n    ~Tracked() { ++destroyed; }\n    static void report() {\n        cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Created=&quot; &lt;&lt; created &lt;&lt; &quot;, Destroyed=&quot; &lt;&lt; destroyed\n             &lt;&lt; &quot;, Alive=&quot; &lt;&lt; created - destroyed &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n    }\n};\nint Tracked::created = 0;\nint Tracked::destroyed = 0;\nint main() {\n    {\n        Tracked a, b;\n        Tracked c(a);\n        Tracked::report();\n    }\n    Tracked::report();\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Created=3, Destroyed=0, Alive=3</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Created=3, Destroyed=3, Alive=0</p>","exam":"mid","year":2026,"branch":"SE","src":"MIDSEM sept 26(SE).pdf"},{"p":0,"q":"4","marks":"4","unit":0,"topic":"OOP and friend function","qh":"<p>What is Object oriented programing? How it is different from the procedure-oriented programming? Explain Friend function with example.</p>","sh":"<p>Object-oriented programming organizes a program around objects that combine state (data members) and behaviour (member functions). Encapsulation controls access to state; inheritance supports reuse; polymorphism lets one interface have several implementations.</p><pre>Aspect          Procedure-oriented          Object-oriented\nMain unit       Functions                   Objects/classes\nData            Often passed/shared         Encapsulated with methods\nDesign focus    Steps of an algorithm       Responsibilities of objects\nReuse           Functions                   Functions, classes, inheritance</pre><p>A friend function is a non-member function declared with friend inside a class. It can access that class&#x27;s private and protected members, but it has no this pointer and is called like an ordinary function. Friendship should be granted only where such access is needed.</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass Account {\n    int balance;\npublic:\n    explicit Account(int b) : balance(b) {}\n    friend int total(const Account&amp; a, const Account&amp; b);\n};\nint total(const Account&amp; a, const Account&amp; b) {\n    return a.balance + b.balance;\n}\nint main() {\n    Account first(150), second(250);\n    cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Total = &quot; &lt;&lt; total(first, second) &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Total = 400</p>","exam":"mid","year":2026,"branch":"SE","src":"MIDSEM sept 26(SE).pdf"},{"p":0,"q":"5","marks":"4","unit":1,"topic":"Resolving inheritance ambiguity","qh":"<p>Define ambiguity in inheritance. How ambiguities can be removed by using Scope resolution operator and virtual base class. Explain your answer with example.</p>","sh":"<p>Ambiguity occurs when an inherited member name has more than one possible base-class source. In multiple inheritance, qualify the intended source (obj.Left::show()). In a diamond, two non-virtual paths to the same base create two base subobjects; virtual inheritance shares one base subobject and removes that duplication.</p><pre>   Base                  Base\n   /  \\                  /  \\\n Left Right          Left  Right  (both inherit virtual Base)\n   \\  /                  \\  /\n    End                   End\n#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nstruct Left {\n    void show() const { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Left\\n&quot;; }\n};\nstruct Right {\n    void show() const { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Right\\n&quot;; }\n};\nstruct Both : Left, Right {};\nstruct Base { int value = 7; };\nstruct Branch1 : virtual Base {};\nstruct Branch2 : virtual Base {};\nstruct Diamond : Branch1, Branch2 {};\nint main() {\n    Both b;\n    // b.show(); would be ambiguous.\n    b.Left::show();\n    b.Right::show();\n    Diamond d;\n    cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Shared value = &quot; &lt;&lt; d.value &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Left</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Right</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Shared value = 7</p><p>With non-virtual Branch1 : Base and Branch2 : Base, d.value would be ambiguous; d.Branch1::value would select one copy but would not unify them.</p>","exam":"mid","year":2026,"branch":"SE","src":"MIDSEM sept 26(SE).pdf"},{"p":1,"q":"1","marks":"4","unit":0,"topic":"Friend function and class","qh":"<p>(i) Differentiate between a friend function and member function with appropriate example.</p><p>(ii) Design a C++ program with two classes, Wallet and Bank. Declare the Bank class as a friend of the Wallet class, and illustrate how Bank Class can access the private data members of Wallet.</p>","sh":"<p>(i) A member function belongs to a class, is called through an object (or class for a static member), and has a this pointer. A friend function is not a class member: the class explicitly grants it access to private/protected members, it has no this, and it is called as an ordinary function. In the program, balanceOf(w) is a friend function while Bank::inspect(w) is a member of the friend class.</p><p>(ii)</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass Wallet;\nint balanceOf(const Wallet&amp; w);\nclass Wallet {\n    int balance;\npublic:\n    explicit Wallet(int amount) : balance(amount) {}\n    friend int balanceOf(const Wallet&amp; w);\n    friend class Bank;\n};\nint balanceOf(const Wallet&amp; w) { return w.balance; }\nclass Bank {\npublic:\n    void inspect(const Wallet&amp; w) const {\n        cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Bank sees: &quot; &lt;&lt; w.balance &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n    }\n};\nint main() {\n    Wallet w(500);\n    cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Friend function sees: &quot; &lt;&lt; balanceOf(w) &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n    Bank b;\n    b.inspect(w);\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Friend function sees: 500</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Bank sees: 500</p><p>Bank can read Wallet::balance because the entire class is a friend; the friendship is one-way and is not inherited.</p>","exam":"mid","year":2026,"branch":"CS-203","src":"oops(CS) midsem sept 2026 .pdf"},{"p":1,"q":"2","marks":"4","unit":0,"topic":"Constructor error and nested class","qh":"<p>(i) Identify whether the following C++ program contains any error. If there is an error, identify and correct it; otherwise, state that there is no error. Also, determine the output.</p><pre>##include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass Student {\n    int rollNo;\npublic:\n    student(int r) {\n        rollNo = r;\n    }\n    void display() {\n        cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Roll No =&quot; &lt;&lt; rollNo &lt;&lt; endl;\n    }\n};\nint main() {\n    Student s1(101);\n    s1.display();\n    return 0;\n}</pre><p>(ii) “Address” is defined as a nested class inside Student. Can Address be accessed directly by its class name from main()? Explain the appropriate syntax for accessing it and need of nested class in C++.</p>","sh":"<p>(i) The first line must be #include, not ##include. C++ is case-sensitive: student(int r) does not match Student, so it is not a constructor; it also lacks a return type. Rename it Student(int r). The corrected, complete program is:</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass Student {\n    int rollNo;\npublic:\n    explicit Student(int r) : rollNo(r) {}\n    void display() const { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Roll No =&quot; &lt;&lt; rollNo &lt;&lt; endl; }\n};\nint main() {\n    Student s1(101);\n    s1.display();\n    return 0;\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Roll No =101</p><p>(ii) The nested type is named in the enclosing class&#x27;s scope: Student::Address, not Address alone (unless an alias or using-declaration introduces that name). It must be accessible, for example declared under public:. Nesting groups a closely related type and controls where its name is visible; it does not automatically create an Address object for every Student.</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass Student {\npublic:\n    class Address {\n    public:\n        void show() const { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Delhi\\n&quot;; }\n    };\n};\nint main() {\n    Student::Address a;\n    a.show();\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Delhi</p>","exam":"mid","year":2026,"branch":"CS-203","src":"oops(CS) midsem sept 2026 .pdf"},{"p":1,"q":"3","marks":"4","unit":1,"topic":"Constructor overload and shapes","qh":"<p>(i) A programmer wants to create objects of a class with different sets of initial values. Explain how constructor overloading provides this flexibility and how the appropriate constructor is selected.</p><p>(ii) Design a class in C++ with common shape properties, and derive two figure classes from it — a three-sided figure and a four-sided figure. Each derived class must compute and display its own area.</p>","sh":"<p>(i) Constructor overloading provides multiple constructors with different parameter lists. At construction, overload resolution chooses the best viable match from the arguments&#x27; number and types. In the program, Triangle unit; selects the no-argument constructor, while Triangle t(6, 4); selects the two-argument constructor. Return type cannot distinguish overloads.</p><p>(ii) Assumption: the three-sided figure is a triangle with base and height; the four-sided figure is a rectangle with length and breadth. Both dimensions are held by the common base. Each derived class overrides the area formula.</p><pre>#include &lt;iomanip&gt;\n#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass Figure {\nprotected:\n    double x, y;\npublic:\n    Figure(double first, double second) : x(first), y(second) {}\n    virtual double area() const = 0;\n    virtual ~Figure() = default;\n};\nclass Triangle : public Figure {\npublic:\n    Triangle() : Figure(1, 1) {}\n    Triangle(double base, double height) : Figure(base, height) {}\n    double area() const override { return x * y / 2; }\n};\nclass Rectangle : public Figure {\npublic:\n    Rectangle(double length, double breadth) : Figure(length, breadth) {}\n    double area() const override { return x * y; }\n};\nint main() {\n    Triangle unit;\n    Triangle t(6, 4);\n    Rectangle r(6, 4);\n    cout &lt;&lt; fixed &lt;&lt; setprecision(2);\n    cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Unit triangle: &quot; &lt;&lt; unit.area() &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n    cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Triangle: &quot; &lt;&lt; t.area() &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n    cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Rectangle: &quot; &lt;&lt; r.area() &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Unit triangle: 0.50</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Triangle: 12.00</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Rectangle: 24.00</p>","exam":"mid","year":2026,"branch":"CS-203","src":"oops(CS) midsem sept 2026 .pdf"},{"p":1,"q":"4","marks":"4","unit":1,"topic":"Name ambiguity and unary minus","qh":"<p>(i) In multiple inheritance, if two base classes have a member function with the same name, how is the ambiguity resolved when called through a derived class object? Explain with example and pseudocode.</p><p>(ii) Create a program in C++ with class “Temperature” storing a temperature value. Overload the unary - operator to negate the value and display the result.</p>","sh":"<p>(i) An unqualified derived.show() is ambiguous if both bases offer equally matching show() functions. Qualify the desired base with the scope-resolution operator, or provide a forwarding override in the derived class.</p><pre>class Left  { show() }\nclass Right { show() }\nclass Child : Left, Right { }\nChild c\nc.Left::show()    // choose Left&#x27;s function\nc.Right::show()   // choose Right&#x27;s function</pre><p>(ii) Unary operator-() takes no explicit operand as a member function; its implicit operand is the current object. Return a new value so the source remains unchanged.</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass Temperature {\n    int value;\npublic:\n    explicit Temperature(int v) : value(v) {}\n    Temperature operator-() const { return Temperature(-value); }\n    void display() const { cout &lt;&lt; value &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;; }\n};\nint main() {\n    Temperature original(25);\n    Temperature negated = -original;\n    cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Original: &quot;; original.display();\n    cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Negated: &quot;; negated.display();\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Original: 25</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Negated: -25</p>","exam":"mid","year":2026,"branch":"CS-203","src":"oops(CS) midsem sept 2026 .pdf"},{"p":1,"q":"5","marks":"4","unit":0,"topic":"Shared static book count","qh":"<p>(i) Suppose five objects of a class are created and a single value must reflect the information common to all five objects. Should the value be maintained separately for each object or at class level? State the appropriate approach with justification.</p><p>(ii) Write a C++ program for a Library class in which every newly created book object updates a common count, and the current count can be displayed using a member function.</p>","sh":"<p>(i) Store the value at class level as a static data member. Every object then observes one shared value, avoiding five inconsistent copies. Initialize the static member once (or use a C++17 inline static member).</p><p>(ii) Assumption: “count” means the number of book objects currently alive, so construction increments it and destruction decrements it. The example creates three books in a scope and then checks the count again.</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass Library {\n    inline static int count = 0;\npublic:\n    Library() { ++count; }\n    Library(const Library&amp;) { ++count; }\n    ~Library() { --count; }\n    void displayCount() const {\n        cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Books: &quot; &lt;&lt; count &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n    }\n    static void showCount() {\n        cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Books: &quot; &lt;&lt; count &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n    }\n};\nint main() {\n    Library first;\n    {\n        Library second, third;\n        second.displayCount();\n    }\n    Library::showCount();\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Books: 3</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Books: 1</p>","exam":"mid","year":2026,"branch":"CS-203","src":"oops(CS) midsem sept 2026 .pdf"},{"p":2,"q":"1","marks":"4","unit":1,"topic":"Non-overloadable operators and friend","qh":"<p>(a) List the operators that cannot be overloaded through member function and friend function. Kindly specify the reason for each operator that why cannot it be overloaded.</p><p>(b) Create a class Employee with private members name and salary. Write a friend function increaseSalary(Employee &amp;e, double percent) to increase an employee’s salary by a given percentage.</p>","sh":"<p>(a) C++ prohibits overloading the following language operators by either member or friend functions:</p><pre>Operator(s)                Why the language keeps the built-in meaning\n::                         Determines scope during name lookup.\n. and .*                   Determine member access and member-pointer binding.\n?:                         Has special conditional evaluation rules.\nsizeof, alignof, typeid,   Are compile-time/type-inspection language operations.\nnoexcept</pre><p>decltype is also a language construct, not an overloadable operator. These restrictions are imposed by C++ grammar and semantics; a friend declaration grants access but cannot expand the set of overloadable operators. Some operators *can* be overloaded only as members, such as =, [], (), and -&gt;.</p><p>(b) The friend receives the employee by reference so it changes the original object. Assumption: a positive percent is supplied.</p><pre>#include &lt;iomanip&gt;\n#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n#include &lt;string&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass Employee {\n    string name;\n    double salary;\npublic:\n    Employee(string n, double s) : name(n), salary(s) {}\n    friend void increaseSalary(Employee&amp; e, double percent);\n    void show() const {\n        cout &lt;&lt; name &lt;&lt; &quot;: &quot; &lt;&lt; fixed &lt;&lt; setprecision(2) &lt;&lt; salary &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n    }\n};\nvoid increaseSalary(Employee&amp; e, double percent) {\n    e.salary += e.salary * percent / 100.0;\n}\nint main() {\n    Employee e(&quot;Asha&quot;, 50000);\n    increaseSalary(e, 10);\n    e.show();\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Asha: 55000.00</p>","exam":"mid","year":2025,"branch":"CS-203","src":"oops cs 2025.pdf (duplicates: two WhatsApp Image 2026-09-19 scans"},{"p":2,"q":"2","marks":"4","unit":0,"topic":"Global and static call counters","qh":"<p>Write a function that, when you call it, displays a message telling how many times it has been called: “I have been called 3 times”, for instance. Write a main() program that calls this function at least 10 times. Try implementing this function in two different ways. First, use a global variable to store the count. Second, use a local static variable. Which is more appropriate? Why can’t you use a local variable?</p>","sh":"<p>The program calls each implementation ten times; each counter starts at zero and persists between calls.</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nint globalCount = 0;\nvoid countGlobally() {\n    cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Global: I have been called &quot; &lt;&lt; ++globalCount &lt;&lt; &quot; times\\n&quot;;\n}\nvoid countStatically() {\n    static int count = 0;\n    cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Static: I have been called &quot; &lt;&lt; ++count &lt;&lt; &quot; times\\n&quot;;\n}\nint main() {\n    for (int i = 0; i &lt; 10; ++i) countGlobally();\n    for (int i = 0; i &lt; 10; ++i) countStatically();\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Global: I have been called 1 times</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Global: I have been called 2 times</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Global: I have been called 3 times</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Global: I have been called 4 times</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Global: I have been called 5 times</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Global: I have been called 6 times</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Global: I have been called 7 times</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Global: I have been called 8 times</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Global: I have been called 9 times</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Global: I have been called 10 times</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Static: I have been called 1 times</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Static: I have been called 2 times</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Static: I have been called 3 times</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Static: I have been called 4 times</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Static: I have been called 5 times</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Static: I have been called 6 times</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Static: I have been called 7 times</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Static: I have been called 8 times</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Static: I have been called 9 times</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Static: I have been called 10 times</p><p>The local static counter is more appropriate because only the function can modify it. An ordinary local variable is recreated on every call, so it would repeatedly start at zero. A global variable retains its value but any function can accidentally change it.</p>","exam":"mid","year":2025,"branch":"CS-203","src":"oops cs 2025.pdf (duplicates: two WhatsApp Image 2026-09-19 scans"},{"p":2,"q":"3","marks":"4","unit":1,"topic":"Student constructors and destructor","qh":"<p>You are required to implement a C++ class Student with the following requirements:</p><p>• Default Constructor: Initializes a student with default values (e.g., name = &quot;Unknown&quot;, age = 0).</p><p>• Parameterized Constructor: Allows initializing a student with a given name and age.</p><p>• Copy Constructor: Creates a new student record by copying the details of an existing student.</p><p>• Dynamic Constructor: Allocate memory dynamically for storing the student’s name.</p><p>• Destructor: Releases dynamically allocated memory when a student object is destroyed.</p>","sh":"<p>Here “dynamic constructor” means that construction allocates the name array dynamically. The copy constructor makes a separate copy; otherwise two objects would own the same array and both destructors would delete it. Copy assignment is included to complete the ownership rule.</p><pre>#include &lt;cstring&gt;\n#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass Student {\n    char* name;\n    int age;\n    static char* duplicate(const char* s) {\n        char* p = new char[strlen(s) + 1];\n        strcpy(p, s);\n        return p;\n    }\npublic:\n    Student() : name(duplicate(&quot;Unknown&quot;)), age(0) {}\n    Student(const char* n, int a) : name(duplicate(n)), age(a) {}\n    Student(const Student&amp; other)\n        : name(duplicate(other.name)), age(other.age) {}\n    Student&amp; operator=(const Student&amp; other) {\n        if (this != &amp;other) {\n            char* replacement = duplicate(other.name);\n            delete[] name;\n            name = replacement;\n            age = other.age;\n        }\n        return *this;\n    }\n    ~Student() { delete[] name; }\n    void show() const { cout &lt;&lt; name &lt;&lt; &quot;, &quot; &lt;&lt; age &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;; }\n};\nint main() {\n    Student a;\n    Student b(&quot;Riya&quot;, 19);\n    Student c(b);\n    a.show();\n    b.show();\n    c.show();\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Unknown, 0</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Riya, 19</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Riya, 19</p>","exam":"mid","year":2025,"branch":"CS-203","src":"oops cs 2025.pdf (duplicates: two WhatsApp Image 2026-09-19 scans"},{"p":2,"q":"4","marks":"4","unit":1,"topic":"Class-to-class time conversion","qh":"<p>Write a program for type conversion to convert class ‘Time’ with data members as hours and mins into class ‘Minutes’ with data member as totalminutes. Implement via both casting operator as well as via constructor. Comment one of the implementation methods.</p>","sh":"<p>Total minutes = 60 × hours + mins. The source class may define a conversion operator, or the destination class may define a converting constructor. This program demonstrates both explicitly to avoid ambiguity when both conversion paths exist. Assumption: hours and minutes are nonnegative.</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass Minutes;\nclass Time {\n    int hours, mins;\npublic:\n    Time(int h, int m) : hours(h), mins(m) {}\n    int total() const { return 60 * hours + mins; }\n    operator Minutes() const;\n};\nclass Minutes {\n    int totalminutes;\npublic:\n    Minutes(int total) : totalminutes(total) {}\n    // Destination-class converting constructor: Time -&gt; Minutes.\n    Minutes(const Time&amp; t) : totalminutes(t.total()) {}\n    int value() const { return totalminutes; }\n};\nTime::operator Minutes() const { return Minutes(total()); }\nint main() {\n    Time t(2, 35);\n    Minutes byOperator = t.operator Minutes();\n    Minutes byConstructor(t);\n    cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Operator: &quot; &lt;&lt; byOperator.value() &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n    cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Constructor: &quot; &lt;&lt; byConstructor.value() &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Operator: 155</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Constructor: 155</p>","exam":"mid","year":2025,"branch":"CS-203","src":"oops cs 2025.pdf (duplicates: two WhatsApp Image 2026-09-19 scans"},{"p":2,"q":"5","marks":"4","unit":1,"topic":"Staff inheritance database","qh":"<p>An educational institution wishes to maintain a database of its employees. The database is divided into a number of classes whose hierarchical relationships are shown in Fig. below. The figure also shows the minimum information required for each class. Specify all the classes and define functions to create the database and retrieve individual information as and when required.</p><p class=\"ln i0\">staff (code, name)</p><pre class=\"grid\">             /       |       \\\nteacher (subject,   typist   officer (grade)\n publication)      (speed)\n                  /       \\\n              regular   casual (daily wages)</pre>","sh":"<p>staff is the common base. teacher, officer, and typist derive from it; regular and casual derive from typist. Each object stores its required fields. Database::add creates a record, and Database::find retrieves it by code. Virtual print() selects the correct record format through a base pointer.</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n#include &lt;memory&gt;\n#include &lt;string&gt;\n#include &lt;utility&gt;\n#include &lt;vector&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass Staff {\n    int code;\n    string name;\nprotected:\n    void printBase() const { cout &lt;&lt; code &lt;&lt; &#x27; &#x27; &lt;&lt; name; }\npublic:\n    Staff(int c, string n) : code(c), name(move(n)) {}\n    virtual ~Staff() = default;\n    int getCode() const { return code; }\n    virtual void print() const = 0;\n};\nclass Teacher : public Staff {\n    string subject, publication;\npublic:\n    Teacher(int c, string n, string s, string p)\n        : Staff(c, move(n)), subject(move(s)), publication(move(p)) {}\n    void print() const override {\n        printBase(); cout &lt;&lt; &quot; Teacher &quot; &lt;&lt; subject &lt;&lt; &#x27; &#x27; &lt;&lt; publication &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n    }\n};\nclass Officer : public Staff {\n    string grade;\npublic:\n    Officer(int c, string n, string g)\n        : Staff(c, move(n)), grade(move(g)) {}\n    void print() const override {\n        printBase(); cout &lt;&lt; &quot; Officer &quot; &lt;&lt; grade &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n    }\n};\nclass Typist : public Staff {\nprotected:\n    int speed;\n    void printTypist() const { printBase(); cout &lt;&lt; &quot; Typist &quot; &lt;&lt; speed; }\npublic:\n    Typist(int c, string n, int s) : Staff(c, move(n)), speed(s) {}\n    void print() const override { printTypist(); cout &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;; }\n};\nclass Regular : public Typist {\npublic:\n    Regular(int c, string n, int s) : Typist(c, move(n), s) {}\n    void print() const override { printTypist(); cout &lt;&lt; &quot; Regular\\n&quot;; }\n};\nclass Casual : public Typist {\n    double dailyWages;\npublic:\n    Casual(int c, string n, int s, double w)\n        : Typist(c, move(n), s), dailyWages(w) {}\n    void print() const override {\n        printTypist(); cout &lt;&lt; &quot; Casual &quot; &lt;&lt; dailyWages &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n    }\n};\nclass Database {\n    vector&lt;unique_ptr&lt;Staff&gt;&gt; records;\npublic:\n    void add(unique_ptr&lt;Staff&gt; person) { records.push_back(move(person)); }\n    const Staff* find(int code) const {\n        for (const auto&amp; person : records)\n            if (person-&gt;getCode() == code) return person.get();\n        return nullptr;\n    }\n};\nint main() {\n    Database db;\n    db.add(make_unique&lt;Teacher&gt;(101, &quot;Asha&quot;, &quot;OOP&quot;, &quot;Book&quot;));\n    db.add(make_unique&lt;Officer&gt;(102, &quot;Ravi&quot;, &quot;A&quot;));\n    db.add(make_unique&lt;Regular&gt;(103, &quot;Neha&quot;, 60));\n    db.add(make_unique&lt;Casual&gt;(104, &quot;Amit&quot;, 45, 500));\n    for (int code : {101, 102, 103, 104}) {\n        const Staff* person = db.find(code);\n        if (person) person-&gt;print();\n    }\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">101 Asha Teacher OOP Book</p><p class=\"ln i0\">102 Ravi Officer A</p><p class=\"ln i0\">103 Neha Typist 60 Regular</p><p class=\"ln i0\">104 Amit Typist 45 Casual 500</p>","exam":"mid","year":2025,"branch":"CS-203","src":"oops cs 2025.pdf (duplicates: two WhatsApp Image 2026-09-19 scans"},{"p":3,"q":"1","marks":"4","unit":0,"topic":"Library design and this","qh":"<p>[a] Suppose you are developing a library management system. Explain how the design would differ if implemented using structured programming versus object-oriented programming.</p><p>[b] Explain the significance of this pointer in C++ with an example.</p>","sh":"<p>[a] In a structured design, records such as Book and Member are passed to separate procedures such as issueBook(book, member) and returnBook(book). In an OOP design, a Book object holds its own details and operations, while a Member or Library object coordinates borrowing. Private fields can enforce rules, such as preventing a book from being issued twice; classes can be reused for different book and member types. [b] this is the implicit pointer to the object on which a non-static member function was called. It distinguishes a data member from a same-named parameter and enables returning the current object for chaining. Static members have no this because they are not called on one particular object.</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass Book {\n    int id;\npublic:\n    Book&amp; setId(int id) { this-&gt;id = id; return *this; }\n    void show() const { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Book ID: &quot; &lt;&lt; id &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;; }\n};\nint main() {\n    Book b;\n    b.setId(42).show();\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Book ID: 42</p>","exam":"mid","year":2025,"branch":"SE205","src":"OOPS MIDSEM 25 (SE).pdf"},{"p":3,"q":"2","marks":"4","unit":0,"topic":"Rectangle constructor overload","qh":"<p>Define a class Rectangle with Private data members: length and breadth. Define a default constructor that initializes both length and breadth to 1 and a parameterized constructor that initializes length and breadth with user-provided values. A member function displayArea() to calculate and print the area.</p><p>[a] Create objects using both the default constructor and the parameterized constructor, and display their areas. [b] Demonstrate what happens if you define only the parameterized constructor in the class, but still try to create an object without passing arguments</p>","sh":"<p>[a] Area is length × breadth. Both constructor overloads initialize the private fields before displayArea() is called.</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass Rectangle {\n    int length, breadth;\npublic:\n    Rectangle() : length(1), breadth(1) {}\n    Rectangle(int l, int b) : length(l), breadth(b) {}\n    void displayArea() const { cout &lt;&lt; length * breadth &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;; }\n};\nint main() {\n    Rectangle defaultRectangle;\n    Rectangle specifiedRectangle(3, 4);\n    defaultRectangle.displayArea();\n    specifiedRectangle.displayArea();\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">1</p><p class=\"ln i0\">12</p><p>[b] If the class declares only Rectangle(int, int), the compiler does not implicitly declare a no-argument constructor. Thus Rectangle r; fails to compile with “no matching constructor for initialization of Rectangle.” Add an explicit default constructor (as above), provide default arguments, or pass two arguments. This is an intentional compile-time error, so there is no runtime output for that attempted declaration.</p>","exam":"mid","year":2025,"branch":"SE205","src":"OOPS MIDSEM 25 (SE).pdf"},{"p":3,"q":"3","marks":"4","unit":1,"topic":"Add and normalize time","qh":"<p>Define a class Time with the following specifications:</p><p>• Private data members: hours and minutes.</p><p>• Parameterized constructor to initialize the time.</p><p>• Overload the + operator to add two Time objects. If minutes ≥ 60, convert the extra minutes into hours.</p><p>• A member function display() to show time in HH:MM format.</p><p>Write a C++ program to create two Time objects, add them using the overloaded operator, and display the result.</p>","sh":"<p>Assumption: hours and minutes are nonnegative. Add the total minutes, then normalize with quotient and remainder on division by 60. setw(2) and setfill(&#x27;0&#x27;) produce HH:MM format.</p><pre>#include &lt;iomanip&gt;\n#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass Time {\n    int hours, minutes;\npublic:\n    Time(int h, int m) : hours(h + m / 60), minutes(m % 60) {}\n    Time operator+(const Time&amp; other) const {\n        return Time(hours + other.hours, minutes + other.minutes);\n    }\n    void display() const {\n        cout &lt;&lt; setfill(&#x27;0&#x27;) &lt;&lt; setw(2) &lt;&lt; hours &lt;&lt; &#x27;:&#x27;\n             &lt;&lt; setw(2) &lt;&lt; minutes &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n    }\n};\nint main() {\n    Time first(1, 45), second(2, 30);\n    Time sum = first + second;\n    sum.display();\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">04:15</p><p>Check: 1:45 + 2:30 = 3 h + 75 min = 4:15.</p>","exam":"mid","year":2025,"branch":"SE205","src":"OOPS MIDSEM 25 (SE).pdf"},{"p":3,"q":"4","marks":"4","unit":1,"topic":"Polymorphism and dynamic array","qh":"<p>[a] Differentiate between compile-time polymorphism and run-time polymorphism with suitable examples. [b] Write a program to dynamically allocate memory for an array of 5 integers using new. Accept values from the user, display them, and then release the memory using delete operator.</p>","sh":"<p>[a]</p><pre>Feature             Compile-time                  Run-time\nBinding             Before execution             During execution\nC++ mechanism       Overloading/templates        Virtual-function override\nExample             print(int), print(double)    Shape* p = &amp;circle; p-&gt;area()\nChoice depends on   Argument types               Actual object type</pre><p>For example, the compiler picks print(5) from overloaded print functions, while a call to virtual area() through Shape* invokes Circle::area() when the pointed-to object is a Circle.</p><p>[b] The allocation uses new int[5]; the matching release must be delete[], not scalar delete. Sample input is 1 2</p><pre>3 4 5.\n#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nint main() {\n    int* values = new int[5];\n    for (int i = 0; i &lt; 5; ++i) cin &gt;&gt; values[i];\n    cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Values:&quot;;\n    for (int i = 0; i &lt; 5; ++i) cout &lt;&lt; &#x27; &#x27; &lt;&lt; values[i];\n    cout &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n    delete[] values;\n}</pre><p>Output for the stated input:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Values: 1 2 3 4 5</p>","exam":"mid","year":2025,"branch":"SE205","src":"OOPS MIDSEM 25 (SE).pdf"},{"p":3,"q":"5","marks":"4","unit":1,"topic":"Pure virtual and pointer errors","qh":"<p>[a] When do we make a virtual function “pure”? What are the implications of making a function a pure virtual function?</p><p>[b] Correct the errors in the following programs.</p><pre>class test\n{\n    private:\n        int m;\n    public:\n        void getdata()\n        {\n            cout &lt;&lt;&quot;Enter number:&quot;;\n            cin &gt;&gt; m;\n        }\n        void display()\n        {\n            cout &lt;&lt; m;\n        }\n};\nmain()\n{\n    test T;\n    T-&gt;getdata();\n    T-&gt;display();\n    test *p;\n    p = new test;\n    p.getdata();\n    (*p).display();\n}</pre>","sh":"<p>[a] Make a virtual function pure by declaring it virtual ReturnType f(...) = 0; when the base supplies an interface but cannot provide a meaningful implementation for every derived class. The base becomes abstract and cannot be instantiated. A derived class must implement all inherited pure virtual functions before it can be instantiated. Calls through a base pointer/reference still use dynamic dispatch. For example, Shape::area() can be pure while Circle::area() computes πr².</p><p>[b] Include &lt;iostream&gt;; use int main(); use . for object T and -&gt; for pointer p; release the dynamically created object. (*p).display() is already valid and equivalent to p-&gt;display(). The original getdata() needs input. With sample input 7 9, the corrected program is:</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass test {\n    int m;\npublic:\n    void getdata() {\n        cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Enter number: &quot;;\n        cin &gt;&gt; m;\n    }\n    void display() const { cout &lt;&lt; m &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;; }\n};\nint main() {\n    test T;\n    T.getdata();\n    T.display();\n    test* p = new test;\n    p-&gt;getdata();\n    (*p).display();\n    delete p;\n}</pre><p>Output for the stated input:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Enter number: 7</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Enter number: 9</p>","exam":"mid","year":2025,"branch":"SE205","src":"OOPS MIDSEM 25 (SE).pdf"},{"p":4,"q":"1","marks":"10","unit":0,"topic":"Short answers: abstraction, specifiers","qh":"<p>Answer any 5 of the following: I. What is abstraction in OOP? II. Why is private inheritance rarely used? III. List two strictly Java-based features. IV. Explain the role of the scope resolution operator ::. V. What is the purpose of access specifiers in a class? VI. Differentiate between shallow copy and deep copy. VII. What do you mean by static data members?</p>","sh":"<p>I. Abstraction shows only the essential features of an object (its interface) and hides implementation details. A Stack class exposes push() and pop() but hides whether it uses an array or a linked list. In C++ it is achieved with classes, access specifiers and abstract classes.</p><p>II. Private inheritance (class D : private B) makes every public/protected member of B private in D, so it models &quot;implemented-in-terms-of&quot;, not &quot;is-a&quot;: a D* cannot be converted to B* outside D, and clients cannot use B&#x27;s interface through D. Composition (a B member) gives the same effect more clearly, so private inheritance is rarely needed (only to override a virtual function or to reach protected members of the base).</p><p>III. Two features that exist only in Java: (1) the JVM/bytecode, which makes compiled code platform independent (&quot;write once, run anywhere&quot;); (2) automatic garbage collection. Others: packages with import, interfaces, built-in multithreading (Thread), no pointers.</p><p>IV. :: (scope resolution) (a) accesses a global name hidden by a local one (::x), (b) defines a member function outside its class (void A::show() {...}), (c) names a base-class member to remove ambiguity (B::f()), (d) reaches a namespace member (std::cout) or a static member (A::count).</p><p>V. Access specifiers (public, private, protected) control which code may use a member. private data is hidden (encapsulation/data hiding), public members form the interface, and protected members are visible to the class and its derived classes.</p><p>VI. A shallow copy copies member values bit by bit, so two objects holding a pointer end up pointing to the same heap block (double delete, unintended sharing). A deep copy allocates a new block and copies the pointed-to data so each object owns its own memory; it needs a user-defined copy constructor and assignment operator.</p><pre>#include &lt;cstring&gt;\n#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass Str {\n    char* p;\npublic:\n    Str(const char* s) { p = new char[strlen(s) + 1]; strcpy(p, s); }\n    Str(const Str&amp; o) { p = new char[strlen(o.p) + 1]; strcpy(p, o.p); }  // deep copy\n    ~Str() { delete[] p; }\n    void set(char c) { p[0] = c; }\n    void show() const { cout &lt;&lt; p &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;; }\n};\nint main() {\n    Str a(&quot;hello&quot;);\n    Str b = a;      // deep copy: separate memory\n    b.set(&#x27;J&#x27;);\n    a.show();\n    b.show();\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">hello</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Jello</p><p>VII. A static data member is declared with static inside a class; only one copy exists, shared by all objects, and it exists even when no object has been created. It is defined once outside the class (int A::count = 0;) and accessed as A::count. It is used for counters and shared settings.</p>","exam":"end","year":2025,"branch":"IT","src":"IT ENDSEM 2025.pdf (duplicates: hash-named copy 2783b64b...IT ENDSEM 2025.pdf"},{"p":4,"q":"2","marks":"9","unit":1,"topic":"Binding, JVM, Java interfaces, new/delete","qh":"<p>Answer any 3: I. Explain early binding and late binding. How does virtual function help in late binding? II. JVM is critical for Java programs. Justify. III. How does Java support multiple inheritance? Explain in detail with complete code. IV. Using new and delete operators, show dynamic allocation and deallocation in a C++ code.</p>","sh":"<p>I. Early (static) binding: the function to call is fixed at compile time from the static type of the pointer/reference (ordinary functions, overloaded functions, non-virtual members). It is fast. Late (dynamic) binding: the function is chosen at run time from the actual type of the object. In C++ a function declared virtual in the base is called through a per-class vtable; each object holds a hidden vptr, and p-&gt;f() looks up f in the vtable of the real object. Hence a base pointer can invoke the derived override.</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass Base {\npublic:\n    void hello() { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Base::hello (early)\\n&quot;; }\n    virtual void show() { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Base::show\\n&quot;; }\n    virtual ~Base() {}\n};\nclass Derived : public Base {\npublic:\n    void hello() { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Derived::hello\\n&quot;; }\n    void show() override { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Derived::show (late)\\n&quot;; }\n};\nint main() {\n    Derived d;\n    Base* p = &amp;d;\n    p-&gt;hello();   // bound at compile time to Base::hello\n    p-&gt;show();    // bound at run time to Derived::show\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><pre>Base::hello (early)\nDerived::show (late)</pre><p>II. The JVM (Java Virtual Machine) is the abstract machine that runs .class bytecode, so it is what makes Java work:</p><p>(1) platform independence: the same bytecode runs on any OS that has a JVM; (2) the class loader loads and links classes, and the bytecode verifier rejects illegal code (security); (3) the JIT compiler turns hot bytecode into native code for speed; (4) memory management: heap, stack, method area and automatic garbage collection; (5) thread management and exception handling at run time. Without a JVM, Java bytecode cannot execute.</p><p>III. A Java class can extend only one class, so multiple inheritance of state is not allowed (this avoids the diamond problem). Multiple inheritance of type is supported by letting a class implement several interfaces (class C implements A, B). Since Java 8 an interface may have default methods; if two interfaces provide the same default method, the class must override it and may choose one with A.super.show().</p><pre>interface Printable {\n    void print();\n}\ninterface Showable {\n    void show();\n    default void info() { System.out.println(&quot;Showable default info&quot;); }\n}\nclass Document implements Printable, Showable {   // multiple inheritance via interfaces\n    public void print() { System.out.println(&quot;Printing document&quot;); }\n    public void show()  { System.out.println(&quot;Showing document&quot;); }\n}\npublic class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Document d = new Document();\n        d.print();\n        d.show();\n        d.info();\n    }\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Printing document</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Showing document</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Showable default info</p><p>IV. new allocates memory on the heap and calls the constructor; delete calls the destructor and frees it. Use new[]/delete[] for arrays.</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass Point {\n    int x, y;\npublic:\n    Point(int a, int b) : x(a), y(b) { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Point constructed\\n&quot;; }\n    ~Point() { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Point destroyed\\n&quot;; }\n    void show() const { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;(&quot; &lt;&lt; x &lt;&lt; &quot;, &quot; &lt;&lt; y &lt;&lt; &quot;)\\n&quot;; }\n};\nint main() {\n    int* n = new int(25);          // single int, initialised to 25\n    int* arr = new int[5];         // array of 5 ints\n    for (int i = 0; i &lt; 5; ++i) arr[i] = (i + 1) * 10;\n    cout &lt;&lt; *n &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n    for (int i = 0; i &lt; 5; ++i) cout &lt;&lt; arr[i] &lt;&lt; &#x27; &#x27;;\n    cout &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n    Point* p = new Point(3, 4);    // object on the heap\n    p-&gt;show();\n    delete n;\n    delete[] arr;\n    delete p;                      // runs ~Point()\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">25</p><p class=\"ln i0\">10 20 30 40 50</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Point constructed</p><p class=\"ln i0\">(3, 4)</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Point destroyed</p>","exam":"end","year":2025,"branch":"IT","src":"IT ENDSEM 2025.pdf (duplicates: hash-named copy 2783b64b...IT ENDSEM 2025.pdf"},{"p":4,"q":"3","marks":"8","unit":1,"topic":"Template calculator and exceptions","qh":"<p>Answer the following using C++ code. I. Create a template class Calculator that performs basic operations like add, subtract, multiply, and divide for int and float types. [4] II. How are exceptions handled in C++? How is rethrowing an exception different from a catch-all block? Explain with code. [4]</p>","sh":"<p>I. One class template serves both int and float; the type is chosen at the instantiation Calculator&lt;int&gt; / Calculator&lt;float&gt;. Division by zero is reported with an exception.</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n#include &lt;stdexcept&gt;\nusing namespace std;\ntemplate &lt;class T&gt;\nclass Calculator {\n    T a, b;\npublic:\n    Calculator(T x, T y) : a(x), b(y) {}\n    T add() const { return a + b; }\n    T subtract() const { return a - b; }\n    T multiply() const { return a * b; }\n    T divide() const {\n        if (b == 0) throw runtime_error(&quot;division by zero&quot;);\n        return a / b;\n    }\n};\nint main() {\n    Calculator&lt;int&gt; ci(20, 6);\n    cout &lt;&lt; &quot;int:   &quot; &lt;&lt; ci.add() &lt;&lt; &#x27; &#x27; &lt;&lt; ci.subtract() &lt;&lt; &#x27; &#x27;\n         &lt;&lt; ci.multiply() &lt;&lt; &#x27; &#x27; &lt;&lt; ci.divide() &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n    Calculator&lt;float&gt; cf(7.5f, 2.5f);\n    cout &lt;&lt; &quot;float: &quot; &lt;&lt; cf.add() &lt;&lt; &#x27; &#x27; &lt;&lt; cf.subtract() &lt;&lt; &#x27; &#x27;\n         &lt;&lt; cf.multiply() &lt;&lt; &#x27; &#x27; &lt;&lt; cf.divide() &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n    try {\n        Calculator&lt;int&gt; bad(5, 0);\n        bad.divide();\n    } catch (const exception&amp; e) {\n        cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Error: &quot; &lt;&lt; e.what() &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n    }\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">int:   26 14 120 3</p><p class=\"ln i0\">float: 10 5 18.75 3</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Error: division by zero</p><p>II. C++ exception handling uses three keywords: try encloses code that may fail, throw signals an error by throwing an object, and catch handlers (matched by type, in order) deal with it. Stack unwinding destroys local objects between throw and the handler. An uncaught exception calls terminate().</p><p>catch (...) (catch-all) catches any exception type but gives no access to the exception object; it is normally the last handler. A rethrow is the statement throw; inside a handler: it passes the same exception object on to an outer handler after partial handling (logging, clean-up). A catch-all stops the exception unless it also rethrows.</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nvoid risky(int x) {\n    try {\n        if (x == 1) throw 10;           // int\n        if (x == 2) throw 2.5;          // double\n    } catch (int e) {\n        cout &lt;&lt; &quot;inner caught int &quot; &lt;&lt; e &lt;&lt; &quot;, rethrowing\\n&quot;;\n        throw;                          // rethrow the same exception\n    }\n}\nint main() {\n    for (int x = 1; x &lt;= 2; ++x) {\n        try {\n            risky(x);\n        } catch (int e) {\n            cout &lt;&lt; &quot;outer caught int &quot; &lt;&lt; e &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n        } catch (...) {                 // catch-all: any other type\n            cout &lt;&lt; &quot;catch-all handled an unknown exception\\n&quot;;\n        }\n    }\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">inner caught int 10, rethrowing</p><p class=\"ln i0\">outer caught int 10</p><p class=\"ln i0\">catch-all handled an unknown exception</p>","exam":"end","year":2025,"branch":"IT","src":"IT ENDSEM 2025.pdf (duplicates: hash-named copy 2783b64b...IT ENDSEM 2025.pdf"},{"p":4,"q":"4","marks":"10","unit":2,"topic":"Differentiate (any 4)","qh":"<p>Differentiate between (any 4): I. Interface in JAVA and abstract class in C++ with the help of code. II. Package in JAVA and namespace in C++. Create either a user-defined package or a user-defined namespace. III. JRE vs JDK vs</p><p>JVM. IV. Virtual destructor and virtual functions in C++. V. Default in JAVA vs private in JAVA.</p>","sh":"<p>I. Interface (Java) vs abstract class (C++)</p><pre>Aspect              Java interface                     C++ abstract class\nMethods             abstract (plus default/static)     at least one pure virtual (= 0); may\n                                                       also have ordinary, implemented methods\nData members        only public static final consts    any data members, any access\nConstructors        none                               allowed (called by derived classes)\nInheritance         a class may implement many         a class may derive from many\n                                                       (multiple inheritance, diamond issues)\nKeyword             interface / implements             class with a pure virtual function\n#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass Shape {                        // abstract class\nprotected:\n    int id;\npublic:\n    Shape(int i) : id(i) {}\n    virtual double area() const = 0; // pure virtual\n    void tag() const { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;shape &quot; &lt;&lt; id &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;; }\n    virtual ~Shape() {}\n};\nclass Square : public Shape {\n    double s;\npublic:\n    Square(int i, double side) : Shape(i), s(side) {}\n    double area() const override { return s * s; }\n};\nint main() {\n    Square q(1, 3);\n    q.tag();\n    cout &lt;&lt; q.area() &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><pre>shape 1\n9\ninterface Shape {                    // Java interface\n    double area();                   // implicitly public abstract\n}\nclass Square implements Shape {\n    double s = 3;\n    public double area() { return s * s; }\n}</pre><p>II. Java package groups related classes and controls access and the class path (one package = one directory; package mypack; as the first statement, used with import). C++ namespace only groups names to avoid clashes (no directory mapping, no access protection beyond the names; used with using or ::). User-defined namespace:</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nnamespace Geometry {\n    const double PI = 3.14159;\n    double circleArea(double r) { return PI * r * r; }\n}\nnamespace Physics {\n    double circleArea(double r) { return r * r; }   // same name, different namespace\n}\nint main() {\n    cout &lt;&lt; Geometry::circleArea(2) &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n    cout &lt;&lt; Physics::circleArea(2) &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">12.5664</p><p class=\"ln i0\">4</p><p>User-defined Java package: file mypack/Greeter.java begins with package mypack; and declares public class</p><pre>Greeter { public void hi() { System.out.println(&quot;Hi&quot;); } }; another file uses import mypack.Greeter; and\ncalls new Greeter().hi();.</pre><p>III. JVM vs JRE vs JDK</p><pre>JVM  Java Virtual Machine: executes bytecode (class loader, verifier, interpreter/JIT, GC).\nJRE  Java Runtime Environment = JVM + core class libraries (rt.jar/java.base). Needed to RUN programs.\nJDK  Java Development Kit = JRE + development tools (javac, jar, javadoc, jdb). Needed to WRITE and compile.</pre><p>JDK contains JRE, and JRE contains JVM.</p><p>IV. A virtual function is a base-class member function that can be overridden and is resolved at run time (dynamic dispatch). A virtual destructor makes delete basePtr call the derived destructor first and then the base destructor. Without it, deleting a derived object through a base pointer is undefined behaviour (derived part is not destroyed, resource leak). A virtual destructor is a special virtual function: it is chosen by the object&#x27;s real type, but it has no return value/arguments and the base destructor still runs after the derived one.</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass B {\npublic:\n    virtual ~B() { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;~B\\n&quot;; }\n};\nclass D : public B {\npublic:\n    ~D() { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;~D\\n&quot;; }\n};\nint main() {\n    B* p = new D;\n    delete p;      // ~D then ~B because the destructor is virtual\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">~D</p><p class=\"ln i0\">~B</p><p>V. In Java, private members are visible only inside the declaring class. A member with no modifier (&quot;default&quot; or package-private) is visible to every class in the same package but not to classes in other packages (even subclasses). So private is stricter than default. (default is also a keyword for interface methods and switch, a different use.)</p>","exam":"end","year":2025,"branch":"IT","src":"IT ENDSEM 2025.pdf (duplicates: hash-named copy 2783b64b...IT ENDSEM 2025.pdf"},{"p":4,"q":"5","marks":"3","unit":3,"topic":"Multithreading in Java","qh":"<p>What is multithreading in JAVA? Implement using any one of the options.</p>","sh":"<p>Multithreading is the concurrent execution of two or more threads (light-weight units of execution) within one program, sharing the same memory. It improves CPU utilisation and responsiveness. A thread is created (a) by extending Thread and overriding run(), or (b) by implementing Runnable and passing it to a Thread. start() creates a new call stack and calls run(); join() waits for a thread to finish.</p><pre>class Worker extends Thread {\n    private String name;\n    Worker(String name) { this.name = name; }\n    public void run() {\n        for (int i = 1; i &lt;= 3; i++) {\n            System.out.println(name + &quot; step &quot; + i);\n        }\n    }\n}\npublic class ThreadDemo {\n    public static void main(String[] args) throws InterruptedException {\n        Worker a = new Worker(&quot;A&quot;);\n        Worker b = new Worker(&quot;B&quot;);\n        a.start();\n        b.start();\n        a.join();\n        b.join();\n        System.out.println(&quot;Both threads finished&quot;);\n    }\n}</pre><p>Output (the lines of A and B may interleave differently in each run; each thread prints its own steps in order):</p><p class=\"ln i0\">A step 1</p><p class=\"ln i0\">A step 2</p><p class=\"ln i0\">A step 3</p><p class=\"ln i0\">B step 1</p><p class=\"ln i0\">B step 2</p><p class=\"ln i0\">B step 3</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Both threads finished</p>","exam":"end","year":2025,"branch":"IT","src":"IT ENDSEM 2025.pdf (duplicates: hash-named copy 2783b64b...IT ENDSEM 2025.pdf"},{"p":5,"q":"1","marks":"6","unit":0,"topic":"this, initializer list, copy constructor","qh":"<p>Answer the following with suitable example (a) Why can&#x27;t we have &#x27;this&#x27; pointer with static members? Explain with an example. (b) Show the use of initializer list in inheritance using a complete C++ code. (c) How is copy constructor used? Explain w.r.t the syntax used.</p>","sh":"<p>(a) this is a hidden pointer to the object on which a non-static member function was called. A static member function belongs to the class, not to any object, and may be called with no object at all (A::f()), so there is no object for this to point to. A static function can therefore use only static data and must receive an object explicitly if it needs one.</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass A {\n    int x;\n    static int count;\npublic:\n    A(int v) : x(v) { ++count; }\n    void show() const { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;x=&quot; &lt;&lt; this-&gt;x &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;; }   // this is available\n    static int total() {\n        // return this-&gt;x;   // error: &#x27;this&#x27; is not available in a static function\n        return count;\n    }\n};\nint A::count = 0;\nint main() {\n    A a(5), b(7);\n    a.show();\n    cout &lt;&lt; &quot;objects: &quot; &lt;&lt; A::total() &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;   // no object needed\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">x=5</p><p class=\"ln i0\">objects: 2</p><p>(b) A base-class constructor with parameters must be called from the derived-class constructor&#x27;s initializer list (after the colon), and const/reference members can only be initialised there. Base subobjects are initialised first, then members in declaration order.</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n#include &lt;string&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass Person {\n    string name;\npublic:\n    Person(string n) : name(n) { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Person(&quot; &lt;&lt; name &lt;&lt; &quot;)\\n&quot;; }\n    void show() const { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Name: &quot; &lt;&lt; name; }\n};\nclass Student : public Person {\n    int roll;\n    const int year;\npublic:\n    Student(string n, int r, int y) : Person(n), roll(r), year(y) {\n        cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Student(&quot; &lt;&lt; roll &lt;&lt; &quot;)\\n&quot;;\n    }\n    void show() const { Person::show(); cout &lt;&lt; &quot;, Roll: &quot; &lt;&lt; roll &lt;&lt; &quot;, Year: &quot; &lt;&lt; year &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;; }\n};\nint main() {\n    Student s(&quot;Asha&quot;, 21, 2);\n    s.show();\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Person(Asha)</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Student(21)</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Name: Asha, Roll: 21, Year: 2</p><p>(c) A copy constructor creates a new object as a copy of an existing one. Syntax: ClassName(const ClassName &amp;obj); The parameter must be a reference (passing by value would call the copy constructor recursively) and const so that the source is not modified. It is called when an object is initialised from another (B b = a; or B b(a);), when an object is passed to a function by value, and when it is returned by value (may be elided). If not defined, the compiler gives a member-wise (shallow) copy.</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass Box {\n    int w, h;\npublic:\n    Box(int a, int b) : w(a), h(b) {}\n    Box(const Box&amp; o) : w(o.w), h(o.h) { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;copy constructor called\\n&quot;; }\n    int area() const { return w * h; }\n};\nint main() {\n    Box a(3, 4);\n    Box b = a;          // copy constructor\n    Box c(a);           // copy constructor\n    cout &lt;&lt; b.area() &lt;&lt; &#x27; &#x27; &lt;&lt; c.area() &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">copy constructor called</p><p class=\"ln i0\">copy constructor called</p><p class=\"ln i0\">12 12</p>","exam":"mid","year":2025,"branch":"IT","src":"2025 IT midsem.pdf (duplicate: hash-named 4c8c60a2...2025 IT midsem.pdf"},{"p":5,"q":"2","marks":"6","unit":1,"topic":"Overloading vs overriding, templates, destructors","qh":"<p>(a) Method overloading is different from method overriding. Explain. [2] (b) Where can we use templates in coding? Explain [2] (c) Can destructors be overloaded? Why or why not? [2]</p>","sh":"<p>(a) Overloading: several functions with the same name in the same scope but different parameter lists (number/types/order); resolved at compile time (compile-time polymorphism); return type alone is not enough. Overriding: a derived class redefines a base-class virtual function with the same signature; resolved at run time through the vtable (run-time polymorphism).</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass A {\npublic:\n    void print(int x) { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;int &quot; &lt;&lt; x &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;; }          // overloaded\n    void print(double x) { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;double &quot; &lt;&lt; x &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;; }\n    virtual void who() { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;A\\n&quot;; }\n};\nclass B : public A {\npublic:\n    void who() override { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;B\\n&quot;; }                       // overridden\n};\nint main() {\n    B b; A* p = &amp;b;\n    b.print(3); b.print(2.5);\n    p-&gt;who();\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">int 3</p><p class=\"ln i0\">double 2.5</p><p class=\"ln i0\">B</p><p>(b) Templates give generic code that works for any type, resolved at compile time. Uses: function templates for algorithms (max, swap, sort); class templates for containers (Stack&lt;T&gt;, vector&lt;T&gt;, pair&lt;K,V&gt;); generic utility/numeric code that must work for int, float, double without rewriting; and the STL itself. Benefits: code reuse, type safety, no run-time cost.</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\ntemplate &lt;class T&gt;\nT maxOf(T a, T b) { return a &gt; b ? a : b; }\nint main() {\n    cout &lt;&lt; maxOf(3, 9) &lt;&lt; &#x27; &#x27; &lt;&lt; maxOf(2.5, 1.5) &lt;&lt; &#x27; &#x27; &lt;&lt; maxOf(&#x27;a&#x27;, &#x27;z&#x27;) &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">9 2.5 z</p><p>(c) No. A destructor has no parameters and no return type and there is exactly one per class (~ClassName()), so there is no way to give it two different signatures. The destructor is called automatically, never explicitly with arguments, so overloading would also make it ambiguous which version the compiler should call at the end of an object&#x27;s lifetime. A destructor can be virtual, though.</p>","exam":"mid","year":2025,"branch":"IT","src":"2025 IT midsem.pdf (duplicate: hash-named 4c8c60a2...2025 IT midsem.pdf"},{"p":5,"q":"3","marks":"8","unit":1,"topic":"Pre/post increment, diamond problem, template overloading","qh":"<p>(a) Suppose you have a student class with marks as one of the data members. Now, you want to increase the marks of the student (object S1) by 1 and you want to execute the following statements in main(): S1++; //increase marks of student S1 by 1 using post increment ++S1; //increase marks of student S1 by 1 using pre increment. Write the complete code in C++ so that both statements can coexist in main() function and increase the data member&#x27;s value by 1. [3] (b) What is diamond shaped class inheritance problem and how is it solved? [3] (c) Function templates can be overloaded too. Explain and justify. [2]</p>","sh":"<p>(a) Both forms are provided by overloading operator++. The prefix form is operator++(); the postfix form takes a dummy int parameter, operator++(int), which only distinguishes it from the prefix form. Prefix returns the updated object by reference; postfix returns the old value (a copy).</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass Student {\n    int marks;\npublic:\n    Student(int m) : marks(m) {}\n    Student&amp; operator++() { ++marks; return *this; }            // ++S1\n    Student operator++(int) { Student old = *this; ++marks; return old; }  // S1++\n    void show() const { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;marks = &quot; &lt;&lt; marks &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;; }\n};\nint main() {\n    Student S1(70);\n    S1++;      // post increment\n    S1.show();\n    ++S1;      // pre increment\n    S1.show();\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">marks = 71</p><p class=\"ln i0\">marks = 72</p><p>(b) Diamond problem: class A is the base of B and C, and D derives from both B and C.</p><p class=\"ln i1\">A</p><p class=\"ln i0\">/ \\</p><p class=\"ln i0\">B   C</p><p class=\"ln i0\">\\ /</p><p class=\"ln i1\">D</p><p>Without virtual inheritance D contains two copies of A&#x27;s members (one through B, one through C); accessing d.value is ambiguous and data is duplicated. Solution: make B and C inherit A virtually (class B : virtual public A). Then D has a single shared A subobject, and the most derived class (D) constructs it.</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass A {\npublic:\n    int value;\n    A() : value(10) { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;A constructed once\\n&quot;; }\n};\nclass B : virtual public A {};\nclass C : virtual public A {};\nclass D : public B, public C {};\nint main() {\n    D d;\n    cout &lt;&lt; d.value &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;      // unambiguous: one shared A\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">A constructed once</p><p class=\"ln i0\">10</p><p>(c) Yes. Function templates can be overloaded with ordinary functions and with other templates that have different parameter lists (number of parameters, or T* vs T). At a call the compiler prefers an exact-match non-template function over a template, and the more specialised template over a general one. Justification: it lets us give a special behaviour for one type (e.g. C-strings) while keeping a generic version for all others.</p><pre>#include &lt;cstring&gt;\n#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\ntemplate &lt;class T&gt; T biggest(T a, T b) { return a &gt; b ? a : b; }                 // template 1\ntemplate &lt;class T&gt; T biggest(T a, T b, T c) { return biggest(biggest(a, b), c); } // template 2 (3 args)\nconst char* biggest(const char* a, const char* b) { return strcmp(a, b) &gt; 0 ? a : b; } // non-template overload\nint main() {\n    cout &lt;&lt; biggest(4, 9) &lt;&lt; &#x27; &#x27; &lt;&lt; biggest(1.5, 7.5, 3.5) &lt;&lt; &#x27; &#x27; &lt;&lt; biggest(&quot;apple&quot;, &quot;pear&quot;) &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">9 7.5 pear</p>","exam":"mid","year":2025,"branch":"IT","src":"2025 IT midsem.pdf (duplicate: hash-named 4c8c60a2...2025 IT midsem.pdf"},{"p":6,"q":"1","marks":"8","unit":0,"topic":"Friend class and new/delete overloading","qh":"<p>a. Design a system to manage access to a private data member called balance in a BankAccount class. Normally, only the BankAccount class has direct access to this member. However, you also want another class, BankManager, to access and modify balance directly when needed, without using a BankAccount method. Explain how you would use a friend function or a friend class to allow BankManager to access the private member balance of BankAccount. Write the necessary code to declare BankManager as a friend class in BankAccount so that it can access balance. [3]</p><p>b. Create a class Student that overloads the new and delete operators to track memory allocation and deallocation. Implement the overloaded new and delete operators to print messages whenever memory is allocated or freed for a Student object. Write the necessary code to demonstrate this functionality. [5]</p>","sh":"<p>(a) A friend declaration inside BankAccount gives another function or class access to its private members. Putting</p><pre>friend class BankManager; in BankAccount lets every member function of BankManager read and change balance</pre><p>directly. Friendship is granted by the class that owns the data, it is not mutual and not inherited. (A single friend function, friend void audit(BankAccount&amp;);, would give access to just that function.) Use it sparingly because it weakens encapsulation.</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass BankAccount {\n    int balance;\npublic:\n    BankAccount(int b) : balance(b) {}\n    friend class BankManager;            // BankManager may touch private data\n    void show() const { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Balance: &quot; &lt;&lt; balance &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;; }\n};\nclass BankManager {\npublic:\n    void addInterest(BankAccount&amp; a, int percent) { a.balance += a.balance * percent / 100; }\n    void setBalance(BankAccount&amp; a, int v) { a.balance = v; }\n};\nint main() {\n    BankAccount acc(1000);\n    BankManager mgr;\n    mgr.addInterest(acc, 5);      // direct access to the private member\n    acc.show();\n    mgr.setBalance(acc, 250);\n    acc.show();\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Balance: 1050</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Balance: 250</p><p>(b) operator new(size_t) and operator delete(void*) can be overloaded as static member functions of a class. new Student then calls our operator new to obtain raw memory and afterwards runs the constructor; delete p runs the destructor and then calls our operator delete.</p><pre>#include &lt;cstdlib&gt;\n#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n#include &lt;new&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass Student {\n    int roll;\npublic:\n    Student(int r = 0) : roll(r) { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Student constructor, roll &quot; &lt;&lt; roll &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;; }\n    ~Student() { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Student destructor, roll &quot; &lt;&lt; roll &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;; }\n    static void* operator new(size_t size) {\n        cout &lt;&lt; &quot;operator new: allocating &quot; &lt;&lt; size &lt;&lt; &quot; bytes\\n&quot;;\n        void* p = malloc(size);\n        if (!p) throw bad_alloc();\n        return p;\n    }\n    static void operator delete(void* p) {\n        cout &lt;&lt; &quot;operator delete: freeing memory\\n&quot;;\n        free(p);\n    }\n};\nint main() {\n    Student* s = new Student(7);\n    delete s;\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">operator new: allocating 4 bytes</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Student constructor, roll 7</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Student destructor, roll 7</p><p class=\"ln i0\">operator delete: freeing memory</p>","exam":"end","year":2024,"branch":"SE","src":"SE endsem 24.pdf (SE-205; Nov-2024; +dup. copies"},{"p":6,"q":"2","marks":"8","unit":3,"topic":"Vector versus ArrayList","qh":"<p>Explain the differences between vector and ArrayList in terms of: 1. Memory management and resizing. 2. Performance when adding or removing elements. 3. How each handles type safety. Provide examples to illustrate each difference. [8]</p>","sh":"<p>Assumption: &quot;vector&quot; means Java&#x27;s java.util.Vector (the paper compares it with Java&#x27;s ArrayList). Both are resizable arrays implementing List.</p><pre>Aspect               Vector                               ArrayList\nMemory/resizing      Backed by Object[]; when full it     Backed by Object[]; when full it grows to\n                     doubles in size (or grows by the     about 1.5x the old size (newCapacity =\n                     capacityIncrement if given).         old + old/2). Wastes less memory.\nThread safety        All methods synchronized.            Not synchronized (faster; use\n                                                          Collections.synchronizedList if needed).\nPerformance          Slower: lock acquired on every       Faster for add/get/remove. Both are O(1)\n(add/remove)         call. Add at the end is amortised    amortised at the end, O(n) at the front or\n                     O(1); insert/remove in the middle    middle (elements are shifted).\n                     is O(n).\nType safety          Both are generic since Java 5: Vector&lt;String&gt;/ArrayList&lt;String&gt; are checked at\n                     compile time. As raw types (no &lt;T&gt;) both hold Object and need casts, giving\n                     a ClassCastException at run time. Vector also has legacy methods\n                     (addElement, elementAt) and Enumeration.\nimport java.util.*;\npublic class Demo {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Vector&lt;Integer&gt; v = new Vector&lt;&gt;();\n        ArrayList&lt;Integer&gt; a = new ArrayList&lt;&gt;();\n        for (int i = 1; i &lt;= 3; i++) { v.add(i * 10); a.add(i * 10); }\n        v.remove(Integer.valueOf(20));          // remove by value\n        a.remove(1);                            // remove by index\n        System.out.println(&quot;Vector    : &quot; + v);\n        System.out.println(&quot;ArrayList : &quot; + a);\n        // type safety\n        List raw = new ArrayList();             // raw type: no compile-time check\n        raw.add(&quot;text&quot;);\n        raw.add(5);\n        try {\n            Integer x = (Integer) raw.get(0);   // run-time failure\n        } catch (ClassCastException e) {\n            System.out.println(&quot;ClassCastException with raw type&quot;);\n        }\n        // a.add(&quot;hello&quot;);  // compile-time error for ArrayList&lt;Integer&gt;\n    }\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0 h\">Vector    : [10, 30]</p><p class=\"ln i0 h\">ArrayList : [10, 30]</p><p class=\"ln i0\">ClassCastException with raw type</p><p>(The compiler prints an &quot;unchecked&quot; note for the raw List; the program still compiles.)</p>","exam":"end","year":2024,"branch":"SE","src":"SE endsem 24.pdf (SE-205; Nov-2024; +dup. copies"},{"p":6,"q":"3","marks":"8","unit":3,"topic":"Synchronized threads / nested exceptions","qh":"<p>Create a multithreading program with two threads: one that prints numbers from 1 to 10 and another that prints letters from A to J. Use synchronization to ensure the threads do not interfere with each other&#x27;s output. Set different priorities for the threads and explain how it affects their execution. Additionally, describe the lifecycle of a thread, explaining each state such as new, runnable, blocked, and terminated, and how these states are represented in your program. Provide the complete code along with comments and explanations. [8]</p><p>OR</p><p>Write a program that demonstrates exception handling. Use nested try-catch blocks to handle different types of exceptions. Implement a throw statement to throw a custom exception and use throws in a method signature to declare an exception. Additionally, include a finally block to ensure that a cleanup action, such as closing a resource, occurs regardless of whether an exception is thrown or not. Provide the complete code along with explanations of how each part works. [8]</p>","sh":"<p>Main option. All printing goes through one synchronized method on a shared object. The monitor lock allows only one thread inside at a time, so each line is printed whole. setPriority() is only a hint to the scheduler (1 = MIN, 5 = NORM, 10 = MAX): a higher-priority thread is more likely to get the CPU first, but order is not guaranteed and differs between runs and operating systems.</p><pre>class Printer {\n    // synchronized: only one thread at a time can execute this method on this object\n    synchronized void print(String who, String value) {\n        System.out.println(who + &quot;: &quot; + value);\n    }\n}\nclass NumberThread extends Thread {\n    private final Printer p;\n    NumberThread(Printer p) { this.p = p; }\n    public void run() {\n        for (int i = 1; i &lt;= 10; i++) {\n            p.print(&quot;Number&quot;, String.valueOf(i));\n            try { Thread.sleep(10); } catch (InterruptedException e) { }   // TIMED_WAITING\n        }\n    }\n}\nclass LetterThread extends Thread {\n    private final Printer p;\n    LetterThread(Printer p) { this.p = p; }\n    public void run() {\n        for (char c = &#x27;A&#x27;; c &lt;= &#x27;J&#x27;; c++) {\n            p.print(&quot;Letter&quot;, String.valueOf(c));\n            try { Thread.sleep(10); } catch (InterruptedException e) { }\n        }\n    }\n}\npublic class TwoThreads {\n    public static void main(String[] args) throws InterruptedException {\n        Printer p = new Printer();\n        Thread t1 = new NumberThread(p);\n        Thread t2 = new LetterThread(p);\n        System.out.println(&quot;t1 state: &quot; + t1.getState());      // NEW\n        t1.setPriority(Thread.MAX_PRIORITY);                    // 10\n        t2.setPriority(Thread.MIN_PRIORITY);                    // 1\n        t1.start();                                             // NEW -&gt; RUNNABLE\n        t2.start();\n        t1.join();                                              // main waits (WAITING)\n        t2.join();\n        System.out.println(&quot;t1 state: &quot; + t1.getState());      // TERMINATED\n        System.out.println(&quot;t2 state: &quot; + t2.getState());\n    }\n}</pre><p>Output (one possible run; the interleaving of the two threads varies, but every line is intact and each thread&#x27;s own lines stay in order):</p><p class=\"ln i0\">t1 state: NEW</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Number: 1</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Letter: A</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Number: 2</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Letter: B</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Number: 3</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Letter: C</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Number: 4</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Letter: D</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Number: 5</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Letter: E</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Number: 6</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Letter: F</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Number: 7</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Letter: G</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Number: 8</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Letter: H</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Number: 9</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Letter: I</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Number: 10</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Letter: J</p><p class=\"ln i0\">t1 state: TERMINATED</p><p class=\"ln i0\">t2 state: TERMINATED</p><p>Thread life cycle and where it appears in the program:</p><pre>NEW            thread object created, start() not yet called          -&gt; getState() before t1.start()\nRUNNABLE       after start(); ready to run or running on a CPU        -&gt; inside run() while printing\nBLOCKED        waiting to enter a synchronized block/method whose     -&gt; a thread calling print() while the other holds the lock\n               monitor is held by another thread\nWAITING /      waiting for another thread (join(), wait()) or for a   -&gt; main in t1.join(); threads in Thread.sleep(10)\nTIMED_WAITING  fixed time (sleep(ms))\nTERMINATED     run() has finished (or an uncaught exception)          -&gt; getState() after join()</pre><p>OR option (exception handling): throw raises an exception, throws declares that a method may pass a checked exception to its caller, nested try blocks let an inner handler deal with one kind and let others propagate, and finally always runs (resource clean-up).</p><pre>class InvalidAgeException extends Exception {           // custom (checked) exception\n    InvalidAgeException(String msg) { super(msg); }\n}\nclass Resource {\n    void close() { System.out.println(&quot;Resource closed (cleanup)&quot;); }\n}\npublic class ExceptionDemo {\n    static void checkAge(int age) throws InvalidAgeException {   // throws: declares the exception\n        if (age &lt; 18) throw new InvalidAgeException(&quot;Age &quot; + age + &quot; is below 18&quot;);   // throw: raises it\n        System.out.println(&quot;Age &quot; + age + &quot; accepted&quot;);\n    }\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Resource r = new Resource();\n        try {                                           // outer try\n            try {                                       // inner try\n                int[] a = new int[2];\n                a[5] = 1;                               // ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException\n            } catch (ArithmeticException e) {\n                System.out.println(&quot;inner: arithmetic&quot;);  // does not match, exception propagates\n            }\n            System.out.println(&quot;not reached&quot;);\n        } catch (ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException e) {\n            System.out.println(&quot;outer caught: &quot; + e.getMessage());\n        }\n        try {\n            checkAge(25);\n            checkAge(15);\n        } catch (InvalidAgeException e) {\n            System.out.println(&quot;Custom exception: &quot; + e.getMessage());\n        } finally {\n            r.close();                                  // runs whether or not an exception occurred\n        }\n    }\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">outer caught: Index 5 out of bounds for length 2</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Age 25 accepted</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Custom exception: Age 15 is below 18</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Resource closed (cleanup)</p>","exam":"end","year":2024,"branch":"SE","src":"SE endsem 24.pdf (SE-205; Nov-2024; +dup. copies"},{"p":6,"q":"4","marks":"8","unit":3,"topic":"Client-server model with sockets","qh":"<p>Explain the client-server model in networking, focusing on the roles of the client and server. Provide a simple example of client-server interaction using sockets. [8]</p>","sh":"<p>In the client-server model the server is a long-running program that owns a resource or service and waits for requests on a known address (IP + port); a client is a program that starts the conversation, sends a request and uses the reply. The server accepts many clients (one thread per connection or an event loop); the client needs to know only the server&#x27;s address.</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Client                                   Server</p><pre class=\"grid\">------                                   ------\nnew Socket(host, 5000)  --connect--&gt;     ServerSocket(5000).accept()\nsend &quot;Hello&quot;            -----------&gt;     read request\nread reply              &lt;-----------     send &quot;Echo: Hello&quot;\nclose()                                  close(), accept() the next client</pre><p>Roles: server = passive (bind, listen, accept, respond); client = active (connect, request, display, close). Java uses java.net.ServerSocket and java.net.Socket over TCP (reliable byte streams).</p><pre>// ---- Server.java ----\nimport java.io.*;\nimport java.net.*;\npublic class Server {\n    public static void main(String[] args) throws IOException {\n        ServerSocket server = new ServerSocket(5000);          // listen on port 5000\n        System.out.println(&quot;Server waiting...&quot;);\n        Socket s = server.accept();                            // block until a client connects\n        BufferedReader in = new BufferedReader(new InputStreamReader(s.getInputStream()));\n        PrintWriter out = new PrintWriter(s.getOutputStream(), true);\n        String msg = in.readLine();                            // read request\n        System.out.println(&quot;Server received: &quot; + msg);\n        out.println(&quot;Echo: &quot; + msg);                           // send reply\n        s.close();\n        server.close();\n    }\n}\n// ---- Client.java ----\nimport java.io.*;\nimport java.net.*;\npublic class Client {\n    public static void main(String[] args) throws IOException {\n        Socket s = new Socket(&quot;localhost&quot;, 5000);              // connect to the server\n        PrintWriter out = new PrintWriter(s.getOutputStream(), true);\n        BufferedReader in = new BufferedReader(new InputStreamReader(s.getInputStream()));\n        out.println(&quot;Hello&quot;);                                  // send request\n        System.out.println(&quot;Client got: &quot; + in.readLine());    // read reply\n        s.close();\n    }\n}</pre><p>Output (start Server first, then Client):</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Server terminal:  Server waiting...</p><p class=\"ln i3\">Server received: Hello</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Client terminal:  Client got: Echo: Hello</p>","exam":"end","year":2024,"branch":"SE","src":"SE endsem 24.pdf (SE-205; Nov-2024; +dup. copies"},{"p":6,"q":"5","marks":"8","unit":5,"topic":"Applet button event and Swing vs Servlets","qh":"<p>a. Create an applet that contains a button and a text field. When the button is clicked, the applet should display a message in the text field. Implement event handling for the button click using an appropriate listener. Write the code for the applet and explain the event handling mechanism used. [4]</p><p>b. Explain the differences between Swing and Servlets in Java. Discuss how Swing provides a GUI for desktop applications and how Servlets are used to create dynamic web applications. [4]</p>","sh":"<p>(a) Delegation event model: the button (event source) is registered with a listener object using addActionListener; when it is clicked the source creates an ActionEvent and calls the listener&#x27;s actionPerformed, where the text field is updated. (Applet is deprecated since Java 9 but is still the syllabus style; run it with appletviewer.)</p><pre>import java.applet.Applet;\nimport java.awt.*;\nimport java.awt.event.*;\npublic class MsgApplet extends Applet implements ActionListener {\n    Button b;\n    TextField t;\n    public void init() {\n        b = new Button(&quot;Click me&quot;);\n        t = new TextField(20);\n        add(b);\n        add(t);\n        b.addActionListener(this);          // register the listener with the source\n    }\n    public void actionPerformed(ActionEvent e) {   // called when the button is clicked\n        t.setText(&quot;Button was clicked!&quot;);\n    }\n}\n/* &lt;applet code=&quot;MsgApplet.class&quot; width=&quot;300&quot; height=&quot;100&quot;&gt;&lt;/applet&gt; */</pre><p>Result: the applet shows a button and an empty text field; after a click the text field shows Button was clicked!. (b)</p><pre>Aspect        Swing                                      Servlet\nPurpose       Desktop GUI toolkit (javax.swing):         Server-side component (javax/jakarta.servlet)\n              JFrame, JButton, JTable, ...               that handles HTTP requests and builds responses\nRuns on       Client machine (JVM of the user)           Web server / servlet container (Tomcat, Jetty)\nOutput        Windows, widgets, dialogs                  HTML/JSON text sent to a browser\nInteraction   Event listeners (ActionListener, ...)      doGet()/doPost() called by the container,\n                                                         lifecycle init() -&gt; service() -&gt; destroy()\nUsage         Standalone applications                    Dynamic web applications (login, forms, APIs)</pre><p>Swing provides lightweight, platform-independent components with pluggable look and feel and an event-driven model for desktop programs. A servlet reads request parameters, runs business logic (database access) and writes a dynamic response, which the browser renders.</p>","exam":"end","year":2024,"branch":"SE","src":"SE endsem 24.pdf (SE-205; Nov-2024; +dup. copies"},{"p":7,"q":"1","marks":"8","unit":0,"topic":"Eight one-mark short answers","qh":"<p>(a) Define the term &quot;object&quot; in Object-Oriented Programming. (b) What is the purpose of a constructor in C++? (c) State one difference between structured programming and object-oriented programming. (d) What is the significance of the this pointer in C++? (e) What is bytecode in Java? (f) Name two access specifiers in Java. (g) Mention one benefit of using Java packages. (h) What is the difference between checked and unchecked exceptions in Java?</p>","sh":"<p>(a) An object is a run-time instance of a class: it bundles data (state) with the functions (behaviour) that operate on it, and has an identity, e.g. Car c;.</p><p>(b) A constructor is a special member function, with the class&#x27;s name and no return type, that is called automatically when an object is created to initialise its data members (and acquire resources).</p><p>(c) Structured programming is organised around functions with data kept separate; OOP organises the program around objects that encapsulate data together with their functions (with inheritance and polymorphism).</p><p>(d) this is an implicit pointer to the current object inside a non-static member function; it resolves name clashes between members and parameters and lets a function return *this (method chaining).</p><p>(e) Bytecode is the platform-independent, intermediate instruction set (.class file) produced by javac and executed by the JVM.</p><p>(f) public, private (also protected and default/package).</p><p>(g) Packages group related classes, avoid name conflicts and control access (also easier reuse and maintenance).</p><p>(h) Checked exceptions (e.g. IOException) are verified at compile time: the code must catch them or declare them with throws. Unchecked exceptions (subclasses of RuntimeException/Error, e.g. NullPointerException) are not checked by the compiler.</p>","exam":"end","year":2024,"branch":"CS","src":"OOPS EndSem Nov2024.pdf (CS203n, Nov-2024"},{"p":7,"q":"2","marks":"8","unit":1,"topic":"Static members, overriding, abstract class, throws","qh":"<p>(a) Illustrate the use of static data members in a C++ class with an example. [2] (b) Write a Java program to demonstrate function overriding. [2] (c) Explain how a pure virtual function is used to create an abstract class in C++. [2] (d) Describe the role of throw and throws in Java exception handling with examples. [2]</p>","sh":"<p>(a) A static data member is shared by all objects of the class (one copy), is defined outside the class and can be used without an object. Here it counts the live objects.</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass Counter {\n    static int count;           // shared by all objects\npublic:\n    Counter() { ++count; }\n    ~Counter() { --count; }\n    static int get() { return count; }\n};\nint Counter::count = 0;         // definition and initialisation\nint main() {\n    Counter a, b;\n    cout &lt;&lt; &quot;objects: &quot; &lt;&lt; Counter::get() &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n    {\n        Counter c;\n        cout &lt;&lt; &quot;objects: &quot; &lt;&lt; Counter::get() &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n    }\n    cout &lt;&lt; &quot;objects: &quot; &lt;&lt; Counter::get() &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">objects: 2</p><p class=\"ln i0\">objects: 3</p><p class=\"ln i0\">objects: 2</p><p>(b) Overriding: a subclass supplies its own version of a superclass method with the same signature; the version run is chosen by the real type of the object.</p><pre>class Animal {\n    void sound() { System.out.println(&quot;Animal makes a sound&quot;); }\n}\nclass Dog extends Animal {\n    @Override\n    void sound() { System.out.println(&quot;Dog barks&quot;); }\n}\npublic class OverrideDemo {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Animal a = new Dog();     // superclass reference, subclass object\n        a.sound();\n    }\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Dog barks</p><p>(c) A pure virtual function has no body in the base class and is declared with = 0: virtual double area() const = 0;. A class containing at least one pure virtual function is abstract: objects of it cannot be created, but pointers/references to it can. It defines an interface that every concrete derived class must implement (otherwise the derived class is also abstract).</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass Shape {\npublic:\n    virtual double area() const = 0;       // pure virtual =&gt; Shape is abstract\n    virtual ~Shape() {}\n};\nclass Rect : public Shape {\n    double w, h;\npublic:\n    Rect(double a, double b) : w(a), h(b) {}\n    double area() const override { return w * h; }\n};\nint main() {\n    // Shape s;            // error: cannot create an object of an abstract class\n    Shape* p = new Rect(3, 4);\n    cout &lt;&lt; p-&gt;area() &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n    delete p;\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">12</p><p>(d) throw is a statement that raises an exception object: throw new IllegalArgumentException(&quot;bad&quot;);. throws is part of a method signature and declares the checked exceptions the method may pass to its caller, which must catch or redeclare them.</p><pre>public class ThrowDemo {\n    static void withdraw(int bal, int amt) throws Exception {     // throws: declaration\n        if (amt &gt; bal) throw new Exception(&quot;Insufficient funds&quot;);  // throw: raising\n        System.out.println(&quot;Withdrawn &quot; + amt);\n    }\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        try {\n            withdraw(500, 200);\n            withdraw(500, 900);\n        } catch (Exception e) {\n            System.out.println(&quot;Caught: &quot; + e.getMessage());\n        }\n    }\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Withdrawn 200</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Caught: Insufficient funds</p>","exam":"end","year":2024,"branch":"CS","src":"OOPS EndSem Nov2024.pdf (CS203n, Nov-2024"},{"p":7,"q":"3","marks":"8","unit":1,"topic":"Operator overloading and Java thread synchronization","qh":"<p>(a) Analyze the use of operator overloading in C++ with an example program. [4] (b) Write a Java program that demonstrates multithreading with thread synchronization. [4]</p>","sh":"<p>(a) Operator overloading gives user-defined types the natural operator syntax (c1 + c2) by defining operator+ etc. as member or friend functions. It adds no new operators, keeps precedence/associativity, and cannot overload ::, ., .*, ?:, sizeof. Benefits: readable code and a natural interface for numeric/string/matrix types. Risk: misleading semantics if overused. Example for a Complex class (+, - and &lt;&lt;):</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass Complex {\n    double re, im;\npublic:\n    Complex(double r = 0, double i = 0) : re(r), im(i) {}\n    Complex operator+(const Complex&amp; o) const { return Complex(re + o.re, im + o.im); }\n    Complex operator-(const Complex&amp; o) const { return Complex(re - o.re, im - o.im); }\n    friend ostream&amp; operator&lt;&lt;(ostream&amp; os, const Complex&amp; c) {\n        return os &lt;&lt; c.re &lt;&lt; (c.im &lt; 0 ? &quot; - &quot; : &quot; + &quot;) &lt;&lt; (c.im &lt; 0 ? -c.im : c.im) &lt;&lt; &quot;i&quot;;\n    }\n};\nint main() {\n    Complex a(3, 4), b(1, -2);\n    cout &lt;&lt; &quot;a + b = &quot; &lt;&lt; a + b &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n    cout &lt;&lt; &quot;a - b = &quot; &lt;&lt; a - b &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">a + b = 4 + 2i</p><p class=\"ln i0\">a - b = 2 + 6i</p><p>(b) Two threads increment one shared counter 1000 times each. Without synchronized, count++ (read-modify-write) can be interleaved and updates lost; with synchronized only one thread runs the method at a time, so the result is always 2000.</p><pre>class Counter {\n    private int count = 0;\n    synchronized void increment() { count++; }     // lock on this object\n    int get() { return count; }\n}\npublic class SyncDemo {\n    public static void main(String[] args) throws InterruptedException {\n        Counter c = new Counter();\n        Runnable task = () -&gt; { for (int i = 0; i &lt; 1000; i++) c.increment(); };\n        Thread t1 = new Thread(task);\n        Thread t2 = new Thread(task);\n        t1.start(); t2.start();\n        t1.join();  t2.join();\n        System.out.println(&quot;Final count = &quot; + c.get());\n    }\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Final count = 2000</p>","exam":"end","year":2024,"branch":"CS","src":"OOPS EndSem Nov2024.pdf (CS203n, Nov-2024"},{"p":7,"q":"4","marks":"8","unit":1,"topic":"Templates; serialization; client-server","qh":"<p>(a) Evaluate the advantages and disadvantages of templates in C++ by designing a function template to swap variables. OR Write a program in Java that demonstrates serialization to save and retrieve an object&#x27;s state. [4] (b) Explain with a program how the client-server model is implemented in Java using socket programming. [4]</p>","sh":"<p>(a) Templates. Advantages: one definition works for any type (reuse, no macros); type-safe and checked at compile time; no run-time overhead (instantiated per type); basis of the STL. Disadvantages: code bloat (a copy per type), long and cryptic error messages, the whole definition must be in headers (slow compilation), the type must support the operations used (e.g. =), and debugging is harder.</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n#include &lt;string&gt;\nusing namespace std;\ntemplate &lt;class T&gt;\nvoid swapValues(T&amp; a, T&amp; b) {\n    T tmp = a;\n    a = b;\n    b = tmp;\n}\nint main() {\n    int x = 3, y = 8;\n    double p = 1.5, q = 9.5;\n    string s = &quot;left&quot;, t = &quot;right&quot;;\n    swapValues(x, y);\n    swapValues(p, q);\n    swapValues(s, t);\n    cout &lt;&lt; x &lt;&lt; &#x27; &#x27; &lt;&lt; y &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n    cout &lt;&lt; p &lt;&lt; &#x27; &#x27; &lt;&lt; q &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n    cout &lt;&lt; s &lt;&lt; &#x27; &#x27; &lt;&lt; t &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">8 3</p><p class=\"ln i0\">9.5 1.5</p><p class=\"ln i0\">right left</p><p>OR serialization: Serializable objects are converted to a byte stream by ObjectOutputStream and rebuilt by ObjectInputStream; fields marked transient are skipped.</p><pre>import java.io.*;\nclass Emp implements Serializable {\n    String name;\n    int id;\n    transient String password;                 // not saved\n    Emp(String n, int i, String p) { name = n; id = i; password = p; }\n}\npublic class SerialDemo {\n    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {\n        Emp e = new Emp(&quot;Asha&quot;, 101, &quot;secret&quot;);\n        try (ObjectOutputStream out = new ObjectOutputStream(new FileOutputStream(&quot;emp.ser&quot;))) {\n            out.writeObject(e);                // save state\n        }\n        try (ObjectInputStream in = new ObjectInputStream(new FileInputStream(&quot;emp.ser&quot;))) {\n            Emp r = (Emp) in.readObject();     // retrieve state\n            System.out.println(r.name + &quot; &quot; + r.id + &quot; &quot; + r.password);\n        }\n    }\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Asha 101 null</p><p>(b) A server creates a ServerSocket on a port and accept()s a connection; a client opens a Socket to the server&#x27;s host and port; both exchange data through the socket&#x27;s input/output streams (TCP).</p><pre>// Server\nimport java.io.*;\nimport java.net.*;\npublic class Server {\n    public static void main(String[] a) throws IOException {\n        ServerSocket ss = new ServerSocket(6000);\n        Socket s = ss.accept();\n        DataInputStream in = new DataInputStream(s.getInputStream());\n        DataOutputStream out = new DataOutputStream(s.getOutputStream());\n        int n = in.readInt();                  // number sent by the client\n        out.writeInt(n * n);                   // reply with its square\n        s.close(); ss.close();\n    }\n}\n// Client\nimport java.io.*;\nimport java.net.*;\npublic class Client {\n    public static void main(String[] a) throws IOException {\n        Socket s = new Socket(&quot;localhost&quot;, 6000);\n        DataOutputStream out = new DataOutputStream(s.getOutputStream());\n        DataInputStream in = new DataInputStream(s.getInputStream());\n        out.writeInt(12);\n        System.out.println(&quot;Square from server = &quot; + in.readInt());\n        s.close();\n    }\n}</pre><p>Output (client terminal, after the server is started):</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Square from server = 144</p>","exam":"end","year":2024,"branch":"CS","src":"OOPS EndSem Nov2024.pdf (CS203n, Nov-2024"},{"p":7,"q":"5","marks":"8","unit":2,"topic":"Abstract classes and Swing calculator","qh":"<p>(a) Investigate how abstract classes are implemented in Java and their role in polymorphism with a program example. [3] (b) Write a Java program to design a GUI-based calculator using Swing, including basic event handling. [5] OR Create a program in C++ to demonstrate the use of friend functions and their applications.</p>","sh":"<p>(a) An abstract class is declared with abstract; it may contain abstract methods (no body) and ordinary methods, cannot be instantiated, and is extended by classes that must implement all abstract methods. Role in polymorphism: a reference of the abstract type can point to any subclass object, and the call is dispatched to the subclass at run time.</p><pre>abstract class Shape {\n    abstract double area();                        // must be implemented by subclasses\n    void describe() { System.out.println(getClass().getSimpleName() + &quot; area = &quot; + area()); }\n}\nclass Circle extends Shape {\n    double r;\n    Circle(double r) { this.r = r; }\n    double area() { return 3.14 * r * r; }\n}\nclass Rectangle extends Shape {\n    double w, h;\n    Rectangle(double w, double h) { this.w = w; this.h = h; }\n    double area() { return w * h; }\n}\npublic class AbstractDemo {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Shape[] shapes = { new Circle(2), new Rectangle(3, 4) };   // Shape reference, subclass objects\n        for (Shape s : shapes) s.describe();                       // run-time polymorphism\n    }\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Circle area = 12.56</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Rectangle area = 12.0</p><p>(b) Swing calculator: JFrame window, a JTextField for the numbers, buttons for digits and operators in a GridLayout, and one ActionListener that handles all buttons.</p><pre>import javax.swing.*;\nimport java.awt.*;\nimport java.awt.event.*;\npublic class Calculator extends JFrame implements ActionListener {\n    JTextField display = new JTextField();\n    double first = 0;\n    String op = &quot;&quot;;\n    boolean newNumber = true;\n    Calculator() {\n        setTitle(&quot;Calculator&quot;);\n        setLayout(new BorderLayout());\n        add(display, BorderLayout.NORTH);\n        JPanel p = new JPanel(new GridLayout(4, 4, 3, 3));\n        String[] keys = {&quot;7&quot;,&quot;8&quot;,&quot;9&quot;,&quot;/&quot;,&quot;4&quot;,&quot;5&quot;,&quot;6&quot;,&quot;*&quot;,&quot;1&quot;,&quot;2&quot;,&quot;3&quot;,&quot;-&quot;,&quot;0&quot;,&quot;C&quot;,&quot;=&quot;,&quot;+&quot;};\n        for (String k : keys) {\n            JButton b = new JButton(k);\n            b.addActionListener(this);                 // register the listener\n            p.add(b);\n        }\n        add(p, BorderLayout.CENTER);\n        setSize(260, 280);\n        setDefaultCloseOperation(EXIT_ON_CLOSE);\n        setVisible(true);\n    }\n    public void actionPerformed(ActionEvent e) {       // event handling\n        String c = e.getActionCommand();\n        if (Character.isDigit(c.charAt(0))) {\n            display.setText(newNumber ? c : display.getText() + c);\n            newNumber = false;\n        } else if (c.equals(&quot;C&quot;)) {\n            display.setText(&quot;&quot;); first = 0; op = &quot;&quot;; newNumber = true;\n        } else if (c.equals(&quot;=&quot;)) {\n            double second = Double.parseDouble(display.getText());\n            double r = first;\n            if (op.equals(&quot;+&quot;)) r = first + second;\n            if (op.equals(&quot;-&quot;)) r = first - second;\n            if (op.equals(&quot;*&quot;)) r = first * second;\n            if (op.equals(&quot;/&quot;)) r = first / second;\n            display.setText(String.valueOf(r));\n            newNumber = true;\n        } else {                                       // + - * /\n            first = Double.parseDouble(display.getText());\n            op = c;\n            newNumber = true;\n        }\n    }\n    public static void main(String[] args) { new Calculator(); }\n}</pre><p>Output (GUI): a window with a display and 16 keys; pressing 7, +, 5, = shows 12.0 in the display. OR (C++ friend functions). A friend function is a non-member function that can read the private members of the class that declares it as a friend. Common uses: operators whose left operand is not an object of the class (&lt;&lt;, + with a number on the left) and a function that needs the private data of two classes at once.</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass B;                                   // forward declaration\nclass A {\n    int x;\npublic:\n    A(int v) : x(v) {}\n    friend int sum(const A&amp;, const B&amp;);    // friend of both classes\n    friend ostream&amp; operator&lt;&lt;(ostream&amp; os, const A&amp; a) { return os &lt;&lt; &quot;A(&quot; &lt;&lt; a.x &lt;&lt; &quot;)&quot;; }\n};\nclass B {\n    int y;\npublic:\n    B(int v) : y(v) {}\n    friend int sum(const A&amp;, const B&amp;);\n};\nint sum(const A&amp; a, const B&amp; b) { return a.x + b.y; }\nint main() {\n    A a(10);\n    B b(32);\n    cout &lt;&lt; a &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n    cout &lt;&lt; &quot;sum = &quot; &lt;&lt; sum(a, b) &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">A(10)</p><p class=\"ln i0\">sum = 42</p>","exam":"end","year":2024,"branch":"CS","src":"OOPS EndSem Nov2024.pdf (CS203n, Nov-2024"},{"p":8,"q":"1","marks":"4","unit":0,"topic":"Distinguish object/class; binding/message passing","qh":"<p>1. Distinguish between the following terms: (a) Object and classes (b) Dynamic binding and message passing [4][CO1]</p>","sh":"<pre>(a) Class                                  Object\n    User-defined data type; blueprint      Instance of a class; real entity with its own data\n    Defines data members and functions     Holds values for those data members\n    No memory for data until an object     Occupies memory when created\n    is created\n    Declared once                          Many objects from one class: Car c1, c2;\n(b) Dynamic binding                        Message passing\n    The call is linked to a function       An object asks another object (or itself) to do work\n    body at RUN time, based on the real    by calling a member function with arguments:\n    type of the object (virtual functions) account.deposit(500);\n    Gives run-time polymorphism            Gives communication between objects</pre>","exam":"mid","year":2024,"branch":"SE","src":"OOP midsem 24 (SE-205; Sept-2024; +dup. copies"},{"p":8,"q":"2","marks":"4","unit":1,"topic":"ColoredBox template and compare()","qh":"<p>2. Write a C++ program to implement the following: [4][CO2] (a) Create a derived class template &#x27;ColoredBox&lt;T&gt;&#x27; that inherits from &#x27;Box&lt;T&gt;&#x27;. This class should have an additional attribute &#x27;color&#x27; of type &#x27;string&#x27; and member functions to set and get the color. Demonstrate its usage with an integer value and color strings. (b) Write a function template compare() that compares two values and returns the larger of the two. Demonstrate its usage with at least two different data types (e.g., integers and floating-point numbers).</p>","sh":"<p>Box&lt;T&gt; is the base class template holding a value of type T. ColoredBox&lt;T&gt; derives from Box&lt;T&gt; with the same T (a template inheriting from a template). compare() is a function template.</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n#include &lt;string&gt;\nusing namespace std;\ntemplate &lt;class T&gt;\nclass Box {\nprotected:\n    T value;\npublic:\n    Box(T v) : value(v) {}\n    T getValue() const { return value; }\n};\ntemplate &lt;class T&gt;\nclass ColoredBox : public Box&lt;T&gt; {\n    string color;\npublic:\n    ColoredBox(T v, string c) : Box&lt;T&gt;(v), color(c) {}\n    void setColor(const string&amp; c) { color = c; }\n    string getColor() const { return color; }\n};\ntemplate &lt;class T&gt;\nT compare(T a, T b) { return a &gt; b ? a : b; }\nint main() {\n    ColoredBox&lt;int&gt; box(42, &quot;red&quot;);\n    cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Value: &quot; &lt;&lt; box.getValue() &lt;&lt; &quot;, color: &quot; &lt;&lt; box.getColor() &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n    box.setColor(&quot;blue&quot;);\n    cout &lt;&lt; &quot;After setColor: &quot; &lt;&lt; box.getColor() &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n    cout &lt;&lt; &quot;compare(7, 12)     = &quot; &lt;&lt; compare(7, 12) &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n    cout &lt;&lt; &quot;compare(3.5, 2.25) = &quot; &lt;&lt; compare(3.5, 2.25) &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Value: 42, color: red</p><p class=\"ln i0\">After setColor: blue</p><p class=\"ln i0\">compare(7, 12)     = 12</p><p class=\"ln i0\">compare(3.5, 2.25) = 3.5</p>","exam":"mid","year":2024,"branch":"SE","src":"OOP midsem 24 (SE-205; Sept-2024; +dup. copies"},{"p":8,"q":"3","marks":"4","unit":1,"topic":"Animal with virtual sound() and pure virtual breed()","qh":"<p>3. Write a program in C++ to create a base class Animal with a virtual function sound() that prints &quot;Some generic animal sound&quot;. Derive a class Dog from Animal that overrides the sound() function to print &quot;Bark&quot;. Add a pure virtual function breed() in the Animal class. Implement the breed() function in the Dog class to print the breed of the dog. Demonstrate the use of the sound() and breed() functions by creating an object of Dog in the main() function and calling these functions. [4][CO1]</p>","sh":"<p>Because breed() is pure virtual, Animal is abstract (no Animal objects), and Dog must implement breed(). The calls are made through a base pointer to show dynamic binding as well as directly on the object.</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass Animal {\npublic:\n    virtual void sound() { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Some generic animal sound\\n&quot;; }\n    virtual void breed() = 0;                 // pure virtual\n    virtual ~Animal() {}\n};\nclass Dog : public Animal {\npublic:\n    void sound() override { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Bark\\n&quot;; }\n    void breed() override { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Breed: Labrador Retriever\\n&quot;; }\n};\nint main() {\n    Dog d;\n    d.sound();\n    d.breed();\n    Animal* a = &amp;d;       // base pointer: calls resolved at run time\n    a-&gt;sound();\n    a-&gt;breed();\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Bark</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Breed: Labrador Retriever</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Bark</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Breed: Labrador Retriever</p>","exam":"mid","year":2024,"branch":"SE","src":"OOP midsem 24 (SE-205; Sept-2024; +dup. copies"},{"p":8,"q":"4","marks":"4","unit":1,"topic":"Intern via virtual inheritance","qh":"<p>4. Write a program in C++ to create a class Intern that inherits from both Employee and Student. Resolve the diamond problem using virtual inheritance so that the Intern class properly inherits the name member from the Person class. Implement the getDetails() function in the Intern class to print the intern&#x27;s name, employee status, and student status. Demonstrate the functionality by creating an object of Intern in the main() function and calling getDetails(). [4][CO2]</p>","sh":"<p>Employee and Student both derive from Person. Without virtual, Intern would contain two Person::name members and name would be ambiguous. With virtual public Person there is one shared Person; the most derived class (Intern) supplies its constructor argument.</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n#include &lt;string&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass Person {\nprotected:\n    string name;\npublic:\n    Person(string n) : name(n) {}\n};\nclass Employee : virtual public Person {\nprotected:\n    bool employed;\npublic:\n    Employee(string n, bool e) : Person(n), employed(e) {}\n};\nclass Student : virtual public Person {\nprotected:\n    bool enrolled;\npublic:\n    Student(string n, bool s) : Person(n), enrolled(s) {}\n};\nclass Intern : public Employee, public Student {\npublic:\n    Intern(string n, bool e, bool s) : Person(n), Employee(n, e), Student(n, s) {}\n    void getDetails() const {\n        cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Name: &quot; &lt;&lt; name &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;                     // unambiguous: one Person subobject\n        cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Employee status: &quot; &lt;&lt; (employed ? &quot;Employed&quot; : &quot;Not employed&quot;) &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n        cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Student status: &quot; &lt;&lt; (enrolled ? &quot;Enrolled&quot; : &quot;Not enrolled&quot;) &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n    }\n};\nint main() {\n    Intern i(&quot;Kabir&quot;, true, true);\n    i.getDetails();\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Name: Kabir</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Employee status: Employed</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Student status: Enrolled</p>","exam":"mid","year":2024,"branch":"SE","src":"OOP midsem 24 (SE-205; Sept-2024; +dup. copies"},{"p":8,"q":"5","marks":"4","unit":2,"topic":"Bytecode portability; abstract class versus interface","qh":"<p>5. (a) Byte code is platform independent. Explain. (b) Difference between abstract class and interface. [4][CO3]</p>","sh":"<p>(a) The Java compiler javac does not produce machine code for a particular CPU; it produces bytecode (.class files), an instruction set for an imaginary machine, the JVM. Every operating system has its own JVM implementation that translates the same bytecode into the native instructions of that machine (interpreter/JIT). Therefore a program is compiled once and runs on Windows, Linux and macOS unchanged (&quot;write once, run anywhere&quot;); only the JVM is platform dependent, the bytecode is not. Primitive types have fixed sizes in Java, which also removes platform differences.</p><pre>Hello.java --javac--&gt; Hello.class (bytecode) --&gt; JVM for Windows / JVM for Linux / JVM for macOS</pre><p>(b)</p><pre>Abstract class                                   Interface\nDeclared with abstract; extends (one only)       Declared with interface; implements (many)\nAbstract and concrete methods, constructors      Abstract methods (plus default/static/private)\nInstance variables of any kind and access        Only public static final constants\nMethods may have any access modifier             Methods are public\nPartial abstraction (&quot;is-a&quot; with shared code)    Full abstraction (a contract/capability)\nabstract class Shape {                 // abstract class: shared state + code\n    String name = &quot;shape&quot;;\n    abstract double area();            // must be implemented\n    void show() { System.out.println(name + &quot; area &quot; + area()); }\n}\ninterface Drawable { void draw(); }    // interface: only a contract\nclass Sq extends Shape implements Drawable {\n    double s = 2;\n    double area() { return s * s; }\n    public void draw() { System.out.println(&quot;drawing square&quot;); }\n}</pre>","exam":"mid","year":2024,"branch":"SE","src":"OOP midsem 24 (SE-205; Sept-2024; +dup. copies"},{"p":9,"q":"1","marks":"4","unit":1,"topic":"Const pointers, virtual functions, new versus malloc","qh":"<p>Q. No. 1 (a) C++ has constant pointer and pointer to a constant; differentiate between the two using suitable examples. (1.5) (b) What is the purpose of virtual functions in C++? Write a short code snippet to demonstrate their usage. (1.5) (c) What are the advantages of using new operator as compared to functions malloc( ) or calloc( )? (1) [4]</p>","sh":"<p>(a) A pointer to a constant (const int* p) cannot change the value it points to, but the pointer itself can be re-pointed. A constant pointer (int* const p) must be initialised and always points to the same address, but the pointed-to value can be changed. (const int* const p fixes both.)</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nint main() {\n    int a = 10, b = 20;\n    const int* p1 = &amp;a;       // pointer to constant\n    p1 = &amp;b;                  // OK: pointer can change\n    // *p1 = 5;               // error: value cannot be changed through p1\n    int* const p2 = &amp;a;       // constant pointer\n    *p2 = 99;                 // OK: value can change\n    // p2 = &amp;b;               // error: pointer cannot be re-pointed\n    cout &lt;&lt; *p1 &lt;&lt; &#x27; &#x27; &lt;&lt; *p2 &lt;&lt; &#x27; &#x27; &lt;&lt; a &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">20 99 99</p><p>(b) A virtual function lets a base-class pointer or reference call the version of a function that belongs to the actual object, so one interface works for many derived types (run-time polymorphism, dynamic binding). It makes frameworks extensible and is needed so that overridden behaviour is used through the base type.</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass Base { public: virtual void show() { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Base\\n&quot;; } virtual ~Base() {} };\nclass Derived : public Base { public: void show() override { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Derived\\n&quot;; } };\nint main() {\n    Base* p = new Derived;\n    p-&gt;show();               // prints Derived because show() is virtual\n    delete p;\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Derived</p><p>(c) new (1) knows the type and returns the correct pointer type (no cast, malloc returns void*); (2) computes the size itself (no sizeof); (3) calls the constructor, and delete calls the destructor, so objects are properly initialised and cleaned; (4) can be overloaded and throws bad_alloc on failure instead of returning NULL; (5) can initialise (new int(5)); (6) new[]/delete[] handle arrays of objects. malloc/calloc only return raw uninitialised (or zeroed) memory and never call constructors.</p>","exam":"mid","year":2024,"branch":"CS","src":"CO Midsem 2024.pdf (CO203n; Sept-2024; +dup. copies"},{"p":9,"q":"2","marks":"4","unit":2,"topic":"Vowel switch in Java; friend class","qh":"<p>Q. No. 2 (a) Write a program in Java for checking a char is vowel or consonant by using switch control statement. (2)</p><p>(b) What is a friend class? What is general form of declaring a friend class? What are the special characteristics of a friendly function in C++? Give a suitable declaration of a class to show how friend functions can be used as a bridge between two classes in C++. (2) [4]</p>","sh":"<p>(a) Case labels for the five lowercase and five uppercase vowels share one body; default handles everything else (after checking that the character is a letter).</p><pre>public class VowelCheck {\n    static String check(char ch) {\n        if (!Character.isLetter(ch)) return ch + &quot; is not a letter&quot;;\n        switch (ch) {\n            case &#x27;a&#x27;: case &#x27;e&#x27;: case &#x27;i&#x27;: case &#x27;o&#x27;: case &#x27;u&#x27;:\n            case &#x27;A&#x27;: case &#x27;E&#x27;: case &#x27;I&#x27;: case &#x27;O&#x27;: case &#x27;U&#x27;:\n                return ch + &quot; is a vowel&quot;;\n            default:\n                return ch + &quot; is a consonant&quot;;\n        }\n    }\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        char[] tests = {&#x27;e&#x27;, &#x27;K&#x27;, &#x27;U&#x27;, &#x27;7&#x27;};\n        for (char c : tests) System.out.println(check(c));\n    }\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">e is a vowel</p><p class=\"ln i0\">K is a consonant</p><p class=\"ln i0\">U is a vowel</p><p class=\"ln i0\">7 is not a letter</p><p>(b) A friend class is a class all of whose member functions are allowed to access the private and protected members of another class. General form: inside class A, write friend class B;. Friendship is granted by the class (not taken), is not mutual and not inherited. Special characteristics of a friend function: it is not in the scope of the class; it is called like an ordinary function (not with ./-&gt;); it has no this pointer and must take objects as arguments; it accesses members through an object name (a.x); it can be declared anywhere in the class (public/private makes no difference); it may be a member of another class. As a bridge, one function is a friend of both classes and can read the private data of both:</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass Beta;\nclass Alpha {\n    int a;\npublic:\n    Alpha(int x) : a(x) {}\n    friend int bridge(const Alpha&amp;, const Beta&amp;);      // bridge between the two classes\n};\nclass Beta {\n    int b;\npublic:\n    Beta(int x) : b(x) {}\n    friend int bridge(const Alpha&amp;, const Beta&amp;);\n};\nint bridge(const Alpha&amp; p, const Beta&amp; q) { return p.a + q.b; }\nint main() {\n    Alpha x(15);\n    Beta y(27);\n    cout &lt;&lt; bridge(x, y) &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">42</p>","exam":"mid","year":2024,"branch":"CS","src":"CO Midsem 2024.pdf (CO203n; Sept-2024; +dup. copies"},{"p":9,"q":"3","marks":"4","unit":1,"topic":"Operator overloading; Complex +","qh":"<p>Q. No. 3 (a) Explain the concept of operator overloading in C++? List the operators that cannot be overloaded by member functions and friend function. (2) (b) Overload the + operator to add two objects of a class Complex which has two data members real and imag representing the real and imaginary parts of a complex number (2) [4]</p>","sh":"<p>(a) Operator overloading gives an existing operator a new meaning for user-defined types by writing an operator function (Complex operator+(const Complex&amp;)); it is compile-time polymorphism, so c1 + c2 reads naturally. Rules: no new operators can be created, precedence/associativity/number of operands cannot change, at least one operand must be a user-defined type. Operators that cannot be overloaded at all (by member or friend functions): :: (scope resolution), . (member access), .* (pointer to member), ?: (conditional), sizeof, typeid, alignof, noexcept and the named casts (static_cast, ...). (=, [], (), -&gt; can be overloaded only as members.)</p><p>(b)</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass Complex {\n    double real, imag;\npublic:\n    Complex(double r = 0, double i = 0) : real(r), imag(i) {}\n    Complex operator+(const Complex&amp; c) const { return Complex(real + c.real, imag + c.imag); }\n    void display() const { cout &lt;&lt; real &lt;&lt; &quot; + &quot; &lt;&lt; imag &lt;&lt; &quot;i\\n&quot;; }\n};\nint main() {\n    Complex a(2, 3), b(4, 5);\n    Complex c = a + b;\n    c.display();\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">6 + 8i</p>","exam":"mid","year":2024,"branch":"CS","src":"CO Midsem 2024.pdf (CO203n; Sept-2024; +dup. copies"},{"p":9,"q":"4","marks":"4","unit":1,"topic":"Abstract class Figure with Circle and Square","qh":"<p>Q. No. 4 Create an abstract class called Figure which contains a pure virtual function called find_area() and a protected attribute named as area. Create two new derived classes from the above class named as Circle and Square having double type attribute named as radius and side respectively. Implement dynamic polymorphism to find out area of a circle and a square, and show the result. [4]</p>","sh":"<pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass Figure {\nprotected:\n    double area;\npublic:\n    virtual void find_area() = 0;                     // pure virtual\n    double getArea() const { return area; }\n    virtual ~Figure() {}\n};\nclass Circle : public Figure {\n    double radius;\npublic:\n    Circle(double r) : radius(r) {}\n    void find_area() override { area = 3.14159 * radius * radius; }\n};\nclass Square : public Figure {\n    double side;\npublic:\n    Square(double s) : side(s) {}\n    void find_area() override { area = side * side; }\n};\nint main() {\n    Figure* f;                                        // base pointer\n    Circle c(5);\n    Square s(4);\n    f = &amp;c;  f-&gt;find_area();  cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Area of circle = &quot; &lt;&lt; f-&gt;getArea() &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n    f = &amp;s;  f-&gt;find_area();  cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Area of square = &quot; &lt;&lt; f-&gt;getArea() &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Area of circle = 78.5397</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Area of square = 16</p>","exam":"mid","year":2024,"branch":"CS","src":"CO Midsem 2024.pdf (CO203n; Sept-2024; +dup. copies"},{"p":9,"q":"5","marks":"4","unit":2,"topic":"Java output tracing; procedural versus OOP","qh":"<p>Q. No. 5 (a) What will be the output of the following Java code snippet? Explain briefly. (2.5) class Test { public static void main(String[] args) { int[] numbers = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5}; int result = 0; for (int i = 0; i &lt; numbers.length; i++) { result += (i % 2 == 0) ? numbers[i] : -numbers[i]; } System.out.println(&quot;Result: &quot; + result); } } (b) Compare Procedure-oriented programming with Object-oriented programming. (1.5) [4]</p>","sh":"<p>(a) The loop adds the element when the index is even and subtracts it when the index is odd:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">i = 0 (even): result = 0 + 1 = 1</p><p class=\"ln i0\">i = 1 (odd):  result = 1 - 2 = -1</p><p class=\"ln i0\">i = 2 (even): result = -1 + 3 = 2</p><p class=\"ln i0\">i = 3 (odd):  result = 2 - 4 = -2</p><p class=\"ln i0\">i = 4 (even): result = -2 + 5 = 3</p><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Result: 3</p><p>(The conditional operator ?: has lower precedence than == and %, so (i % 2 == 0) ? a : b is evaluated first and then added to result.)</p><p>(b)</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Procedure-oriented (C)                   Object-oriented (C++/Java)</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Program = functions acting on data       Program = objects (data + functions)</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Top-down design                          Bottom-up design</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Data is global/shared, little hiding     Data is encapsulated and hidden Adding new data types is hard            Inheritance and polymorphism ease extension Less secure and reusable                 More secure, reusable and maintainable</p>","exam":"mid","year":2024,"branch":"CS","src":"CO Midsem 2024.pdf (CO203n; Sept-2024; +dup. copies"},{"p":10,"q":"1","marks":"8","unit":0,"topic":"Short notes with examples","qh":"<p>Q.1 Write Short notes with examples: i. Object oriented programming ii. Dynamic binding iii. Operator overloading iv. Abstract Class (4*2=8)</p>","sh":"<p>i. Object-oriented programming (OOP) builds software from objects that combine data and the functions that use it. Its principles: encapsulation (data + functions in a class, hidden behind access specifiers), abstraction (expose essentials only), inheritance (reuse a base class), polymorphism (one interface, many forms). Benefits: reuse, modularity, easier maintenance.</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass Account {\n    double balance = 0;\npublic:\n    void deposit(double a) { balance += a; }\n    double get() const { return balance; }\n};\nint main() {\n    Account acc;            // object\n    acc.deposit(500);\n    cout &lt;&lt; acc.get() &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">500</p><p>ii. Dynamic (late) binding: the function that is called is selected at run time from the real type of the object, using virtual functions and the vtable.</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass Animal { public: virtual void speak() { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;...\\n&quot;; } virtual ~Animal() {} };\nclass Cat : public Animal { public: void speak() override { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Meow\\n&quot;; } };\nint main() {\n    Animal* a = new Cat;\n    a-&gt;speak();            // resolved at run time -&gt; Meow\n    delete a;\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Meow</p><p>iii. Operator overloading lets an operator work on class objects. operator+ for Money:</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass Money {\n    int rs;\npublic:\n    Money(int r) : rs(r) {}\n    Money operator+(const Money&amp; o) const { return Money(rs + o.rs); }\n    void show() const { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Rs &quot; &lt;&lt; rs &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;; }\n};\nint main() {\n    Money a(40), b(60);\n    (a + b).show();\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Rs 100</p><p>iv. An abstract class has at least one pure virtual function (= 0), cannot be instantiated and serves as an interface/base for derived classes, which must override the pure virtual functions.</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass Shape { public: virtual double area() const = 0; virtual ~Shape() {} };\nclass Square : public Shape {\n    double s;\npublic:\n    Square(double x) : s(x) {}\n    double area() const override { return s * s; }\n};\nint main() {\n    Shape* p = new Square(5);\n    cout &lt;&lt; p-&gt;area() &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n    delete p;\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">25</p>","exam":"end","year":2024,"branch":"IT","src":"2024 EndSem OOPS.pdf (IT104; May-2024; +dup. copies"},{"p":10,"q":"2","marks":"8","unit":1,"topic":"Perimeter by overloading; template inheritance","qh":"<p>Q.2 (a) Write a program to calculate the circumference/perimeter of square, circle, rectangle and triangle using function overloading. (4) (b) Explain inheritance of templates using example. (4)</p>","sh":"<p>(a) Four functions share the name perimeter and differ in their parameters: perimeter(int side) square,</p><p class=\"ln i0\">perimeter(double r) circle, perimeter(int l, int b) rectangle and perimeter(int a, int b, int c) triangle.</p><p>(Square takes an int and circle a double, so the argument type selects the version.)</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nconst double PI = 3.14159;\nint    perimeter(int side)               { return 4 * side; }              // square\ndouble perimeter(double r)               { return 2 * PI * r; }            // circle\nint    perimeter(int l, int b)           { return 2 * (l + b); }           // rectangle\nint    perimeter(int a, int b, int c)    { return a + b + c; }             // triangle\nint main() {\n    cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Square(5)        = &quot; &lt;&lt; perimeter(5) &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n    cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Circle(r=3.0)    = &quot; &lt;&lt; perimeter(3.0) &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n    cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Rectangle(6,4)   = &quot; &lt;&lt; perimeter(6, 4) &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n    cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Triangle(3,4,5)  = &quot; &lt;&lt; perimeter(3, 4, 5) &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Square(5)        = 20</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Circle(r=3.0)    = 18.8495</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Rectangle(6,4)   = 20</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Triangle(3,4,5)  = 12</p><p>(b) A class template can be a base class, and templates can be inherited in three ways: (1) a class template derived from a class template (template&lt;class T&gt; class D : public B&lt;T&gt;); (2) a normal class derived from a particular instantiation (class D : public B&lt;int&gt;); (3) a template derived from a normal class. Inside a derived template, members of a dependent base must be reached with this-&gt; or B&lt;T&gt;::.</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\ntemplate &lt;class T&gt;\nclass Base {\nprotected:\n    T value;\npublic:\n    Base(T v) : value(v) {}\n    void show() const { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;value = &quot; &lt;&lt; value &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;; }\n};\ntemplate &lt;class T&gt;\nclass Derived : public Base&lt;T&gt; {          // template inherits from template\n    T factor;\npublic:\n    Derived(T v, T f) : Base&lt;T&gt;(v), factor(f) {}\n    T scaled() const { return this-&gt;value * factor; }\n};\nclass IntBox : public Base&lt;int&gt; {         // normal class from an instantiation\npublic:\n    IntBox(int v) : Base&lt;int&gt;(v) {}\n};\nint main() {\n    Derived&lt;double&gt; d(2.5, 4);\n    d.show();\n    cout &lt;&lt; d.scaled() &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n    IntBox b(9);\n    b.show();\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">value = 2.5</p><p class=\"ln i0\">10</p><p class=\"ln i0\">value = 9</p>","exam":"end","year":2024,"branch":"IT","src":"2024 EndSem OOPS.pdf (IT104; May-2024; +dup. copies"},{"p":10,"q":"3","marks":"8","unit":2,"topic":"Access modifiers; overloading versus templates","qh":"<p>Q.3 (a) Explain the access modifiers in Java with example. (4) (b) Distinguish between overloaded functions and function templates with examples. (4)</p>","sh":"<p>(a) Java has four access levels:</p><pre>Modifier        Same class   Same package   Subclass (other package)   Everywhere\nprivate            yes           no                 no                     no\n(default)          yes           yes                no                     no\nprotected          yes           yes                yes                    no\npublic             yes           yes                yes                    yes\npackage p1;\npublic class A {\n    private int a = 1;          // only inside A\n    int b = 2;                  // default: package p1\n    protected int c = 3;        // package p1 and subclasses\n    public int d = 4;           // everywhere\n    public int getA() { return a; }\n}\n// in another package p2\npackage p2;\nimport p1.A;\npublic class B extends A {\n    void test() {\n        // System.out.println(a);   // error: private\n        // System.out.println(b);   // error: default, other package\n        System.out.println(c);      // OK: protected, via inheritance\n        System.out.println(d);      // OK: public\n    }\n}</pre><p>Calling new B().test() prints 3 and then 4.</p><p>(b)</p><pre>Overloaded functions                              Function templates\nSeparate hand-written definitions with the        One generic definition; the compiler generates\nsame name and different parameter lists.          a function for each type used.\nBody may differ in each version.                  Same logic (body) for every type.\nOnly the listed types are supported.             Works for any type that supports the operations used.\nNeeded for different behaviour per type.          Needed for the same behaviour on many types.\n#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nvoid show(int x)    { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;int: &quot; &lt;&lt; x &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;; }          // overloaded functions\nvoid show(double x) { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;double: &quot; &lt;&lt; x &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;; }\ntemplate &lt;class T&gt;\nT bigger(T a, T b) { return a &gt; b ? a : b; }                   // one template\nint main() {\n    show(7); show(2.5);\n    cout &lt;&lt; bigger(3, 8) &lt;&lt; &#x27; &#x27; &lt;&lt; bigger(2.5, 1.5) &lt;&lt; &#x27; &#x27; &lt;&lt; bigger(&#x27;a&#x27;, &#x27;c&#x27;) &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">int: 7</p><p class=\"ln i0\">double: 2.5</p><p class=\"ln i0\">8 2.5 c</p>","exam":"end","year":2024,"branch":"IT","src":"2024 EndSem OOPS.pdf (IT104; May-2024; +dup. copies"},{"p":10,"q":"4","marks":"8","unit":3,"topic":"Rethrow; thread life cycle","qh":"<p>Q.4 (a) What is an exception? Explain the concept of Rethrowing exceptions? Explain with example. (4) (b) What is multithreading in Java? Explain the life cycle of a thread. (4)</p>","sh":"<p>(a) An exception is an abnormal run-time event (division by zero, bad index, file not found) that disrupts the normal flow. C++ handles it with try, throw and catch. Rethrowing: a handler that cannot fully handle an exception passes it to an outer handler with an empty throw; (which rethrows the original object, keeping its type).</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n#include &lt;stdexcept&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nvoid process(int x) {\n    try {\n        if (x &lt; 0) throw invalid_argument(&quot;negative value&quot;);\n        cout &lt;&lt; &quot;processed &quot; &lt;&lt; x &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n    } catch (const invalid_argument&amp; e) {\n        cout &lt;&lt; &quot;process(): logging error -&gt; &quot; &lt;&lt; e.what() &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n        throw;                              // rethrow to the caller\n    }\n}\nint main() {\n    try {\n        process(5);\n        process(-3);\n    } catch (const exception&amp; e) {\n        cout &lt;&lt; &quot;main(): handled -&gt; &quot; &lt;&lt; e.what() &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n    }\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">processed 5</p><p class=\"ln i0\">process(): logging error → negative value</p><p class=\"ln i0\">main(): handled → negative value</p><p>(b) Multithreading: running several threads (independent paths of execution sharing one process&#x27;s memory) at the same time, so a program can do many tasks concurrently (UI + download). Java threads are created by extending Thread or implementing Runnable.</p><pre>       start()            scheduler picks it               run() completes\nNEW ---------&gt; RUNNABLE &lt;---------------&gt; RUNNING -------------------------&gt; TERMINATED\n                  ^   |                       |\n                  |   +-- waiting for lock -&gt; BLOCKED\n                  +------ sleep(ms)/wait()/join() -&gt; WAITING / TIMED_WAITING</pre><p>NEW: object created, not started. RUNNABLE: started, ready or running. BLOCKED: waiting for a monitor lock. WAITING: waits indefinitely (wait(), join()). TIMED_WAITING: waits for a time (sleep(ms)). TERMINATED: run() finished or an uncaught exception ended it.</p>","exam":"end","year":2024,"branch":"IT","src":"2024 EndSem OOPS.pdf (IT104; May-2024; +dup. copies"},{"p":10,"q":"5","marks":"8","unit":2,"topic":"Interface programs and Vector operations","qh":"<p>Q.5 (a) What is Interface? Write a Java program to create an Animal interface with a method called Sound() that takes no arguments and returns void. Create a class each for Dog, Cat, and Lion that implements Animal to print their sound. (4) (b) Write a Java program to insert, update, iterate and delete the elements in a Vector. (4)</p>","sh":"<p>(a) An interface is a completely abstract type: it lists methods (and constants) that implementing classes must provide. A class implements it; a class may implement many interfaces (Java&#x27;s form of multiple inheritance), and an interface reference can hold any implementing object.</p><pre>interface Animal {\n    void Sound();                      // public abstract by default\n}\nclass Dog implements Animal {\n    public void Sound() { System.out.println(&quot;Dog: Woof&quot;); }\n}\nclass Cat implements Animal {\n    public void Sound() { System.out.println(&quot;Cat: Meow&quot;); }\n}\nclass Lion implements Animal {\n    public void Sound() { System.out.println(&quot;Lion: Roar&quot;); }\n}\npublic class AnimalDemo {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Animal[] zoo = { new Dog(), new Cat(), new Lion() };\n        for (Animal a : zoo) a.Sound();\n    }\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Dog: Woof</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Cat: Meow</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Lion: Roar</p><p>(b)</p><pre>import java.util.Vector;\npublic class VectorDemo {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Vector&lt;String&gt; v = new Vector&lt;&gt;();\n        v.add(&quot;Apple&quot;);                    // insert\n        v.add(&quot;Banana&quot;);\n        v.add(&quot;Cherry&quot;);\n        v.add(1, &quot;Mango&quot;);                 // insert at index 1\n        System.out.println(&quot;After insert : &quot; + v);\n        v.set(0, &quot;Apricot&quot;);               // update\n        System.out.println(&quot;After update : &quot; + v);\n        System.out.print(&quot;Iterating    :&quot;);\n        for (String s : v) System.out.print(&quot; &quot; + s);\n        System.out.println();\n        v.remove(&quot;Banana&quot;);                // delete by value\n        v.remove(0);                       // delete by index\n        System.out.println(&quot;After delete : &quot; + v);\n    }\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0 h\">After insert : [Apple, Mango, Banana, Cherry]</p><p class=\"ln i0 h\">After update : [Apricot, Mango, Banana, Cherry]</p><p class=\"ln i0 h\">Iterating    : Apricot Mango Banana Cherry</p><p class=\"ln i0 h\">After delete : [Mango, Cherry]</p>","exam":"end","year":2024,"branch":"IT","src":"2024 EndSem OOPS.pdf (IT104; May-2024; +dup. copies"},{"p":11,"q":"1","marks":"4","unit":0,"topic":"Constructor overloading with Item","qh":"<p>Q1 What is constructor overloading? Write a program using constructor overloading to print the item_id of the item by creating an Item class. If no item_id is passed while creating an object of the Item class, then the item_id should be 1001, otherwise, the item_id should be equal to the number value passed while creating the object of the Item class. [CO1] [4 Marks]</p>","sh":"<p>Constructor overloading means a class has several constructors with the same name and different parameter lists; the compiler selects the one that matches the arguments given when the object is created. Here the no-argument constructor sets item_id to 1001 and the one-argument constructor uses the passed number.</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass Item {\n    int item_id;\npublic:\n    Item() : item_id(1001) {}                  // used when no id is passed\n    Item(int id) : item_id(id) {}              // used when an id is passed\n    void print() const { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;item_id = &quot; &lt;&lt; item_id &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;; }\n};\nint main() {\n    Item a;            // default constructor\n    Item b(2045);      // parameterized constructor\n    a.print();\n    b.print();\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">item_id = 1001</p><p class=\"ln i0\">item_id = 2045</p>","exam":"mid","year":2024,"branch":"IT","src":"2024 Mid Sem OOPs.pdf (IT104; March-2024; +dup. copies"},{"p":11,"q":"2","marks":"4","unit":0,"topic":"Swap with references; OOP versus POP","qh":"<p>Q2 a) Write a C++ program using reference variables as arguments to swap the values of a pair of integers. [CO1] [2 Marks] b) Differentiate between Object-oriented vs procedure-oriented programming languages. [CO1] [2 Marks]</p>","sh":"<p>(a) Reference parameters are aliases of the caller&#x27;s variables, so changes made inside swapValues are visible in main.</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nvoid swapValues(int&amp; a, int&amp; b) {\n    int t = a;\n    a = b;\n    b = t;\n}\nint main() {\n    int x = 10, y = 25;\n    cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Before: x = &quot; &lt;&lt; x &lt;&lt; &quot;, y = &quot; &lt;&lt; y &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n    swapValues(x, y);\n    cout &lt;&lt; &quot;After:  x = &quot; &lt;&lt; x &lt;&lt; &quot;, y = &quot; &lt;&lt; y &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Before: x = 10, y = 25</p><p class=\"ln i0\">After:  x = 25, y = 10</p><p>(b)</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Procedure-oriented (C, Pascal)             Object-oriented (C++, Java)</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Functions are the building blocks          Objects/classes are the building blocks Top-down approach                          Bottom-up approach</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Data is exposed; global data is shared     Data is hidden (encapsulation)</p><p class=\"ln i0\">No inheritance or polymorphism             Inheritance, polymorphism, abstraction Reuse by copying/calling functions         Reuse by inheritance and composition</p>","exam":"mid","year":2024,"branch":"IT","src":"2024 Mid Sem OOPs.pdf (IT104; March-2024; +dup. copies"},{"p":11,"q":"3","marks":"4","unit":0,"topic":"BankAccount class; swapping private data across classes","qh":"<p>Q3 a) Define a class named &#x27;Bank Account&#x27; to represent following members: Data members: Account Number, Name of Depositor, Account Type, Balance Amount. Member functions: Initialize members, Deposit Amount, Withdraw Amount, Display Balance. Write a C++ program to test the Bank Account class for 10 customers. [CO2] [2 Marks] b) Write a program in C++ to swap two private data variables of two different classes (int a is data variable of class X in private scope and int b is data variable of class Y in private scope). [CO2] [2 Marks]</p>","sh":"<p>(a) BankAccount keeps the four data members private and exposes the four operations. main creates an array of 10 accounts, initialises them in a loop, applies a deposit and a withdrawal to each, and displays them.</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n#include &lt;string&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass BankAccount {\n    long accNo;\n    string name;\n    string type;\n    double balance;\npublic:\n    void init(long n, const string&amp; nm, const string&amp; t, double b) { accNo = n; name = nm; type = t; balance = b; }\n    void deposit(double amt) { balance += amt; }\n    void withdraw(double amt) {\n        if (amt &gt; balance) cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Insufficient balance for account &quot; &lt;&lt; accNo &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n        else balance -= amt;\n    }\n    void display() const {\n        cout &lt;&lt; accNo &lt;&lt; &quot;  &quot; &lt;&lt; name &lt;&lt; &quot;  &quot; &lt;&lt; type &lt;&lt; &quot;  Balance: &quot; &lt;&lt; balance &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n    }\n};\nint main() {\n    const int N = 10;\n    BankAccount acc[N];\n    for (int i = 0; i &lt; N; ++i)\n        acc[i].init(1000 + i, &quot;Customer&quot; + to_string(i + 1), i % 2 ? &quot;Current&quot; : &quot;Savings&quot;, 5000.0 * (i + 1));\n    for (int i = 0; i &lt; N; ++i) { acc[i].deposit(500); acc[i].withdraw(1000); }\n    for (int i = 0; i &lt; N; ++i) acc[i].display();\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">1000  Customer1  Savings  Balance: 4500</p><p class=\"ln i0\">1001  Customer2  Current  Balance: 9500</p><p class=\"ln i0\">1002  Customer3  Savings  Balance: 14500</p><p class=\"ln i0\">1003  Customer4  Current  Balance: 19500</p><p class=\"ln i0\">1004  Customer5  Savings  Balance: 24500</p><p class=\"ln i0\">1005  Customer6  Current  Balance: 29500</p><p class=\"ln i0\">1006  Customer7  Savings  Balance: 34500</p><p class=\"ln i0\">1007  Customer8  Current  Balance: 39500</p><p class=\"ln i0\">1008  Customer9  Savings  Balance: 44500</p><p class=\"ln i0\">1009  Customer10  Current  Balance: 49500</p><p>(b) A function that is a friend of both classes can read and write both private variables.</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass Y;\nclass X {\n    int a;\npublic:\n    X(int v) : a(v) {}\n    friend void swapPrivate(X&amp;, Y&amp;);\n    void show() const { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;a = &quot; &lt;&lt; a &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;; }\n};\nclass Y {\n    int b;\npublic:\n    Y(int v) : b(v) {}\n    friend void swapPrivate(X&amp;, Y&amp;);\n    void show() const { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;b = &quot; &lt;&lt; b &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;; }\n};\nvoid swapPrivate(X&amp; x, Y&amp; y) {\n    int t = x.a;\n    x.a = y.b;\n    y.b = t;\n}\nint main() {\n    X x(10);\n    Y y(99);\n    swapPrivate(x, y);\n    x.show();\n    y.show();\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">a = 99</p><p class=\"ln i0\">b = 10</p>","exam":"mid","year":2024,"branch":"IT","src":"2024 Mid Sem OOPs.pdf (IT104; March-2024; +dup. copies"},{"p":11,"q":"4","marks":"4","unit":1,"topic":"Initializer list; pure virtual versus virtual","qh":"<p>Q4 a) What is the initializer list in C++? When do we use the Initializer List in C++? Explain with Example. [CO2] [2 Marks] b) What is the purpose of pure virtual function. How it is different from virtual function? Elaborate with suitable example. [CO2] [2 Marks]</p>","sh":"<p>(a) The initializer list is the part of a constructor after the colon (Point(int a, int b) : x(a), y(b) {}) that initialises members and base classes before the constructor body runs. It is needed for: const members, reference members, members of class type without a default constructor, passing arguments to a base-class constructor, and when a parameter name equals a member name. It is also more efficient (direct initialisation instead of default-construct then assign). Members are initialised in the order of declaration, not the order in the list.</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass Circle {\n    const double pi;       // const member: must use the initializer list\n    double&amp; ref;           // reference member: must use the initializer list\n    double r;\npublic:\n    Circle(double radius, double&amp; out) : pi(3.14159), ref(out), r(radius) { ref = pi * r * r; }\n};\nint main() {\n    double area = 0;\n    Circle c(2, area);\n    cout &lt;&lt; area &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">12.5664</p><p>(b) A pure virtual function (virtual void f() = 0;) declares an interface that the base class cannot implement; the base becomes abstract, and every concrete derived class must override it. A virtual function has an implementation in the base class that derived classes may override. Both are called through base pointers at run time.</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass Shape {\npublic:\n    virtual void draw() { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Shape::draw (default)\\n&quot;; }   // virtual: default body\n    virtual double area() const = 0;                              // pure virtual: no body\n    virtual ~Shape() {}\n};\nclass Rect : public Shape {\n    double l, b;\npublic:\n    Rect(double x, double y) : l(x), b(y) {}\n    double area() const override { return l * b; }               // must be defined\n};\nint main() {\n    Shape* s = new Rect(3, 4);\n    s-&gt;draw();                           // uses the base default\n    cout &lt;&lt; s-&gt;area() &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n    delete s;\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><pre>Shape::draw (default)\n12</pre>","exam":"mid","year":2024,"branch":"IT","src":"2024 Mid Sem OOPs.pdf (IT104; March-2024; +dup. copies"},{"p":11,"q":"5","marks":"4","unit":1,"topic":"String class with operator ==; dynamic versus static binding","qh":"<p>Q5 a) Define a class string. Use overloaded == operator to compare two strings. [CO3] [2 Marks] b) Differentiate Dynamic binding vs Static Binding with suitable example. [CO3] [2 Marks]</p>","sh":"<p>(a)</p><pre>#include &lt;cstring&gt;\n#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass String {\n    char* s;\npublic:\n    String(const char* t) { s = new char[strlen(t) + 1]; strcpy(s, t); }\n    String(const String&amp; o) { s = new char[strlen(o.s) + 1]; strcpy(s, o.s); }\n    ~String() { delete[] s; }\n    bool operator==(const String&amp; o) const { return strcmp(s, o.s) == 0; }\n};\nint main() {\n    String a(&quot;hello&quot;), b(&quot;hello&quot;), c(&quot;world&quot;);\n    cout &lt;&lt; boolalpha;\n    cout &lt;&lt; &quot;a == b : &quot; &lt;&lt; (a == b) &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n    cout &lt;&lt; &quot;a == c : &quot; &lt;&lt; (a == c) &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">a == b : true</p><p class=\"ln i0\">a == c : false</p><p>(b)</p><pre>Static (early) binding                        Dynamic (late) binding\nResolved at compile time                      Resolved at run time\nNormal functions, overloading, templates      virtual functions via base pointer/reference\nFaster, no vtable                             Slower (vtable lookup), more flexible\n#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass A { public: void f() { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;A::f (static)\\n&quot;; } virtual void g() { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;A::g\\n&quot;; } virtual ~A() {} };\nclass B : public A { public: void f() { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;B::f\\n&quot;; } void g() override { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;B::g (dynamic)\\n&quot;; } };\nint main() {\n    A* p = new B;\n    p-&gt;f();      // static binding: decided by the pointer type A*\n    p-&gt;g();      // dynamic binding: decided by the object type B\n    delete p;\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><pre>A::f (static)\nB::g (dynamic)</pre>","exam":"mid","year":2024,"branch":"IT","src":"2024 Mid Sem OOPs.pdf (IT104; March-2024; +dup. copies"},{"p":12,"q":"1","marks":"8","unit":2,"topic":"Interface, overloading kinds / abstract class, packages","qh":"<p>Q1. a) What is an interface. Write a code in Java for defining an interface called cricket_team. Define it to be used with a class, highlighting the features of an interface. 4 [CO#4,5] b) What is the difference between function overloading, function overriding, constructor overloading and template overloading. 4 [CO#1,2,3] OR a) What is an abstract class (in C++). Explain its role with the help of a complete example. 4 [CO#3] b) What is the role of packages in Java. Create a user-defined package and show its use in a different file. 4 [CO#4,5,6]</p>","sh":"<p>(a) An interface is a reference type that declares what a class must do (abstract methods, constants, default/static methods) without how. A class implements it and must define every abstract method. Features: methods are public abstract by default; fields are public static final; no constructors or instance state; cannot be instantiated; a class can implement many interfaces; an interface can extend other interfaces; achieves 100% abstraction and multiple inheritance of type.</p><pre>interface cricket_team {\n    int MAX_PLAYERS = 11;                 // public static final constant\n    void bat();                           // abstract methods\n    void bowl();\n    default void teamInfo() { System.out.println(&quot;A cricket team has &quot; + MAX_PLAYERS + &quot; players&quot;); }\n}\nclass India implements cricket_team {\n    public void bat()  { System.out.println(&quot;India: batting first&quot;); }\n    public void bowl() { System.out.println(&quot;India: fast bowlers attack&quot;); }\n}\npublic class Match {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        cricket_team t = new India();      // interface reference to an implementing object\n        t.bat();\n        t.bowl();\n        t.teamInfo();\n    }\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">India: batting first</p><p class=\"ln i0\">India: fast bowlers attack</p><p class=\"ln i0\">A cricket team has 11 players</p><p>(b)</p><pre>Function overloading     Several functions, same name, different parameter lists, same scope; compile-time.\nFunction overriding      Derived class redefines a base-class virtual function with the same signature;\n                         run-time (dynamic) binding.\nConstructor overloading  Several constructors in one class with different parameters (default, parameterized,\n                         copy); chosen by the arguments at object creation.\nTemplate overloading     Several function templates with the same name but different template/parameter\n                         lists (or a template plus a normal function); the most specialised match is chosen\n                         at compile time.\n#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\ntemplate &lt;class T&gt; T add(T a, T b)       { return a + b; }         // template\ntemplate &lt;class T&gt; T add(T a, T b, T c)  { return a + b + c; }     // overloaded template\nint add(int a, int b)                    { return a + b + 1000; }  // overloaded normal function\nclass P { public: virtual void f() { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;P::f\\n&quot;; } virtual ~P() {} };\nclass Q : public P { public: void f() override { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Q::f\\n&quot;; } };   // overriding\nclass R { public: R() { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;R()\\n&quot;; } R(int) { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;R(int)\\n&quot;; } };  // constructor overloading\nint main() {\n    cout &lt;&lt; add(1.5, 2.5) &lt;&lt; &#x27; &#x27; &lt;&lt; add(1, 2, 3) &lt;&lt; &#x27; &#x27; &lt;&lt; add(1, 2) &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n    P* p = new Q; p-&gt;f(); delete p;\n    R r1; R r2(5);\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><pre>4 6 1003\nQ::f\nR()\nR(int)</pre><p>OR (a) An abstract class (C++) has at least one pure virtual function (= 0); it cannot be instantiated and defines a common interface. Derived classes must override all pure virtual functions to be concrete; base pointers then give run-time polymorphism.</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass Employee {                              // abstract class\nprotected:\n    string name;\npublic:\n    Employee(string n) : name(n) {}\n    virtual double pay() const = 0;           // pure virtual\n    void print() const { cout &lt;&lt; name &lt;&lt; &quot; gets &quot; &lt;&lt; pay() &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;; }\n    virtual ~Employee() {}\n};\nclass Salaried : public Employee {\n    double monthly;\npublic:\n    Salaried(string n, double m) : Employee(n), monthly(m) {}\n    double pay() const override { return monthly; }\n};\nclass Hourly : public Employee {\n    double rate; int hours;\npublic:\n    Hourly(string n, double r, int h) : Employee(n), rate(r), hours(h) {}\n    double pay() const override { return rate * hours; }\n};\nint main() {\n    Employee* list[2] = { new Salaried(&quot;Asha&quot;, 30000), new Hourly(&quot;Ravi&quot;, 250, 40) };\n    for (Employee* e : list) { e-&gt;print(); delete e; }\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Asha gets 30000</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Ravi gets 10000</p><p>OR (b) A Java package groups related classes and interfaces, prevents name clashes, gives access protection (default/protected) and maps to a directory. Create it with package name; as the first line and compile with javac -d</p><pre>. File.java; use it with import.\n// File 1: mathpack/Calc.java\npackage mathpack;\npublic class Calc {\n    public int add(int a, int b) { return a + b; }\n    public int square(int a) { return a * a; }\n}\n// File 2: UsePack.java (in a different directory/package)\nimport mathpack.Calc;\npublic class UsePack {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Calc c = new Calc();\n        System.out.println(c.add(4, 5));\n        System.out.println(c.square(7));\n    }\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">9</p><p class=\"ln i0\">49</p>","exam":"end","year":2023,"branch":"IT","src":"oops endsem_copy.pdf (IT203; Nov-2023; +dup. copies"},{"p":12,"q":"2","marks":"8","unit":1,"topic":"Shallow/deep copy, Java access specifiers, templates","qh":"<p>Q2. a) Define the terms shallow copy and deep copy 2 [CO#1,3] b) Explain the access specifiers in java with respect to their privileges by giving an example. 3 [CO#5,6,7] c) What are templates in C++ and define its types. 3 [CO#4,7]</p>","sh":"<p>(a) Shallow copy copies the member values (including pointers) so both objects share the same heap memory; changing or deleting one affects the other. Deep copy allocates separate memory for the copy and copies the contents.</p><pre>#include &lt;cstring&gt;\n#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass Shallow {\npublic:\n    char* s;\n    Shallow(const char* t) { s = new char[strlen(t) + 1]; strcpy(s, t); }\n};\nclass Deep {\npublic:\n    char* s;\n    Deep(const char* t) { s = new char[strlen(t) + 1]; strcpy(s, t); }\n    Deep(const Deep&amp; o) { s = new char[strlen(o.s) + 1]; strcpy(s, o.s); }   // deep copy\n};\nint main() {\n    Shallow a(&quot;abc&quot;); Shallow b = a;     // default member-wise (shallow) copy\n    Deep c(&quot;abc&quot;);    Deep d = c;\n    cout &lt;&lt; (a.s == b.s ? &quot;shallow: same memory&quot; : &quot;different memory&quot;) &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n    cout &lt;&lt; (c.s == d.s ? &quot;deep: same memory&quot; : &quot;deep: different memory&quot;) &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">shallow: same memory</p><p class=\"ln i0\">deep: different memory</p><p>(b)</p><pre>Specifier     Same class   Same package   Subclass (other package)   Other packages\nprivate           Y             N                 N                       N\ndefault           Y             Y                 N                       N\nprotected         Y             Y                 Y                       N\npublic            Y             Y                 Y                       Y\nclass Account {\n    private int pin = 1234;            // only inside Account\n    int branch = 7;                    // default: this package\n    protected String owner = &quot;Asha&quot;;   // this package + subclasses\n    public String bank = &quot;DTU Bank&quot;;   // everywhere\n    public boolean check(int p) { return p == pin; }\n}\npublic class AccessDemo {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Account a = new Account();\n        System.out.println(a.bank + &quot; &quot; + a.owner + &quot; &quot; + a.branch);\n        System.out.println(a.check(1234));\n        // System.out.println(a.pin);   // error: pin has private access\n    }\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">DTU Bank Asha 7</p><p class=\"ln i0\">true</p><p>(c) A template is a C++ mechanism for generic programming: one definition works for many data types; the compiler generates a version for each type used (compile-time polymorphism). Types: (1) function templates (template</p><pre>&lt;class T&gt; T maxOf(T a, T b)), (2) class templates (template &lt;class T&gt; class Stack). (Templates can also have</pre><p>non-type parameters, multiple parameters, default arguments and specialisations.)</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\ntemplate &lt;class T&gt; T maxOf(T a, T b) { return a &gt; b ? a : b; }     // function template\ntemplate &lt;class T&gt; class Pair {                                     // class template\n    T first, second;\npublic:\n    Pair(T a, T b) : first(a), second(b) {}\n    T larger() const { return maxOf(first, second); }\n};\nint main() {\n    cout &lt;&lt; maxOf(3, 7) &lt;&lt; &#x27; &#x27; &lt;&lt; maxOf(2.5, 1.5) &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n    Pair&lt;int&gt; p(4, 9);\n    Pair&lt;char&gt; q(&#x27;x&#x27;, &#x27;b&#x27;);\n    cout &lt;&lt; p.larger() &lt;&lt; &#x27; &#x27; &lt;&lt; q.larger() &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">7 2.5</p><p class=\"ln i0\">9 x</p>","exam":"end","year":2023,"branch":"IT","src":"oops endsem_copy.pdf (IT203; Nov-2023; +dup. copies"},{"p":12,"q":"3","marks":"8","unit":2,"topic":"Java commands and template inheritance","qh":"<p>Q3 a) What is the purpose of the following functions/commands in Java. Give the code snippet in support of your answer: (i) toString() (ii) nextDouble() (iii) System.out.println() (iv) Public static void main(String args[]) 4 [CO#5,6]</p><p>b) What is the concept of inheritance of templates? Write a code for inheritance of a template base class and template derived class. 4 [CO#4]</p>","sh":"<p>(a) (i) toString() (from Object) returns a text representation of an object; overriding it controls what println(obj) shows. (ii) nextDouble() is a Scanner method that reads the next token as a double. (iii) System.out.println() prints its argument followed by a newline on standard output (System class, out is a static PrintStream). (iv) public static void main(String[] args) is the program entry point: public so the JVM can call it from outside, static so it runs without creating an object, void because it returns nothing to the JVM, String[] args receives command-line arguments (the keyword is public, lower case).</p><pre>import java.util.Scanner;\nclass Point {\n    int x = 3, y = 4;\n    @Override\n    public String toString() { return &quot;Point(&quot; + x + &quot;, &quot; + y + &quot;)&quot;; }   // (i)\n}\npublic class Demo {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {                              // (iv)\n        Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);\n        double r = sc.nextDouble();                                       // (ii) reads 2.5\n        System.out.println(&quot;Radius = &quot; + r);                              // (iii)\n        System.out.println(new Point());                                  // calls toString()\n    }\n}</pre><p>Output (for input 2.5):</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Radius = 2.5</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Point(3, 4)</p><p>(b) Template inheritance: a class template may be derived from another class template, so the derived template reuses the generic base for the same type parameter (template&lt;class T&gt; class Derived : public Base&lt;T&gt;). Members of a dependent base class are accessed with this-&gt; or Base&lt;T&gt;::.</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\ntemplate &lt;class T&gt;\nclass Base {\nprotected:\n    T data;\npublic:\n    Base(T d) : data(d) {}\n    void show() const { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;data = &quot; &lt;&lt; data &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;; }\n};\ntemplate &lt;class T&gt;\nclass Derived : public Base&lt;T&gt; {\n    T extra;\npublic:\n    Derived(T d, T e) : Base&lt;T&gt;(d), extra(e) {}\n    T total() const { return this-&gt;data + extra; }\n};\nint main() {\n    Derived&lt;int&gt; a(10, 5);\n    Derived&lt;double&gt; b(1.5, 2.25);\n    a.show();\n    cout &lt;&lt; a.total() &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n    cout &lt;&lt; b.total() &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">data = 10</p><p class=\"ln i0\">15</p><p class=\"ln i0\">3.75</p>","exam":"end","year":2023,"branch":"IT","src":"oops endsem_copy.pdf (IT203; Nov-2023; +dup. copies"},{"p":12,"q":"4","marks":"8","unit":3,"topic":"Thread states, rethrow, copy constructor","qh":"<p>Q4. a) Explain the states of a thread in detail with a diagram. 3 b) What is rethrowing an exception. Explain with a C++ example. 3 c) What is an explicit call and implicit call of a copy constructor? 2 OR a) What are the two ways to implement multithreading in Java. Write the code. 3 b) How is run-time polymorphism achieved in C++? Explain with a code to support your answer. 3 c) What is the difference between delete and delete []? 2</p>","sh":"<p>(a)</p><p class=\"ln i0\">start()                 CPU scheduler</p><pre class=\"grid\">NEW  ---------------&gt; RUNNABLE &lt;------------------&gt; RUNNING\n                       ^  ^  ^                          |\n     lock acquired     |  |  | notify()/notifyAll()/    | run() ends\nBLOCKED &lt;--------------+  |  | sleep over / join over   v\n(waiting for monitor)     |  +--- WAITING / TIMED_WAITING   TERMINATED\n                          +------ (wait(), join(), sleep(ms))</pre><p>NEW: created, not started. RUNNABLE: started; ready to run or running. BLOCKED: waiting for a monitor lock held by another thread (synchronized). WAITING: waits indefinitely for another thread (wait(), join()). TIMED_WAITING: waits for a given time (sleep(ms), wait(ms)). TERMINATED: run() finished or an uncaught exception ended the thread (a terminated thread cannot be restarted).</p><p>(b) Rethrowing: a catch block handles part of the problem and then passes the same exception to the enclosing handler with throw;.</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nvoid divide(int a, int b) {\n    try {\n        if (b == 0) throw &quot;division by zero&quot;;\n        cout &lt;&lt; a / b &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n    } catch (const char* msg) {\n        cout &lt;&lt; &quot;divide(): caught &#x27;&quot; &lt;&lt; msg &lt;&lt; &quot;&#x27;, rethrowing\\n&quot;;\n        throw;                                  // rethrow to the caller\n    }\n}\nint main() {\n    try {\n        divide(8, 2);\n        divide(5, 0);\n    } catch (const char* msg) {\n        cout &lt;&lt; &quot;main(): handled &#x27;&quot; &lt;&lt; msg &lt;&lt; &quot;&#x27;\\n&quot;;\n    }\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">4</p><p class=\"ln i0\">divide(): caught &#x27;division by zero&#x27;, rethrowing</p><p class=\"ln i0\">main(): handled &#x27;division by zero&#x27;</p><p>(c) Implicit call: made automatically by the compiler when an object is initialised from another (Box b = a;), when an object is passed by value to a function, or returned by value. Explicit call: the programmer writes the constructor call,</p><pre>Box b(a); or Box b = Box(a);.\n#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass Box {\n    int v;\npublic:\n    Box(int x) : v(x) {}\n    Box(const Box&amp; o) : v(o.v) { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;copy constructor\\n&quot;; }\n};\nvoid take(Box b) {}                // pass by value: implicit call\nint main() {\n    Box a(1);\n    Box b(a);                      // explicit call\n    Box c = a;                     // implicit (initialisation syntax)\n    take(a);                       // implicit (call by value)\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">copy constructor</p><p class=\"ln i0\">copy constructor</p><p class=\"ln i0\">copy constructor</p><p>OR (a) Two ways: (1) extend Thread and override run(); (2) implement Runnable and pass it to a Thread (preferred: the class can still extend another class).</p><pre>class T1 extends Thread {\n    public void run() { System.out.println(&quot;Thread by extending Thread&quot;); }\n}\nclass T2 implements Runnable {\n    public void run() { System.out.println(&quot;Thread by implementing Runnable&quot;); }\n}\npublic class Both {\n    public static void main(String[] args) throws InterruptedException {\n        Thread a = new T1();\n        Thread b = new Thread(new T2());\n        a.start();\n        a.join();                   // wait so that the order is fixed\n        b.start();\n        b.join();\n    }\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Thread by extending Thread</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Thread by implementing Runnable</p><p>OR (b) Run-time polymorphism: a base-class pointer or reference calls a virtual function, and the call is bound at run time (through the vptr/vtable) to the function of the object&#x27;s actual class.</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass Shape { public: virtual void draw() const { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Shape\\n&quot;; } virtual ~Shape() {} };\nclass Circle : public Shape { public: void draw() const override { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Circle\\n&quot;; } };\nclass Square : public Shape { public: void draw() const override { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Square\\n&quot;; } };\nint main() {\n    Shape* s[3] = { new Circle, new Square, new Shape };\n    for (Shape* p : s) { p-&gt;draw(); delete p; }\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Circle</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Square</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Shape</p><p>OR (c) delete p frees a single object (calls one destructor); delete[] p frees an array created with new[] and calls the destructor of every element. Using delete on an array (or delete[] on a single object) is undefined behaviour.</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nstruct Item { ~Item() { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;~Item\\n&quot;; } };\nint main() {\n    Item* one = new Item;\n    Item* many = new Item[3];\n    delete one;           // 1 destructor\n    delete[] many;        // 3 destructors\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">~Item</p><p class=\"ln i0\">~Item</p><p class=\"ln i0\">~Item</p><p class=\"ln i0\">~Item</p>","exam":"end","year":2023,"branch":"IT","src":"oops endsem_copy.pdf (IT203; Nov-2023; +dup. copies"},{"p":12,"q":"5","marks":"8","unit":2,"topic":"Short notes (any 4)","qh":"<p>Q5. Write short note on (Any 4) i) reference variable ii) virtual base class iii) bytecode in Java iv) lifecycle of a java code v) JVM 2X4</p>","sh":"<p>i) A reference variable is an alias for an existing variable, declared int &amp;r = x;. It must be initialised, cannot be re-seated, and shares the memory of x. Used for call by reference (void swap(int &amp;a, int &amp;b)), to avoid copying large objects and as a return type.</p><p>ii) A virtual base class is a base inherited with virtual (class B : virtual public A) so that when a class derives from two classes that share the same base (the diamond), only one copy of the base subobject exists, which removes ambiguity and duplication. The most derived class constructs the virtual base.</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass A { public: int x = 1; };\nclass B : virtual public A {};\nclass C : virtual public A {};\nclass D : public B, public C {};\nint main() { D d; d.x = 5; cout &lt;&lt; d.x &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;; }   // one shared A, so d.x is unambiguous</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">5</p><p>iii) Bytecode is the platform-independent intermediate code (.class file) generated by javac; it is executed by the JVM (interpreted or JIT-compiled), which gives &quot;write once, run anywhere&quot;, plus security checks by the verifier.</p><p>iv) Life cycle of a Java code: write Hello.java (source) -&gt; javac compiles it to Hello.class (bytecode) -&gt; class loader loads it -&gt; bytecode verifier checks it -&gt; interpreter/JIT executes it on the JVM -&gt; output.</p><pre>Hello.java --javac--&gt; Hello.class --ClassLoader--&gt; Verifier --&gt; Interpreter/JIT --&gt; Machine code --&gt; Output</pre><p>v) The JVM (Java Virtual Machine) is the abstract machine that executes bytecode. Components: class loader subsystem; runtime data areas (method area, heap, stacks, PC registers, native stacks); execution engine (interpreter, JIT compiler, garbage collector); native interface. It makes Java portable (a JVM exists per platform) and manages memory and security.</p>","exam":"end","year":2023,"branch":"IT","src":"oops endsem_copy.pdf (IT203; Nov-2023; +dup. copies"},{"p":13,"q":"1","marks":"8","unit":0,"topic":"OOP features; complex numbers with operators","qh":"<p>1. (a) Explain the features of object oriented programming. Describe how each of these is implemented in C++. [4][CO2] (b) Write a C++ program to perform addition and subtraction of 2 complex number using operator overloading Give a sample output. [4][CO5]</p>","sh":"<p>(a)</p><pre>Feature            Idea                                           C++ implementation\nClass and object   Class = user-defined type (data + functions);  class Account {...}; Account a;\n                   object = instance\nEncapsulation      Bind data and functions; hide data             private / protected / public\nData abstraction   Expose only the essential interface            public member functions, abstract classes\nInheritance        Reuse and extend an existing class             class D : public B (single, multiple,\n                                                                  multilevel, hierarchical, hybrid)\nPolymorphism       One name, many forms                           function/operator overloading (compile\n                                                                  time), virtual functions (run time)\nDynamic binding    Function chosen at run time                    virtual functions + base pointer\nMessage passing    Objects interact by calling each other&#x27;s       obj.deposit(100)\n                   member functions</pre><p>(b)</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass Complex {\n    double re, im;\npublic:\n    Complex(double r = 0, double i = 0) : re(r), im(i) {}\n    Complex operator+(const Complex&amp; o) const { return Complex(re + o.re, im + o.im); }\n    Complex operator-(const Complex&amp; o) const { return Complex(re - o.re, im - o.im); }\n    void show() const { cout &lt;&lt; re &lt;&lt; (im &lt; 0 ? &quot; - &quot; : &quot; + &quot;) &lt;&lt; (im &lt; 0 ? -im : im) &lt;&lt; &quot;i\\n&quot;; }\n};\nint main() {\n    Complex a(5, 3), b(2, 7);\n    cout &lt;&lt; &quot;a = &quot;; a.show();\n    cout &lt;&lt; &quot;b = &quot;; b.show();\n    cout &lt;&lt; &quot;a + b = &quot;; (a + b).show();\n    cout &lt;&lt; &quot;a - b = &quot;; (a - b).show();\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">a = 5 + 3i</p><p class=\"ln i0\">b = 2 + 7i</p><p class=\"ln i0\">a + b = 7 + 10i</p><p class=\"ln i0\">a - b = 3 - 4i</p>","exam":"end","year":2023,"branch":"CO","src":"CO Endsem 23.pdf (CO203; Nov-2023; +dup. copies"},{"p":13,"q":"2","marks":"8","unit":1,"topic":"MATRIX class; virtual versus pure virtual","qh":"<p>2. (a) Write a C++ program to perform 2D matrix operations determinant and transpose using class MATRIX, use appropriate constructor(s). [4][CO1] (b) What are virtual function and pure virtual function?. Explain the difference with a suitable examples. [4][CO4]</p>","sh":"<p>(a) MATRIX stores a square matrix up to 5x5. Constructors: default (zero matrix of given size) and one that fills from an array. The determinant is computed by cofactor expansion along the first row (recursion on minors); the transpose returns a new matrix.</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass MATRIX {\n    int n;\n    double a[5][5];\n    static double det(double m[5][5], int k) {\n        if (k == 1) return m[0][0];\n        double d = 0, sub[5][5];\n        for (int c = 0; c &lt; k; ++c) {              // expand along row 0\n            for (int i = 1; i &lt; k; ++i) {\n                int cc = 0;\n                for (int j = 0; j &lt; k; ++j) if (j != c) sub[i - 1][cc++] = m[i][j];\n            }\n            d += (c % 2 ? -1 : 1) * m[0][c] * det(sub, k - 1);\n        }\n        return d;\n    }\npublic:\n    MATRIX(int size = 2) : n(size) {\n        for (int i = 0; i &lt; 5; ++i) for (int j = 0; j &lt; 5; ++j) a[i][j] = 0;\n    }\n    MATRIX(int size, const double* v) : n(size) {\n        for (int i = 0; i &lt; n; ++i) for (int j = 0; j &lt; n; ++j) a[i][j] = v[i * n + j];\n    }\n    double determinant() { return det(a, n); }\n    MATRIX transpose() const {\n        MATRIX t(n);\n        for (int i = 0; i &lt; n; ++i) for (int j = 0; j &lt; n; ++j) t.a[j][i] = a[i][j];\n        return t;\n    }\n    void show() const {\n        for (int i = 0; i &lt; n; ++i) { for (int j = 0; j &lt; n; ++j) cout &lt;&lt; a[i][j] &lt;&lt; &#x27; &#x27;; cout &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;; }\n    }\n};\nint main() {\n    double v[9] = {2, 0, 1,\n                   1, 3, 2,\n                   1, 1, 4};\n    MATRIX m(3, v);\n    cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Matrix:\\n&quot;;     m.show();\n    cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Determinant = &quot; &lt;&lt; m.determinant() &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n    cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Transpose:\\n&quot;;  m.transpose().show();\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0 h\">Matrix:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">2 0 1</p><p class=\"ln i0\">1 3 2</p><p class=\"ln i0\">1 1 4</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Determinant = 18</p><p class=\"ln i0 h\">Transpose:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">2 1 1</p><p class=\"ln i0\">0 3 1</p><p class=\"ln i0\">1 2 4</p><p>Check: det = 2(3*4 - 2*1) - 0 + 1(1*1 - 3*1) = 2*10 - 2 = 18.</p><p>(b) A virtual function is declared virtual in the base class; it has a body, may be overridden in a derived class, and is bound at run time. Overriding is optional. A pure virtual function is declared virtual ... = 0;; it normally has no body, must be overridden in a derived class (otherwise that class is abstract), and makes its class abstract (cannot be instantiated).</p><pre>Virtual function                        Pure virtual function\nHas a definition in the base class      No definition required (= 0)\nOverride optional                       Override compulsory in a concrete derived class\nClass stays instantiable                Class becomes abstract\n#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass Animal {\npublic:\n    virtual void eat() { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Animal eats\\n&quot;; }       // virtual: default behaviour\n    virtual void sound() = 0;                              // pure virtual: no default\n    virtual ~Animal() {}\n};\nclass Dog : public Animal {\npublic:\n    void sound() override { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Dog barks\\n&quot;; }       // must override sound(); eat() inherited\n};\nint main() {\n    Animal* a = new Dog;\n    a-&gt;eat();\n    a-&gt;sound();\n    delete a;\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Animal eats</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Dog barks</p>","exam":"end","year":2023,"branch":"CO","src":"CO Endsem 23.pdf (CO203; Nov-2023; +dup. copies"},{"p":13,"q":"3","marks":"8","unit":1,"topic":"Constructors, divide-by-zero exception, scope resolution","qh":"<p>3 (a) What are the difference between default and parameterized constructors? Give suitable examples [3][CO3] (b) Explain the exception handling mechanism available in C++ with suitable example. Write a program to illustrate divide-by-zero exception. [3][CO9] (c) What are the applications of scope resolution operator in C++? [2][CO7]</p>","sh":"<p>(a) A default constructor takes no arguments (or all arguments have defaults); it creates objects with fixed initial values and is generated by the compiler only if no other constructor exists. A parameterized constructor takes arguments, so each object can be initialised with its own values at creation.</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass Rect {\n    int l, b;\npublic:\n    Rect() : l(1), b(1) {}                     // default\n    Rect(int x, int y) : l(x), b(y) {}         // parameterized\n    int area() const { return l * b; }\n};\nint main() {\n    Rect r1;               // default constructor\n    Rect r2(4, 5);         // parameterized constructor\n    cout &lt;&lt; r1.area() &lt;&lt; &#x27; &#x27; &lt;&lt; r2.area() &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">1 20</p><p>(b) C++ exception handling: code that may fail is placed in a try block; when an error occurs the code executes throw expr; which transfers control (unwinding the stack) to the nearest catch block whose parameter type matches; catch (...) matches anything. Unhandled exceptions end the program (terminate).</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\ndouble divide(int a, int b) {\n    if (b == 0) throw &quot;Divide by zero exception&quot;;\n    return (double)a / b;\n}\nint main() {\n    int pairs[2][2] = { {10, 4}, {7, 0} };\n    for (auto&amp; p : pairs) {\n        try {\n            cout &lt;&lt; p[0] &lt;&lt; &quot; / &quot; &lt;&lt; p[1] &lt;&lt; &quot; = &quot; &lt;&lt; divide(p[0], p[1]) &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n        } catch (const char* msg) {\n            cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Error: &quot; &lt;&lt; msg &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n        }\n    }\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">10 / 4 = 2.5</p><p class=\"ln i0\">7 / 0 = Error: Divide by zero exception</p><p>(c) Scope resolution operator ::: (1) access a global variable hidden by a local variable (::x); (2) define a member function outside its class (int A::f() {}); (3) access static members (A::count); (4) call a hidden/ambiguous base-class member (Base::show()); (5) access names in a namespace (std::cout); (6) refer to a nested class</p><pre>(Outer::Inner).</pre>","exam":"end","year":2023,"branch":"CO","src":"CO Endsem 23.pdf (CO203; Nov-2023; +dup. copies"},{"p":13,"q":"4","marks":"8","unit":1,"topic":"Inheritance types; bytecode, JVM, JDK, Applet","qh":"<p>4 (a) What is meant by inheritance? Explain with examples, the various types of inheritance supported in C++. [4][CO4] (b) Explain the terms (i) bytecode (ii) JVM (iii) JDK (iv) Applet [4][CO7]</p>","sh":"<p>(a) Inheritance is the mechanism by which a derived class acquires the data and functions of a base class, giving code reuse and the &quot;is-a&quot; relationship. Types:</p><pre>Single:        A -&gt; B                 Multilevel:   A -&gt; B -&gt; C\nMultiple:      A, B -&gt; C              Hierarchical: A -&gt; B, A -&gt; C\nHybrid:        a combination (e.g. A -&gt; B, A -&gt; C, B + C -&gt; D; needs a virtual base)\n#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass A { public: void a() { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;A &quot;; } };\nclass B : public A { public: void b() { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;B &quot;; } };      // single (A-&gt;B)\nclass C : public B { public: void c() { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;C &quot;; } };      // multilevel (A-&gt;B-&gt;C)\nclass P { public: void p() { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;P &quot;; } };\nclass Q { public: void q() { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Q &quot;; } };\nclass R : public P, public Q { public: void r() { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;R &quot;; } };   // multiple\nclass H1 : public A {};                                                  // hierarchical: A -&gt; H1, H2\nclass H2 : public A {};\nint main() {\n    C c; c.a(); c.b(); c.c(); cout &lt;&lt; &quot;&lt;- multilevel\\n&quot;;\n    R r; r.p(); r.q(); r.r(); cout &lt;&lt; &quot;&lt;- multiple\\n&quot;;\n    H1 h1; H2 h2; h1.a(); h2.a(); cout &lt;&lt; &quot;&lt;- hierarchical\\n&quot;;\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">A B C &lt;- multilevel</p><p class=\"ln i0\">P Q R &lt;- multiple</p><p class=\"ln i0\">A A &lt;- hierarchical</p><p>(b) (i) Bytecode: the platform-independent instruction set (.class) produced by javac. (ii) JVM: the virtual machine that loads, verifies and executes bytecode (with JIT and garbage collection) on each platform. (iii) JDK: Java Development Kit = JRE + tools (javac, java, jar, javadoc, debugger) used to develop Java programs. (iv) Applet: a small Java program (extends java.applet.Applet) embedded in a web page and run by a browser/appletviewer in a restricted sandbox; its life cycle is init(), start(), paint(), stop(), destroy().</p><pre>import java.applet.Applet;\nimport java.awt.Graphics;\npublic class Hello extends Applet {\n    public void paint(Graphics g) { g.drawString(&quot;Hello Applet&quot;, 20, 20); }\n}\n/* &lt;applet code=&quot;Hello.class&quot; width=&quot;200&quot; height=&quot;60&quot;&gt;&lt;/applet&gt; */</pre><p>Output: the applet window displays Hello Applet.</p>","exam":"end","year":2023,"branch":"CO","src":"CO Endsem 23.pdf (CO203; Nov-2023; +dup. copies"},{"p":13,"q":"5","marks":"8","unit":0,"topic":"Reference, inline, destructor; getcount","qh":"<p>5. (a) Explain the use of (i) reference variable (ii) inline function (iii) destructors [3][CO3] (b) Write a class which contains a method called getcount(). This method should return the number of instances of the class. [4][CO5]</p>","sh":"<p>(a) (i) Reference variable: an alias for another variable; used for call by reference (no copying, the callee changes the caller&#x27;s variable), to return values from functions and to pass big objects efficiently. (ii) Inline function: the compiler substitutes the body at the call site, removing call overhead; used for small, frequently called functions. (iii) Destructor: ~Class() runs automatically when an object is destroyed to release resources (memory, files, locks).</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nvoid swapRef(int&amp; a, int&amp; b) { int t = a; a = b; b = t; }     // (i)\ninline int cube(int x) { return x * x * x; }                  // (ii)\nstruct Res {\n    Res() { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;acquired\\n&quot;; }\n    ~Res() { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;released\\n&quot;; }                          // (iii)\n};\nint main() {\n    int p = 1, q = 2;\n    swapRef(p, q);\n    cout &lt;&lt; p &lt;&lt; &#x27; &#x27; &lt;&lt; q &lt;&lt; &#x27; &#x27; &lt;&lt; cube(3) &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n    Res r;\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">2 1 27</p><p class=\"ln i0\">acquired</p><p class=\"ln i0\">released</p><p>(b)</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass Counter {\n    static int instances;\npublic:\n    Counter() { ++instances; }\n    Counter(const Counter&amp;) { ++instances; }\n    ~Counter() { --instances; }\n    static int getcount() { return instances; }\n};\nint Counter::instances = 0;\nint main() {\n    Counter a, b, c;\n    cout &lt;&lt; Counter::getcount() &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n    { Counter d; cout &lt;&lt; Counter::getcount() &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;; }\n    cout &lt;&lt; Counter::getcount() &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">3</p><p class=\"ln i0\">4</p><p class=\"ln i0\">3</p>","exam":"end","year":2023,"branch":"CO","src":"CO Endsem 23.pdf (CO203; Nov-2023; +dup. copies"},{"p":13,"q":"6","marks":"8","unit":1,"topic":"Distinguish between (a)-(d)","qh":"<p>6. Distinguish between [8] (a) static and dynamic binding. [CO4] (b) mutli-level and multipath inheritance [CO4] (c) operator overloading using friend function and member function [CO3] (d) pointer to member and pointer to object [CO7]</p>","sh":"<p>(a)</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Static (early) binding                      Dynamic (late) binding</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Function chosen at compile time             Function chosen at run time</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Normal/overloaded/non-virtual functions     virtual functions through a base pointer/reference Faster                                      Slight overhead (vtable lookup)</p><p>(b) Multilevel: a chain A -&gt; B -&gt; C (each class derives from the previous). Multipath: a class derives from two classes that have a common ancestor (the diamond A -&gt; B, A -&gt; C, B and C -&gt; D); it gives two copies of the ancestor unless virtual inheritance is used.</p><p>(c)</p><pre>Member function                               Friend function\nLeft operand must be an object of the class   Any type allowed on the left (5 + obj, cout &lt;&lt; obj)\nBinary operator: one explicit argument        Binary operator: two arguments\nUnary operator: no argument                   Unary operator: one argument\nCan overload =, [], (), -&gt;                    Cannot overload =, [], (), -&gt;</pre><p>(d) A pointer to an object holds the address of an object and uses -&gt; (Car* p = &amp;c; p-&gt;speed()). A pointer to a member holds the offset of a class member (int Car::*pm = &amp;Car::km;) and is used with an object through .* or</p><pre>-&gt;* (c.*pm).\n#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass Car {\npublic:\n    int km = 120;\n    void show() { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;km = &quot; &lt;&lt; km &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;; }\n};\nint main() {\n    Car c;\n    Car* po = &amp;c;                  // pointer to object\n    po-&gt;show();\n    int Car::*pm = &amp;Car::km;       // pointer to data member\n    c.*pm = 150;\n    cout &lt;&lt; po-&gt;*pm &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">km = 120</p><p class=\"ln i0\">150</p>","exam":"end","year":2023,"branch":"CO","src":"CO Endsem 23.pdf (CO203; Nov-2023; +dup. copies"},{"p":13,"q":"7","marks":"8","unit":1,"topic":"Short notes: templates, function-call stack, memory","qh":"<p>7. Write short notes (any TWO) [8] (a) Templates [CO8] (b) stack used in function call [CO6] (c) Memory management on C [CO7]</p>","sh":"<p>(a) Templates give generic programming: a function template template &lt;class T&gt; T maxOf(T a, T b) or class template template &lt;class T&gt; class Stack is a pattern from which the compiler creates a function/class for each type (maxOf&lt;int&gt;, Stack&lt;double&gt;). Benefits: reuse, type safety, no run-time cost. Costs: larger code, long error messages. Specialisation gives special code for one type.</p><p>(b) Each function call pushes an activation record (stack frame) on the call stack containing the return address, arguments, saved registers and local variables; when the function returns the frame is popped and control returns to the saved address. This allows recursion (every call has its own frame), and is why very deep recursion causes stack overflow. Example for f(2) calling g:</p><pre>top  | g: locals, args       |  &lt;- g running\n     | return address to f   |\n     | f: locals, args       |\n     | return address to main|\nbase | main: locals          |</pre><p>(c) Memory is divided into code, static/global data, stack (automatic variables, freed on return) and heap (dynamic memory). In C the heap is managed with malloc, calloc, realloc and free (from &lt;stdlib.h&gt;) and memory must be freed by the programmer; forgetting leaks memory, freeing twice or using freed memory is a bug. In C++ the operators new/delete (new[]/delete[]) do the same job but also call constructors and destructors; new throws bad_alloc on failure, malloc returns NULL.</p><pre>#include &lt;cstdlib&gt;\n#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nint main() {\n    int* a = (int*)malloc(3 * sizeof(int));   // C style\n    int* b = new int[3];                       // C++ style\n    for (int i = 0; i &lt; 3; ++i) { a[i] = i; b[i] = i * i; }\n    cout &lt;&lt; a[2] &lt;&lt; &#x27; &#x27; &lt;&lt; b[2] &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n    free(a);\n    delete[] b;\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">2 4</p>","exam":"end","year":2023,"branch":"CO","src":"CO Endsem 23.pdf (CO203; Nov-2023; +dup. copies"},{"p":14,"q":"1","marks":"8","unit":1,"topic":"Four distinctions (new, static, friend, ++)","qh":"<p>Q.1 Differentiate between [2x4] [CO#1,2,3] i) new operator and operator new ii) class variable and static member function iii) friend function and member function iv) pre-increment and post-increment operator overloading functions (++)</p>","sh":"<p>i) The new operator is the language operator used in new T(args): it calls operator new to get raw memory and then runs the constructor; it cannot be overloaded. operator new(size_t) is the function that only allocates raw memory (like malloc); it can be overloaded globally or per class and does not call any constructor.</p><p>ii) A class variable (static data member) holds data shared by all objects: one copy, defined outside the class. A static member function is a function of the class (no this), can use only static members and is called as Class::f(). One is data, the other is behaviour on that shared data.</p><pre>iii) Member function                         Friend function\n     Declared in the class, has this         Not a member, no this; takes objects as arguments\n     Called as obj.f()                       Called as f(obj)\n     Inherited; can be virtual               Not inherited; not virtual\n     Left operand of operator is *this       Any left operand (5 + obj, cout &lt;&lt; obj)\niv)  Pre-increment ++x                       Post-increment x++\n     operator++()                            operator++(int)  (dummy int distinguishes it)\n     Increments first, returns the           Returns the old value (a copy), then increments\n     updated object (by reference)\n#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass Counter {\n    int n;\npublic:\n    Counter(int v = 0) : n(v) {}\n    Counter&amp; operator++()    { ++n; return *this; }            // pre\n    Counter  operator++(int) { Counter old = *this; ++n; return old; }   // post\n    int get() const { return n; }\n};\nint main() {\n    Counter c(5);\n    Counter a = ++c;            // pre: c becomes 6, a gets 6\n    Counter b = c++;            // post: b gets 6, c becomes 7\n    cout &lt;&lt; a.get() &lt;&lt; &#x27; &#x27; &lt;&lt; b.get() &lt;&lt; &#x27; &#x27; &lt;&lt; c.get() &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">6 6 7</p>","exam":"mid","year":2023,"branch":"IT","src":"IT Midsem 2023.pdf (IT203; Sept-2023; +dup. copies"},{"p":14,"q":"2","marks":"4","unit":1,"topic":"Nested classes and friend class","qh":"<p>Q.2 What are nested classes? Why they are needed? How is a friend class different in terms of logic and implementation? [4] [CO#3]</p>","sh":"<p>A nested class is a class declared inside another class (class Outer { class Inner {...}; };). Its name is in the scope of the outer class (Outer::Inner); the enclosing class has no special access to the nested class&#x27;s private members, but the nested class (being a member) can use the enclosing class&#x27;s private names and static members through an object. They are needed to group helper classes that make sense only inside the outer class (e.g. a Node inside LinkedList, an Iterator inside a container), to avoid polluting the global namespace and to hide implementation details.</p><p>Friend class: a separate, independent class that is granted access (friend class B;) to the private members of the class that declares the friendship. Differences: logic: nesting expresses &quot;belongs to / is part of&quot;; friendship expresses &quot;trusted to access&quot;. Implementation: a nested class is defined inside the other class and named Outer::Inner; a friend class is defined elsewhere and named normally, and the friendship is one-way and must be declared by the class giving access.</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass Outer {\n    int secret = 7;\npublic:\n    class Inner {                        // nested class\n    public:\n        void show() { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Inner inside Outer\\n&quot;; }\n    };\n    friend class Spy;                    // friend class (separate class)\n};\nclass Spy {\npublic:\n    int peek(const Outer&amp; o) { return o.secret; }   // allowed by friendship\n};\nint main() {\n    Outer::Inner i;\n    i.show();\n    Outer o;\n    Spy s;\n    cout &lt;&lt; s.peek(o) &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Inner inside Outer</p><p class=\"ln i0\">7</p>","exam":"mid","year":2023,"branch":"IT","src":"IT Midsem 2023.pdf (IT203; Sept-2023; +dup. copies"},{"p":14,"q":"3","marks":"4","unit":0,"topic":"& in copy constructor; void* and size_t","qh":"<p>Q.3 Answer the following [2x2] [CO#2,1] i) What is the role of the &#x27;&amp;&#x27; symbol in the case of a copy constructor? Explain with an example. ii) What are void* and size_t? Explain w.r.t the code where they are used.</p>","sh":"<p>i) The copy constructor is Box(const Box&amp; other). The &amp; makes the parameter a reference. If it were passed by value (Box(Box other)), copying the argument would require calling the copy constructor, which would again copy its argument, and so on without end (infinite recursion), so the compiler rejects it. const stops the constructor from modifying the source and allows copying from temporaries.</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass Box {\n    int w;\npublic:\n    Box(int x) : w(x) {}\n    Box(const Box&amp; other) : w(other.w) { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;copied\\n&quot;; }   // &amp; avoids infinite recursion\n    int get() const { return w; }\n};\nint main() {\n    Box a(8);\n    Box b(a);\n    cout &lt;&lt; b.get() &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">copied</p><p class=\"ln i0\">8</p><p>ii) void* is a generic (typeless) pointer that can hold the address of any object type; it cannot be dereferenced or used in pointer arithmetic until cast to a concrete type (malloc returns void*). size_t is an unsigned integer type defined in &lt;cstddef&gt; that holds the size of any object or the count of elements (result of sizeof, argument of malloc and of operator new, container sizes).</p><pre>#include &lt;cstddef&gt;\n#include &lt;cstdlib&gt;\n#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nint main() {\n    size_t n = 4;                                     // a count/size\n    void* raw = malloc(n * sizeof(int));              // sizeof gives size_t; malloc returns void*\n    int* a = static_cast&lt;int*&gt;(raw);                  // cast void* to a typed pointer\n    for (size_t i = 0; i &lt; n; ++i) a[i] = (int)(i * i);\n    cout &lt;&lt; a[3] &lt;&lt; &#x27; &#x27; &lt;&lt; sizeof(int) &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n    free(raw);\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">9 4</p>","exam":"mid","year":2023,"branch":"IT","src":"IT Midsem 2023.pdf (IT203; Sept-2023; +dup. copies"},{"p":14,"q":"4","marks":"4","unit":1,"topic":"Digit menu program with friend, new/delete, this","qh":"<p>Q.4 Write a complete C++ code for the given expected output using a friend function. You can enter your own values</p><p>too. Show the use of new, delete, constructor(s), destructors, and this pointer within the same code. [4] [CO#1,2,3] Expected output: Welcome! Please enter a number: 891 Please enter the choice based on the given menu: 1: if you wish to print the sum of the digits 2: if you wish to print the reverse of the number 3: Exit 2 You have selected option</p><p>2: The reverse of the number 891 is 198 Again! (menu) 1 You have selected option 1: The sum of the digits of the number 891 is 18 Again! (menu) 3 Thank you!</p>","sh":"<p>Number stores the number; sumDigits and reverseDigits are friend functions that read the private value. The object is created with new, its constructor stores the value (using this-&gt;), and delete runs the destructor. The sample input is 891, then the choices 2, 1, 3.</p><p>Sample input:</p><pre>891\n2\n1\n3\n#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass Number {\n    int value;\npublic:\n    Number(int value) { this-&gt;value = value; }       // constructor, this pointer\n    ~Number() { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Thank you!\\n&quot;; }            // destructor\n    friend int sumDigits(const Number&amp;);             // friend functions\n    friend int reverseDigits(const Number&amp;);\n    int get() const { return this-&gt;value; }\n};\nint sumDigits(const Number&amp; n) {\n    int s = 0, v = n.value;\n    while (v &gt; 0) { s += v % 10; v /= 10; }\n    return s;\n}\nint reverseDigits(const Number&amp; n) {\n    int r = 0, v = n.value;\n    while (v &gt; 0) { r = r * 10 + v % 10; v /= 10; }\n    return r;\n}\nint main() {\n    cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Welcome!\\n&quot;;\n    int x;\n    cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Please enter a number: &quot;;\n    cin &gt;&gt; x;\n    Number* num = new Number(x);                     // dynamic object\n    int choice;\n    do {\n        cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Please enter the choice based on the given menu:\\n&quot;\n                &quot;1: if you wish to print the sum of the digits\\n&quot;\n                &quot;2: if you wish to print the reverse of the number\\n&quot;\n                &quot;3: Exit\\n&quot;;\n        cin &gt;&gt; choice;\n        if (choice == 1) {\n            cout &lt;&lt; &quot;You have selected option 1:\\nThe sum of the digits of the number &quot; &lt;&lt; num-&gt;get()\n                 &lt;&lt; &quot; is &quot; &lt;&lt; sumDigits(*num) &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n            cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Again!\\n&quot;;\n        } else if (choice == 2) {\n            cout &lt;&lt; &quot;You have selected option 2:\\nThe reverse of the number &quot; &lt;&lt; num-&gt;get()\n                 &lt;&lt; &quot; is &quot; &lt;&lt; reverseDigits(*num) &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n            cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Again!\\n&quot;;\n        }\n    } while (choice != 3);\n    delete num;                                      // destructor prints &quot;Thank you!&quot;\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Welcome!</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Please enter a number: Please enter the choice based on the given menu:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">1: if you wish to print the sum of the digits</p><p class=\"ln i0\">2: if you wish to print the reverse of the number</p><p class=\"ln i0\">3: Exit</p><p class=\"ln i0 h\">You have selected option 2:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">The reverse of the number 891 is 198</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Again!</p><p class=\"ln i0 h\">Please enter the choice based on the given menu:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">1: if you wish to print the sum of the digits</p><p class=\"ln i0\">2: if you wish to print the reverse of the number</p><p class=\"ln i0\">3: Exit</p><p class=\"ln i0 h\">You have selected option 1:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">The sum of the digits of the number 891 is 18</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Again!</p><p class=\"ln i0 h\">Please enter the choice based on the given menu:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">1: if you wish to print the sum of the digits</p><p class=\"ln i0\">2: if you wish to print the reverse of the number</p><p class=\"ln i0\">3: Exit</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Thank you!</p><p>(Input is read from a file here, so the typed values 891, 2, 1 and 3 do not appear in the captured text; on a terminal they are shown right after each prompt, which gives exactly the expected output.)</p>","exam":"mid","year":2023,"branch":"IT","src":"IT Midsem 2023.pdf (IT203; Sept-2023; +dup. copies"},{"p":15,"q":"1","marks":"4","unit":2,"topic":"import statement; Java secure and platform independent","qh":"<p>Q.1 (a) What is the purpose of the import statement? Does a Java program always have to include an import statement? (b) &quot;Java is secure and platform independent&quot;. Comment. [4][CO3]</p>","sh":"<p>(a) import makes classes of other packages usable by their simple name (import java.util.Scanner; lets us write Scanner instead of java.util.Scanner); import package.*; imports all public classes of a package. It does not copy code; it only tells the compiler where to find names. It is not always needed: classes in java.lang (String, System, Math) are imported automatically, classes in the same package need no import, and a class can always be</p><p class=\"ln i0\">used by its fully qualified name (java.util.ArrayList&lt;String&gt; a = new java.util.ArrayList&lt;&gt;();).</p><p>(b) Platform independent: javac produces bytecode, which any JVM executes, so the same .class file runs on any OS (the JVM is platform specific, the bytecode is not); primitive types have fixed sizes, so results do not depend on the machine. Secure: there are no pointers or pointer arithmetic; array bounds are checked; the class loader and bytecode verifier reject illegal code; a security manager/sandbox restricts untrusted code (applets); strong typing and exception handling; garbage collection prevents memory errors. Both properties come from the JVM design.</p>","exam":"mid","year":2023,"branch":"SE","src":"SE Midsem 2023.pdf (SE-203; Sept-2023; +dup. copies"},{"p":15,"q":"2","marks":"4","unit":0,"topic":"TravelPlan class","qh":"<p>Q.2 Define a class TravelPlan in C++ with the following descriptions: Private Members: PlanCode of type long; Place of type character array (string); Number_of_travellers of type integer; Number_of_buses of type integer. Public Members: A constructor to assign initial values of PlanCode as 1001, place as &quot;Agra&quot;, Number_of_travellers as 5, Number_of_buses as 1. A function NewPlan() which allows user to enter PlanCode, Place and Number_of_travellers. Also, assign the value of Number_of_buses as per the following conditions: Number of Travellers &lt; 20 -&gt; 1 bus; &gt;= 20 and &lt; 40 -&gt; 2 buses; &gt;= 40 -&gt; 3 buses. A function ShowPlan() to display the content of all the data members on screen. [4][CO1]</p>","sh":"<p>Sample input:</p><pre>2002 Jaipur 45\n#include &lt;cstring&gt;\n#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass TravelPlan {\n    long PlanCode;\n    char Place[30];\n    int Number_of_travellers;\n    int Number_of_buses;\npublic:\n    TravelPlan() {\n        PlanCode = 1001;\n        strcpy(Place, &quot;Agra&quot;);\n        Number_of_travellers = 5;\n        Number_of_buses = 1;\n    }\n    void NewPlan() {\n        cin &gt;&gt; PlanCode &gt;&gt; Place &gt;&gt; Number_of_travellers;\n        if (Number_of_travellers &lt; 20) Number_of_buses = 1;\n        else if (Number_of_travellers &lt; 40) Number_of_buses = 2;\n        else Number_of_buses = 3;\n    }\n    void ShowPlan() const {\n        cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Plan code: &quot; &lt;&lt; PlanCode &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;\n             &lt;&lt; &quot;Place: &quot; &lt;&lt; Place &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;\n             &lt;&lt; &quot;Travellers: &quot; &lt;&lt; Number_of_travellers &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;\n             &lt;&lt; &quot;Buses: &quot; &lt;&lt; Number_of_buses &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n    }\n};\nint main() {\n    TravelPlan t;\n    cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Default plan:\\n&quot;;\n    t.ShowPlan();\n    cout &lt;&lt; &quot;After NewPlan():\\n&quot;;\n    t.NewPlan();\n    t.ShowPlan();\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0 h\">Default plan:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Plan code: 1001</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Place: Agra</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Travellers: 5</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Buses: 1</p><p class=\"ln i0 h\">After NewPlan():</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Plan code: 2002</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Place: Jaipur</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Travellers: 45</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Buses: 3</p>","exam":"mid","year":2023,"branch":"SE","src":"SE Midsem 2023.pdf (SE-203; Sept-2023; +dup. copies"},{"p":15,"q":"3","marks":"4","unit":1,"topic":"Employee and Manager classes","qh":"<p>Q.3 Write a C++ program to read and display information about employees and managers. Employee is a class that contains employee number, name, address, and department. Manager class contains all information of the employee class and a list of employees working under a manager. [4][CO2]</p>","sh":"<p>Manager inherits from Employee (a manager is an employee) and also holds a list (a vector of pointers) of the employees who work under the manager.</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n#include &lt;string&gt;\n#include &lt;vector&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass Employee {\nprotected:\n    int empNo;\n    string name, address, department;\npublic:\n    Employee(int n, string nm, string a, string d) : empNo(n), name(nm), address(a), department(d) {}\n    virtual void display() const {\n        cout &lt;&lt; empNo &lt;&lt; &quot;  &quot; &lt;&lt; name &lt;&lt; &quot;  &quot; &lt;&lt; address &lt;&lt; &quot;  &quot; &lt;&lt; department &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n    }\n    string getName() const { return name; }\n    virtual ~Employee() {}\n};\nclass Manager : public Employee {\n    vector&lt;Employee*&gt; team;                       // employees working under this manager\npublic:\n    Manager(int n, string nm, string a, string d) : Employee(n, nm, a, d) {}\n    void addEmployee(Employee* e) { team.push_back(e); }\n    void display() const override {\n        Employee::display();\n        cout &lt;&lt; &quot;  Team (&quot; &lt;&lt; team.size() &lt;&lt; &quot;):\\n&quot;;\n        for (const Employee* e : team) { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;    &quot;; e-&gt;display(); }\n    }\n};\nint main() {\n    Employee e1(101, &quot;Asha&quot;, &quot;Delhi&quot;, &quot;Sales&quot;);\n    Employee e2(102, &quot;Ravi&quot;, &quot;Noida&quot;, &quot;Sales&quot;);\n    Manager m(1, &quot;Meera&quot;, &quot;Gurugram&quot;, &quot;Sales&quot;);\n    m.addEmployee(&amp;e1);\n    m.addEmployee(&amp;e2);\n    cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Employees:\\n&quot;;\n    e1.display(); e2.display();\n    cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Manager:\\n&quot;;\n    m.display();\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0 h\">Employees:</p><pre class=\"grid\">101  Asha  Delhi  Sales\n102  Ravi  Noida  Sales\nManager:\n1  Meera  Gurugram  Sales\n  Team (2):\n    101  Asha  Delhi  Sales\n    102  Ravi  Noida  Sales</pre>","exam":"mid","year":2023,"branch":"SE","src":"SE Midsem 2023.pdf (SE-203; Sept-2023; +dup. copies"},{"p":15,"q":"4","marks":"4","unit":1,"topic":"Class template with get_min","qh":"<p>Q.4 Write a C++ program to explain class template by creating a template class named pair having three data members of type T which are inputted by a constructor and a member function get_min() returns the smallest of three numbers to main. Note: the value of T depends upon the data type specified during object creation. [4][CO2]</p>","sh":"<pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\ntemplate &lt;class T&gt;\nclass pair3 {                         // the paper calls it &quot;pair&quot;; renamed to avoid clashing with std::pair\n    T a, b, c;\npublic:\n    pair3(T x, T y, T z) : a(x), b(y), c(z) {}\n    T get_min() const {\n        T m = a;\n        if (b &lt; m) m = b;\n        if (c &lt; m) m = c;\n        return m;\n    }\n};\nint main() {\n    pair3&lt;int&gt; p1(14, 6, 9);              // T = int\n    pair3&lt;double&gt; p2(2.5, 7.25, 1.75);    // T = double\n    pair3&lt;char&gt; p3(&#x27;m&#x27;, &#x27;c&#x27;, &#x27;x&#x27;);        // T = char\n    cout &lt;&lt; p1.get_min() &lt;&lt; &#x27; &#x27; &lt;&lt; p2.get_min() &lt;&lt; &#x27; &#x27; &lt;&lt; p3.get_min() &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">6 1.75 c</p>","exam":"mid","year":2023,"branch":"SE","src":"SE Midsem 2023.pdf (SE-203; Sept-2023; +dup. copies"},{"p":15,"q":"5","marks":"4","unit":1,"topic":"Shape, triangle and rectangle with virtual display_area","qh":"<p>Q.5 Create a base class called shape. Use this class to store two double type values that could be used to compute the area of shapes. Derive two specific classes called triangle and rectangle from the base class shape. Add to the base class, a member function get_data() to initialize base class data members and another member function display_area() to compute and display the area. Make display_area() as a virtual function and redefine this function in the derived class. Write a C++ program using these three classes that will accept dimensions of a triangle or rectangle and display the area. [4][CO2]</p>","sh":"<p>For a triangle the two values are base and height (area = 1/2 x base x height); for a rectangle they are length and breadth (area = length x breadth). The program asks which shape to use (t or r), reads two numbers through get_data() and calls display_area() through a base pointer. Sample input: rectangle 6 x 4.</p><p>Sample input:</p><pre>r 6 4\n#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass shape {\nprotected:\n    double d1, d2;\npublic:\n    void get_data(double a, double b) { d1 = a; d2 = b; }\n    virtual void display_area() { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Area not defined for a generic shape\\n&quot;; }\n    virtual ~shape() {}\n};\nclass triangle : public shape {\npublic:\n    void display_area() override { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Area of triangle = &quot; &lt;&lt; 0.5 * d1 * d2 &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;; }\n};\nclass rectangle : public shape {\npublic:\n    void display_area() override { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Area of rectangle = &quot; &lt;&lt; d1 * d2 &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;; }\n};\nint main() {\n    char kind;\n    double a, b;\n    cin &gt;&gt; kind &gt;&gt; a &gt;&gt; b;\n    triangle t;\n    rectangle r;\n    shape* p = (kind == &#x27;t&#x27;) ? static_cast&lt;shape*&gt;(&amp;t) : static_cast&lt;shape*&gt;(&amp;r);\n    p-&gt;get_data(a, b);\n    p-&gt;display_area();            // run-time binding to the right version\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Area of rectangle = 24</p>","exam":"mid","year":2023,"branch":"SE","src":"SE Midsem 2023.pdf (SE-203; Sept-2023; +dup. copies"},{"p":16,"q":"1","marks":"12","unit":1,"topic":"Six short answers: Java I/O, generics, applets, operators, STL","qh":"<p>Q.1 Answer the following questions: [2x6=12] a) I/O streams in Java [CO5] b) What is the need for generic programming? [CO4] c) What are applets? Discuss their use. [CO7] d) Which operators cannot be overloaded? Demonstrate overloading of ! operator via C++ code. [CO3] e) Compare public, protected and private visibility modes. [CO6] f) What are the major components of STL? Discuss briefly. [CO6]</p>","sh":"<p>a) A stream is a flow of data between a program and a source/destination. Java&#x27;s java.io has byte streams</p><pre>(InputStream/OutputStream: FileInputStream, FileOutputStream, DataInputStream) and character streams\n(Reader/Writer: FileReader, BufferedReader, PrintWriter). Standard streams: System.in, System.out,\nSystem.err. Streams are wrapped (new BufferedReader(new InputStreamReader(System.in))) to add buffering</pre><p>and text conversion.</p><pre>import java.io.*;\npublic class IOdemo {\n    public static void main(String[] args) throws IOException {\n        BufferedReader br = new BufferedReader(new InputStreamReader(System.in));\n        String s = br.readLine();                       // read a line from the keyboard\n        System.out.println(&quot;You typed: &quot; + s);\n    }\n}</pre><p>Output (for the input hello):</p><p class=\"ln i0\">You typed: hello</p><p>b) Generic programming writes one algorithm/class that works for many types: avoids duplicated code, gives compile-time type safety (no casts, no ClassCastException), and keeps efficiency. C++ does it with templates; Java with generics (ArrayList&lt;String&gt;).</p><p>c) An applet is a small Java program (extends Applet/JApplet) that is embedded in a web page with the &lt;applet&gt; tag and run by the browser&#x27;s JVM (or appletviewer) in a security sandbox. Life cycle: init(), start(), paint(), stop(), destroy(). Uses: interactive web content, animations, small GUI programs distributed over the web. (Applets are deprecated in modern Java.)</p><p>d) Operators that cannot be overloaded: :: (scope), . (member access), .* (pointer to member), ?: (conditional), sizeof, typeid, and the casts static_cast etc. Also =, [], (), -&gt; can be overloaded only as members.</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass Flag {\n    int v;\npublic:\n    Flag(int x) : v(x) {}\n    bool operator!() const { return v == 0; }     // unary ! : true when the value is zero\n};\nint main() {\n    Flag a(0), b(7);\n    cout &lt;&lt; boolalpha &lt;&lt; &quot;!a = &quot; &lt;&lt; !a &lt;&lt; &quot;, !b = &quot; &lt;&lt; !b &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">!a = true, !b = false</p><p>e)</p><pre>Inheritance mode   public members of base   protected members of base   private members of base\npublic             public in derived        protected in derived        not accessible\nprotected          protected in derived     protected in derived        not accessible\nprivate            private in derived       private in derived          not accessible</pre><p>As access specifiers inside a class: public = usable everywhere; protected = the class, its friends and derived classes; private = only the class and its friends.</p><p>f) The STL has three main components plus helpers: (1) containers that hold data (vector, list, deque, set, map, stack, queue); (2) algorithms that work on ranges (sort, find, reverse, accumulate); (3) iterators that connect containers and algorithms (like generalised pointers: begin(), end()); also function objects (functors) and allocators.</p><pre>#include &lt;algorithm&gt;\n#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n#include &lt;vector&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nint main() {\n    vector&lt;int&gt; v = {5, 2, 9, 1};          // container\n    sort(v.begin(), v.end());              // algorithm with iterators\n    for (vector&lt;int&gt;::iterator it = v.begin(); it != v.end(); ++it) cout &lt;&lt; *it &lt;&lt; &#x27; &#x27;;\n    cout &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">1 2 5 9</p>","exam":"end","year":2022,"branch":"IT","src":"IT ENDSEM 2022.pdf (IT203; Nov/Dec-2022; +dup. copies"},{"p":16,"q":"2","marks":"7","unit":0,"topic":"Structures in C and C++; constructors","qh":"<p>Q.2 a) What is the difference between structures in C and C++? Support your answer with help of examples. [3][CO1] OR Explain the features of object-oriented programming with suitable code and examples. [3][CO1] b) What are the different types of constructors? Which constructors are present in the class by default? How does a child class constructor pass arguments to base class constructor? Explain with examples. [4][CO2]</p>","sh":"<p>(a)</p><pre>C structure                                C++ structure\nOnly data members                          Data members and member functions\nNo access specifiers (all public)          public/private/protected (default public)\nNo constructors/destructors                Constructors, destructors allowed\nNo inheritance/polymorphism                Can inherit and have virtual functions\nNeeds the keyword: struct P p;             struct keyword not needed: P p;\nNo static members, no operator             Static members, operator overloading allowed\noverloading\n#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nstruct Point {                 // C++ struct: members + functions + constructor\n    int x, y;\n    Point(int a, int b) : x(a), y(b) {}\n    int sum() const { return x + y; }\n};\nint main() {\n    Point p(3, 4);\n    cout &lt;&lt; p.sum() &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">7</p><p>OR (features of OOP): objects and classes, encapsulation (private data, public functions), abstraction, inheritance (reuse), polymorphism (overloading, virtual functions), dynamic binding and message passing.</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass Shape {                                       // encapsulation + abstraction\npublic:\n    virtual double area() const = 0;                // polymorphism (run time)\n    virtual ~Shape() {}\n};\nclass Circle : public Shape {                       // inheritance\n    double r;\npublic:\n    Circle(double x) : r(x) {}\n    double area() const override { return 3.14 * r * r; }\n};\nint main() {\n    Shape* s = new Circle(2);                       // dynamic binding\n    cout &lt;&lt; s-&gt;area() &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n    delete s;\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">12.56</p><p>(b) Types: default (no parameters), parameterized, copy (X(const X&amp;)), and (C++11) move constructor X(X&amp;&amp;); plus converting and dynamic constructors as uses. If the programmer writes no constructor, the compiler supplies by default: a default constructor, a copy constructor and (C++11) a move constructor (and also copy assignment and destructor). A derived-class constructor passes arguments to its base constructor in its initializer list: Derived(int a,</p><pre>int b) : Base(a) {...}.\n#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass Base {\n    int a;\npublic:\n    Base() : a(0) { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Base()\\n&quot;; }\n    Base(int x) : a(x) { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Base(&quot; &lt;&lt; a &lt;&lt; &quot;)\\n&quot;; }\n    Base(const Base&amp; o) : a(o.a) { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Base copy\\n&quot;; }\n};\nclass Derived : public Base {\n    int b;\npublic:\n    Derived(int x, int y) : Base(x), b(y) { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Derived(&quot; &lt;&lt; b &lt;&lt; &quot;)\\n&quot;; }   // passes x to Base\n};\nint main() {\n    Base d;               // default\n    Base p(5);            // parameterized\n    Base c(p);            // copy\n    Derived x(7, 8);\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Base()</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Base(5)</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Base copy</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Base(7)</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Derived(8)</p>","exam":"end","year":2022,"branch":"IT","src":"IT ENDSEM 2022.pdf (IT203; Nov/Dec-2022; +dup. copies"},{"p":16,"q":"3","marks":"7","unit":1,"topic":"Dynamic objects / friend; abstract classes","qh":"<p>Q.3 a) How can you create dynamic objects? Demonstrate with code. What are the advantages of creating dynamic objects? [3][CO2] OR What are friend functions? Create two classes Square and Rectangle with 2 data members. Both have a friend function CompareArea that prints which shape has greater area based on the dimensions entered by user for respective objects. [3][CO2] b) What are abstract classes? Describe the difference in inheritance behaviour achieved through pure virtual function, ordinary virtual function and non-virtual function. [4][CO3]</p>","sh":"<p>(a) A dynamic object is created at run time on the heap with new (Box* p = new Box(2,3);), accessed through the pointer (p-&gt;area()), and destroyed with delete p; (which calls the destructor). Advantages: lifetime is controlled by the programmer (not tied to scope); size and number of objects decided at run time (new Box[n]); objects can be shared/returned from functions; enables polymorphism through base pointers and data structures (linked lists, trees); large objects avoid stack overflow.</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass Box {\n    int l, b;\npublic:\n    Box(int x, int y) : l(x), b(y) { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Box created\\n&quot;; }\n    ~Box() { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Box destroyed\\n&quot;; }\n    int area() const { return l * b; }\n};\nint main() {\n    Box* p = new Box(2, 3);          // dynamic object\n    cout &lt;&lt; p-&gt;area() &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n    delete p;                        // destructor runs here\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Box created</p><p class=\"ln i0\">6</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Box destroyed</p><p>OR friend functions are non-member functions declared with friend inside a class; they can read the private members of that class. CompareArea is a friend of both classes.</p><p>Sample input:</p><pre>4 4 5 3\n#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass Rectangle;\nclass Square {\n    double side;\npublic:\n    void read() { cin &gt;&gt; side; }\n    friend void CompareArea(const Square&amp;, const Rectangle&amp;);\n};\nclass Rectangle {\n    double l, b;\npublic:\n    void read() { cin &gt;&gt; l &gt;&gt; b; }\n    friend void CompareArea(const Square&amp;, const Rectangle&amp;);\n};\nvoid CompareArea(const Square&amp; s, const Rectangle&amp; r) {\n    double a1 = s.side * s.side, a2 = r.l * r.b;\n    if (a1 &gt; a2) cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Square has greater area (&quot; &lt;&lt; a1 &lt;&lt; &quot; &gt; &quot; &lt;&lt; a2 &lt;&lt; &quot;)\\n&quot;;\n    else if (a2 &gt; a1) cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Rectangle has greater area (&quot; &lt;&lt; a2 &lt;&lt; &quot; &gt; &quot; &lt;&lt; a1 &lt;&lt; &quot;)\\n&quot;;\n    else cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Both areas are equal (&quot; &lt;&lt; a1 &lt;&lt; &quot;)\\n&quot;;\n}\nint main() {\n    Square s; Rectangle r;\n    s.read();      // side\n    r.read();      // length breadth\n    CompareArea(s, r);\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Rectangle has greater area (20 &gt; 16)</p><p>(b) An abstract class has at least one pure virtual function and cannot be instantiated; it is a base that defines an interface.</p><pre>Pure virtual (= 0)        No body in the base (usually); derived class MUST override it, otherwise it is also\n                          abstract. Base class cannot be instantiated.\nOrdinary virtual          Body in the base; derived class MAY override; the call is bound at run time through\n                          a base pointer. Base class can be instantiated.\nNon-virtual               Bound at compile time from the pointer type. A derived function with the same name\n                          only HIDES the base one (no run-time polymorphism).\n#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass A {\npublic:\n    virtual void pure() = 0;                          // pure virtual\n    virtual void ordinary() { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;A::ordinary\\n&quot;; }\n    void nonvirtual() { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;A::nonvirtual\\n&quot;; }\n    virtual ~A() {}\n};\nclass B : public A {\npublic:\n    void pure() override { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;B::pure\\n&quot;; }\n    void ordinary() override { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;B::ordinary\\n&quot;; }\n    void nonvirtual() { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;B::nonvirtual\\n&quot;; }  // hides A::nonvirtual\n};\nint main() {\n    A* p = new B;\n    p-&gt;pure();          // B::pure\n    p-&gt;ordinary();      // B::ordinary (run-time binding)\n    p-&gt;nonvirtual();    // A::nonvirtual (compile-time binding)\n    delete p;\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><pre>B::pure\nB::ordinary\nA::nonvirtual</pre>","exam":"end","year":2022,"branch":"IT","src":"IT ENDSEM 2022.pdf (IT203; Nov/Dec-2022; +dup. copies"},{"p":16,"q":"4","marks":"7","unit":3,"topic":"Exceptions, templates, typecasting","qh":"<p>Q.4 a) What is the need of exception handling? How can we catch all types of exception? Write a C++ code for exception handling that prints &quot;Arithmatic Exception caught&quot; when a negative number is input for integer variable x. [3][CO4] OR Write a C++ code to show the use of function template and class template. [3][CO4] b) What is typecasting and its types? Write a code to show typecasting of one object type to another object type. [4][CO5] OR Why is Java secure? What is byte code and why is Java called a platform-independent language? What is widening and narrowing of data types in Java? [4][CO5]</p>","sh":"<p>(a) Need: errors (bad input, division by zero, allocation failure) should be separated from normal logic, propagated from deep calls to a place that can handle them, and must not be ignored or crash the program; cleanup happens through stack unwinding. All exception types are caught by catch (...).</p><p>Sample input:</p><pre>-5\n#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nint main() {\n    int x;\n    cin &gt;&gt; x;\n    try {\n        if (x &lt; 0) throw x;                      // throw the offending value\n        cout &lt;&lt; &quot;x = &quot; &lt;&lt; x &lt;&lt; &quot; accepted\\n&quot;;\n    } catch (int v) {\n        cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Arithmatic Exception caught (x = &quot; &lt;&lt; v &lt;&lt; &quot;)\\n&quot;;\n    } catch (...) {                              // catch-all for any other type\n        cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Unknown exception\\n&quot;;\n    }\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Arithmatic Exception caught (x = -5)</p><p>OR function template and class template:</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\ntemplate &lt;class T&gt; T smaller(T a, T b) { return a &lt; b ? a : b; }     // function template\ntemplate &lt;class T&gt;\nclass Stack {                                                       // class template\n    T data[10];\n    int top = -1;\npublic:\n    void push(T v) { data[++top] = v; }\n    T pop() { return data[top--]; }\n    bool empty() const { return top &lt; 0; }\n};\nint main() {\n    cout &lt;&lt; smaller(8, 3) &lt;&lt; &#x27; &#x27; &lt;&lt; smaller(2.5, 4.5) &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n    Stack&lt;int&gt; s;  s.push(1); s.push(2); s.push(3);\n    while (!s.empty()) cout &lt;&lt; s.pop() &lt;&lt; &#x27; &#x27;;\n    cout &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n    Stack&lt;char&gt; t; t.push(&#x27;a&#x27;); t.push(&#x27;b&#x27;);\n    cout &lt;&lt; t.pop() &lt;&lt; t.pop() &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">3 2.5</p><p class=\"ln i0\">3 2 1</p><p class=\"ln i0\">ba</p><p>(b) Typecasting converts a value or object of one type to another. Types: implicit (automatic, e.g. int to double) and explicit (programmer-requested): C-style (double)x, and C++ casts static_cast, dynamic_cast (safe up/down-casts in polymorphic hierarchies), const_cast, reinterpret_cast. Object-to-object conversion is done with a converting constructor or a conversion operator.</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass Rupee;\nclass Dollar {\n    double d;\npublic:\n    Dollar(double x) : d(x) {}\n    double value() const { return d; }\n    operator Rupee() const;                      // conversion operator Dollar -&gt; Rupee\n};\nclass Rupee {\n    double r;\npublic:\n    Rupee(double x) : r(x) {}\n    double value() const { return r; }\n};\nDollar::operator Rupee() const { return Rupee(d * 80); }\nint main() {\n    int i = 7;\n    double d = (double)i / 2;                    // explicit C-style cast\n    cout &lt;&lt; d &lt;&lt; &#x27; &#x27; &lt;&lt; static_cast&lt;int&gt;(9.99) &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n    Dollar usd(5);\n    Rupee inr = usd;                             // object of one type converted to another\n    cout &lt;&lt; inr.value() &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">3.5 9</p><p class=\"ln i0\">400</p><p>OR Java: Security: no explicit pointers, bytecode verifier, class loader and sandbox (security manager), automatic bounds checking and garbage collection. Bytecode: the intermediate .class code produced by javac; any JVM executes it, so the same program runs unchanged on Windows, Linux or macOS (&quot;write once, run anywhere&quot;), which makes Java platform independent. Widening (implicit) converts a smaller type to a larger one without loss: byte -&gt;</p><p class=\"ln i0\">short → int → long → float → double (int i = 5; double d = i;). Narrowing (explicit cast) converts a</p><p>larger type to a smaller one and may lose information: double d = 9.7; int i = (int) d; gives 9.</p><pre>public class Cast {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        int i = 5;\n        double d = i;              // widening (automatic)\n        double pi = 9.7;\n        int n = (int) pi;          // narrowing (explicit cast), fraction lost\n        System.out.println(d + &quot; &quot; + n);\n    }\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">5.0 9</p>","exam":"end","year":2022,"branch":"IT","src":"IT ENDSEM 2022.pdf (IT203; Nov/Dec-2022; +dup. copies"},{"p":16,"q":"5","marks":"7","unit":1,"topic":"Polymorphism types / new-delete; multithreading","qh":"<p>Q.5 a) What is polymorphism? Explain its types with the help of suitable code. [3][CO6] OR Write a C++ code to overload new and delete operators. [3][CO6] b) What is multithreading? What are the two ways of achieving multithreading in Java? Show synchronization of threads with the help of code. [4][CO7]</p>","sh":"<p>(a) Polymorphism: one name or interface with many forms. Compile-time (static): function overloading, operator overloading, templates. Run-time (dynamic): virtual functions through base pointers.</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass Calc {\npublic:\n    int add(int a, int b) { return a + b; }                  // compile time: function overloading\n    double add(double a, double b) { return a + b; }\n};\nclass Animal { public: virtual void speak() const { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;...\\n&quot;; } virtual ~Animal() {} };\nclass Dog : public Animal { public: void speak() const override { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Woof\\n&quot;; } };   // run time\nint main() {\n    Calc c;\n    cout &lt;&lt; c.add(2, 3) &lt;&lt; &#x27; &#x27; &lt;&lt; c.add(1.5, 2.5) &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n    Animal* a = new Dog;\n    a-&gt;speak();\n    delete a;\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">5 4</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Woof</p><p>OR overloading new and delete:</p><pre>#include &lt;cstdlib&gt;\n#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass Item {\n    int id;\npublic:\n    Item(int i = 0) : id(i) {}\n    void* operator new(size_t sz) { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;custom new (&quot; &lt;&lt; sz &lt;&lt; &quot; bytes)\\n&quot;; return malloc(sz); }\n    void operator delete(void* p) { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;custom delete\\n&quot;; free(p); }\n};\nint main() {\n    Item* p = new Item(1);\n    delete p;\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">custom new (4 bytes)</p><p class=\"ln i0\">custom delete</p><p>(b) Multithreading is running several threads (independent paths of execution sharing the process memory) concurrently to use the CPU better and keep programs responsive. Two ways in Java: extend Thread, or implement Runnable. Synchronisation: when threads share data, a synchronized method/block lets only one thread at a time (monitor lock) execute the critical section, preventing lost updates.</p><pre>class Account {\n    private int balance = 0;\n    synchronized void deposit(int amt) { balance += amt; }    // critical section\n    int get() { return balance; }\n}\nclass Depositor extends Thread {                              // way 1: extend Thread\n    Account a;\n    Depositor(Account a) { this.a = a; }\n    public void run() { for (int i = 0; i &lt; 500; i++) a.deposit(2); }\n}\npublic class SyncDemo {\n    public static void main(String[] args) throws InterruptedException {\n        Account acc = new Account();\n        Thread t1 = new Depositor(acc);\n        Thread t2 = new Thread(new Runnable() {               // way 2: implement Runnable\n            public void run() { for (int i = 0; i &lt; 500; i++) acc.deposit(2); }\n        });\n        t1.start(); t2.start();\n        t1.join(); t2.join();\n        System.out.println(&quot;Balance = &quot; + acc.get());\n    }\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Balance = 2000</p>","exam":"end","year":2022,"branch":"IT","src":"IT ENDSEM 2022.pdf (IT203; Nov/Dec-2022; +dup. copies"},{"p":17,"q":"1","marks":"8","unit":0,"topic":"OOP versus structured; exception handling","qh":"<p>Q.1 (a) Describe the features that differentiate object oriented programming languages from structured programming languages which do not support objects. Name a few OOP languages. [3][CO2] (b) What is exception, and type of exceptions in c++? Give a code to handle different exceptions. Analyse the mechanism of handling the exception. How it is different from if-else or switch statements. [5][CO5]</p>","sh":"<p>(a) Structured languages (C, Pascal) divide a program into functions with separate, mostly global data. OOP languages add: objects/classes (data + functions together), encapsulation and data hiding, abstraction, inheritance (reuse), polymorphism (overloading, virtual functions), dynamic binding, and message passing. OOP languages: C++, Java, C#, Python, Smalltalk, Ruby, Kotlin.</p><p>(b) An exception is a run-time error condition (division by zero, array index out of range, memory shortage, invalid input) that interrupts normal execution. Types: (1) synchronous exceptions, caused by the program itself and detected at the point of occurrence (index out of range, overflow); (2) asynchronous exceptions, caused by events outside program control (keyboard interrupt, hardware failure), which C++ exception handling does not handle. Also user-defined exceptions (a class/object thrown) and standard exceptions (std::exception: bad_alloc,</p><pre>out_of_range, invalid_argument, ...).\n#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n#include &lt;stdexcept&gt;\n#include &lt;vector&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nint main() {\n    vector&lt;int&gt; v = {1, 2, 3};\n    for (int test = 1; test &lt;= 4; ++test) {\n        try {\n            if (test == 1) throw 42;                              // int\n            if (test == 2) throw string(&quot;text exception&quot;);        // string\n            if (test == 3) v.at(10);                              // std::out_of_range\n            if (test == 4) throw 3.5;                             // anything else\n        } catch (int e) {\n            cout &lt;&lt; &quot;int exception &quot; &lt;&lt; e &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n        } catch (const string&amp; e) {\n            cout &lt;&lt; &quot;string exception: &quot; &lt;&lt; e &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n        } catch (const exception&amp; e) {\n            cout &lt;&lt; &quot;standard exception caught\\n&quot;;\n        } catch (...) {\n            cout &lt;&lt; &quot;other exception\\n&quot;;\n        }\n    }\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">int exception 42</p><p class=\"ln i0\">string exception: text exception</p><p class=\"ln i0\">standard exception caught</p><p class=\"ln i0\">other exception</p><p>Mechanism: try guards a block; throw creates an exception object and starts stack unwinding (destructors of locals</p><p>run) until a catch whose type matches is found (handlers are tested in order; catch (...) last); execution continues after the handler. If none matches, terminate() is called.</p><p>Difference from if-else/switch: if/switch check a condition at the place where the error might happen, so the error must be handled there (or returned as a code that every caller must check); exceptions carry the error automatically through any number of function calls to the right handler, keep normal code uncluttered, cannot be silently ignored, and release resources during unwinding.</p>","exam":"end","year":2022,"branch":"CO","src":"CO Endsem 2022.pdf (CO-203; Nov/Dec-2022; +dup. copies"},{"p":17,"q":"2","marks":"8","unit":1,"topic":"Function and class templates","qh":"<p>Q.2 (a) Write a function template for finding the minimum value contained in an array. Distinguish between overloaded functions and function templates [4][CO8] (b) A class template is known as a parameterized class comment. Distinguish between the term class template and template class. [4][CO8]</p>","sh":"<p>(a)</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\ntemplate &lt;class T&gt;\nT minimum(const T arr[], int n) {\n    T m = arr[0];\n    for (int i = 1; i &lt; n; ++i)\n        if (arr[i] &lt; m) m = arr[i];\n    return m;\n}\nint main() {\n    int a[] = {7, 3, 9, 1, 5};\n    double b[] = {2.5, 0.5, 4.0};\n    char c[] = {&#x27;q&#x27;, &#x27;e&#x27;, &#x27;z&#x27;};\n    cout &lt;&lt; minimum(a, 5) &lt;&lt; &#x27; &#x27; &lt;&lt; minimum(b, 3) &lt;&lt; &#x27; &#x27; &lt;&lt; minimum(c, 3) &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">1 0.5 e</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Overloaded functions                       Function templates</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Separate definition for each type          One definition; compiler generates one function per type Body may differ per version                Same body for all types</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Programmer writes every version            Compiler instantiates on demand</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Resolved at compile time                   Resolved at compile time (instantiation)</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Can handle unrelated behaviour             Only for the same logic with different types</p><p>(b) A class template is the generic blueprint (template &lt;class T&gt; class Stack {...};); it is not a class by itself. A template class is the actual class generated when the template is instantiated with a type (Stack&lt;int&gt;, Stack&lt;double&gt;). So &quot;class template&quot; = the template; &quot;template class&quot; = an instance of it.</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\ntemplate &lt;class T&gt;\nclass Box {                       // class template\n    T v;\npublic:\n    Box(T x) : v(x) {}\n    T get() const { return v; }\n};\nint main() {\n    Box&lt;int&gt; a(5);                // Box&lt;int&gt; and Box&lt;string&gt; are template classes\n    Box&lt;double&gt; b(2.5);\n    cout &lt;&lt; a.get() &lt;&lt; &#x27; &#x27; &lt;&lt; b.get() &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">5 2.5</p>","exam":"end","year":2022,"branch":"CO","src":"CO Endsem 2022.pdf (CO-203; Nov/Dec-2022; +dup. copies"},{"p":17,"q":"3","marks":"8","unit":1,"topic":"Dynamic binding square/cube; overloading vs overriding","qh":"<p>Q.3 (a) Write a program to find square and cube of a number using late/dynamic binding using base pointer. Write functions for getdata, show data, show result. [4][CO5] (b) Differentiate between (i) Overloading and overriding (ii) class variable and object variable [4][CO4][CO3]</p>","sh":"<p>(a) The base class Op declares the virtual functions getdata(), showdata() and showresult(); Square and Cube override showresult(). A base pointer chooses the version at run time.</p><p>Sample input:</p><pre>5\n3\n#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass Op {\nprotected:\n    int n;\npublic:\n    void getdata() { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Enter a number: &quot;; cin &gt;&gt; n; }\n    void showdata() const { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Number = &quot; &lt;&lt; n &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;; }\n    virtual void showresult() const = 0;\n    virtual ~Op() {}\n};\nclass Square : public Op {\npublic:\n    void showresult() const override { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Square = &quot; &lt;&lt; n * n &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;; }\n};\nclass Cube : public Op {\npublic:\n    void showresult() const override { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Cube = &quot; &lt;&lt; n * n * n &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;; }\n};\nint main() {\n    Op* p;                       // base class pointer\n    Square s;\n    Cube c;\n    p = &amp;s;                      // points to a Square: late binding picks Square::showresult\n    p-&gt;getdata(); p-&gt;showdata(); p-&gt;showresult();\n    p = &amp;c;                      // now points to a Cube\n    p-&gt;getdata(); p-&gt;showdata(); p-&gt;showresult();\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Enter a number: Number = 5</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Square = 25</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Enter a number: Number = 3</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Cube = 27</p><p>(The sample input is 5 for the square and 3 for the cube.)</p><p>(b) (i)</p><pre>Overloading                                   Overriding\nSame name, different parameter lists          Same name and signature in base and derived class\nSame class/scope; no inheritance needed       Needs inheritance; base function should be virtual\nCompile-time binding                          Run-time binding</pre><p>(ii)</p><pre>Class variable (static)                       Object (instance) variable\nOne copy shared by all objects of the class   Separate copy in every object\nDeclared with static; defined outside class   Declared normally inside the class\nExists even with no object                    Exists only with an object\nAccessed as Class::var or obj.var             Accessed as obj.var\n#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass Student {\npublic:\n    static int total;      // class variable\n    int roll;              // object variable\n    Student(int r) : roll(r) { ++total; }\n};\nint Student::total = 0;\nint main() {\n    Student a(1), b(2);\n    cout &lt;&lt; a.roll &lt;&lt; &#x27; &#x27; &lt;&lt; b.roll &lt;&lt; &#x27; &#x27; &lt;&lt; Student::total &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">1 2 2</p>","exam":"end","year":2022,"branch":"CO","src":"CO Endsem 2022.pdf (CO-203; Nov/Dec-2022; +dup. copies"},{"p":17,"q":"4","marks":"8","unit":1,"topic":"Polymorphism kinds with new overload; function kinds","qh":"<p>Q.4 (a) Differentiate between compile time polymorphism and run time polymorphism. Write a code to implement overloading new operator. [4][CO4,8] (b) Compare normal function, inline function, macros in c++. Discuss memory usage, advantages, disadvantages, purpose, conditions of these mechanism. Give suitable examples. [4][CO5,7]</p>","sh":"<p>(a)</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Compile-time polymorphism                     Run-time polymorphism</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Function/operator overloading, templates      Virtual functions via base pointer/reference Bound at compile time (early binding)         Bound at run time (late binding, vtable)</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Faster                                        Slight overhead, more flexible</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Example: add(int,int), add(double,double)     Example: Shape* p; p-&gt;area()</p><p>Overloading new (class-specific operator new):</p><pre>#include &lt;cstdlib&gt;\n#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass Node {\n    int v;\npublic:\n    Node(int x) : v(x) {}\n    void* operator new(size_t size) {\n        cout &lt;&lt; &quot;operator new called for &quot; &lt;&lt; size &lt;&lt; &quot; bytes\\n&quot;;\n        return malloc(size);\n    }\n    void operator delete(void* p) { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;operator delete called\\n&quot;; free(p); }\n    int get() const { return v; }\n};\nint main() {\n    Node* n = new Node(9);\n    cout &lt;&lt; n-&gt;get() &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n    delete n;\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">operator new called for 4 bytes</p><p class=\"ln i0\">9</p><p class=\"ln i0\">operator delete called</p><p>(b)</p><pre>              Normal function              Inline function                Macro (#define)\nHandled by    Compiler: real call          Compiler: body substituted     Preprocessor: text substitution\nMemory        One copy of code; call       Code copied at each call       Text expanded at each use\n              uses stack frame             (larger executable)\nType checking Yes                          Yes                            No (can give surprises)\nSpeed         Call overhead                No call overhead               No call overhead\nDebugging     Easy                         Fairly easy                    Hard\nUse for       General code, large bodies   Small, frequently used         Constants, conditional compilation\n              functions                    functions                      (avoid for functions)\nCondition     --                           Only a request: loops/        --\n                                           recursion may not be inlined\n#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\n#define SQUARE_M(x) x * x                     // macro: no type check, no parentheses -&gt; surprise\ninline int squareI(int x) { return x * x; }   // inline function\nint squareN(int x) { return x * x; }          // normal function\nint main() {\n    cout &lt;&lt; SQUARE_M(2 + 3) &lt;&lt; &#x27; &#x27; &lt;&lt; squareI(2 + 3) &lt;&lt; &#x27; &#x27; &lt;&lt; squareN(2 + 3) &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">11 25 25</p><p>The macro expands to 2 + 3 * 2 + 3 = 11, which shows why macros are error-prone; the functions evaluate the argument first and give 25.</p>","exam":"end","year":2022,"branch":"CO","src":"CO Endsem 2022.pdf (CO-203; Nov/Dec-2022; +dup. copies"},{"p":17,"q":"5","marks":"8","unit":1,"topic":"Multipath inheritance; string class operators","qh":"<p>Q.5 (a) How the multipath inheritance are handled. Give suiable example with code. [4][CO4] (b) Define a class &quot;string&quot; with members to initialize and determine the length of the string. Overload the operators &#x27;+&#x27; and &#x27;+=&#x27; for the class &quot;string&quot;. [4][CO3,4]</p>","sh":"<p>(a) In multipath inheritance (the diamond) a class gets the common base through more than one path, so it would hold two copies of the base members and d.x would be ambiguous. It is handled with a virtual base class: class B : virtual public A, class C : virtual public A. Then D has a single shared A; the constructor of the most derived class (D) initialises A.</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass Person {\npublic:\n    string name;\n    Person(string n = &quot;none&quot;) : name(n) { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Person(&quot; &lt;&lt; name &lt;&lt; &quot;)\\n&quot;; }\n};\nclass Student : virtual public Person {\npublic:\n    Student(string n) : Person(n) {}\n};\nclass Employee : virtual public Person {\npublic:\n    Employee(string n) : Person(n) {}\n};\nclass TA : public Student, public Employee {\npublic:\n    TA(string n) : Person(n), Student(n), Employee(n) {}   // most derived class initialises the virtual base\n};\nint main() {\n    TA t(&quot;Neha&quot;);\n    cout &lt;&lt; t.name &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;        // unambiguous: only one Person\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Person(Neha)</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Neha</p><p>(b)</p><pre>#include &lt;cstring&gt;\n#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass String {\n    char* s;\n    int len;\npublic:\n    String(const char* t = &quot;&quot;) { len = strlen(t); s = new char[len + 1]; strcpy(s, t); }\n    String(const String&amp; o) { len = o.len; s = new char[len + 1]; strcpy(s, o.s); }\n    ~String() { delete[] s; }\n    int length() const { return len; }\n    String operator+(const String&amp; o) const {              // concatenation\n        char* t = new char[len + o.len + 1];\n        strcpy(t, s); strcat(t, o.s);\n        String r(t);\n        delete[] t;\n        return r;\n    }\n    String&amp; operator+=(const String&amp; o) { *this = *this + o; return *this; }\n    String&amp; operator=(const String&amp; o) {\n        if (this != &amp;o) { char* t = new char[o.len + 1]; strcpy(t, o.s); delete[] s; s = t; len = o.len; }\n        return *this;\n    }\n    void show() const { cout &lt;&lt; s &lt;&lt; &quot; (length &quot; &lt;&lt; len &lt;&lt; &quot;)\\n&quot;; }\n};\nint main() {\n    String a(&quot;Object &quot;), b(&quot;Oriented&quot;);\n    String c = a + b;\n    c.show();\n    c += String(&quot; Programming&quot;);\n    c.show();\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Object Oriented (length 15)</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Object Oriented Programming (length 27)</p>","exam":"end","year":2022,"branch":"CO","src":"CO Endsem 2022.pdf (CO-203; Nov/Dec-2022; +dup. copies"},{"p":17,"q":"6","marks":"8","unit":1,"topic":"Function-call stack; pointer-to-member operators","qh":"<p>Q.6 (a) Analyze how the stack is used to implement function calls, and parameter passing options. [4][CO6] (b) C++ also provides us a few operators through we could access the data members and member functions of a class by using pointers. Explain these operators with code. [4][CO7]</p>","sh":"<p>(a) Every call pushes an activation record (stack frame): the arguments, the return address, saved registers and the local variables; the callee runs, then its frame is popped and control returns to the saved address. Recursion works because each call has its own frame. Parameter passing options: call by value (a copy is pushed; the original is unchanged), call by reference (the address/alias is passed; changes affect the caller; no copy), call by pointer (the address is passed by value and dereferenced), and constant references to avoid copying big objects.</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nvoid byValue(int a)     { a = 99; }\nvoid byReference(int&amp; a) { a = 99; }\nvoid byPointer(int* a)  { *a = 99; }\nint fact(int n) { return n &lt;= 1 ? 1 : n * fact(n - 1); }     // each call adds a stack frame\nint main() {\n    int x = 1, y = 1, z = 1;\n    byValue(x); byReference(y); byPointer(&amp;z);\n    cout &lt;&lt; x &lt;&lt; &#x27; &#x27; &lt;&lt; y &lt;&lt; &#x27; &#x27; &lt;&lt; z &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n    cout &lt;&lt; fact(5) &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">1 99 99</p><p class=\"ln i0\">120</p><pre class=\"grid\">fact(3) call stack (grows down):   main -&gt; fact(3) -&gt; fact(2) -&gt; fact(1)   (returns 1, 2, 6)</pre><p>(b) Operators for pointers and classes: -&gt; accesses a member through a pointer to an object (p-&gt;f(), same as (*p).f()); .* and -&gt;* access a member through a pointer to member (a pointer to a data member or a member function) using an object or an object pointer; ::* declares such a pointer (int A::*pm = &amp;A::x;).</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass A {\npublic:\n    int x = 10;\n    void show() { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;show: x = &quot; &lt;&lt; x &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;; }\n};\nint main() {\n    A a;\n    A* p = &amp;a;\n    p-&gt;x = 20;                       // -&gt; member access through an object pointer\n    p-&gt;show();\n    int A::*pd = &amp;A::x;              // pointer to data member\n    void (A::*pf)() = &amp;A::show;      // pointer to member function\n    a.*pd = 30;                      // .*  with an object\n    (p-&gt;*pf)();                      // -&gt;* with an object pointer\n    cout &lt;&lt; p-&gt;*pd &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">show: x = 20</p><p class=\"ln i0\">show: x = 30</p><p class=\"ln i0\">30</p>","exam":"end","year":2022,"branch":"CO","src":"CO Endsem 2022.pdf (CO-203; Nov/Dec-2022; +dup. copies"},{"p":17,"q":"7","marks":"8","unit":4,"topic":"Data files, friend versus member, Java features","qh":"<p>Q.7 Discuss any two in details and suitable examples [8] (a) Working with data files [CO9] (b) Friend function vs member function [CO3] (c) Features of Java [CO2]</p>","sh":"<p>(a) C++ file I/O uses &lt;fstream&gt;: ofstream writes, ifstream reads, fstream does both. Steps: create the stream object, open the file (constructor or open(name, mode); modes ios::in, ios::out, ios::app, ios::binary), check is_open()/fail(), read or write with &lt;&lt;, &gt;&gt;, getline, read, write, detect the end with eof() or by testing the read, and close(). Random access uses seekg/seekp/tellg.</p><pre>#include &lt;fstream&gt;\n#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n#include &lt;string&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nint main() {\n    ofstream out(&quot;marks.txt&quot;);                    // create/overwrite, write mode\n    out &lt;&lt; &quot;Asha 85\\n&quot; &lt;&lt; &quot;Ravi 72\\n&quot;;\n    out.close();\n    ofstream add(&quot;marks.txt&quot;, ios::app);          // append mode\n    add &lt;&lt; &quot;Neha 91\\n&quot;;\n    add.close();\n    ifstream in(&quot;marks.txt&quot;);                     // read mode\n    if (!in) { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;cannot open file\\n&quot;; return 1; }\n    string name; int m, total = 0;\n    while (in &gt;&gt; name &gt;&gt; m) {                     // loop ends at end of file\n        cout &lt;&lt; name &lt;&lt; &quot; -&gt; &quot; &lt;&lt; m &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n        total += m;\n    }\n    cout &lt;&lt; &quot;total = &quot; &lt;&lt; total &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Asha → 85</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Ravi → 72</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Neha → 91</p><p class=\"ln i0\">total = 248</p><p>(b)</p><pre>Member function                               Friend function\nDeclared inside the class, belongs to it      Declared with friend; not a member\nCalled with an object (obj.f())               Called like a normal function f(obj)\nHas this pointer                              No this pointer\nAccesses members directly                     Accesses private members only through an object\nInherited, can be virtual                     Not inherited, cannot be virtual\nNeeded for =, [], (), -&gt;                      Needed for cout &lt;&lt; obj, 5 + obj; can be shared by two classes\n#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass Length {\n    int cm;\npublic:\n    Length(int c) : cm(c) {}\n    int doubled() const { return cm * 2; }                // member function\n    friend int addLengths(const Length&amp; a, const Length&amp; b);   // friend function\n};\nint addLengths(const Length&amp; a, const Length&amp; b) { return a.cm + b.cm; }\nint main() {\n    Length a(30), b(45);\n    cout &lt;&lt; a.doubled() &lt;&lt; &#x27; &#x27; &lt;&lt; addLengths(a, b) &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">60 75</p><p>(c) Features of Java: simple (no pointers, no operator overloading, no multiple inheritance of classes); object-oriented; platform independent (bytecode + JVM); robust (strong typing, exception handling, garbage collection); secure (verifier, sandbox); architecture neutral and portable (fixed size of primitive types); multithreaded (built-in Thread); distributed (RMI, sockets, URL); dynamic (run-time class loading); high performance (JIT compiler); interpreted and compiled.</p>","exam":"end","year":2022,"branch":"CO","src":"CO Endsem 2022.pdf (CO-203; Nov/Dec-2022; +dup. copies"},{"p":18,"q":"1","marks":"4","unit":0,"topic":"OOP features and uses of &","qh":"<p>1. (a) Explain the features of object oriented programming. Describe how each of these is implemented in C++. [2]</p><p>(b) What is the different uses of &quot;&amp;&quot; operator in C++? Give relevant code for each use in support of your answer. [2]</p>","sh":"<p>(a)</p><pre>Feature           Meaning                                       How C++ implements it\nClass/Object      Class = blueprint; object = instance          class / struct, Car c;\nEncapsulation     Data and functions bound in one unit,         private/protected/public sections\n                  data hidden from outside\nAbstraction       Show essentials, hide details                 public interface, abstract classes (= 0)\nInheritance       New class acquires members of an old one      class D : public B\nPolymorphism      One name, many forms                          overloading, operator overloading (compile\n                                                                time); virtual functions (run time)\nDynamic binding   Call resolved at run time                     virtual functions + base pointer\nMessage passing   Objects communicate by calling each           obj.method(args)\n                  other&#x27;s member functions</pre><p>(b) &amp; has four uses: (1) address-of operator (&amp;x); (2) reference declaration (int &amp;r = x; an alias); (3) reference parameter / return type (void swap(int &amp;a, int &amp;b)); (4) bitwise AND (a &amp; b). (&amp;&amp; is logical AND, or an rvalue reference in a declaration.)</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nvoid incr(int&amp; v) { ++v; }                       // 3: reference parameter\nint main() {\n    int x = 6, y = 3;\n    int* p = &amp;x;                                 // 1: address-of\n    int&amp; r = x;                                  // 2: reference (alias of x)\n    r = 10;\n    incr(y);\n    cout &lt;&lt; &quot;x = &quot; &lt;&lt; x &lt;&lt; &quot;, *p = &quot; &lt;&lt; *p &lt;&lt; &quot;, y = &quot; &lt;&lt; y &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n    cout &lt;&lt; &quot;6 &amp; 3 = &quot; &lt;&lt; (6 &amp; 3) &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;       // 4: bitwise AND (110 &amp; 011 = 010)\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">x = 10, *p = 10, y = 4</p><p class=\"ln i0\">6 &amp; 3 = 2</p>","exam":"mid","year":2022,"branch":"CO","src":"CO Midsem 2022.pdf (CO-203; Sept 2022; +dup. copies"},{"p":18,"q":"2","marks":"4","unit":1,"topic":"Overload resolution; overloading vs overriding","qh":"<p>2. (a) What are the steps to invoke the suitable function if the function overloading is done. Write a code to illustrate the same. [2] (b) Explain the difference between overloading and overriding in C++. Give a suitable code to demonstrate overriding [2]</p>","sh":"<p>(a) The compiler selects the function at compile time in this order: (1) exact match (same types, or trivial conversions such as array-to-pointer); (2) promotion (char/short to int, float to double); (3) standard conversion (int to double, double to int, int to long); (4) user-defined conversion (converting constructor / conversion operator). The first step that gives exactly one best function wins; if two functions match equally well the call is ambiguous and is a compile-time error. The return type is never used.</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nvoid show(int)    { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;show(int)\\n&quot;; }\nvoid show(double) { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;show(double)\\n&quot;; }\nvoid show(const char*) { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;show(const char*)\\n&quot;; }\nint main() {\n    show(5);          // exact match: int\n    show(2.5);        // exact match: double\n    show(&#x27;A&#x27;);        // promotion char -&gt; int\n    show(2.5f);       // promotion float -&gt; double\n    show(&quot;text&quot;);     // exact match: const char*\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">show(int)</p><p class=\"ln i0\">show(double)</p><p class=\"ln i0\">show(int)</p><p class=\"ln i0\">show(double)</p><p class=\"ln i0\">show(const char*)</p><p>(b)</p><pre>Overloading                                 Overriding\nSame name, different parameters,            Same name AND same signature in base and derived\nusually in the same class/scope.            classes.\nCompile-time (static) binding.              Run-time (dynamic) binding; base function is virtual.\nDoes not need inheritance.                  Needs inheritance.\n#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass Base {\npublic:\n    virtual void greet() { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Hello from Base\\n&quot;; }\n    virtual ~Base() {}\n};\nclass Derived : public Base {\npublic:\n    void greet() override { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Hello from Derived\\n&quot;; }   // overriding\n};\nint main() {\n    Base* p = new Derived;\n    p-&gt;greet();                 // run-time binding: Derived&#x27;s version\n    delete p;\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Hello from Derived</p>","exam":"mid","year":2022,"branch":"CO","src":"CO Midsem 2022.pdf (CO-203; Sept 2022; +dup. copies"},{"p":18,"q":"3","marks":"4","unit":0,"topic":"Constructors of TEST; scope resolution","qh":"<p>3. (a) Create a class TEST with all possible types of constructors and in main() use following statements. Discuss the outcome of each of the following statements with reasons. (i) TEST a; (ii) TEST a(10,12); (iii) TEST b = a; (iv) TEST c(a); (v) TEST d; d = a; (vi) TEST e = 10; [2] (b) What are the applications of scope resolution operator in C++? Give suitable code/examples [2]</p>","sh":"<p>(a) TEST has a default constructor, a one-argument constructor (also a converting constructor), a two-argument constructor and a copy constructor. Statements (i) and (ii) both declare a; in the same scope that is a redeclaration error, so the program below uses a separate block for each statement (assumption).</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass TEST {\n    int x, y;\npublic:\n    TEST() : x(0), y(0) { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;default constructor\\n&quot;; }\n    TEST(int a) : x(a), y(0) { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;one-argument (converting) constructor\\n&quot;; }\n    TEST(int a, int b) : x(a), y(b) { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;parameterized constructor\\n&quot;; }\n    TEST(const TEST&amp; o) : x(o.x), y(o.y) { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;copy constructor\\n&quot;; }\n    TEST&amp; operator=(const TEST&amp; o) { x = o.x; y = o.y; cout &lt;&lt; &quot;copy assignment\\n&quot;; return *this; }\n};\nint main() {\n    { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;(i)   TEST a;\\n&quot;;          TEST a; }\n    { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;(ii)  TEST a(10,12);\\n&quot;;   TEST a(10, 12); }\n    { TEST a(10, 12);\n      cout &lt;&lt; &quot;(iii) TEST b = a;\\n&quot;;      TEST b = a; }\n    { TEST a(10, 12);\n      cout &lt;&lt; &quot;(iv)  TEST c(a);\\n&quot;;       TEST c(a); }\n    { TEST a(10, 12);\n      cout &lt;&lt; &quot;(v)   TEST d; d = a;\\n&quot;;   TEST d; d = a; }\n    { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;(vi)  TEST e = 10;\\n&quot;;     TEST e = 10; }\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><pre>(i)   TEST a;\ndefault constructor\n(ii)  TEST a(10,12);\nparameterized constructor\nparameterized constructor\n(iii) TEST b = a;\ncopy constructor\nparameterized constructor\n(iv)  TEST c(a);\ncopy constructor\nparameterized constructor\n(v)   TEST d; d = a;\ndefault constructor\ncopy assignment\n(vi)  TEST e = 10;\none-argument (converting) constructor</pre><p>Reasons: (i) object created with no arguments, so the default constructor runs. (ii) two arguments select the parameterized constructor. (iii) and (iv) a new object is initialised from an existing one, so the copy constructor runs (initialisation, not assignment). (v) TEST d; runs the default constructor, and d = a; is an assignment to an existing object, so the copy-assignment operator (not a constructor) runs. (vi) 10 is converted to TEST by the one-argument constructor (it is not explicit). In the program the extra constructor lines in blocks (iii)-(v) belong to the helper object a.</p><p>(b) The scope resolution operator :: is used (1) to access a global variable hidden by a local one (::count); (2) to define a member function outside its class (void Car::start() {}); (3) to access a static member (Car::total);</p><p>(4) to call a base-class member hidden or made ambiguous in a derived class (Base::show()); (5) to reach a member of a namespace or the standard library (std::cout); (6) to name a nested class (Outer::Inner).</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nint value = 100;                           // global\nclass Car {\n    static int total;\npublic:\n    void start();\n    static int count() { return total; }\n};\nint Car::total = 5;                        // static member defined with ::\nvoid Car::start() { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;car started\\n&quot;; }   // function defined outside the class\nint main() {\n    int value = 7;                         // local hides the global\n    cout &lt;&lt; value &lt;&lt; &#x27; &#x27; &lt;&lt; ::value &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n    Car c;\n    c.start();\n    cout &lt;&lt; Car::count() &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">7 100</p><p class=\"ln i0\">car started</p><p class=\"ln i0\">5</p>","exam":"mid","year":2022,"branch":"CO","src":"CO Midsem 2022.pdf (CO-203; Sept 2022; +dup. copies"},{"p":18,"q":"4","marks":"4","unit":1,"topic":"Complex sum with friends; inline and destructors","qh":"<p>4. (a) Write a program to find the sum of two complex numbers using (i) friend function (ii) member function (iii) friend class. [2] (b) Explain the following with suitable examples (i) inline function (ii) destructors [2]</p>","sh":"<p>(a) One Complex class with all three versions: addFriend is a friend function, add is a member function, and Adder is a friend class whose member functions may read the private parts.</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass Complex {\n    double re, im;\npublic:\n    Complex(double r = 0, double i = 0) : re(r), im(i) {}\n    Complex add(const Complex&amp; o) const { return Complex(re + o.re, im + o.im); }   // (ii) member function\n    friend Complex addFriend(const Complex&amp;, const Complex&amp;);                        // (i) friend function\n    friend class Adder;                                                              // (iii) friend class\n    void show() const { cout &lt;&lt; re &lt;&lt; &quot; + &quot; &lt;&lt; im &lt;&lt; &quot;i\\n&quot;; }\n};\nComplex addFriend(const Complex&amp; a, const Complex&amp; b) { return Complex(a.re + b.re, a.im + b.im); }\nclass Adder {\npublic:\n    Complex sum(const Complex&amp; a, const Complex&amp; b) { return Complex(a.re + b.re, a.im + b.im); }\n};\nint main() {\n    Complex a(3, 4), b(1, 2);\n    cout &lt;&lt; &quot;friend function: &quot;; addFriend(a, b).show();\n    cout &lt;&lt; &quot;member function: &quot;; a.add(b).show();\n    Adder ad;\n    cout &lt;&lt; &quot;friend class:    &quot;; ad.sum(a, b).show();\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><pre>friend function: 4 + 6i\nmember function: 4 + 6i\nfriend class:    4 + 6i</pre><p>(b) (i) An inline function asks the compiler to replace the call by the function body, avoiding call overhead; suitable for small, frequently called functions. It is only a request, so loops, recursion or large bodies may not be inlined. Member functions defined inside the class are inline by default. (ii) A destructor ~ClassName() has no arguments or return type, is called automatically when an object goes out of scope or is deleted, and releases resources (memory, files). Objects are destroyed in the reverse order of construction.</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\ninline int square(int x) { return x * x; }\nclass Res {\n    int id;\npublic:\n    Res(int i) : id(i) { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;acquire &quot; &lt;&lt; id &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;; }\n    ~Res() { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;release &quot; &lt;&lt; id &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;; }\n};\nint main() {\n    cout &lt;&lt; square(7) &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n    Res a(1), b(2);\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">49</p><p class=\"ln i0\">acquire 1</p><p class=\"ln i0\">acquire 2</p><p class=\"ln i0\">release 2</p><p class=\"ln i0\">release 1</p>","exam":"mid","year":2022,"branch":"CO","src":"CO Midsem 2022.pdf (CO-203; Sept 2022; +dup. copies"},{"p":18,"q":"5","marks":"4","unit":0,"topic":"Instance counting; class versus structure","qh":"<p>5. (a) Write a program by creating a class which contains a method called getNumberOfInstances. This method should return the number of instances of the class. [2] (c) Differentiate between class and structure in c and c++ [2]</p>","sh":"<p>(The paper has parts (a) and (c) only.) (a) A static data member counts constructions (and copies); a static member function returns it.</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass Widget {\n    static int instances;\npublic:\n    Widget() { ++instances; }\n    Widget(const Widget&amp;) { ++instances; }\n    ~Widget() { --instances; }\n    static int getNumberOfInstances() { return instances; }\n};\nint Widget::instances = 0;\nint main() {\n    Widget a, b;\n    cout &lt;&lt; Widget::getNumberOfInstances() &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n    {\n        Widget c(a);\n        cout &lt;&lt; Widget::getNumberOfInstances() &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n    }\n    cout &lt;&lt; Widget::getNumberOfInstances() &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">2</p><p class=\"ln i0\">3</p><p class=\"ln i0\">2</p><p>(c)</p><pre>Aspect                C structure                      C++ class (and C++ struct)\nMembers               data only                        data and member functions\nDefault access        public (no access control)       class: private; C++ struct: public\nFunctions inside      not allowed (function pointers   allowed\n                      only)\nConstructors/dtor     none                             yes\nInheritance           no                               yes (single, multiple, ...)\nPolymorphism          no                               virtual functions, overloading\nDeclaration           struct S s; (keyword needed)     S s; (keyword not needed)</pre>","exam":"mid","year":2022,"branch":"CO","src":"CO Midsem 2022.pdf (CO-203; Sept 2022; +dup. copies"},{"p":19,"q":"1","marks":"2","unit":0,"topic":"Dynamic initialization","qh":"<p>Q.1 What do you mean by dynamic initialization of a variable? Give an example. [2][CO1]</p>","sh":"<p>Dynamic initialization means initialising a variable at run time with a value computed (or read) during execution rather than a compile-time constant. C++ allows it for ordinary variables, objects (constructor arguments given at run time) and new.</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nint main() {\n    int a = 6, b = 7;\n    int product = a * b;          // initialised with an expression evaluated at run time\n    float ratio = (float)b / a;\n    cout &lt;&lt; product &lt;&lt; &#x27; &#x27; &lt;&lt; ratio &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">42 1.16667</p>","exam":"mid","year":2022,"branch":"IT","src":"IT MIDSEM 2022.pdf (IT203; Sept-2022; +dup. copies"},{"p":19,"q":"2","marks":"3","unit":0,"topic":"Procedure-oriented versus object-oriented","qh":"<p>Q.2 What is the difference between procedure-oriented programming and object-oriented programming? [3][CO1]</p>","sh":"<pre>Aspect            Procedure-oriented (C)                Object-oriented (C++/Java)\nMain unit         Functions (procedures)                Objects/classes\nApproach          Top-down, algorithm first             Bottom-up, data first\nData              Global, shared, freely accessible     Encapsulated; access via member functions\nSecurity          Little data hiding                    Access specifiers give data hiding\nReuse             Copy/call functions                   Inheritance and polymorphism\nReal-world model  Poor                                  Close (objects = real entities)\nAdding features   May change many functions             Extend with a new class\nExamples          C, Pascal                             C++, Java</pre>","exam":"mid","year":2022,"branch":"IT","src":"IT MIDSEM 2022.pdf (IT203; Sept-2022; +dup. copies"},{"p":19,"q":"3","marks":"4","unit":0,"topic":"Static members counting objects","qh":"<p>Q.3 What is the use of static members in a class? Write a program to maintain the count of objects being created and destroyed for a class named Count with the help of constructors, destructors and static data member. [4][CO2]</p>","sh":"<p>A static data member is shared by all objects (one copy per class, defined outside the class); a static member function can use only static members and is called as Count::show(). They hold data common to the whole class (counters, totals).</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass Count {\n    static int created, destroyed;\npublic:\n    Count() { ++created; cout &lt;&lt; &quot;object created\\n&quot;; }\n    ~Count() { ++destroyed; cout &lt;&lt; &quot;object destroyed\\n&quot;; }\n    static void report() {\n        cout &lt;&lt; &quot;created = &quot; &lt;&lt; created &lt;&lt; &quot;, destroyed = &quot; &lt;&lt; destroyed\n             &lt;&lt; &quot;, alive = &quot; &lt;&lt; created - destroyed &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n    }\n};\nint Count::created = 0;\nint Count::destroyed = 0;\nint main() {\n    Count a;\n    {\n        Count b, c;\n        Count::report();\n    }\n    Count::report();\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">object created</p><p class=\"ln i0\">object created</p><p class=\"ln i0\">object created</p><p class=\"ln i0\">created = 3, destroyed = 0, alive = 3</p><p class=\"ln i0\">object destroyed</p><p class=\"ln i0\">object destroyed</p><p class=\"ln i0\">created = 3, destroyed = 2, alive = 1</p><p class=\"ln i0\">object destroyed</p>","exam":"mid","year":2022,"branch":"IT","src":"IT MIDSEM 2022.pdf (IT203; Sept-2022; +dup. copies"},{"p":19,"q":"4","marks":"3","unit":1,"topic":"Friend functions for operator overloading","qh":"<p>Q.4 What is the advantage/disadvantage of using friend functions for operator overloading? [3][CO3]</p>","sh":"<p>Advantages: (1) A friend can overload an operator whose left operand is not an object of the class: 5 + obj, cout &lt;&lt; obj and cin &gt;&gt; obj cannot be members. (2) It treats both operands symmetrically, so implicit conversions apply to both (2 + c and c + 2). (3) It gives access to private data without extra getters. Disadvantages: (1) It breaks encapsulation (non-member with access to private data). (2) It is not a member, so it is not inherited and is not polymorphic (cannot be virtual). (3) =, (), [], -&gt; cannot be overloaded with friends. (4) Friendship is granted per function, which couples classes tightly and is hard to maintain.</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass Money {\n    int rs;\npublic:\n    Money(int r) : rs(r) {}\n    friend Money operator+(int left, const Money&amp; m) { return Money(left + m.rs); }   // int on the left\n    friend ostream&amp; operator&lt;&lt;(ostream&amp; os, const Money&amp; m) { return os &lt;&lt; &quot;Rs &quot; &lt;&lt; m.rs; }\n};\nint main() {\n    Money m(40);\n    cout &lt;&lt; 10 + m &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Rs 50</p>","exam":"mid","year":2022,"branch":"IT","src":"IT MIDSEM 2022.pdf (IT203; Sept-2022; +dup. copies"},{"p":19,"q":"5","marks":"5","unit":1,"topic":"Overload >= and constructors in Vector","qh":"<p>Q.5 Write a program to overload &gt;= operator in class Vector with three int variables x, y and z. The function compares the sum of squares of variables x, y and z in both the objects and returns true or false. Also achieve constructor overloading while creating objects of the Vector class. [5][CO2]</p>","sh":"<p>operator&gt;= is a member taking the right operand; it compares x²+y²+z² (the squared length) of the two objects. Constructor overloading: a default constructor, a one-argument constructor (x=y=z=v) and a three-argument constructor.</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass Vector {\n    int x, y, z;\npublic:\n    Vector() : x(0), y(0), z(0) {}\n    Vector(int v) : x(v), y(v), z(v) {}\n    Vector(int a, int b, int c) : x(a), y(b), z(c) {}\n    int sumSquares() const { return x * x + y * y + z * z; }\n    bool operator&gt;=(const Vector&amp; o) const { return sumSquares() &gt;= o.sumSquares(); }\n};\nint main() {\n    Vector a;               // default constructor: 0\n    Vector b(2);            // one argument: 12\n    Vector c(1, 2, 3);      // three arguments: 14\n    Vector d(3, 2, 1);      // 14\n    cout &lt;&lt; boolalpha;\n    cout &lt;&lt; &quot;c &gt;= b : &quot; &lt;&lt; (c &gt;= b) &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n    cout &lt;&lt; &quot;b &gt;= c : &quot; &lt;&lt; (b &gt;= c) &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n    cout &lt;&lt; &quot;c &gt;= d : &quot; &lt;&lt; (c &gt;= d) &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n    cout &lt;&lt; &quot;a &gt;= b : &quot; &lt;&lt; (a &gt;= b) &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">c &gt;= b : true</p><p class=\"ln i0\">b &gt;= c : false</p><p class=\"ln i0\">c &gt;= d : true</p><p class=\"ln i0\">a &gt;= b : false</p>","exam":"mid","year":2022,"branch":"IT","src":"IT MIDSEM 2022.pdf (IT203; Sept-2022; +dup. copies"},{"p":19,"q":"6","marks":"3","unit":1,"topic":"Multiple inheritance demonstration","qh":"<p>Q6. Write a code to demonstrate multiple inheritance. Write a main function to demonstrate inheritance. [3][CO3]</p>","sh":"<p>In multiple inheritance a derived class has more than one direct base class: class Teaching : public Person, public Employee. The bases are constructed in the order they are listed.</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n#include &lt;string&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass Person {\nprotected:\n    string name;\npublic:\n    Person(string n) : name(n) { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Person constructed\\n&quot;; }\n};\nclass Employee {\nprotected:\n    double salary;\npublic:\n    Employee(double s) : salary(s) { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Employee constructed\\n&quot;; }\n};\nclass Teacher : public Person, public Employee {\npublic:\n    Teacher(string n, double s) : Person(n), Employee(s) { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Teacher constructed\\n&quot;; }\n    void show() const { cout &lt;&lt; name &lt;&lt; &quot; earns &quot; &lt;&lt; salary &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;; }\n};\nint main() {\n    Teacher t(&quot;Meera&quot;, 52000);\n    t.show();\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Person constructed</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Employee constructed</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Teacher constructed</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Meera earns 52000</p>","exam":"mid","year":2022,"branch":"IT","src":"IT MIDSEM 2022.pdf (IT203; Sept-2022; +dup. copies"},{"p":20,"q":"1","marks":"4","unit":0,"topic":"Distinguish OOP terms","qh":"<p>Q1. Distinguish between the following terms: (a) Objects and Classes (b) Data Abstraction and Data Encapsulation (c) Inheritance and Polymorphism (d) Dynamic Binding and Message Passing (1*4=4) (CO 2)</p>","sh":"<pre>(a) Class                              Object\n    User-defined type; blueprint       Instance of a class; real entity\n    Logical, no memory per class       Occupies memory\n    Declared once: class Car {...};    Many created: Car c1, c2;\n(b) Data abstraction                   Data encapsulation\n    Shows essential features, hides    Binds data and functions into one unit and hides the\n    background details (design level)  data using access specifiers (implementation level)\n    Achieved by abstract classes,      Achieved by classes with private data\n    interfaces\n(c) Inheritance                        Polymorphism\n    A class acquires properties of     One name/interface, many behaviours\n    another (reuse, &quot;is-a&quot;)            Compile time: overloading; run time: virtual functions\n(d) Dynamic binding                    Message passing\n    The function called is decided     An object invokes another object&#x27;s member function by\n    at run time (virtual function)     sending name + arguments: obj.deposit(500)</pre>","exam":"mid","year":2022,"branch":"SE","src":"SE MIDSEM 2022.pdf (SE203; Sept 2022; +dup. copies"},{"p":20,"q":"2","marks":"4","unit":0,"topic":"Election ballot counting","qh":"<p>Q2. An election is contested by five candidates. The candidates are numbered 1 to 5 and the voting is done by marking the candidate number on the ballot paper. Write a program to read the ballots and count the votes cast for each candidate using an array variable count. In case, a number read is outside the range 1 to 5, the ballot should be considered as a &quot;spoilt ballot&quot; and the program should also count the numbers of &quot;spoilt ballots&quot;. (4) (CO 1)</p>","sh":"<p>Assumption: the first number read is the number of ballots, followed by the ballots. count[1..5] holds the votes and spoilt counts invalid ballots.</p><p>Sample input:</p><pre>10\n1 3 3 5 2 3 7 1 5 0\n#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nint main() {\n    int count[6] = {0};          // count[1]..count[5] used\n    int spoilt = 0, n, ballot;\n    cin &gt;&gt; n;\n    for (int i = 0; i &lt; n; ++i) {\n        cin &gt;&gt; ballot;\n        if (ballot &gt;= 1 &amp;&amp; ballot &lt;= 5) ++count[ballot];\n        else ++spoilt;           // outside 1..5\n    }\n    for (int c = 1; c &lt;= 5; ++c)\n        cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Candidate &quot; &lt;&lt; c &lt;&lt; &quot;: &quot; &lt;&lt; count[c] &lt;&lt; &quot; votes\\n&quot;;\n    cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Spoilt ballots: &quot; &lt;&lt; spoilt &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Candidate 1: 2 votes</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Candidate 2: 1 votes</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Candidate 3: 3 votes</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Candidate 4: 0 votes</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Candidate 5: 2 votes</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Spoilt ballots: 2</p>","exam":"mid","year":2022,"branch":"SE","src":"SE MIDSEM 2022.pdf (SE203; Sept 2022; +dup. copies"},{"p":20,"q":"3","marks":"3","unit":0,"topic":"Accessing members in three contexts","qh":"<p>Q3. Describe the mechanism of accessing data members and member functions in the following cases: (a) Inside the main program. (b) Inside a member function of the same class. (c) Inside a member function of another class. (1*3=3) (CO 2)</p>","sh":"<p>(a) In main() (outside the class) only public members can be used, through an object and the dot operator (obj.f()) or a pointer and the arrow (p-&gt;f()); private members are reached only via public member functions. (b) Inside a member function of the same class all members (private, protected, public) are used directly by name, because the function runs on an object (this). (c) In a member function of another class the object of the first class must be available (a parameter, a member or a local object) and only its public members can be used (a.get()), unless the first class declares the other class a friend.</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass A {\n    int secret = 5;                         // private\npublic:\n    int open = 9;                           // public\n    int get() const { return secret; }      // (b) direct access inside own member function\n};\nclass B {\npublic:\n    void use(const A&amp; a) { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;B sees &quot; &lt;&lt; a.open &lt;&lt; &quot; and &quot; &lt;&lt; a.get() &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;; }   // (c) public only\n};\nint main() {\n    A a;\n    cout &lt;&lt; a.open &lt;&lt; &#x27; &#x27; &lt;&lt; a.get() &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;     // (a) object.member, public only\n    // cout &lt;&lt; a.secret;                          // error: private\n    B b;\n    b.use(a);\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">9 5</p><p class=\"ln i0\">B sees 9 and 5</p>","exam":"mid","year":2022,"branch":"SE","src":"SE MIDSEM 2022.pdf (SE203; Sept 2022; +dup. copies"},{"p":20,"q":"4","marks":"4","unit":0,"topic":"True or false statements","qh":"<p>Q4. State whether the following statements are TRUE or FALSE. (a) Wrapping up of data of different types into a single unit is known as encapsulation. (b) In C++, a function contained within a class is called a member function. (c) When arguments are passed by value, the function works with the original arguments in the calling program. (d) Friend functions have access to only public members of a class. (e) Member functions defined inside a class specifier become inline functions by default. (f) Constructors, like other member functions, can be declared anywhere in the class. (g) Destructors never take any argument. (h) Operator overloading works when applied to class objects only. (0.5 * 8 = 4) (CO 2 &amp; 3)</p>","sh":"<pre>(a) FALSE  Encapsulation wraps data AND the functions that operate on it into one unit (a class).\n           Grouping data of different types alone is what a structure does.\n(b) TRUE   A function declared inside a class is a member function.\n(c) FALSE  Pass by value gives the function a COPY; the original is unchanged.\n(d) FALSE  A friend can access private and protected members as well as public ones.\n(e) TRUE   Member functions defined inside the class body are implicitly inline.\n(f) TRUE   A constructor may be declared in any section (public, protected or private);\n           usually it is public, a private constructor is used e.g. for singletons.\n(g) TRUE   A destructor has no parameters (and no return type) and cannot be overloaded.\n(h) TRUE   At least one operand must be of a user-defined (class/enum) type; the meaning of\n           operators on built-in types cannot be changed.</pre>","exam":"mid","year":2022,"branch":"SE","src":"SE MIDSEM 2022.pdf (SE203; Sept 2022; +dup. copies"},{"p":20,"q":"5","marks":"5","unit":0,"topic":"Shopping list with arrays","qh":"<p>Q5. Let us consider a shopping list of items for which we place an order with a dealer every month. The list includes details such as the code number and price of each item. We would like to perform operations such as adding an item to the list, deleting an item from the list and printing the total value of the order. WAP to show how these operations are implemented using a class with arrays as data members. (5) (CO 1 &amp; 3)</p>","sh":"<p>Class ShoppingList keeps parallel arrays code[] and price[] and the item count. add appends, remove finds the code and shifts later items left, total sums the prices.</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass ShoppingList {\n    static const int MAX = 50;\n    int code[MAX];\n    float price[MAX];\n    int count;\npublic:\n    ShoppingList() : count(0) {}\n    void add(int c, float p) {\n        if (count == MAX) { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;List full\\n&quot;; return; }\n        code[count] = c; price[count] = p; ++count;\n    }\n    void remove(int c) {\n        for (int i = 0; i &lt; count; ++i)\n            if (code[i] == c) {\n                for (int j = i; j &lt; count - 1; ++j) { code[j] = code[j + 1]; price[j] = price[j + 1]; }\n                --count;\n                return;\n            }\n        cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Item &quot; &lt;&lt; c &lt;&lt; &quot; not found\\n&quot;;\n    }\n    float total() const {\n        float t = 0;\n        for (int i = 0; i &lt; count; ++i) t += price[i];\n        return t;\n    }\n    void display() const {\n        for (int i = 0; i &lt; count; ++i) cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Item &quot; &lt;&lt; code[i] &lt;&lt; &quot;  Rs &quot; &lt;&lt; price[i] &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n    }\n};\nint main() {\n    ShoppingList s;\n    s.add(101, 250.5f);\n    s.add(102, 99.5f);\n    s.add(103, 450);\n    s.display();\n    cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Total = &quot; &lt;&lt; s.total() &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n    s.remove(102);\n    s.remove(999);\n    s.display();\n    cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Total = &quot; &lt;&lt; s.total() &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Item 101  Rs 250.5</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Item 102  Rs 99.5</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Item 103  Rs 450</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Total = 800</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Item 999 not found</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Item 101  Rs 250.5</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Item 103  Rs 450</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Total = 700.5</p>","exam":"mid","year":2022,"branch":"SE","src":"SE MIDSEM 2022.pdf (SE203; Sept 2022; +dup. copies"},{"p":21,"q":"1","marks":"10","unit":1,"topic":"Five short questions","qh":"<p>Q.1 Answer all the following questions: [10] a) What is an abstract class? b) Explain inline functions. c) Define deconstructor and give example. d) Explain the two types of exceptions. f) What are the two ways of detecting end of file?</p>","sh":"<p>(The paper labels its fifth part &quot;f)&quot;.)</p><p>a) An abstract class has at least one pure virtual function (virtual void f() = 0;). It cannot be instantiated and acts as an interface/base class; derived classes must override the pure virtual functions to become concrete.</p><p>b) An inline function (inline int sq(int x) { return x * x; }) is a request to the compiler to substitute the function body at each call, removing call overhead. Good for small, frequently called functions; the compiler may ignore the request for loops/recursion; member functions defined in the class body are inline by default.</p><p>c) The paper&#x27;s &quot;deconstructor&quot; is the destructor: ~ClassName(), no arguments, no return type, called automatically when an object is destroyed to release resources.</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass File {\npublic:\n    File() { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;file opened\\n&quot;; }\n    ~File() { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;file closed\\n&quot;; }     // destructor\n};\nint main() { File f; cout &lt;&lt; &quot;working\\n&quot;; }</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">file opened</p><p class=\"ln i0\">working</p><p class=\"ln i0\">file closed</p><p>d) Synchronous exceptions arise from the program&#x27;s own operations at a known point (division by zero, array index out of range, bad allocation) and can be thrown and caught with try/throw/catch. Asynchronous exceptions are caused by events outside the program&#x27;s control (keyboard interrupt, hardware failure, power loss) and are not handled by C++ exception handling.</p><p>f) (1) eof() member function of the stream returns true when the end of the file has been reached (while (!in.eof())); (2) testing the result of the read operation itself (while (in &gt;&gt; x) or while (getline(in, s))), which becomes false at end of file (the stream returns 0/false).</p>","exam":"supp","year":2020,"branch":"CO","src":"CO Supp 2K20.pdf (CO203, Feb-2020"},{"p":21,"q":"2","marks":"8","unit":1,"topic":"Nested class; objects in files","qh":"<p>Q.2 a) What is nested class? Explain its features with help of an example. [4] b) Illustrate how class objects can be written and read from the files. [4]</p>","sh":"<p>a) A nested class is a class defined inside another class. Features: it is in the scope of the enclosing class and named Outer::Inner; it is used to group helper types, hide them (declare in private:), and avoid name clashes; the enclosing class has no special access to the nested class&#x27;s private members and vice versa (the nested class can use the enclosing class&#x27;s static members and type names); access specifiers of the outer class control who may use the nested class.</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass LinkedList {\n    class Node {                                   // private nested class: hidden helper\n    public:\n        int data;\n        Node* next;\n        Node(int d) : data(d), next(nullptr) {}\n    };\n    Node* head = nullptr;\npublic:\n    void push(int d) { Node* n = new Node(d); n-&gt;next = head; head = n; }\n    void print() const { for (Node* p = head; p; p = p-&gt;next) cout &lt;&lt; p-&gt;data &lt;&lt; &#x27; &#x27;; cout &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;; }\n    ~LinkedList() { while (head) { Node* t = head; head = head-&gt;next; delete t; } }\n};\nint main() {\n    LinkedList l;\n    l.push(1); l.push(2); l.push(3);\n    l.print();\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">3 2 1</p><p>b) Objects are written to a file as raw bytes with write((char*)&amp;obj, sizeof(obj)) and read back with read((char*)&amp;obj, sizeof(obj)), using a binary file (ios::binary). This is safe for classes with plain data members (no pointers or std::string).</p><pre>#include &lt;fstream&gt;\n#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass Student {\n    int roll;\n    char name[20];\n    float marks;\npublic:\n    Student(int r = 0, const char* n = &quot;&quot;, float m = 0) : roll(r), marks(m) {\n        int i = 0;\n        for (; n[i] &amp;&amp; i &lt; 19; ++i) name[i] = n[i];\n        name[i] = &#x27;\\0&#x27;;\n    }\n    void show() const { cout &lt;&lt; roll &lt;&lt; &#x27; &#x27; &lt;&lt; name &lt;&lt; &#x27; &#x27; &lt;&lt; marks &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;; }\n};\nint main() {\n    {\n        ofstream out(&quot;student.dat&quot;, ios::binary);\n        Student s1(1, &quot;Asha&quot;, 88.5f), s2(2, &quot;Ravi&quot;, 74.0f);\n        out.write((char*)&amp;s1, sizeof(s1));            // write objects\n        out.write((char*)&amp;s2, sizeof(s2));\n    }\n    ifstream in(&quot;student.dat&quot;, ios::binary);\n    Student s;\n    while (in.read((char*)&amp;s, sizeof(s))) s.show();   // read objects back\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">1 Asha 88.5</p><p class=\"ln i0\">2 Ravi 74</p>","exam":"supp","year":2020,"branch":"CO","src":"CO Supp 2K20.pdf (CO203, Feb-2020"},{"p":21,"q":"3","marks":"8","unit":4,"topic":"File open/close/write; friend and data hiding","qh":"<p>Q.3 a) Explain with an example to open, close, and write data to the file. [4] b) What is a friend function? Are they a threat to the data hiding? [4]</p>","sh":"<p>a) Create an ofstream (output file stream) object, open the file (in the constructor or with open(&quot;name&quot;, mode)), check that it opened, write with &lt;&lt; (or write), then close(). Modes: ios::out (default, truncates), ios::app</p><pre>(append), ios::in, ios::binary.\n#include &lt;fstream&gt;\n#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n#include &lt;string&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nint main() {\n    ofstream fout;\n    fout.open(&quot;notes.txt&quot;);                         // open (creates/truncates)\n    if (!fout) { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;cannot open file\\n&quot;; return 1; }\n    fout &lt;&lt; &quot;Object Oriented Programming\\n&quot;;        // write\n    fout &lt;&lt; 2020 &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n    fout.close();                                   // close\n    ifstream fin(&quot;notes.txt&quot;);                      // read back to verify\n    string line;\n    while (getline(fin, line)) cout &lt;&lt; line &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Object Oriented Programming</p><p class=\"ln i0\">2020</p><p>b) A friend function is a non-member function declared with friend inside a class; it can access the class&#x27;s private and protected members. It breaks the strict form of encapsulation, but only in a controlled way: the class itself decides who its friends are (a friend cannot be forced on a class), friendship is not inherited and not mutual. Used carefully (for operators like &lt;&lt;, or for tightly related classes) it does not harm data hiding, while overuse does. So it is a controlled exception to data hiding, a threat only when misused.</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass Safe {\n    int code = 1234;\n    friend void audit(const Safe&amp;);          // the class chooses its friend\n};\nvoid audit(const Safe&amp; s) { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;audit sees &quot; &lt;&lt; s.code &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;; }\nint main() { Safe s; audit(s); }</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">audit sees 1234</p>","exam":"supp","year":2020,"branch":"CO","src":"CO Supp 2K20.pdf (CO203, Feb-2020"},{"p":21,"q":"4","marks":"8","unit":1,"topic":"Returning objects; STL components","qh":"<p>Q.4. a) Illustrate how an object can be created (within a function) and return to another function. [4] b) Explain different components of standard template library. [4]</p>","sh":"<p>a) A function can create a local object and return it by value (the copy/move is made for the caller; modern compilers elide it), or create it with new and return its pointer (the caller must delete). Never return a reference/pointer to a local object (it dies when the function ends).</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass Point {\n    int x, y;\npublic:\n    Point(int a, int b) : x(a), y(b) {}\n    void show() const { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;(&quot; &lt;&lt; x &lt;&lt; &quot;, &quot; &lt;&lt; y &lt;&lt; &quot;)\\n&quot;; }\n};\nPoint makePoint() {                        // object created inside this function ...\n    Point p(3, 4);\n    return p;                              // ... and returned by value\n}\nPoint* makeHeapPoint() { return new Point(5, 6); }   // returned by pointer\nvoid useIt() {                             // another function receives it\n    Point a = makePoint();\n    a.show();\n    Point* b = makeHeapPoint();\n    b-&gt;show();\n    delete b;\n}\nint main() { useIt(); }</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">(3, 4)</p><p class=\"ln i0\">(5, 6)</p><p>b) STL components: (1) Containers store data: sequence (vector, list, deque), associative (set, map, multiset, multimap), adaptors (stack, queue, priority_queue); (2) Algorithms are generic functions on ranges (sort, find, count, reverse, accumulate); (3) Iterators are generalised pointers linking containers and algorithms (input, output, forward, bidirectional, random-access); (4) Function objects (functors) and (5) Allocators (memory management).</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Example: sort(v.begin(), v.end());.</p>","exam":"supp","year":2020,"branch":"CO","src":"CO Supp 2K20.pdf (CO203, Feb-2020"},{"p":21,"q":"5","marks":"8","unit":0,"topic":"Dynamic initialization; operator overloading and polymorphism","qh":"<p>Q.5 a) What do you mean by dynamic initialization of class. Why do we need this? [4] b) How does operator overloading support the concept of polymorphism. [4]</p>","sh":"<p>a) Dynamic initialization of objects means the initial values given to an object&#x27;s constructor are only known at run time (read from the user, computed, read from a file), so the object is initialised during execution, using any overloaded constructor that matches. It is needed when initial data is not fixed at compile time (input-dependent objects) and to choose between constructors at run time.</p><p>Sample input:</p><pre>5000 3 4.5\n#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass Deposit {\n    long principal; int years; float rate; double amount;\npublic:\n    Deposit(long p, int y, float r) : principal(p), years(y), rate(r) {   // values known only at run time\n        amount = p;\n        for (int i = 0; i &lt; y; ++i) amount *= (1 + r / 100);\n    }\n    void show() const { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Amount after &quot; &lt;&lt; years &lt;&lt; &quot; years = &quot; &lt;&lt; amount &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;; }\n};\nint main() {\n    long p; int y; float r;\n    cin &gt;&gt; p &gt;&gt; y &gt;&gt; r;\n    Deposit d(p, y, r);          // dynamic initialization\n    d.show();\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Amount after 3 years = 5705.83</p><p>b) Polymorphism = one name, many forms. Operator overloading is compile-time polymorphism: the same operator symbol (+) performs different operations depending on the operand types (int + int adds, string + string joins, Complex + Complex adds parts) without any change to the operator&#x27;s syntax. It makes user-defined types behave like built-in ones.</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n#include &lt;string&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass Vec {\n    int x, y;\npublic:\n    Vec(int a, int b) : x(a), y(b) {}\n    Vec operator+(const Vec&amp; o) const { return Vec(x + o.x, y + o.y); }\n    void show() const { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;(&quot; &lt;&lt; x &lt;&lt; &quot;,&quot; &lt;&lt; y &lt;&lt; &quot;)\\n&quot;; }\n};\nint main() {\n    cout &lt;&lt; 2 + 3 &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;                        // int +\n    cout &lt;&lt; string(&quot;ab&quot;) + string(&quot;cd&quot;) &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;  // string +\n    (Vec(1, 2) + Vec(3, 4)).show();               // user-defined +\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">5</p><p class=\"ln i0\">abcd</p><p class=\"ln i0\">(4,6)</p>","exam":"supp","year":2020,"branch":"CO","src":"CO Supp 2K20.pdf (CO203, Feb-2020"},{"p":21,"q":"6","marks":"8","unit":1,"topic":"Inheritance visibility; virtual versus pure virtual","qh":"<p>Q.6 a) Differentiate between public, private, and protected inheritance with suitable example. [4] b) Differentiate between virtual and pure virtual function? [4]</p>","sh":"<p>a)</p><pre>Base member      public inheritance   protected inheritance   private inheritance\npublic           public               protected               private\nprotected        protected            protected               private\nprivate          not accessible       not accessible          not accessible</pre><p>Public inheritance models &quot;is-a&quot; (the interface of the base stays visible to everyone); protected makes the base&#x27;s public members visible only inside the derived class and its descendants; private makes them usable only inside the derived class (&quot;implemented-in-terms-of&quot;).</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass Base {\npublic:    int pub = 1;\nprotected: int pro = 2;\n};\nclass Pub : public Base    { public: int f() { return pub + pro; } };   // pub, pro stay public/protected\nclass Pro : protected Base { public: int f() { return pub + pro; } };   // both become protected\nclass Pri : private Base   { public: int f() { return pub + pro; } };   // both become private\nint main() {\n    Pub a; Pro b; Pri c;\n    cout &lt;&lt; a.pub &lt;&lt; &#x27; &#x27; &lt;&lt; a.f() &lt;&lt; &#x27; &#x27; &lt;&lt; b.f() &lt;&lt; &#x27; &#x27; &lt;&lt; c.f() &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n    // b.pub, c.pub: error, not accessible from outside\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">1 3 3 3</p><p>b)</p><pre>Virtual function                          Pure virtual function\nvirtual void f() { ... }                  virtual void f() = 0;\nHas a body in the base class              No body required in the base\nDerived class may override                Derived class must override (else abstract)\nClass can be instantiated                 Class becomes abstract</pre>","exam":"supp","year":2020,"branch":"CO","src":"CO Supp 2K20.pdf (CO203, Feb-2020"},{"p":21,"q":"7","marks":"8","unit":1,"topic":"Templates; throwing exceptions from a class","qh":"<p>Q.7 a) What do we need templates. How does it benefit programmers? Explain with example. [4] b) Write a program that throws atleast two exception from a class. [4]</p>","sh":"<p>a) Templates are needed to write one generic function/class for many types instead of duplicating code for int, float, double... Benefits: code reuse, type safety (checked at compile time, no casts), less code to maintain, efficient (resolved at compile time), basis of the STL.</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\ntemplate &lt;class T&gt; void swapValues(T&amp; a, T&amp; b) { T t = a; a = b; b = t; }\nint main() {\n    int a = 1, b = 2;  double x = 1.5, y = 2.5;\n    swapValues(a, b); swapValues(x, y);\n    cout &lt;&lt; a &lt;&lt; &#x27; &#x27; &lt;&lt; b &lt;&lt; &#x27; &#x27; &lt;&lt; x &lt;&lt; &#x27; &#x27; &lt;&lt; y &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">2 1 2.5 1.5</p><p>b) The class Stack throws a different exception for overflow and underflow (two kinds), which main catches separately.</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass Stack {\n    int a[3];\n    int top = -1;\npublic:\n    class Overflow  { };                              // exception classes\n    class Underflow { };\n    void push(int v) { if (top == 2) throw Overflow(); a[++top] = v; }\n    int pop() { if (top &lt; 0) throw Underflow(); return a[top--]; }\n};\nint main() {\n    Stack s;\n    try {\n        for (int i = 1; i &lt;= 4; ++i) { s.push(i * 10); cout &lt;&lt; &quot;pushed &quot; &lt;&lt; i * 10 &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;; }\n    } catch (Stack::Overflow&amp;) { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Overflow exception caught\\n&quot;; }\n    try {\n        for (int i = 0; i &lt; 4; ++i) cout &lt;&lt; &quot;popped &quot; &lt;&lt; s.pop() &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n    } catch (Stack::Underflow&amp;) { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Underflow exception caught\\n&quot;; }\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">pushed 10</p><p class=\"ln i0\">pushed 20</p><p class=\"ln i0\">pushed 30</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Overflow exception caught</p><p class=\"ln i0\">popped 30</p><p class=\"ln i0\">popped 20</p><p class=\"ln i0\">popped 10</p><p class=\"ln i0\">popped Underflow exception caught</p>","exam":"supp","year":2020,"branch":"CO","src":"CO Supp 2K20.pdf (CO203, Feb-2020"},{"p":22,"q":"1","marks":"12","unit":1,"topic":"Six short questions (2 each)","qh":"<p>Q1. Answer the following 2x6: a) Write a program to concatenate two strings using operator overloading b) Differentiate between Java application vs applet c) Differentiate between inheritance and containership d) Differentiate between Class and union e) What is dynamic and static binding in C++? f) What is the role of :: in inheritance?</p>","sh":"<p>a) operator+ creates a new string holding both texts.</p><pre>#include &lt;cstring&gt;\n#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass MyString {\n    char* s;\npublic:\n    MyString(const char* t = &quot;&quot;) { s = new char[strlen(t) + 1]; strcpy(s, t); }\n    MyString(const MyString&amp; o) { s = new char[strlen(o.s) + 1]; strcpy(s, o.s); }\n    ~MyString() { delete[] s; }\n    MyString operator+(const MyString&amp; o) const {\n        char* t = new char[strlen(s) + strlen(o.s) + 1];\n        strcpy(t, s); strcat(t, o.s);\n        MyString r(t);\n        delete[] t;\n        return r;\n    }\n    void show() const { cout &lt;&lt; s &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;; }\n};\nint main() {\n    MyString a(&quot;Hello, &quot;), b(&quot;World&quot;);\n    MyString c = a + b;\n    c.show();\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Hello, World</p><p>b)</p><pre>Java application                           Java applet\nStandalone program, run by java            Embedded in a web page, run by browser/appletviewer\nEntry point is main()                      No main(); life cycle init, start, paint, stop, destroy\nFull access to files/system                Restricted by the sandbox security manager\nExtends any class                          Extends Applet / JApplet</pre><p>c) Inheritance (&quot;is-a&quot;): a derived class acquires the members of a base class (class Car : public Vehicle). Containership/composition (&quot;has-a&quot;): a class has an object of another class as a member (class Car { Engine e; }) and uses it through its interface; it does not become a kind of that class and keeps coupling lower.</p><p>d) A class holds data members and member functions with access control and supports inheritance; every member has its own memory. A union keeps all its data members at the same memory location (size of the largest member), so only one member holds a valid value at a time; in C++ a union may have functions but cannot inherit or have virtual functions or base classes. Default access: class private, union public.</p><p>e) Static (early) binding: the function call is resolved at compile time (normal/overloaded functions). Dynamic (late) binding: resolved at run time through virtual functions and the object&#x27;s vtable, based on the actual type of the object pointed to by a base pointer.</p><p>f) The scope resolution operator :: names a base-class member explicitly when a derived class hides it or when several bases provide the same name (Base::show()), and it defines member functions outside the class.</p>","exam":"supp","year":2020,"branch":"IT","src":"IT Suppl(2K20).pdf (IT203; Feb-2020; +dup. copies"},{"p":22,"q":"2","marks":"7","unit":1,"topic":"Operator overloading methods; friend function and class","qh":"<p>Q2. a) Explain the methods to overload operators. Why is it required? Overload the &lt;&lt; and &gt;&gt; operators. [3] b) What is a friend function? Explain the concept of friend function and friend class in detail with an example. Also discuss inheritance in case of a friend function giving reasons for your answer. [4]</p>","sh":"<p>a) Two methods: as a member function (the left operand is the object: a + b calls a.operator+(b); unary operators take no argument) and as a friend/non-member function (all operands are arguments; required when the left operand is not of the class, e.g. cout &lt;&lt; obj). It is required to give user-defined types natural operator syntax. &lt;&lt; and &gt;&gt; must be non-members (friends) because their left operand is a stream.</p><p>Sample input:</p><pre>3 4\n#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass Point {\n    int x, y;\npublic:\n    Point(int a = 0, int b = 0) : x(a), y(b) {}\n    friend ostream&amp; operator&lt;&lt;(ostream&amp; os, const Point&amp; p) { return os &lt;&lt; &quot;(&quot; &lt;&lt; p.x &lt;&lt; &quot;, &quot; &lt;&lt; p.y &lt;&lt; &quot;)&quot;; }\n    friend istream&amp; operator&gt;&gt;(istream&amp; is, Point&amp; p) { return is &gt;&gt; p.x &gt;&gt; p.y; }\n};\nint main() {\n    Point p;\n    cin &gt;&gt; p;\n    cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Point = &quot; &lt;&lt; p &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Point = (3, 4)</p><p>b) A friend function is a non-member function with permission to access the private/protected members of a class; a friend class gives all of its member functions that permission. Friendship is declared by the class that gives access, is not mutual and not transitive. Inheritance: friendship is not inherited. If A declares friend void f(A&amp;), f is not a friend of a class B derived from A; likewise a friend of A cannot access the private members that B adds. (A friend of A may use a derived object as an A through its public/protected inherited part only as far as A allows.) Reason: friendship is a grant of trust by one class and must be explicit, otherwise derived classes would extend the trust without the base&#x27;s consent.</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass A {\n    int secret = 5;\n    friend void show(const A&amp;);          // friend function\n    friend class Spy;                    // friend class\n};\nclass B : public A { int own = 9; friend void tryB(const B&amp;); };\nvoid show(const A&amp; a) { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;friend sees &quot; &lt;&lt; a.secret &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;; }\nclass Spy { public: int get(const A&amp; a) { return a.secret * 2; } };\nvoid tryB(const B&amp; b) { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;B&#x27;s friend sees own = &quot; &lt;&lt; b.own &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;; }\nint main() {\n    A a; B b;\n    show(a);\n    show(b);                  // works: b is used as an A (A&#x27;s friend, A&#x27;s data)\n    cout &lt;&lt; Spy().get(a) &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n    tryB(b);\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">friend sees 5</p><p class=\"ln i0\">friend sees 5</p><p class=\"ln i0\">10</p><p class=\"ln i0\">B&#x27;s friend sees own = 9</p>","exam":"supp","year":2020,"branch":"IT","src":"IT Suppl(2K20).pdf (IT203; Feb-2020; +dup. copies"},{"p":22,"q":"3","marks":"7","unit":2,"topic":"Student class for 10 students; Java applet","qh":"<p>Q3 a) Write a java program to declare a class &#x27;student&#x27;, consisting of data members roll_no and stud_name. Write the member functions accept() to accept and display() to display the data for 10 students. [3] b) What is java applet? Give its properties in detail. Give an example of the same by creating a basic applet which will print the string &quot;Hello User&quot; on the screen. [4]</p>","sh":"<p>a) An array of 10 student objects is filled with accept() and printed with display() (input from Scanner).</p><pre>import java.util.Scanner;\nclass student {\n    int roll_no;\n    String stud_name;\n    void accept(Scanner sc) {\n        roll_no = sc.nextInt();\n        stud_name = sc.next();\n    }\n    void display() {\n        System.out.println(roll_no + &quot;  &quot; + stud_name);\n    }\n}\npublic class StudentDemo {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);\n        student[] s = new student[10];\n        for (int i = 0; i &lt; 10; i++) {\n            s[i] = new student();\n            s[i].accept(sc);                  // reads roll number and name\n        }\n        System.out.println(&quot;Roll  Name&quot;);\n        for (int i = 0; i &lt; 10; i++) s[i].display();\n    }\n}</pre><p>Output (for the input 1 Asha 2 Ravi 3 Neha ... 10 Zoya): the line Roll Name followed by the ten records, one per line, e.g. 1 Asha, 2 Ravi, ..., 10 Zoya.</p><p>b) An applet is a Java program that runs inside a web browser (or appletviewer) and is embedded with the &lt;applet&gt;</p><p>tag. Properties: it extends java.applet.Applet (or JApplet); there is no main(), the life cycle methods init(), start(), paint(Graphics), stop(), destroy() are called by the browser; it runs in a security sandbox (no local file access, no connections other than to its host); it is downloaded and executed on the client; GUI is built with AWT/Swing; parameters come from &lt;param&gt; tags; it is compiled to bytecode like any class.</p><pre>import java.applet.Applet;\nimport java.awt.Graphics;\npublic class HelloUser extends Applet {\n    public void paint(Graphics g) {\n        g.drawString(&quot;Hello User&quot;, 50, 50);\n    }\n}\n/* &lt;applet code=&quot;HelloUser.class&quot; width=&quot;300&quot; height=&quot;150&quot;&gt;&lt;/applet&gt; */</pre><p>Output: the applet window shows the text Hello User.</p>","exam":"supp","year":2020,"branch":"IT","src":"IT Suppl(2K20).pdf (IT203; Feb-2020; +dup. copies"},{"p":22,"q":"4","marks":"7","unit":2,"topic":"Bytecode life stages; virtual base class","qh":"<p>Q4. Answer the following with examples a) Explain the concept of bytecode in detail with life stages of a java code. [3]</p><p>b) Write a program to show the use of virtual base class. [4]</p>","sh":"<p>a) Bytecode is the platform-independent intermediate code (.class) generated by the Java compiler; it is not machine code but an instruction set for the JVM. Life stages: (1) write source Hello.java; (2) compile with javac Hello.java, which checks syntax and produces Hello.class (bytecode); (3) the class loader loads the class; (4) the bytecode verifier checks it for safety; (5) the interpreter executes the bytecode (and the JIT compiler turns hot methods into native machine code); (6) output appears. Because step 5 happens on a JVM that exists for each platform, the same .class runs everywhere.</p><p>b) A virtual base class removes the duplicate base subobject in the diamond.</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass Person { public: int age = 20; Person() { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Person constructed once\\n&quot;; } };\nclass Student : virtual public Person { };\nclass Employee : virtual public Person { };\nclass Intern : public Student, public Employee { };\nint main() {\n    Intern i;\n    cout &lt;&lt; &quot;age = &quot; &lt;&lt; i.age &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;          // unambiguous\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Person constructed once</p><p class=\"ln i0\">age = 20</p>","exam":"supp","year":2020,"branch":"IT","src":"IT Suppl(2K20).pdf (IT203; Feb-2020; +dup. copies"},{"p":22,"q":"5","marks":"7","unit":0,"topic":"Nested class; run-time polymorphism","qh":"<p>Q5. Answer the following with examples. a) What is the role of a nested class? How to use it? [3] b) How does runtime polymorphism occur in C++? [4]</p>","sh":"<p>a) A nested class is defined inside another class to keep a helper type local (it is a part of the outer concept, e.g. Node in a list). It is used by qualifying the name Outer::Inner, if it is public; a private nested class can be used only inside</p><pre>Outer.\n#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass University {\npublic:\n    class Department {                     // nested class\n        string name;\n    public:\n        Department(string n) : name(n) {}\n        void show() const { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Department of &quot; &lt;&lt; name &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;; }\n    };\n};\nint main() {\n    University::Department d(&quot;Computer Engineering&quot;);   // use with Outer::Inner\n    d.show();\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Department of Computer Engineering</p><p>b) A base class declares a function virtual; derived classes override it. Each class with virtual functions has a vtable (table of function pointers) and each object a hidden vptr to its class&#x27;s vtable. A call through a base pointer/reference (p-&gt;f()) is compiled as &quot;look up f in the vtable of the object&#x27;s real class and jump&quot;, so the version of the actual object runs, decided at run time.</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass Bird { public: virtual void fly() { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Bird flies\\n&quot;; } virtual ~Bird() {} };\nclass Penguin : public Bird { public: void fly() override { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Penguin cannot fly\\n&quot;; } };\nclass Eagle : public Bird { public: void fly() override { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Eagle soars\\n&quot;; } };\nint main() {\n    Bird* b[3] = { new Bird, new Penguin, new Eagle };\n    for (Bird* p : b) { p-&gt;fly(); delete p; }\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Bird flies</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Penguin cannot fly</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Eagle soars</p>","exam":"supp","year":2020,"branch":"IT","src":"IT Suppl(2K20).pdf (IT203; Feb-2020; +dup. copies"},{"p":22,"q":"6","marks":"7","unit":1,"topic":"this keyword; C++ exception handling","qh":"<p>Q6 a) Write a program to show the role of this keyword in C++. Explain. [3] b) Explain exception handling in C++. How is an exception rethrown? Also explain the concept of multiple catch and catch all. [4]</p>","sh":"<p>a) this is a pointer to the object for which a non-static member function runs. Uses: (1) distinguish a member from a parameter with the same name; (2) return the current object (return *this;) to allow chaining; (3) pass the current object&#x27;s address to another function.</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass Counter {\n    int count;\npublic:\n    Counter(int count) { this-&gt;count = count; }                // (1) member vs parameter\n    Counter&amp; add(int n) { this-&gt;count += n; return *this; }    // (2) chaining\n    void show() const { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;count = &quot; &lt;&lt; count &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;; }\n};\nint main() {\n    Counter c(5);\n    c.add(3).add(2).show();\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">count = 10</p><p>b) Exception handling separates error handling from normal code: try { ... } guards code, throw raises an exception of any type, catch handlers deal with it. Multiple catch: several catch blocks after one try, tested in order, the first with a matching type runs. Catch-all: catch (...) matches any type (put it last). Rethrow: throw; inside a handler passes the current exception to the next outer handler.</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nvoid check(int code) {\n    try {\n        if (code == 1) throw 7;\n        if (code == 2) throw &#x27;x&#x27;;\n        if (code == 3) throw 2.5;\n    } catch (int e) {\n        cout &lt;&lt; &quot;int &quot; &lt;&lt; e &lt;&lt; &quot; caught, rethrowing\\n&quot;;\n        throw;                                  // rethrow\n    }\n}\nint main() {\n    for (int c = 1; c &lt;= 3; ++c) {\n        try { check(c); }\n        catch (int e)  { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;main: int &quot; &lt;&lt; e &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;; }\n        catch (char e) { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;main: char &quot; &lt;&lt; e &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;; }\n        catch (...)    { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;main: catch-all (other type)\\n&quot;; }\n    }\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">int 7 caught, rethrowing</p><p class=\"ln i0\">main: int 7</p><p class=\"ln i0\">main: char x</p><p class=\"ln i0\">main: catch-all (other type)</p>","exam":"supp","year":2020,"branch":"IT","src":"IT Suppl(2K20).pdf (IT203; Feb-2020; +dup. copies"},{"p":22,"q":"7","marks":"7","unit":1,"topic":"Templates; initialiser list in multilevel inheritance","qh":"<p>Q7 a) Explain templates and its types with detailed example. [3] b) What is initialiser list and discuss its role in constructor calls in multilevel inheritance in C++ with examples. [4]</p>","sh":"<p>a) Templates give generic code. Types: function templates (template &lt;class T&gt; T maxOf(T a, T b)) and class templates (template &lt;class T&gt; class Stack). The compiler generates the code for each type used.</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\ntemplate &lt;class T&gt; T maxOf(T a, T b) { return a &gt; b ? a : b; }\ntemplate &lt;class T&gt;\nclass Holder {\n    T v;\npublic:\n    Holder(T x) : v(x) {}\n    T twice() const { return v + v; }\n};\nint main() {\n    cout &lt;&lt; maxOf(4, 9) &lt;&lt; &#x27; &#x27; &lt;&lt; maxOf(1.5, 0.5) &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n    Holder&lt;int&gt; a(21); Holder&lt;double&gt; b(1.25);\n    cout &lt;&lt; a.twice() &lt;&lt; &#x27; &#x27; &lt;&lt; b.twice() &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">9 1.5</p><p class=\"ln i0\">42 2.5</p><p>b) The initialiser list follows the constructor&#x27;s parameter list after a colon and initialises base classes and members before the body runs. In multilevel inheritance (A -&gt; B -&gt; C) each class constructor passes arguments only to its</p><p class=\"ln i0\">immediate base through its list, so C(int x,int y,int z) : B(x, y), c(z) and B(int x,int y) : A(x), b(y).</p><p>Constructors run from the top: A, then B, then C; destructors in the reverse order.</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass A { int a; public: A(int x) : a(x) { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;A(&quot; &lt;&lt; a &lt;&lt; &quot;)\\n&quot;; } ~A() { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;~A\\n&quot;; } };\nclass B : public A { int b; public: B(int x, int y) : A(x), b(y) { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;B(&quot; &lt;&lt; b &lt;&lt; &quot;)\\n&quot;; } ~B() { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;~B\\n&quot;; } };\nclass C : public B { int c; public: C(int x, int y, int z) : B(x, y), c(z) { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;C(&quot; &lt;&lt; c &lt;&lt; &quot;)\\n&quot;; } ~C() { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;~C\\n&quot;; } };\nint main() { C obj(1, 2, 3); }</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">A(1)</p><p class=\"ln i0\">B(2)</p><p class=\"ln i0\">C(3)</p><p class=\"ln i0\">~C</p><p class=\"ln i0\">~B</p><p class=\"ln i0\">~A</p>","exam":"supp","year":2020,"branch":"IT","src":"IT Suppl(2K20).pdf (IT203; Feb-2020; +dup. copies"},{"p":23,"q":"1","marks":"12","unit":0,"topic":"Six short questions (2 each)","qh":"<p>Q1. Answer all the following questions: [12] [a] Explain array of pointers with suitable example. [b] Explain extraction and insertion operator. [c] When scope resolution operator is used. [d] What precautions should we take while at the time of function overloading? [e] What do you mean by abstract class? [f] What is type casting and when is it used?</p>","sh":"<p>[a] An array of pointers holds addresses, e.g. int* p[3] (three int pointers) or char* names[3] (array of strings). It saves memory for ragged data and lets many objects be handled through pointers (Shape* s[3] for polymorphism).</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nint main() {\n    int a = 10, b = 20, c = 30;\n    int* p[3] = { &amp;a, &amp;b, &amp;c };              // array of pointers\n    const char* names[3] = { &quot;Asha&quot;, &quot;Ravi&quot;, &quot;Neha&quot; };\n    for (int i = 0; i &lt; 3; ++i) cout &lt;&lt; names[i] &lt;&lt; &quot; -&gt; &quot; &lt;&lt; *p[i] &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Asha → 10</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Ravi → 20</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Neha → 30</p><p>[b] The insertion operator &lt;&lt; sends data to an output stream (cout &lt;&lt; x); the extraction operator &gt;&gt; takes data from an input stream (cin &gt;&gt; x). Both are overloaded in ostream/istream, return the stream reference so they can be chained (cout &lt;&lt; a &lt;&lt; b), and can be overloaded for user types as friend functions: ostream&amp;</p><p class=\"ln i0\">operator&lt;&lt;(ostream&amp;, const T&amp;).</p><p>[c] The scope resolution operator :: is used to (1) access a global variable hidden by a local one, (2) define a member function outside its class, (3) access static members, (4) refer to a base-class member, (5) use names from a namespace (std::cout).</p><p>[d] Precautions: the functions must differ in the number, types or order of parameters (the return type alone is not enough); avoid ambiguity caused by implicit conversions (f(int) and f(double) called with a long) and by default arguments (f(int) vs f(int, int = 0)); do not overload to give very different meanings; const and reference qualifiers also differentiate parameters (f(int&amp;) vs f(const int&amp;)); do not overload on typedef names that are the same type.</p><p>[e] An abstract class has at least one pure virtual function (= 0); it cannot be instantiated and serves as a base that defines an interface; derived classes must implement the pure virtual functions.</p><p>[f] Type casting converts a value from one type to another. Implicit (automatic: int to float in a + 2.5) or explicit ((float)x, static_cast&lt;float&gt;(x)). Used for exact division ((double)a / b), to convert between class types, to call functions with different parameter types, and for pointer conversions in inheritance (dynamic_cast).</p>","exam":"end","year":2019,"branch":"CO","src":"CO Endsem 2019.pdf (CO203; Nov/Dec-2019; +dup. copies"},{"p":23,"q":"2","marks":"7","unit":0,"topic":"OOP and advantages; this pointer","qh":"<p>Q2. [a] What is OOP? State advantages of OOP. [3] [b] Explain this pointer with suitable example. [4]</p>","sh":"<p>[a] Object-oriented programming is a programming paradigm that organises software around objects, which combine data and the functions that operate on it, instead of around functions. Basic concepts: class, object, encapsulation, abstraction, inheritance, polymorphism, dynamic binding, message passing. Advantages: (1) code reuse through inheritance; (2) data hiding gives security; (3) modular, easier to maintain and extend; (4) models real-world entities naturally; (5) polymorphism makes programs flexible; (6) easier to scale large projects and to divide among programmers; (7) fewer errors through encapsulation.</p><p>[b] this is the implicit pointer to the current object inside a non-static member function. It resolves name clashes, returns the current object and is not available in static functions.</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass Box {\n    int length;\npublic:\n    Box(int length) { this-&gt;length = length; }\n    Box&amp; grow(int by) { this-&gt;length += by; return *this; }\n    bool bigger(const Box&amp; o) const { return this-&gt;length &gt; o.length; }\n    void show() const { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;length = &quot; &lt;&lt; length &lt;&lt; &quot; (object at this = same as &amp;object: yes)\\n&quot;; }\n};\nint main() {\n    Box a(5), b(8);\n    a.grow(4).show();\n    cout &lt;&lt; boolalpha &lt;&lt; a.bigger(b) &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">length = 9 (object at this = same as &amp;object: yes)</p><p class=\"ln i0\">true</p>","exam":"end","year":2019,"branch":"CO","src":"CO Endsem 2019.pdf (CO203; Nov/Dec-2019; +dup. copies"},{"p":23,"q":"3","marks":"7","unit":1,"topic":"Class template; operator overloading prime check","qh":"<p>Q3. [a] Explain Class Template with suitable example. How overloading of template functions is resolved? [3] [b] Write a C++ program using operator overloading to check whether given number is prime or not. [4]</p>","sh":"<p>[a] A class template defines a generic class (template &lt;class T&gt; class Stack); a template class is created when it is used with a type (Stack&lt;int&gt;). Overload resolution of template functions: (1) an exact match from a non-template function is preferred; (2) otherwise the compiler considers every template that can be instantiated with exact match and chooses the most specialised one; (3) if arguments need conversions, ordinary overloads are tried with standard conversions; (4) if two candidates are equally good the call is ambiguous (error).</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\ntemplate &lt;class T&gt;\nclass Stack {\n    T a[10]; int top = -1;\npublic:\n    void push(T v) { a[++top] = v; }\n    T pop() { return a[top--]; }\n};\ntemplate &lt;class T&gt; T twice(T x) { return x + x; }       // generic\nint twice(int x) { return x * 2 + 1000; }               // ordinary function: preferred for int\nint main() {\n    Stack&lt;char&gt; s; s.push(&#x27;a&#x27;); s.push(&#x27;b&#x27;);\n    cout &lt;&lt; s.pop() &lt;&lt; s.pop() &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n    cout &lt;&lt; twice(5) &lt;&lt; &#x27; &#x27; &lt;&lt; twice(2.5) &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">ba</p><p class=\"ln i0\">1010 5</p><p>[b] The unary operator! is overloaded to mean &quot;is prime&quot; (any suitable operator works, ! returns true when the number is prime).</p><p>Sample input:</p><pre>29\n#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass Number {\n    int n;\npublic:\n    Number(int x = 0) : n(x) {}\n    bool operator!() const {                 // overloaded: true if prime\n        if (n &lt; 2) return false;\n        for (int i = 2; i * i &lt;= n; ++i)\n            if (n % i == 0) return false;\n        return true;\n    }\n    void read() { cin &gt;&gt; n; }\n    int get() const { return n; }\n};\nint main() {\n    Number x;\n    x.read();\n    cout &lt;&lt; x.get() &lt;&lt; (!x ? &quot; is prime\\n&quot; : &quot; is not prime\\n&quot;);\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">29 is prime</p>","exam":"end","year":2019,"branch":"CO","src":"CO Endsem 2019.pdf (CO203; Nov/Dec-2019; +dup. copies"},{"p":23,"q":"4","marks":"7","unit":4,"topic":"Copy constructor; count vowels in a file","qh":"<p>Q4. [a] What is Copy Constructor? Describe merits and demerits of it. [3] [b] Write a C++ program to read a file and count the no. of vowels and consonants. [4]</p>","sh":"<p>[a] A copy constructor X(const X&amp;) initialises a new object from an existing one; it is called for X b = a;, X b(a);, pass/return by value. Merits: gives correct copies of objects with pointers (deep copy); lets the programmer control copying (resources, counters, logging); needed for safe pass-by-value. Demerits: copying large objects is expensive; if forgotten for classes owning memory, the default shallow copy causes double deletion; extra code to maintain (rule of three/five); passing by value invokes it implicitly and may hurt performance.</p><p>[b] The program writes a small sample file, then reads it character by character and counts vowels and consonants (letters only).</p><pre>#include &lt;cctype&gt;\n#include &lt;fstream&gt;\n#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nint main() {\n    ofstream out(&quot;text.txt&quot;);\n    out &lt;&lt; &quot;Object Oriented Programming in C++\\n&quot;;   // sample data file\n    out.close();\n    ifstream in(&quot;text.txt&quot;);\n    char ch;\n    int vowels = 0, consonants = 0;\n    while (in.get(ch)) {\n        if (isalpha((unsigned char)ch)) {\n            ch = tolower(ch);\n            if (ch == &#x27;a&#x27; || ch == &#x27;e&#x27; || ch == &#x27;i&#x27; || ch == &#x27;o&#x27; || ch == &#x27;u&#x27;) ++vowels;\n            else ++consonants;\n        }\n    }\n    cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Vowels: &quot; &lt;&lt; vowels &lt;&lt; &quot;\\nConsonants: &quot; &lt;&lt; consonants &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Vowels: 10</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Consonants: 18</p>","exam":"end","year":2019,"branch":"CO","src":"CO Endsem 2019.pdf (CO203; Nov/Dec-2019; +dup. copies"},{"p":23,"q":"5","marks":"7","unit":3,"topic":"Catch all; constructor/destructor order","qh":"<p>Q5. [a] Write a program that catches all type of exceptions. [3] [b] Illustrate with C++ program the execution of constructor and destructor when single inheritance is involved. [4]</p>","sh":"<p>[a]</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nint main() {\n    for (int k = 0; k &lt; 3; ++k) {\n        try {\n            if (k == 0) throw 10;\n            if (k == 1) throw &quot;message&quot;;\n            if (k == 2) throw 3.14;\n        } catch (...) {                       // catches every type of exception\n            cout &lt;&lt; &quot;caught an exception (type unknown), k = &quot; &lt;&lt; k &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n        }\n    }\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">caught an exception (type unknown), k = 0</p><p class=\"ln i0\">caught an exception (type unknown), k = 1</p><p class=\"ln i0\">caught an exception (type unknown), k = 2</p><p>[b] In single inheritance the base-class constructor runs first, then the derived-class constructor; destructors run in the opposite order (derived first, then base).</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass Base {\npublic:\n    Base() { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Base constructor\\n&quot;; }\n    ~Base() { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Base destructor\\n&quot;; }\n};\nclass Derived : public Base {\npublic:\n    Derived() { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Derived constructor\\n&quot;; }\n    ~Derived() { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Derived destructor\\n&quot;; }\n};\nint main() {\n    Derived d;\n    cout &lt;&lt; &quot;inside main\\n&quot;;\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Base constructor</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Derived constructor</p><p class=\"ln i0\">inside main</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Derived destructor</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Base destructor</p>","exam":"end","year":2019,"branch":"CO","src":"CO Endsem 2019.pdf (CO203; Nov/Dec-2019; +dup. copies"},{"p":23,"q":"6","marks":"7","unit":1,"topic":"Pure virtual; why virtual functions","qh":"<p>Q6. [a] When do we make a function pure virtual Function? [3] [b] What is virtual function? Why do we need virtual Function? [4]</p>","sh":"<p>[a] We make a function pure virtual when the base class cannot give a meaningful implementation but every derived class must provide one: the base only specifies the interface (virtual double area() const = 0;). Making it pure makes the class abstract, so it cannot be instantiated and derived classes that do not override it are abstract too. [b] A virtual function is a base-class member function declared with virtual that can be redefined in derived classes and is called through a base pointer/reference according to the object&#x27;s actual type (run-time binding). Needed because without virtual a base pointer always calls the base version (compile-time binding), so a collection of different derived objects accessed through Base* could not behave polymorphically.</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass Employee {\npublic:\n    virtual void pay() { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Employee pay\\n&quot;; }\n    void notVirtual() { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Employee::notVirtual\\n&quot;; }\n    virtual ~Employee() {}\n};\nclass Manager : public Employee {\npublic:\n    void pay() override { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Manager pay + bonus\\n&quot;; }\n    void notVirtual() { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Manager::notVirtual\\n&quot;; }\n};\nint main() {\n    Employee* e = new Manager;\n    e-&gt;pay();            // virtual: Manager&#x27;s version\n    e-&gt;notVirtual();     // non-virtual: Employee&#x27;s version\n    delete e;\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><pre>Manager pay + bonus\nEmployee::notVirtual</pre>","exam":"end","year":2019,"branch":"CO","src":"CO Endsem 2019.pdf (CO203; Nov/Dec-2019; +dup. copies"},{"p":23,"q":"7","marks":"7","unit":4,"topic":"Streams; Java features","qh":"<p>Q7. [a] What are streams in C++? Explain C++&#x27;s predefined streams. [3] [b] What are the major features of Java Programming Language? [4]</p>","sh":"<p>[a] A stream is a sequence of bytes flowing between a program and a device (keyboard, screen, file). Input streams supply data to the program, output streams take data from it. The stream classes (istream, ostream, iostream, ifstream, ofstream) are declared in &lt;iostream&gt; and &lt;fstream&gt;. Predefined (standard) streams: cin (standard input, keyboard, an istream), cout (standard output, screen, an ostream), cerr (standard error, unbuffered, for error messages) and clog (standard log, buffered error output).</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nint main() {\n    cout &lt;&lt; &quot;to standard output\\n&quot;;\n    cerr &lt;&lt; &quot;to standard error (unbuffered)\\n&quot;;\n    clog &lt;&lt; &quot;to standard log (buffered)\\n&quot;;\n}</pre><p>Output (all three lines appear on the screen; cerr and clog write to the error stream):</p><p class=\"ln i0\">to standard output</p><p class=\"ln i0\">to standard error (unbuffered)</p><p class=\"ln i0\">to standard log (buffered)</p><p>[b] Java features: simple and familiar syntax; object-oriented; platform independent (bytecode + JVM); secure (no pointers, verifier, sandbox); robust (strong typing, exception handling, garbage collection); multithreaded; architecture neutral and portable (fixed-size primitive types); interpreted with a JIT compiler for high performance; distributed (networking, RMI); dynamic (run-time class loading).</p>","exam":"end","year":2019,"branch":"CO","src":"CO Endsem 2019.pdf (CO203; Nov/Dec-2019; +dup. copies"},{"p":24,"q":"1","marks":"8","unit":2,"topic":"Applets and user-defined exception","qh":"<p>Q1. A) Explain Java applets in detail. Create an applet using any 4 functions from Graphics class. [4] B) What is an exception? Define the user defined exception &#x27;MyException&#x27;. Override the what() function in the class which will print &quot;weight is over 100kgs&quot; for a commodity&#x27;s weight if its value exceeds 100. The value of the commodity will be entered by user. [4]</p>","sh":"<p>A) An applet is a Java class that extends java.applet.Applet/JApplet and runs inside a browser or appletviewer; the browser&#x27;s JVM drives its life cycle: init() (once, initialise), start() (start/resume), paint(Graphics g) (draw), stop() (page hidden), destroy() (cleanup). Applets have no main(), are embedded with &lt;applet code= width= height=&gt;, run in a sandbox (no local file/network access to other hosts), and use the AWT Graphics object for drawing.</p><pre>import java.applet.Applet;\nimport java.awt.Color;\nimport java.awt.Graphics;\npublic class ShapesApplet extends Applet {\n    public void paint(Graphics g) {\n        g.drawString(&quot;Graphics demo&quot;, 20, 20);      // 1: text\n        g.drawLine(20, 30, 220, 30);                // 2: line\n        g.drawRect(20, 50, 100, 60);                // 3: rectangle outline\n        g.setColor(Color.RED);\n        g.fillOval(140, 50, 80, 60);                // 4: filled oval\n    }\n}\n/* &lt;applet code=&quot;ShapesApplet.class&quot; width=&quot;260&quot; height=&quot;140&quot;&gt;&lt;/applet&gt; */</pre><p>Output: the applet window shows the text, a line, a rectangle and a red filled oval.</p><p>B) An exception is a run-time error condition that disrupts normal flow. A user-defined exception is a class created by the programmer; here (C++ what() style) MyException derives from std::exception and overrides what(). Sample input:</p><pre>120\n#include &lt;exception&gt;\n#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass MyException : public exception {\npublic:\n    const char* what() const noexcept override { return &quot;weight is over 100kgs&quot;; }\n};\nint main() {\n    double weight;\n    cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Enter weight of commodity: &quot;;\n    cin &gt;&gt; weight;\n    try {\n        if (weight &gt; 100) throw MyException();\n        cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Weight &quot; &lt;&lt; weight &lt;&lt; &quot; kg accepted\\n&quot;;\n    } catch (const MyException&amp; e) {\n        cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Exception: &quot; &lt;&lt; e.what() &lt;&lt; &quot; (value = &quot; &lt;&lt; weight &lt;&lt; &quot;)\\n&quot;;\n    }\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Enter weight of commodity: Exception: weight is over 100kgs (value = 120)</p><p>For input 80 the program prints Weight 80 kg accepted.</p>","exam":"end","year":2019,"branch":"IT","src":"EndSem Papers.pdf pp.12-14 (IT203, Nov-2019"},{"p":24,"q":"2","marks":"8","unit":3,"topic":"Catch blocks and rethrow; exam interfaces","qh":"<p>Q2. A) What are the different catch blocks? Why and how to rethrow an exception. [4] B) Implement the following scenario using java code: Exam (interface) [parent]; Mid sem exam (interface) [child 1]; End sem exam (interface) [child 2]; IT_BRANCH (class); DTU (class): containing the main() function. Include functions in each class/interface and show the use of each in detail. [4]</p>","sh":"<p>A) Kinds of catch blocks: a catch with a specific type (catch (int e)), a catch by reference to a class (catch (const exception&amp; e) which also catches derived classes), and the catch-all catch (...); several catch blocks can follow one try and the first matching one runs. Rethrow: a handler that cannot finish handling the exception writes throw; to pass the same exception to the outer handler (to log it and let the caller decide, or to translate it).</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nvoid level2() { throw 404; }\nvoid level1() {\n    try { level2(); }\n    catch (int e) { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;level1: logged error &quot; &lt;&lt; e &lt;&lt; &quot;, rethrowing\\n&quot;; throw; }\n}\nint main() {\n    try { level1(); }\n    catch (int e)  { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;main: handled int &quot; &lt;&lt; e &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;; }\n    catch (...)    { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;main: other exception\\n&quot;; }\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">level1: logged error 404, rethrowing</p><p class=\"ln i0\">main: handled int 404</p><p>B) Exam is the parent interface; MidSemExam and EndSemExam extend it; IT_BRANCH implements both child interfaces, so it must implement the inherited Exam method once and the two child methods; DTU holds main().</p><pre>interface Exam {\n    void conduct();                                  // parent method\n}\ninterface MidSemExam extends Exam {\n    void midMarks(int marks);\n}\ninterface EndSemExam extends Exam {\n    void endMarks(int marks);\n}\nclass IT_BRANCH implements MidSemExam, EndSemExam {\n    int mid, end;\n    public void conduct()          { System.out.println(&quot;IT branch conducts the exam&quot;); }\n    public void midMarks(int m)    { mid = m; System.out.println(&quot;Mid sem marks: &quot; + m); }\n    public void endMarks(int m)    { end = m; System.out.println(&quot;End sem marks: &quot; + m); }\n    void total()                   { System.out.println(&quot;Total = &quot; + (mid + end)); }\n}\npublic class DTU {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        IT_BRANCH b = new IT_BRANCH();\n        b.conduct();\n        b.midMarks(18);\n        b.endMarks(35);\n        b.total();\n        Exam e = b;                                  // an interface reference can hold the object\n        e.conduct();\n    }\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">IT branch conducts the exam</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Mid sem marks: 18</p><p class=\"ln i0\">End sem marks: 35</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Total = 53</p><p class=\"ln i0\">IT branch conducts the exam</p>","exam":"end","year":2019,"branch":"IT","src":"EndSem Papers.pdf pp.12-14 (IT203, Nov-2019"},{"p":24,"q":"3","marks":"8","unit":1,"topic":"Four distinctions","qh":"<p>Q3. A) What is the difference between (2x4) i) Local class vs inheritance vs containership ii) Static vs dynamic binding</p><p>iii) new operator vs operator new iv) abstract class vs virtual base class</p>","sh":"<p>i) A local class is defined inside a function; it is visible only in that function, cannot have static data members and cannot use the function&#x27;s non-static local variables. Inheritance (is-a): a derived class gets the members of a base class (class Car : public Vehicle). Containership (has-a): a class holds an object of another class as a member</p><pre>(class Car { Engine e; }).</pre><p>ii) Static binding: the call is bound at compile time (normal and overloaded functions). Dynamic binding: bound at run time through a virtual function and the vtable using the object&#x27;s real type.</p><p>iii) The new operator allocates memory and runs the constructor (cannot be overloaded); operator new is only the allocation function, void* operator new(size_t), which can be overloaded and does not call a constructor.</p><p>iv) An abstract class has a pure virtual function and cannot be instantiated; it defines an interface. A virtual base class is a base inherited with virtual so that a diamond has only one copy of it; it can be an ordinary, instantiable class.</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nint main() {\n    class Local { public: void hi() { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;local class\\n&quot;; } };   // class local to main()\n    Local l;\n    l.hi();\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><pre>local class</pre>","exam":"end","year":2019,"branch":"IT","src":"EndSem Papers.pdf pp.12-14 (IT203, Nov-2019"},{"p":24,"q":"4","marks":"8","unit":1,"topic":"Friend classes for IT and mechanical students; packages","qh":"<p>Q4. A) Implement the following using friend concept. Class IT_student: Name, age, marks, roll [private]; Public functions: Get() -&gt; input from user; Show() -&gt; output on screen. Class mechanical_student: Name, age, marks, roll [private]; Public functions: Get() -&gt; input from user; Show() -&gt; output on screen. Add the following concepts to the program: i) a friend function add() to add marks of both students (IT, Mech) and display the output. ii) to make mechanical_student class friend class of IT_student so that show() functions of each class can first display their own details and then other class&#x27;s details. i.e. IT&#x27;s show() will display details of IT student first and then mechanical student&#x27;s details and vice versa. [4] B) What are packages in java. Explain in detail with example. [4]</p>","sh":"<p>A) add() is a friend of both classes. For (ii) each class is made a friend of the other (friend class</p><pre>mechanical_student; in IT_student and friend class IT_student; in mechanical_student), so each show() can</pre><p>print its own record and then the other&#x27;s private data.</p><p>Sample input:</p><pre>Asha 19 85 1  Ravi 20 78 2\n#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n#include &lt;string&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass mechanical_student;\nclass IT_student {\n    string name; int age, marks, roll;\npublic:\n    void Get() { cin &gt;&gt; name &gt;&gt; age &gt;&gt; marks &gt;&gt; roll; }\n    void Show(const mechanical_student&amp; m) const;\n    friend int add(const IT_student&amp;, const mechanical_student&amp;);\n    friend class mechanical_student;\n};\nclass mechanical_student {\n    string name; int age, marks, roll;\npublic:\n    void Get() { cin &gt;&gt; name &gt;&gt; age &gt;&gt; marks &gt;&gt; roll; }\n    void Show(const IT_student&amp; s) const;\n    friend int add(const IT_student&amp;, const mechanical_student&amp;);\n    friend class IT_student;\n};\nvoid IT_student::Show(const mechanical_student&amp; m) const {\n    cout &lt;&lt; &quot;IT student:   &quot; &lt;&lt; name &lt;&lt; &quot;, &quot; &lt;&lt; age &lt;&lt; &quot;, marks &quot; &lt;&lt; marks &lt;&lt; &quot;, roll &quot; &lt;&lt; roll &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n    cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Mech student: &quot; &lt;&lt; m.name &lt;&lt; &quot;, &quot; &lt;&lt; m.age &lt;&lt; &quot;, marks &quot; &lt;&lt; m.marks &lt;&lt; &quot;, roll &quot; &lt;&lt; m.roll &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n}\nvoid mechanical_student::Show(const IT_student&amp; s) const {\n    cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Mech student: &quot; &lt;&lt; name &lt;&lt; &quot;, &quot; &lt;&lt; age &lt;&lt; &quot;, marks &quot; &lt;&lt; marks &lt;&lt; &quot;, roll &quot; &lt;&lt; roll &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n    cout &lt;&lt; &quot;IT student:   &quot; &lt;&lt; s.name &lt;&lt; &quot;, &quot; &lt;&lt; s.age &lt;&lt; &quot;, marks &quot; &lt;&lt; s.marks &lt;&lt; &quot;, roll &quot; &lt;&lt; s.roll &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n}\nint add(const IT_student&amp; a, const mechanical_student&amp; b) { return a.marks + b.marks; }\nint main() {\n    IT_student it; mechanical_student me;\n    it.Get(); me.Get();\n    cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Total marks = &quot; &lt;&lt; add(it, me) &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n    it.Show(me);\n    me.Show(it);\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Total marks = 163</p><p class=\"ln i0\">IT student:   Asha, 19, marks 85, roll 1</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Mech student: Ravi, 20, marks 78, roll 2</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Mech student: Ravi, 20, marks 78, roll 2</p><p class=\"ln i0\">IT student:   Asha, 19, marks 85, roll 1</p><p>B) A package is a namespace that groups related classes, interfaces and sub-packages (like a folder). Uses: avoids name conflicts, organises code, gives access control (default/protected), makes classes easy to find and reuse. Built-in: java.lang (auto-imported), java.util, java.io, java.awt. User-defined: first line package pack;, saved</p><pre>in a folder pack, compiled with javac -d . A.java, used by import pack.A; or import pack.*;.\n// File pack/Message.java\npackage pack;\npublic class Message {\n    public void show() { System.out.println(&quot;Hello from package pack&quot;); }\n}\n// File Test.java\nimport pack.Message;\npublic class Test {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Message m = new Message();\n        m.show();\n    }\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Hello from package pack</p>","exam":"end","year":2019,"branch":"IT","src":"EndSem Papers.pdf pp.12-14 (IT203, Nov-2019"},{"p":24,"q":"5","marks":"8","unit":1,"topic":"Templates; run-time polymorphism","qh":"<p>Q5. A) What is the concept of templates? Explain with example from each category. [4] B) What is run-time polymorphism? Explain in detail. [4]</p>","sh":"<p>A) A template is a blueprint for generic code: the compiler produces a concrete function or class for each type it is used with. Categories: function templates and class templates.</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\ntemplate &lt;class T&gt; T larger(T a, T b) { return a &gt; b ? a : b; }        // function template\ntemplate &lt;class T&gt;\nclass Pair {                                                           // class template\n    T a, b;\npublic:\n    Pair(T x, T y) : a(x), b(y) {}\n    T sum() const { return a + b; }\n};\nint main() {\n    cout &lt;&lt; larger(3, 8) &lt;&lt; &#x27; &#x27; &lt;&lt; larger(2.5, 1.5) &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n    Pair&lt;int&gt; p(2, 3); Pair&lt;double&gt; q(1.5, 2.5);\n    cout &lt;&lt; p.sum() &lt;&lt; &#x27; &#x27; &lt;&lt; q.sum() &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">8 2.5</p><p class=\"ln i0\">5 4</p><p>B) Run-time (dynamic) polymorphism means the function to execute is chosen at run time from the actual object type. It needs inheritance, a base-class function declared virtual, overriding in derived classes, and a call through a base pointer/reference. Internally each class has a vtable and each object a vptr.</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass Shape { public: virtual double area() const { return 0; } virtual ~Shape() {} };\nclass Circle : public Shape { double r; public: Circle(double x) : r(x) {} double area() const override { return 3.14 * r * r; } };\nclass Rect : public Shape { double l, b; public: Rect(double x, double y) : l(x), b(y) {} double area() const override { return l * b; } };\nint main() {\n    Shape* s[2] = { new Circle(1), new Rect(2, 3) };\n    for (Shape* p : s) { cout &lt;&lt; p-&gt;area() &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;; delete p; }\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">3.14</p><p class=\"ln i0\">6</p>","exam":"end","year":2019,"branch":"IT","src":"EndSem Papers.pdf pp.12-14 (IT203, Nov-2019"},{"p":24,"q":"6","marks":"8","unit":2,"topic":"Java architecture; predict the output","qh":"<p>Q6. A) Explain the java architecture and the lifecycle of a java code in detail. [4] B) Explain and predict the output: [1x4] (i) class Test { int value; public: Test(int v = 0) { value = v; } int getValue() { return value; } }; int main() { const Test t; cout &lt;&lt; t.getValue(); return 0; } (ii) class A { public: void print() { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;A::print()&quot;; } }; class B : private A { public: void print() { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;B::print()&quot;; } }; class C : public B { public: void print() { A::print(); } }; int main() { C b; b.print(); } (iii) class Test { public: Test(); ~Test(); }; Test::Test() { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Constructor is executed\\n&quot;; } Test::~Test() { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Destructor is executed\\n&quot;; } int main() { delete new Test(); return 0; } (iv) int fun() { static int num = 40; return num--; } int main() { for (fun(); fun(); fun()) printf(&quot;%d &quot;, fun()); getchar(); return 0; }</p>","sh":"<p>A) Java architecture: the source file .java is compiled by javac (part of the JDK) into bytecode .class. The JVM runs</p><p>it. JVM components: (1) class loader subsystem (loading, linking: verify/prepare/resolve, initialisation); (2) runtime data areas: method area (class data), heap (objects, garbage collected), Java stacks (frames per thread), PC registers, native method stacks; (3) execution engine: interpreter, JIT compiler, garbage collector; (4) native method interface and libraries. JRE = JVM + libraries; JDK = JRE + tools.</p><pre>Hello.java --javac--&gt; Hello.class --ClassLoader--&gt; Bytecode Verifier --&gt; Interpreter / JIT --&gt; Native code --&gt; Output</pre><p>Life cycle of a Java program: write source, compile to bytecode, load by the class loader, verify, execute (interpret/JIT) with automatic memory management and garbage collection, and exit.</p><p>B) (i) Compile-time error: t is a const object, so only const member functions may be called on it, but getValue() is not declared const. (Fix: int getValue() const; the program would then print 0.)</p><p>(ii) Compile-time error: class B : private A makes A a private base, so inside the class C (derived from B) the name A is inaccessible; A::print() is therefore rejected (the compiler says &quot;A is a private member of A&quot; / &quot;A is an inaccessible base&quot;). With public inheritance C::print() would print A::print().</p><p>(iii) new Test() runs the constructor and delete runs the destructor.</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Constructor is executed</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Destructor is executed</p><p>(iv) fun() returns num and then decrements it (post-decrement), the static num persisting between calls. Each loop iteration calls fun() in the condition, in the body (printf) and in the update, i.e. 3 calls per iteration. Calls return 40 (init), then condition 39, body 38 (printed), update 37; condition 36, body 35, update 34; and so on until the condition returns 0 (false). Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">38 35 32 29 26 23 20 17 14 11 8 5 2</p><p>(The loop ends when the condition fun() returns 0, which happens when num goes from 0 to -1.)</p>","exam":"end","year":2019,"branch":"IT","src":"EndSem Papers.pdf pp.12-14 (IT203, Nov-2019"},{"p":25,"q":"1","marks":"9","unit":0,"topic":"Static members; call by value/reference; private constructor","qh":"<p>Q1. Answer the following: [3x3] a) What is static in data members and member functions? Explain b) Differentiate between Call by value vs call by reference using &#x27;object as argument&#x27; concept. c) How to invoke a private constructor?</p>","sh":"<p>a) A static data member is shared by all objects of the class (one copy, defined outside the class, exists even with no objects). A static member function belongs to the class, has no this, can use only static members and is called as</p><pre>Class::f().\n#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass Student {\n    static int count;                      // static data member\npublic:\n    Student() { ++count; }\n    static int total() { return count; }   // static member function\n};\nint Student::count = 0;\nint main() {\n    Student a, b, c;\n    cout &lt;&lt; Student::total() &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">3</p><p>b) With an object passed by value the function receives a copy (the copy constructor runs, the caller&#x27;s object is unchanged); with a reference the function works on the original object (no copy, changes are visible).</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass Acc { public: int bal; Acc(int b) : bal(b) {} Acc(const Acc&amp; o) : bal(o.bal) { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;copy made\\n&quot;; } };\nvoid byValue(Acc a)      { a.bal += 100; }\nvoid byReference(Acc&amp; a) { a.bal += 100; }\nint main() {\n    Acc x(500);\n    byValue(x);       cout &lt;&lt; &quot;after value:     &quot; &lt;&lt; x.bal &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n    byReference(x);   cout &lt;&lt; &quot;after reference: &quot; &lt;&lt; x.bal &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">copy made</p><p class=\"ln i0\">after value:     500</p><p class=\"ln i0\">after reference: 600</p><p>c) A private constructor cannot be called from outside, so it is invoked from inside the class: from a static member function (factory/singleton getInstance()), from a friend, or from another constructor of the same class (delegation).</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass Single {\n    static Single* inst;\n    Single() { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;private constructor called\\n&quot;; }\npublic:\n    static Single* getInstance() {          // invokes the private constructor\n        if (!inst) inst = new Single();\n        return inst;\n    }\n};\nSingle* Single::inst = nullptr;\nint main() {\n    Single* a = Single::getInstance();\n    Single* b = Single::getInstance();\n    cout &lt;&lt; (a == b ? &quot;same object&quot; : &quot;different objects&quot;) &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">private constructor called</p><p class=\"ln i0\">same object</p>","exam":"mid","year":2019,"branch":"IT","src":"OOPS MIDSEM ALL BRANCHES Compiled.pdf p.18 (IT203, Sept-2019"},{"p":25,"q":"2","marks":"9","unit":0,"topic":"Class versus structure; :: in inheritance; destructor overloading","qh":"<p>Q2. a) Differentiate between Class and structure with examples from each. [3] b) What is the role of :: in inheritance? [3] c) Can destructors be overloaded? Why or why not. [3]</p>","sh":"<p>a)</p><pre>Class                                     Structure (struct)\nDefault access: private                   Default access: public (in C++); C structs have no access control\nMembers and functions, inheritance        C++ struct also allows these; C struct: data only\nUsed for objects with behaviour           Used for plain data grouping\nclass Car { int speed; public: ... };     struct Point { int x, y; };\n#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass C { int hidden = 1; public: int open = 2; };    // hidden is private by default\nstruct S { int visible = 3; };                        // visible is public by default\nint main() {\n    C c; S s;\n    cout &lt;&lt; c.open &lt;&lt; &#x27; &#x27; &lt;&lt; s.visible &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;       // c.hidden would be an error\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">2 3</p><p>b) In inheritance :: selects the class whose member is meant: Base::show() calls the base version hidden/overridden by the derived class, removes ambiguity when two bases have the same member name (A::f() vs B::f()), and defines members outside the class (void Derived::f() {}).</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass Base { public: void show() { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Base::show\\n&quot;; } };\nclass Derived : public Base { public: void show() { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Derived::show -&gt; &quot;; Base::show(); } };\nint main() { Derived d; d.show(); d.Base::show(); }</pre><p>Output:</p><pre>Derived::show -&gt; Base::show\nBase::show</pre><p>c) No. A destructor takes no arguments and has no return type, and a class has exactly one destructor (~X()), so there is nothing to distinguish two overloads; the compiler calls it automatically at the end of the object&#x27;s life, so it must be unique.</p>","exam":"mid","year":2019,"branch":"IT","src":"OOPS MIDSEM ALL BRANCHES Compiled.pdf p.18 (IT203, Sept-2019"},{"p":25,"q":"3","marks":"6","unit":1,"topic":"Operator overloading (delete, <<); virtual base class","qh":"<p>Q3. a) What is operator overloading and which feature of OOP does it represent? Explain with example of overloading delete operator and &lt;&lt; operator. [3] b) Write a program to show the use of virtual base class. [3]</p>","sh":"<p>a) Operator overloading gives operators new meaning for user-defined types. It represents polymorphism (compile-time, static polymorphism). operator delete is overloaded as a static class function void operator delete(void*), called after the destructor; &lt;&lt; is overloaded as a friend function taking ostream&amp;.</p><pre>#include &lt;cstdlib&gt;\n#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass Item {\n    int id;\npublic:\n    Item(int i) : id(i) {}\n    void* operator new(size_t s) { return malloc(s); }\n    void operator delete(void* p) { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;custom delete called\\n&quot;; free(p); }\n    friend ostream&amp; operator&lt;&lt;(ostream&amp; os, const Item&amp; it) { return os &lt;&lt; &quot;Item#&quot; &lt;&lt; it.id; }\n};\nint main() {\n    Item* p = new Item(7);\n    cout &lt;&lt; *p &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n    delete p;\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Item#7</p><p class=\"ln i0\">custom delete called</p><p>b)</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass A { public: int x = 1; };\nclass B : virtual public A {};\nclass C : virtual public A {};\nclass D : public B, public C {};\nint main() { D d; d.x = 9; cout &lt;&lt; d.x &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;; }      // one shared A, no ambiguity</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">9</p>","exam":"mid","year":2019,"branch":"IT","src":"OOPS MIDSEM ALL BRANCHES Compiled.pdf p.18 (IT203, Sept-2019"},{"p":25,"q":"4","marks":"6","unit":0,"topic":"Nested class; initialiser list in multilevel inheritance","qh":"<p>Q4. a) What is the role of a nested class? How to use it? [3] b) What is initialiser list and discuss its role in constructor calls in multilevel inheritance in C++ with examples. [3]</p>","sh":"<p>a) A nested class is declared inside another class; its role is to group a helper type with the class that uses it, hide it when private and keep names local. Use it as Outer::Inner obj; (if public) or only inside Outer (if private).</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass Library {\npublic:\n    class Book {                                // nested class\n        string title;\n    public:\n        Book(string t) : title(t) {}\n        void show() const { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Book: &quot; &lt;&lt; title &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;; }\n    };\n};\nint main() { Library::Book b(&quot;OOP with C++&quot;); b.show(); }</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Book: OOP with C++</p><p>b) The initialiser list (: Base(args), member(value)) initialises bases and members before the constructor body. In multilevel inheritance each class passes arguments to its immediate base; constructors run base-first (A, B, C) and destructors in reverse.</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass A { public: A(int a) { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;A(&quot; &lt;&lt; a &lt;&lt; &quot;)\\n&quot;; } };\nclass B : public A { public: B(int a, int b) : A(a) { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;B(&quot; &lt;&lt; b &lt;&lt; &quot;)\\n&quot;; } };\nclass C : public B { public: C(int a, int b, int c) : B(a, b) { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;C(&quot; &lt;&lt; c &lt;&lt; &quot;)\\n&quot;; } };\nint main() { C obj(1, 2, 3); }</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">A(1)</p><p class=\"ln i0\">B(2)</p><p class=\"ln i0\">C(3)</p>","exam":"mid","year":2019,"branch":"IT","src":"OOPS MIDSEM ALL BRANCHES Compiled.pdf p.18 (IT203, Sept-2019"},{"p":26,"q":"1","marks":"4","unit":0,"topic":"Procedure-oriented versus object-oriented","qh":"<p>Q1. Differentiate between characteristics of procedure-oriented programming and object-oriented programing languages. (4)</p>","sh":"<pre>Characteristic        Procedure-oriented (C, Pascal)             Object-oriented (C++, Java)\nBasic unit            Function/procedure                         Object/class\nApproach              Top-down                                   Bottom-up\nData                  Global/shared, exposed                     Encapsulated, hidden\nData security         None (any function can change data)        Access specifiers protect data\nReuse                 Functions                                  Inheritance, composition, templates\nPolymorphism          Not supported                              Overloading, virtual functions\nReal-world modelling  Weak                                       Strong (objects = entities)\nExtension             Modify existing functions                  Add derived classes</pre>","exam":"mid","year":2019,"branch":"SE","src":"OOPS MIDSEM ALL BRANCHES Compiled.pdf p.20 (SE203, B.Tech (CSE), Sept-2019"},{"p":26,"q":"2","marks":"3","unit":1,"topic":"Operators that cannot be overloaded","qh":"<p>Q2. List the operators that cannot be overloaded through member function and friend function. (3)</p>","sh":"<p>These operators cannot be overloaded at all, by either a member or a friend function:</p><pre>::    scope resolution\n.     member access\n.*    pointer-to-member access\n?:    conditional (ternary)\nsizeof, typeid, alignof, noexcept   compile-time/inspection operators</pre><p>(Named casts such as static_cast also cannot be overloaded. =, [], () and -&gt; can be overloaded only as member functions, not as friends.)</p>","exam":"mid","year":2019,"branch":"SE","src":"OOPS MIDSEM ALL BRANCHES Compiled.pdf p.20 (SE203, B.Tech (CSE), Sept-2019"},{"p":26,"q":"3","marks":"3","unit":0,"topic":"Inline functions","qh":"<p>Q3. What is inline function? Explain situations where inline expansion may not work. (3)</p>","sh":"<p>An inline function (inline int sq(int x) { return x * x; }) is one whose body the compiler substitutes at the call site, avoiding function-call overhead (good for small, frequently called functions). inline is only a request. Inline expansion may not work when: the function contains a loop, switch or goto; it is recursive; it contains static variables; it is too large; the address of the function is taken or it is called through a pointer; it is a virtual function called polymorphically; the function is defined after its use in a way the compiler cannot see the body.</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\ninline int sq(int x) { return x * x; }\nint main() { cout &lt;&lt; sq(6) &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;; }</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">36</p>","exam":"mid","year":2019,"branch":"SE","src":"OOPS MIDSEM ALL BRANCHES Compiled.pdf p.20 (SE203, B.Tech (CSE), Sept-2019"},{"p":26,"q":"4","marks":"5","unit":1,"topic":"Adding numbers of two classes with a friend member function","qh":"<p>Q4. Write a C++ program that adds two numbers belonging to different classes. Demonstrate the use of friend function in one class that is a member function of the second class. (5)</p>","sh":"<p>Beta::add(const Alpha&amp;) is a member of Beta and is declared a friend of Alpha, so it can read Alpha&#x27;s private number.</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass Alpha;\nclass Beta {\n    int b;\npublic:\n    Beta(int v) : b(v) {}\n    int add(const Alpha&amp; a);          // member of Beta, friend of Alpha\n};\nclass Alpha {\n    int a;\npublic:\n    Alpha(int v) : a(v) {}\n    friend int Beta::add(const Alpha&amp;);\n};\nint Beta::add(const Alpha&amp; x) { return x.a + b; }\nint main() {\n    Alpha p(12);\n    Beta q(30);\n    cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Sum = &quot; &lt;&lt; q.add(p) &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Sum = 42</p>","exam":"mid","year":2019,"branch":"SE","src":"OOPS MIDSEM ALL BRANCHES Compiled.pdf p.20 (SE203, B.Tech (CSE), Sept-2019"},{"p":26,"q":"5","marks":"5","unit":0,"topic":"Counting objects created, destroyed, active","qh":"<p>Q5. Write a C++ program to keep a track of number of objects created, number of objects destroyed, and number of active objects in a program. (5)</p>","sh":"<pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass Tracker {\n    static int created, destroyed;\npublic:\n    Tracker() { ++created; }\n    Tracker(const Tracker&amp;) { ++created; }\n    ~Tracker() { ++destroyed; }\n    static void report() {\n        cout &lt;&lt; &quot;created = &quot; &lt;&lt; created &lt;&lt; &quot;, destroyed = &quot; &lt;&lt; destroyed\n             &lt;&lt; &quot;, active = &quot; &lt;&lt; created - destroyed &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n    }\n};\nint Tracker::created = 0;\nint Tracker::destroyed = 0;\nint main() {\n    Tracker a, b;\n    Tracker::report();\n    {\n        Tracker c(a);\n        Tracker* d = new Tracker;\n        Tracker::report();\n        delete d;\n    }\n    Tracker::report();\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">created = 2, destroyed = 0, active = 2</p><p class=\"ln i0\">created = 4, destroyed = 0, active = 4</p><p class=\"ln i0\">created = 4, destroyed = 2, active = 2</p>","exam":"mid","year":2019,"branch":"SE","src":"OOPS MIDSEM ALL BRANCHES Compiled.pdf p.20 (SE203, B.Tech (CSE), Sept-2019"},{"p":26,"q":"6","marks":"5","unit":1,"topic":"Virtual base class","qh":"<p>Q6. Explain virtual base class with suitable example. (5)</p>","sh":"<p>In multipath inheritance the derived class receives the common base through two routes. A virtual base class makes the two intermediate classes share a single base subobject: class B : virtual public A; class C : virtual public A;. This removes the duplicate copy and the ambiguity of members, and saves memory. The most derived class calls the virtual base&#x27;s constructor.</p><pre>        Person\n        /    \\\n   Teacher  Student        (both inherit Person with &quot;virtual&quot;)\n        \\    /\n         TA                (only one Person inside TA)\n#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass Person {\npublic:\n    string name;\n    Person(string n = &quot;?&quot;) : name(n) { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Person(&quot; &lt;&lt; name &lt;&lt; &quot;)\\n&quot;; }\n};\nclass Teacher : virtual public Person { public: Teacher(string n) : Person(n) {} };\nclass Student : virtual public Person { public: Student(string n) : Person(n) {} };\nclass TA : public Teacher, public Student {\npublic:\n    TA(string n) : Person(n), Teacher(n), Student(n) {}\n};\nint main() {\n    TA t(&quot;Neha&quot;);\n    cout &lt;&lt; t.name &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;           // no ambiguity\n    // Without virtual inheritance t.name would be ambiguous: Teacher::name and Student::name\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Person(Neha)</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Neha</p>","exam":"mid","year":2019,"branch":"SE","src":"OOPS MIDSEM ALL BRANCHES Compiled.pdf p.20 (SE203, B.Tech (CSE), Sept-2019"},{"p":26,"q":"7","marks":"5","unit":1,"topic":"Time to Minutes conversion","qh":"<p>Q7. Write a C++ program to convert class time with data members as hrs and mins into class minutes with data member as totalminutes. (5)</p>","sh":"<p>Conversion from one class to another can be done with a conversion operator in the source class or a converting constructor in the destination class. Here the source class Time has operator Minutes(). Total minutes = hours x 60 + mins.</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass Minutes {\n    int totalminutes;\npublic:\n    Minutes(int m = 0) : totalminutes(m) {}\n    void show() const { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Total minutes = &quot; &lt;&lt; totalminutes &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;; }\n};\nclass Time {\n    int hrs, mins;\npublic:\n    Time(int h, int m) : hrs(h), mins(m) {}\n    operator Minutes() const { return Minutes(hrs * 60 + mins); }   // class-to-class conversion\n};\nint main() {\n    Time t(2, 35);\n    Minutes m = t;          // implicit conversion calls Time::operator Minutes()\n    m.show();\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Total minutes = 155</p>","exam":"mid","year":2019,"branch":"SE","src":"OOPS MIDSEM ALL BRANCHES Compiled.pdf p.20 (SE203, B.Tech (CSE), Sept-2019"},{"p":27,"q":"1","marks":"14","unit":0,"topic":"Constructors and destructors; type conversion","qh":"<p>Q1. (a) Explain constructors and destructors. Give suitable code to explain. [7] (b) What is type conversion? Write suitable code for conversion from class type to basic type. [7]</p>","sh":"<p>(a) A constructor is a special member function with the class name and no return type, called automatically when an object is created, to initialise it. Types: default, parameterized, copy. A destructor ~X() is called automatically when an object is destroyed, to release resources; one per class, no arguments; objects are destroyed in reverse order of construction.</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass Demo {\n    int id;\npublic:\n    Demo() : id(0) { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;default constructor\\n&quot;; }\n    Demo(int i) : id(i) { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;parameterized constructor &quot; &lt;&lt; id &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;; }\n    Demo(const Demo&amp; o) : id(o.id) { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;copy constructor &quot; &lt;&lt; id &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;; }\n    ~Demo() { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;destructor &quot; &lt;&lt; id &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;; }\n};\nint main() {\n    Demo a;\n    Demo b(5);\n    Demo c = b;\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">default constructor</p><p class=\"ln i0\">parameterized constructor 5</p><p class=\"ln i0\">copy constructor 5</p><p class=\"ln i0\">destructor 5</p><p class=\"ln i0\">destructor 5</p><p class=\"ln i0\">destructor 0</p><p>(b) Type conversion changes a value from one type to another. Class to basic type is done with a conversion operator operator type() (no return type, no arguments) in the class. Basic to class uses a converting constructor.</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass Distance {\n    int feet, inches;\npublic:\n    Distance(int f, int i) : feet(f), inches(i) {}\n    operator float() const { return feet * 12 + inches; }     // class type -&gt; basic type\n};\nint main() {\n    Distance d(5, 6);\n    float total = d;               // calls operator float()\n    cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Total inches = &quot; &lt;&lt; total &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Total inches = 66</p>","exam":"supp","year":2019,"branch":"CO","src":"EndSem Papers.pdf pp.17-18 (CO-203, B.Tech (CO), Feb-2019"},{"p":27,"q":"2","marks":"14","unit":0,"topic":"Keywords added in C++; inline versus macros","qh":"<p>Q2. (a) What are the additional keywords added in C++ other than C? Explain 5 keywords of C++. [7] (b) What is the difference between inline function and macros? Write a program using inline function to find sum of two numbers. [7]</p>","sh":"<pre>(a) Added keywords include: class, public, private, protected, virtual, friend, inline, new, delete, this,\ntemplate, typename, try, catch, throw, namespace, using, operator, bool, true, false, mutable, explicit,</pre><p>export, wchar_t, typeid, and the cast keywords. Five explained: class defines a user-defined type with data and functions; new/delete allocate/free memory with constructor/destructor calls; virtual enables run-time polymorphism; friend grants a function/class access to private members; template defines generic functions/classes; (also try/catch/throw for exceptions).</p><p>(b)</p><pre>Inline function                           Macro\nHandled by the compiler                   Handled by the preprocessor (text substitution)\nType checked                              No type checking\nArguments evaluated once                  Argument expressions may be evaluated several times\nCan be a member function, can be debugged Cannot be a member; hard to debug\ninline int sum(int a, int b) { return a+b; }   #define SUM(a,b) a+b\n#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\ninline int sum(int a, int b) { return a + b; }\nint main() {\n    int x = 17, y = 25;\n    cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Sum = &quot; &lt;&lt; sum(x, y) &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Sum = 42</p>","exam":"supp","year":2019,"branch":"CO","src":"EndSem Papers.pdf pp.17-18 (CO-203, B.Tech (CO), Feb-2019"},{"p":27,"q":"3","marks":"14","unit":1,"topic":"Friend functions; function overloading for area","qh":"<p>Q3. (a) Explain the use of friend function. Give suitable example. [7] (b) What is function overloading? Write a program to overload function area using function overloading. [7]</p>","sh":"<p>(a) A friend function is a non-member function that may use the private/protected members of a class. Uses: operators with a non-class left operand (cout &lt;&lt; obj), a function that works on private data of two classes (comparison, bridge), and non-member helpers that need access.</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass Rect;\nclass Circle {\n    double r;\npublic:\n    Circle(double x) : r(x) {}\n    friend bool larger(const Circle&amp;, const Rect&amp;);\n};\nclass Rect {\n    double l, b;\npublic:\n    Rect(double x, double y) : l(x), b(y) {}\n    friend bool larger(const Circle&amp;, const Rect&amp;);\n};\nbool larger(const Circle&amp; c, const Rect&amp; r) { return 3.14 * c.r * c.r &gt; r.l * r.b; }\nint main() {\n    Circle c(2); Rect r(3, 4);\n    cout &lt;&lt; boolalpha &lt;&lt; &quot;circle larger than rectangle: &quot; &lt;&lt; larger(c, r) &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">circle larger than rectangle: true</p><p>(b) Function overloading: several functions with the same name and different parameter lists; the compiler picks the one that matches the call.</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nconst double PI = 3.14159;\ndouble area(double r)               { return PI * r * r; }       // circle\nint    area(int s)                  { return s * s; }            // square\ndouble area(double l, double b)     { return l * b; }           // rectangle\ndouble area(double b, double h, char) { return 0.5 * b * h; }    // triangle (extra tag parameter)\nint main() {\n    cout &lt;&lt; &quot;circle    &quot; &lt;&lt; area(2.0) &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n    cout &lt;&lt; &quot;square    &quot; &lt;&lt; area(4) &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n    cout &lt;&lt; &quot;rectangle &quot; &lt;&lt; area(3.0, 5.0) &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n    cout &lt;&lt; &quot;triangle  &quot; &lt;&lt; area(6.0, 4.0, &#x27;t&#x27;) &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">circle    12.5664</p><p class=\"ln i0\">square    16</p><p class=\"ln i0\">rectangle 15</p><p class=\"ln i0\">triangle  12</p>","exam":"supp","year":2019,"branch":"CO","src":"EndSem Papers.pdf pp.17-18 (CO-203, B.Tech (CO), Feb-2019"},{"p":27,"q":"4","marks":"14","unit":3,"topic":"Typed exceptions; person inheritance","qh":"<p>Q4. (a) Create a code for exception handling for exception type int, char and float. [6] (b) Write a program to add, show details of employee and student using inheritance where class &quot;person&quot; is inherited by two different classes &quot;employee&quot; and &quot;student&quot;. [8]</p>","sh":"<p>(a)</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nvoid test(int k) {\n    if (k == 1) throw 42;           // int\n    if (k == 2) throw &#x27;Z&#x27;;          // char\n    if (k == 3) throw 3.5f;         // float\n}\nint main() {\n    for (int k = 1; k &lt;= 3; ++k) {\n        try { test(k); }\n        catch (int e)   { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;int exception: &quot; &lt;&lt; e &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;; }\n        catch (char e)  { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;char exception: &quot; &lt;&lt; e &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;; }\n        catch (float e) { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;float exception: &quot; &lt;&lt; e &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;; }\n    }\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">int exception: 42</p><p class=\"ln i0\">char exception: Z</p><p class=\"ln i0\">float exception: 3.5</p><p>(b) person holds the common data (name, age); employee adds salary; student adds marks. Both are derived from person (hierarchical inheritance); each has add() (store data) and show().</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n#include &lt;string&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass person {\nprotected:\n    string name;\n    int age;\npublic:\n    void add(string n, int a) { name = n; age = a; }\n    void show() const { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Name: &quot; &lt;&lt; name &lt;&lt; &quot;, Age: &quot; &lt;&lt; age; }\n};\nclass employee : public person {\n    double salary;\npublic:\n    void add(string n, int a, double s) { person::add(n, a); salary = s; }\n    void show() const { person::show(); cout &lt;&lt; &quot;, Salary: &quot; &lt;&lt; salary &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;; }\n};\nclass student : public person {\n    int marks;\npublic:\n    void add(string n, int a, int m) { person::add(n, a); marks = m; }\n    void show() const { person::show(); cout &lt;&lt; &quot;, Marks: &quot; &lt;&lt; marks &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;; }\n};\nint main() {\n    employee e; student s;\n    e.add(&quot;Asha&quot;, 30, 55000);\n    s.add(&quot;Ravi&quot;, 19, 88);\n    e.show();\n    s.show();\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Name: Asha, Age: 30, Salary: 55000</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Name: Ravi, Age: 19, Marks: 88</p>","exam":"supp","year":2019,"branch":"CO","src":"EndSem Papers.pdf pp.17-18 (CO-203, B.Tech (CO), Feb-2019"},{"p":27,"q":"5","marks":"14","unit":0,"topic":"Abstraction, encapsulation, data hiding; visibility modes","qh":"<p>Q5. (a) Give the meaning of the following terms: i) Abstraction; ii) Encapsulation; iii) Data hiding. [9] (b) Explain different visibility modes available in C++ along with their purpose. [5]</p>","sh":"<p>(a) i) Abstraction: showing only the essential features (the interface) and hiding the implementation details; achieved with classes, public interfaces, abstract classes (a Car driver uses the steering wheel, not the engine internals). ii) Encapsulation: binding data and the functions that operate on it into a single unit, the class. iii) Data hiding: restricting direct access to the data members by making them private/protected, so that they can be changed only through the class&#x27;s public member functions; it protects the integrity of data. Encapsulation is the mechanism, data hiding is its result, abstraction is the design idea.</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass Account {\n    double balance = 0;                        // hidden data\npublic:\n    void deposit(double a) { if (a &gt; 0) balance += a; }    // controlled access\n    double get() const { return balance; }\n};\nint main() {\n    Account acc;\n    acc.deposit(250);\n    acc.deposit(-5);          // rejected by the class\n    cout &lt;&lt; acc.get() &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">250</p><p>(b) public: members accessible from anywhere (the class interface). private: accessible only inside the class (and its friends); default for classes. protected: accessible inside the class, friends and derived classes, not from outside. Visibility in inheritance (public, protected, private derivation) decides how the base&#x27;s members appear in the derived class: public inheritance keeps their access levels; protected derivation turns public members into protected; private derivation turns them into private.</p>","exam":"supp","year":2019,"branch":"CO","src":"EndSem Papers.pdf pp.17-18 (CO-203, B.Tech (CO), Feb-2019"},{"p":27,"q":"6","marks":"14","unit":1,"topic":"Overloading rules; virtual functions; operators that cannot be overloaded","qh":"<p>Q6. (a) What are the rules for function overloading? [5] (b) How are the virtual functions declared in C++? [6] (c) List operators which can&#x27;t be overloaded. [3]</p>","sh":"<p>(a) Rules: functions must have the same name and differ in the number, types or order of parameters (the return type alone cannot distinguish them); they must be in the same scope; default arguments must not create ambiguity; const-qualification of the object or parameters can differ; the compiler resolves the call at compile time by exact match, promotion, standard conversion and then user conversion; an ambiguous call is an error; static and non-static member functions with the same parameters cannot be overloaded.</p><p>(b) A virtual function is declared by writing the keyword virtual before the return type in the base class: virtual void show();. Rules: it must be a non-static member function; it is declared in the base class and redefined (same signature) in derived classes (override can be added); it is called through a base pointer/reference for run-time binding; the virtual keyword is not repeated in the out-of-class definition; constructors cannot be virtual, destructors can be (and should be for base classes). A pure virtual function adds = 0.</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass Base {\npublic:\n    virtual void show() { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Base show\\n&quot;; }     // declaration with virtual\n    virtual ~Base() {}\n};\nclass Derived : public Base {\npublic:\n    void show() override { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Derived show\\n&quot;; }\n};\nint main() { Base* b = new Derived; b-&gt;show(); delete b; }</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Derived show</p><p>(c) ::, ., .*, ?:, sizeof (also typeid, alignof, and the named casts).</p>","exam":"supp","year":2019,"branch":"CO","src":"EndSem Papers.pdf pp.17-18 (CO-203, B.Tech (CO), Feb-2019"},{"p":27,"q":"7","marks":"14","unit":1,"topic":"Short notes (any two)","qh":"<p>Q7. Write short notes on any two :- (a) Virtual class (b) Templates (c) Call by value (d) Features of Java</p>","sh":"<p>(a) A virtual base class (class B : virtual public A) is a base shared by all paths of a multipath inheritance, so the most derived class contains one A subobject; it removes ambiguity and duplication; the most derived class constructs it. (b) Templates: generic functions/classes (template &lt;class T&gt; T maxOf(T a, T b)), instantiated by the compiler for each type; gives reuse and type safety; function templates and class templates; may be specialised.</p><p>(c) Call by value: the function receives a copy of the argument, so changes inside do not affect the caller&#x27;s variable; safe but copies cost time/memory; to change the caller&#x27;s variable use references or pointers.</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nvoid byValue(int x) { x = 100; }\nvoid byRef(int&amp; x) { x = 100; }\nint main() {\n    int a = 1, b = 1;\n    byValue(a); byRef(b);\n    cout &lt;&lt; a &lt;&lt; &#x27; &#x27; &lt;&lt; b &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">1 100</p><p>(d) Features of Java: simple, object-oriented, platform independent (bytecode + JVM), secure, robust (exception handling, garbage collection), multithreaded, portable, high performance (JIT), distributed, dynamic.</p>","exam":"supp","year":2019,"branch":"CO","src":"EndSem Papers.pdf pp.17-18 (CO-203, B.Tech (CO), Feb-2019"},{"p":28,"q":"1","marks":"12","unit":0,"topic":"Six short questions","qh":"<p>Q1. Answer all the following questions: [12] (a) Compare the OOP Language and structured programming language</p><p>(b) Define constructor and give example (c) What is Abstract class? Explain (d) List the operators which are not possible to overload. (e) How to protect the data with private inheritance? (f) Explain the guidelines for defining template.</p>","sh":"<p>(a)</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Structured (procedural) language           OOP language</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Program = functions; top-down              Program = objects; bottom-up</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Data and functions separate                Data and functions bound in classes (encapsulation)</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Data exposed/global                        Data hidden by access specifiers</p><p class=\"ln i0\">No inheritance/polymorphism                Inheritance, polymorphism, abstraction</p><p>(b) A constructor is a special member function with the class name and no return type, called automatically when an object is created.</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass Point { int x, y; public: Point(int a, int b) : x(a), y(b) { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Point(&quot; &lt;&lt; x &lt;&lt; &quot;,&quot; &lt;&lt; y &lt;&lt; &quot;)\\n&quot;; } };\nint main() { Point p(3, 4); }</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Point(3,4)</p><p>(c) A class with at least one pure virtual function (virtual void f() = 0;); cannot be instantiated; serves as an interface; derived classes must implement the pure virtual functions.</p><p>(d) ::, ., .*, ?:, sizeof (also typeid); and =, (), [], -&gt; cannot be overloaded with friend functions.</p><p>(e) With private inheritance (class D : private B) all public and protected members of B become private members of D, so outside code (and classes derived from D) cannot reach the base&#x27;s members through D; only D&#x27;s own member functions can use them. The base&#x27;s data is thus protected from outside access while D still reuses it.</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass Base { protected: int secret = 42; public: int open = 7; };\nclass Derived : private Base {\npublic:\n    int reveal() { return secret + open; }          // usable inside Derived only\n};\nint main() {\n    Derived d;\n    cout &lt;&lt; d.reveal() &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n    // d.open, d.secret : error, inaccessible outside Derived\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">49</p><p>(f) Guidelines for defining templates: use the template &lt;class T&gt; (or typename T) header before the function/class; every template parameter must be used in the function signature (for function templates); a function template and class template definition (not just declaration) must be visible where it is used (put in the header); the types used must support the operations in the body (+, &lt;); the same type must be given for all arguments that share T unless explicit conversion or several parameters are used; explicit specialisation can handle special types; avoid ambiguity between a template and an ordinary overload; for class templates give the type at instantiation (Stack&lt;int&gt;).</p>","exam":"supp","year":2019,"branch":"SE","src":"CO SUPP 2019.pdf (SE203; Feb-2019; +dup. copies"},{"p":28,"q":"2","marks":"7","unit":0,"topic":"Structure of a C++ program; encapsulation and polymorphism","qh":"<p>Q2. Answer all the following questions: (a) Give the structure of a C++ Program. (b) Demonstrate encapsulation and polymorphism. [3,4]</p>","sh":"<p>(a) Structure: (1) documentation/comments; (2) preprocessor directives (#include &lt;iostream&gt;); (3) using namespace std;; (4) global declarations; (5) class declarations (data members and member function declarations);</p><p>(6) member function definitions; (7) main() that creates objects and calls functions.</p><pre>// 1. documentation section\n#include &lt;iostream&gt;               // 2. include section\nusing namespace std;              // 3. namespace\nint counter = 0;                  // 4. global declaration\nclass Student {                   // 5. class declaration\n    int roll;\npublic:\n    void set(int r);\n    void show() const;\n};\nvoid Student::set(int r) { roll = r; ++counter; }        // 6. member function definitions\nvoid Student::show() const { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Roll &quot; &lt;&lt; roll &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;; }\nint main() {                      // 7. main function\n    Student s;\n    s.set(5);\n    s.show();\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Roll 5</p><p>(b) Encapsulation: data bound with functions and hidden (private balance). Polymorphism: one name many forms (overloading, virtual functions).</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass Account {\n    double balance = 0;                                   // encapsulated data\npublic:\n    void deposit(double a) { if (a &gt; 0) balance += a; }\n    double get() const { return balance; }\n};\nclass Shape { public: virtual double area() const = 0; virtual ~Shape() {} };       // polymorphism\nclass Circle : public Shape { double r; public: Circle(double x) : r(x) {} double area() const override { return 3.14 * r * r; } };\nclass Square : public Shape { double s; public: Square(double x) : s(x) {} double area() const override { return s * s; } };\nint main() {\n    Account a; a.deposit(500); cout &lt;&lt; a.get() &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n    Shape* shapes[2] = { new Circle(1), new Square(2) };\n    for (Shape* s : shapes) { cout &lt;&lt; s-&gt;area() &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;; delete s; }\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">500</p><p class=\"ln i0\">3.14</p><p class=\"ln i0\">4</p>","exam":"supp","year":2019,"branch":"SE","src":"CO SUPP 2019.pdf (SE203; Feb-2019; +dup. copies"},{"p":28,"q":"3","marks":"7","unit":0,"topic":"Scope resolution; dynamic initialization of objects (fixed deposit)","qh":"<p>Q3. Answer all the following questions: (a) Explain about scope resolution operator? (b) Illustrate the dynamic initialization of objects for long term fixed deposit system program. [3,4]</p>","sh":"<p>(a) :: is used to access a global variable hidden by a local one, define member functions outside the class, access static members, name a base class member and use namespace members (std::cout).</p><p>(b) The principal, number of years and interest rate are read at run time and passed to the constructor, so the object is initialised dynamically; the constructor selects the rate for a long term deposit and computes the maturity value (compound interest).</p><p>Sample input:</p><pre>50000 10 7.5\n#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass FixedDeposit {\n    long principal; int years; float rate; double maturity;\npublic:\n    FixedDeposit(long p, int y, float r) : principal(p), years(y), rate(r) {   // dynamic initialization\n        maturity = p;\n        for (int i = 0; i &lt; y; ++i) maturity *= (1 + r / 100);\n    }\n    void show() const { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Principal &quot; &lt;&lt; principal &lt;&lt; &quot;, &quot; &lt;&lt; years &lt;&lt; &quot; years at &quot; &lt;&lt; rate\n                             &lt;&lt; &quot;% -&gt; maturity &quot; &lt;&lt; maturity &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;; }\n};\nint main() {\n    long p; int y; float r;\n    cin &gt;&gt; p &gt;&gt; y &gt;&gt; r;\n    FixedDeposit fd(p, y, r);\n    fd.show();\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Principal 50000, 10 years at 7.5% → maturity 103052</p>","exam":"supp","year":2019,"branch":"SE","src":"CO SUPP 2019.pdf (SE203; Feb-2019; +dup. copies"},{"p":28,"q":"4","marks":"7","unit":1,"topic":"Call by value versus address; friend classes swap","qh":"<p>Q4. Answer all the following questions: (a) Distinguish between call by value and call by address with an example (b) Write a program to exchange values between two classes using friend classes [3,4]</p>","sh":"<p>(a) Call by value passes a copy of the argument; the caller&#x27;s variable is unchanged. Call by address passes the address (a pointer) of the variable; the function changes the original through *p.</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nvoid swapValue(int a, int b)     { int t = a; a = b; b = t; }\nvoid swapAddress(int* a, int* b) { int t = *a; *a = *b; *b = t; }\nint main() {\n    int x = 1, y = 2, p = 1, q = 2;\n    swapValue(x, y);        cout &lt;&lt; x &lt;&lt; &#x27; &#x27; &lt;&lt; y &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n    swapAddress(&amp;p, &amp;q);    cout &lt;&lt; p &lt;&lt; &#x27; &#x27; &lt;&lt; q &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">1 2</p><p class=\"ln i0\">2 1</p><p>(b) Each class is made a friend of the other, so X::exchange and Y::exchange can reach the other&#x27;s private data.</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass Y;\nclass X {\n    int a;\npublic:\n    X(int v) : a(v) {}\n    void exchange(Y&amp; y);\n    int get() const { return a; }\n};\nclass Y {\n    int b;\npublic:\n    Y(int v) : b(v) {}\n    friend class X;                      // X is a friend class of Y\n    int get() const { return b; }\n};\nvoid X::exchange(Y&amp; y) { int t = a; a = y.b; y.b = t; }\nint main() {\n    X x(10); Y y(20);\n    cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Before: &quot; &lt;&lt; x.get() &lt;&lt; &#x27; &#x27; &lt;&lt; y.get() &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n    x.exchange(y);\n    cout &lt;&lt; &quot;After:  &quot; &lt;&lt; x.get() &lt;&lt; &#x27; &#x27; &lt;&lt; y.get() &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Before: 10 20</p><p class=\"ln i0\">After:  20 10</p>","exam":"supp","year":2019,"branch":"SE","src":"CO SUPP 2019.pdf (SE203; Feb-2019; +dup. copies"},{"p":28,"q":"5","marks":"7","unit":1,"topic":"Class conversions; derived versus base constructor","qh":"<p>Q5. Answer all the following questions: (a) Explain two ways of converting an object of one class to an object of another? (b) Differentiate between derived constructor and base constructor. [3,4]</p>","sh":"<p>(a) (1) A conversion constructor in the destination class: Minutes(const Time&amp; t). (2) A conversion operator in the</p><pre>source class: Time::operator Minutes() const.\n#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass Time;\nclass Minutes {\n    int m;\npublic:\n    Minutes(int v = 0) : m(v) {}\n    Minutes(const Time&amp; t);                // way 1: constructor in destination class\n    void show() const { cout &lt;&lt; m &lt;&lt; &quot; minutes\\n&quot;; }\n};\nclass Time {\n    int h, mn;\npublic:\n    Time(int a, int b) : h(a), mn(b) {}\n    int total() const { return h * 60 + mn; }\n};\nMinutes::Minutes(const Time&amp; t) : m(t.total()) {}\nclass Money;\nclass Rupees { public: int r; Rupees(int x) : r(x) {} };\nclass Paise {\npublic:\n    int p;\n    Paise(int x) : p(x) {}\n    Paise(const Rupees&amp; r) : p(r.r * 100) {}\n    operator Rupees() const { return Rupees(p / 100); }   // way 2: conversion operator in source class\n};\nint main() {\n    Time t(1, 45);\n    Minutes m(t);              // way 1\n    m.show();\n    Paise ps(250);\n    Rupees rs = ps;            // way 2\n    cout &lt;&lt; rs.r &lt;&lt; &quot; rupees\\n&quot;;\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">105 minutes</p><p class=\"ln i0\">2 rupees</p><p>(b)</p><pre>Base class constructor                        Derived class constructor\nInitialises the base part of the object      Initialises the derived part (and calls the base constructor)\nExecuted first                               Executed after the base constructor(s)\nCalled implicitly (default) or through the   Declares the base arguments in its initializer list\nderived initializer list\nDestroyed last (destructor runs last)        Its destructor runs first\n#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass Base { public: Base(int x) { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Base(&quot; &lt;&lt; x &lt;&lt; &quot;)\\n&quot;; } };\nclass Derived : public Base { public: Derived(int x, int y) : Base(x) { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Derived(&quot; &lt;&lt; y &lt;&lt; &quot;)\\n&quot;; } };\nint main() { Derived d(1, 2); }</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Base(1)</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Derived(2)</p>","exam":"supp","year":2019,"branch":"SE","src":"CO SUPP 2019.pdf (SE203; Feb-2019; +dup. copies"},{"p":28,"q":"6","marks":"7","unit":1,"topic":"Pure virtual, ordinary virtual and non-virtual inheritance behaviour","qh":"<p>Q6. Describe the three different inheritance behaviors achieved through the use of pure virtual, ordinary virtual and non virtual functions? [7]</p>","sh":"<pre>Pure virtual (= 0)        Interface inheritance only: the base gives the signature, the derived class MUST\n                          supply the implementation (class is abstract).\nOrdinary virtual          Interface + default implementation: the derived class MAY override; call through a\n                          base pointer resolves at run time.\nNon-virtual               Interface + mandatory implementation: derived classes should not change it; the\n                          call is bound at compile time from the static type.\n#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass Account {\npublic:\n    virtual double fee() const = 0;                              // pure virtual: each type must define\n    virtual void statement() const { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Standard statement\\n&quot;; }   // ordinary virtual: default behaviour\n    void id() const { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Account id printed by base\\n&quot;; }          // non-virtual: fixed behaviour\n    virtual ~Account() {}\n};\nclass Savings : public Account {\npublic:\n    double fee() const override { return 0; }\n};\nclass Current : public Account {\npublic:\n    double fee() const override { return 25; }\n    void statement() const override { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Detailed current-account statement\\n&quot;; }\n};\nint main() {\n    Account* a[2] = { new Savings, new Current };\n    for (Account* p : a) { p-&gt;id(); p-&gt;statement(); cout &lt;&lt; &quot;fee &quot; &lt;&lt; p-&gt;fee() &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;; delete p; }\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Account id printed by base</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Standard statement</p><p class=\"ln i0\">fee 0</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Account id printed by base</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Detailed current-account statement</p><p class=\"ln i0\">fee 25</p>","exam":"supp","year":2019,"branch":"SE","src":"CO SUPP 2019.pdf (SE203; Feb-2019; +dup. copies"},{"p":28,"q":"7","marks":"7","unit":1,"topic":"Class templates for addition; sequential and random access files","qh":"<p>Q7. Answer all the following questions: (a) Write a C++ Program to add two integers, two floats and two complex numbers using class templates. (b) Explain about the sequential and random access file operations [3,4]</p>","sh":"<p>(a) Adder&lt;T&gt; stores two values of type T and adds them; for Complex the + operator is overloaded.</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nstruct Complex {\n    double re, im;\n    Complex operator+(const Complex&amp; o) const { return {re + o.re, im + o.im}; }\n};\nostream&amp; operator&lt;&lt;(ostream&amp; os, const Complex&amp; c) { return os &lt;&lt; c.re &lt;&lt; &quot; + &quot; &lt;&lt; c.im &lt;&lt; &quot;i&quot;; }\ntemplate &lt;class T&gt;\nclass Adder {\n    T a, b;\npublic:\n    Adder(T x, T y) : a(x), b(y) {}\n    T add() const { return a + b; }\n};\nint main() {\n    Adder&lt;int&gt; i(3, 4);\n    Adder&lt;float&gt; f(1.5f, 2.25f);\n    Adder&lt;Complex&gt; c({1, 2}, {3, 4});\n    cout &lt;&lt; i.add() &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27; &lt;&lt; f.add() &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27; &lt;&lt; c.add() &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">7</p><p class=\"ln i0\">3.75</p><p class=\"ln i0\">4 + 6i</p><p>(b) Sequential access reads/writes a file from the beginning item by item in order (&gt;&gt;, &lt;&lt;, getline, get, put); to reach record n all earlier ones are read. Random access moves the file pointer directly to any position with seekg(pos, origin) (get/read pointer) and seekp(pos, origin) (put/write pointer) and reports it with tellg()/tellp(); origins ios::beg, ios::cur, ios::end; used for fixed-size records opened in binary mode.</p><pre>#include &lt;fstream&gt;\n#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nint main() {\n    {\n        ofstream out(&quot;nums.dat&quot;, ios::binary);              // sequential write\n        for (int i = 1; i &lt;= 5; ++i) { int v = i * 10; out.write((char*)&amp;v, sizeof(v)); }\n    }\n    ifstream in(&quot;nums.dat&quot;, ios::binary);\n    int v;\n    in.seekg(3 * sizeof(int), ios::beg);                    // random access: jump to the 4th record\n    in.read((char*)&amp;v, sizeof(v));\n    cout &lt;&lt; &quot;record 4 = &quot; &lt;&lt; v &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n    in.seekg(0, ios::beg);                                  // back to start, read sequentially\n    while (in.read((char*)&amp;v, sizeof(v))) cout &lt;&lt; v &lt;&lt; &#x27; &#x27;;\n    cout &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">record 4 = 40</p><p class=\"ln i0\">10 20 30 40 50</p>","exam":"supp","year":2019,"branch":"SE","src":"CO SUPP 2019.pdf (SE203; Feb-2019; +dup. copies"},{"p":29,"q":"1","marks":"12","unit":1,"topic":"Six short questions (2 each)","qh":"<p>Q1. Answer the following 2x6 a) Write a program to find the length of a string using operator overloading b) Differentiate between Java application vs applet c) Differentiate between Call by value vs call by reference d) Differentiate between Class and structure e) What is dynamic and static binding in C++? f) What is the role of :: in inheritance?</p>","sh":"<p>a) A MyString class overloads the unary operator! ... here simply operator~ is used to return the length (any operator is accepted; operator int() is another way).</p><pre>#include &lt;cstring&gt;\n#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass MyString {\n    char s[50];\npublic:\n    MyString(const char* t) { strcpy(s, t); }\n    int operator~() const {                  // overloaded unary ~ returns the length\n        int n = 0;\n        while (s[n]) ++n;\n        return n;\n    }\n};\nint main() {\n    MyString a(&quot;Object Oriented&quot;);\n    cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Length = &quot; &lt;&lt; ~a &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Length = 15</p><p>b) A Java application is a standalone program with main() run by java; it can access local files and the system. An applet extends Applet/JApplet, has no main() (life-cycle methods init, start, paint, stop, destroy), is embedded in a web page, runs in a browser/appletviewer and is limited by the sandbox.</p><p>c) Call by value: a copy of the argument is passed; the caller&#x27;s variable cannot change. Call by reference: the parameter is an alias (int &amp;a) of the caller&#x27;s variable; changes are visible; no copy.</p><p>d) Class: default private members; data + functions, inheritance, constructors. Structure: default public (C++ struct may also have functions); in C only data.</p><p>e) Static (early) binding is resolved at compile time (ordinary/overloaded functions); dynamic (late) binding is resolved at run time through virtual functions and the vtable.</p><p>f) :: names the class of a member: Base::show() calls the base version hidden by the derived class, resolves ambiguity between multiple bases, and defines members outside the class.</p>","exam":"supp","year":2019,"branch":"IT","src":"EndSem Papers.pdf p.21 (IT203; Feb-2019"},{"p":29,"q":"2","marks":"7","unit":1,"topic":"Operator overloading methods; friend function and class","qh":"<p>Q2. a) Explain the methods to overload operators. Why is it required? Overload the &lt;&lt; and &gt;&gt; operators. [3] b) What is a friend function? Explain the concept of friend function and friend class in detail with an example. Also discuss inheritance in case of a friend function giving reasons for your answer. [4]</p>","sh":"<p>These are the same questions as Q2(a) and Q2(b) of the IT Supplementary paper of Feb-2020 (IT 203); the answers are repeated here in brief. (a) Operators are overloaded either as member functions (left operand is the object; a + b is a.operator+(b)) or as friend/non-member functions (all operands are parameters; necessary for cout &lt;&lt; obj and cin &gt;&gt; obj since the left operand is a stream). Overloading is required to give user-defined types natural operator syntax.</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass Point {\n    int x, y;\npublic:\n    Point(int a = 0, int b = 0) : x(a), y(b) {}\n    friend ostream&amp; operator&lt;&lt;(ostream&amp; os, const Point&amp; p) { return os &lt;&lt; &quot;(&quot; &lt;&lt; p.x &lt;&lt; &quot;, &quot; &lt;&lt; p.y &lt;&lt; &quot;)&quot;; }\n    friend istream&amp; operator&gt;&gt;(istream&amp; is, Point&amp; p) { return is &gt;&gt; p.x &gt;&gt; p.y; }\n};\nint main() { Point p(3, 4); cout &lt;&lt; p &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;; }</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">(3, 4)</p><p>(b) A friend function is a non-member function allowed to use a class&#x27;s private members; a friend class lets all its member functions do so. Friendship is granted by the class itself, is not mutual and is not inherited: a friend of the base class is not a friend of a derived class, because friendship is a specific grant of trust and would otherwise break encapsulation silently.</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass Base { int secret = 5; friend void spy(const Base&amp;); friend class Agent; };\nclass Derived : public Base { int own = 8; };\nvoid spy(const Base&amp; b) { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;friend of Base reads &quot; &lt;&lt; b.secret &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;; }\nclass Agent { public: int peek(const Base&amp; b) { return b.secret; } };\nint main() { Derived d; spy(d); cout &lt;&lt; Agent().peek(d) &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;; }   // only the Base part of Derived is reachable</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">friend of Base reads 5</p><p class=\"ln i0\">5</p>","exam":"supp","year":2019,"branch":"IT","src":"EndSem Papers.pdf p.21 (IT203; Feb-2019"},{"p":29,"q":"3","marks":"7","unit":2,"topic":"Student class for 10 students; applet","qh":"<p>Q3. a) Write a java program to declare a class &#x27;student&#x27;, consisting of data members roll_no and stud_name. Write the member functions accept() to accept and display() to display the data for 10 students. [3] b) What is java applet? Give its properties in detail. Give an example of the same by creating a basic applet which will print the string &quot;Hello User&quot; on the screen. [4]</p>","sh":"<p>Same as Q3 of the IT Supplementary paper of Feb-2020. (a)</p><pre>import java.util.Scanner;\nclass student {\n    int roll_no;\n    String stud_name;\n    void accept(Scanner sc) { roll_no = sc.nextInt(); stud_name = sc.next(); }\n    void display() { System.out.println(roll_no + &quot;  &quot; + stud_name); }\n}\npublic class Ten {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);\n        student[] s = new student[10];\n        for (int i = 0; i &lt; 10; i++) { s[i] = new student(); s[i].accept(sc); }\n        for (int i = 0; i &lt; 10; i++) s[i].display();\n    }\n}</pre><p>Output: for the ten input pairs 1 Asha ... 10 Zoya, ten lines 1 Asha, ..., 10 Zoya.</p><p>(b) An applet is a Java program embedded in a web page that runs in the browser&#x27;s JVM (or appletviewer) inside a sandbox. Properties: extends Applet/JApplet; no main(); life cycle init(), start(), paint(), stop(), destroy(); no local file access; runs on the client; uses the AWT/Swing for drawing; parameters via &lt;param&gt;.</p><pre>import java.applet.Applet;\nimport java.awt.Graphics;\npublic class HelloUser extends Applet {\n    public void paint(Graphics g) { g.drawString(&quot;Hello User&quot;, 50, 50); }\n}\n/* &lt;applet code=&quot;HelloUser.class&quot; width=&quot;300&quot; height=&quot;150&quot;&gt;&lt;/applet&gt; */</pre><p>Output: the applet window shows Hello User.</p>","exam":"supp","year":2019,"branch":"IT","src":"EndSem Papers.pdf p.21 (IT203; Feb-2019"},{"p":29,"q":"4","marks":"7","unit":2,"topic":"Java is a pure OO language?; virtual base class","qh":"<p>Q4. Answer the following with examples: a) &quot;Java is a pure object oriented language&quot;. Explain [3] b) Write a program to show the use of virtual base class. [4]</p>","sh":"<p>a) A language is purely object oriented if everything is an object and every operation is a message to an object. Java is &quot;almost pure&quot;: all code lives inside classes, every class derives from Object, and it provides encapsulation, inheritance, polymorphism and abstraction. But it is not 100% pure, because it has eight primitive types (int, char, ...) that are not objects, static members and methods that work without objects, and operators on primitives. (Wrapper classes Integer etc. and autoboxing bridge the gap.) So the statement is true for the structure of programs, and false in the strict sense.</p><pre>public class Pure {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {          // even main() is inside a class\n        Integer boxed = 5;                              // wrapper object for a primitive\n        int primitive = 5;                              // primitive: not an object\n        System.out.println(boxed.equals(primitive) + &quot; &quot; + boxed.getClass().getName());\n    }\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">true java.lang.Integer</p><p>b)</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass Person { public: int age = 21; Person() { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Person constructed once\\n&quot;; } };\nclass Student : virtual public Person {};\nclass Employee : virtual public Person {};\nclass Intern : public Student, public Employee {};\nint main() { Intern i; cout &lt;&lt; i.age &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;; }</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Person constructed once</p><p class=\"ln i0\">21</p>","exam":"supp","year":2019,"branch":"IT","src":"EndSem Papers.pdf p.21 (IT203; Feb-2019"},{"p":29,"q":"5","marks":"7","unit":0,"topic":"Nested class; run-time polymorphism","qh":"<p>Q5. Answer the following with examples. a) What is the role of a nested class? How to use it? [3] b) How does runtime polymorphism occur in C++? [4]</p>","sh":"<p>(Same as Q5 of the IT Supplementary paper of Feb-2020.) a) A nested class is declared inside another class to keep a helper type local to it; it is used as Outer::Inner obj; (or only inside Outer if private). b) Run-time polymorphism: a base-class function is declared virtual; derived classes override it; a base pointer/reference call is resolved at run time through the vtable (each object has a vptr) to the override of the real object.</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass Outer {\npublic:\n    class Inner { public: void hi() { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Inner of Outer\\n&quot;; } };\n};\nclass Animal { public: virtual void speak() const { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;...\\n&quot;; } virtual ~Animal() {} };\nclass Cat : public Animal { public: void speak() const override { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Meow\\n&quot;; } };\nclass Dog : public Animal { public: void speak() const override { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Woof\\n&quot;; } };\nint main() {\n    Outer::Inner i; i.hi();\n    Animal* a[2] = { new Cat, new Dog };\n    for (Animal* p : a) { p-&gt;speak(); delete p; }\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Inner of Outer</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Meow</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Woof</p>","exam":"supp","year":2019,"branch":"IT","src":"EndSem Papers.pdf p.21 (IT203; Feb-2019"},{"p":29,"q":"6","marks":"7","unit":1,"topic":"this keyword; exception handling","qh":"<p>Q6 a) Write a program to show the role of this keyword in C++. Explain. [3] b) Explain exception handling in C++. How is an exception rethrown? Also explain the concept of multiple catch and catch all. [4]</p>","sh":"<p>(Same as Q6 of the IT Supplementary paper of Feb-2020.) a) this points to the current object: it distinguishes a member from a same-named parameter and lets a function return the object.</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass Box {\n    int w;\npublic:\n    Box(int w) { this-&gt;w = w; }\n    Box&amp; widen(int d) { this-&gt;w += d; return *this; }\n    void show() const { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;w = &quot; &lt;&lt; w &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;; }\n};\nint main() { Box b(2); b.widen(3).widen(4).show(); }</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">w = 9</p><p>b) try guards code; throw raises an exception; catch handles it (first matching handler); several catch blocks can follow one try; catch (...) catches any type; throw; inside a handler rethrows the same exception to the outer handler.</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nvoid f(int k) {\n    try { if (k == 1) throw 5; if (k == 2) throw &#x27;c&#x27;; throw 1.5; }\n    catch (int e) { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;f: int, rethrowing\\n&quot;; throw; }\n    catch (char e) { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;f: char handled\\n&quot;; }\n}\nint main() {\n    for (int k = 1; k &lt;= 3; ++k) {\n        try { f(k); } catch (int e) { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;main: int &quot; &lt;&lt; e &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;; } catch (...) { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;main: catch-all\\n&quot;; }\n    }\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">f: int, rethrowing</p><p class=\"ln i0\">main: int 5</p><p class=\"ln i0\">f: char handled</p><p class=\"ln i0\">main: catch-all</p>","exam":"supp","year":2019,"branch":"IT","src":"EndSem Papers.pdf p.21 (IT203; Feb-2019"},{"p":29,"q":"7","marks":"7","unit":1,"topic":"Templates; initialiser list in multilevel inheritance","qh":"<p>Q7 a) Explain templates and its types with detailed example. [3] b) What is initialiser list and discuss its role in constructor calls in multilevel inheritance in C++ with examples. [4]</p>","sh":"<p>(Same as Q7 of the IT Supplementary paper of Feb-2020.) a) Templates are generic blueprints; types: function templates and class templates.</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\ntemplate &lt;class T&gt; T smaller(T a, T b) { return a &lt; b ? a : b; }\ntemplate &lt;class T&gt; class Triple { T a, b, c; public: Triple(T x, T y, T z) : a(x), b(y), c(z) {} T sum() const { return a + b + c; } };\nint main() { cout &lt;&lt; smaller(4, 9) &lt;&lt; &#x27; &#x27; &lt;&lt; smaller(2.5, 1.5) &lt;&lt; &#x27; &#x27; &lt;&lt; Triple&lt;int&gt;(1, 2, 3).sum() &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;; }</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">4 1.5 6</p><p>b) The initialiser list (: Base(args), member(value)) initialises bases and members before the body; in multilevel inheritance each class passes arguments to its immediate base.</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass A { public: A(int a) { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;A(&quot; &lt;&lt; a &lt;&lt; &quot;)\\n&quot;; } };\nclass B : public A { public: B(int a, int b) : A(a) { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;B(&quot; &lt;&lt; b &lt;&lt; &quot;)\\n&quot;; } };\nclass C : public B { public: C(int a, int b, int c) : B(a, b) { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;C(&quot; &lt;&lt; c &lt;&lt; &quot;)\\n&quot;; } };\nint main() { C obj(1, 2, 3); }</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">A(1)</p><p class=\"ln i0\">B(2)</p><p class=\"ln i0\">C(3)</p>","exam":"supp","year":2019,"branch":"IT","src":"EndSem Papers.pdf p.21 (IT203; Feb-2019"},{"p":30,"q":"1","marks":"14","unit":1,"topic":"Seven short questions (2 each)","qh":"<p>Q1. Answer the following with suitable codes/examples 2x7 a) What is namespace feature? Explain with example. b) What are the steps of java code execution? Explain each component. c) Write a java code for displaying &quot;Hello World&quot; string inside a rectangle using applet. Also show the commands of execution. d) What is the difference between inheritance in Java and C++. e) A destructor can be virtual. Explain and justify its need. f) Can there exist multiple virtual functions inside an abstract class with only one pure virtual function. Comment and explain using example. g) What is &#x27;this&#x27; pointer? Explain atleast two scenarios in which &#x27;this&#x27; is used.</p>","sh":"<p>a) A namespace is a named scope that groups identifiers (functions, classes, variables) so that equal names in different libraries do not clash. Declared with namespace name { ... }, accessed as name::member, with using namespace name; or using name::member;. The standard library lives in std.</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nnamespace Geo { double area(double r) { return 3.14 * r * r; } }\nnamespace Sq  { double area(double s) { return s * s; } }\nint main() {\n    cout &lt;&lt; Geo::area(2) &lt;&lt; &#x27; &#x27; &lt;&lt; Sq::area(2) &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;     // same name, different namespaces\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">12.56 4</p><p>b) Steps: (1) write Hello.java; (2) compile with javac Hello.java (compiler checks syntax, produces Hello.class bytecode); (3) run with java Hello: the class loader loads the class; (4) the bytecode verifier checks it; (5) the execution engine (interpreter plus JIT compiler) runs it on the JVM using the runtime data areas (method area, heap, stacks); (6) output is produced.</p><p>c) The applet draws a rectangle and writes the text inside it. Commands: javac HelloWorld.java, then appletviewer HelloWorld.java (the &lt;applet&gt; tag is in a comment in the source) or run it from an HTML page.</p><pre>import java.applet.Applet;\nimport java.awt.Graphics;\n/* &lt;applet code=&quot;HelloWorld.class&quot; width=&quot;300&quot; height=&quot;120&quot;&gt;&lt;/applet&gt; */\npublic class HelloWorld extends Applet {\n    public void paint(Graphics g) {\n        g.drawRect(40, 30, 200, 60);               // the rectangle\n        g.drawString(&quot;Hello World&quot;, 100, 65);      // text inside it\n    }\n}</pre><p>Output: an applet window with a rectangle containing Hello World.</p><p>d) Java: a class can extend only one class (single inheritance of classes); multiple inheritance of type through interfaces; every class extends Object; keyword extends/implements; super refers to the parent; no virtual/private-protected inheritance modes; final stops inheritance. C++: multiple inheritance of classes is allowed (with diamond problem solved by virtual base classes); inheritance modes public/protected/private; syntax class D : public B; no universal base class; functions are non-virtual unless declared virtual.</p><p>e) Yes, a destructor may be virtual, and a base class with virtual functions should have a virtual destructor. Need: when a derived object is deleted through a base pointer (delete basePtr;), a non-virtual destructor calls only the base destructor, so the derived part is never destroyed (undefined behaviour, leak). A virtual destructor calls the derived destructor first and then the base destructor.</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass B { public: virtual ~B() { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;~B\\n&quot;; } };\nclass D : public B { public: ~D() { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;~D\\n&quot;; } };\nint main() { B* p = new D; delete p; }</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">~D</p><p class=\"ln i0\">~B</p><p>f) Yes. An abstract class only needs one pure virtual function; it may also have any number of ordinary virtual functions (with bodies) and non-virtual functions, data members and constructors. The derived class must override the pure virtual function; the ordinary virtual ones may be overridden optionally.</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass Shape {\npublic:\n    virtual double area() const = 0;                          // the only pure virtual function\n    virtual void describe() const { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;I am a shape\\n&quot;; }   // ordinary virtual\n    virtual void colour() const { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;no colour\\n&quot;; }        // ordinary virtual\n    virtual ~Shape() {}\n};\nclass Sq : public Shape {\n    double s;\npublic:\n    Sq(double x) : s(x) {}\n    double area() const override { return s * s; }\n    void colour() const override { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;red\\n&quot;; }\n};\nint main() {\n    Sq q(3);\n    Shape* p = &amp;q;\n    p-&gt;describe();          // inherited ordinary virtual function\n    p-&gt;colour();            // overridden\n    cout &lt;&lt; p-&gt;area() &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">I am a shape</p><p class=\"ln i0\">red</p><p class=\"ln i0\">9</p><p>g) this is a pointer to the object on which a non-static member function was called. Scenarios: (1) when a parameter has the same name as a data member (this-&gt;x = x;); (2) to return the current object for chaining (return *this;); also to pass the object to another function or compare addresses (this == &amp;other).</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass P {\n    int x;\npublic:\n    P(int x) { this-&gt;x = x; }                       // scenario 1\n    P&amp; add(int n) { x += n; return *this; }         // scenario 2: chaining\n    void show() const { cout &lt;&lt; x &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;; }\n};\nint main() { P p(1); p.add(2).add(3).show(); }</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">6</p>","exam":"end","year":2018,"branch":"IT","src":"EndSem Papers.pdf pp.8-11 (IT203, Nov/Dec-2018"},{"p":30,"q":"2","marks":"9","unit":1,"topic":"Template inheritance; operator new; initialiser list","qh":"<p>Q2. Answer with example 3x3 a) What is inheritance in class template? Explain with an example of each scenario in detail. b) What are the differences between &quot;operator new&quot; and the &quot;new&quot; operator? c) What is the role of initialiser list in case of inheritance (C++)? Explain with example w.r.t constructor call to base class.</p>","sh":"<p>a) A class template can take part in inheritance in three scenarios: (1) a class template derived from a class template (template&lt;class T&gt; class D : public B&lt;T&gt;); (2) a normal class derived from a particular instantiation (class D : public B&lt;int&gt;); (3) a class template derived from a normal (non-template) class.</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass Plain { public: void hello() { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Plain base\\n&quot;; } };\ntemplate &lt;class T&gt; class Base { protected: T v; public: Base(T x) : v(x) {} T get() const { return v; } };\ntemplate &lt;class T&gt; class D1 : public Base&lt;T&gt; {            // (1) template from template\npublic: D1(T x) : Base&lt;T&gt;(x) {} T twice() const { return this-&gt;v * 2; } };\nclass D2 : public Base&lt;int&gt; { public: D2(int x) : Base&lt;int&gt;(x) {} };       // (2) normal class from Base&lt;int&gt;\ntemplate &lt;class T&gt; class D3 : public Plain { T t; public: D3(T x) : t(x) {} T val() const { return t; } };  // (3)\nint main() {\n    D1&lt;double&gt; a(1.5); D2 b(7); D3&lt;char&gt; c(&#x27;z&#x27;);\n    cout &lt;&lt; a.twice() &lt;&lt; &#x27; &#x27; &lt;&lt; b.get() &lt;&lt; &#x27; &#x27; &lt;&lt; c.val() &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n    c.hello();\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">3 7 z</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Plain base</p><p>b) The new operator is the keyword used in new T(args): it calls operator new to allocate memory and then the constructor; it cannot be overloaded. operator new is the function void* operator new(size_t) that only allocates raw memory (a replaceable allocation function) and can be overloaded globally or per class; it calls no constructor.</p><p>c) The initialiser list Derived(int a, int b) : Base(a), member(b) calls the base-class constructor with the needed arguments before the derived constructor body runs. It is the only way to call a base constructor that takes arguments (the base is built first), and to initialise const/reference members.</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass Base { int a; public: Base(int x) : a(x) { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Base(&quot; &lt;&lt; a &lt;&lt; &quot;)\\n&quot;; } };\nclass Derived : public Base { int b; public: Derived(int x, int y) : Base(x), b(y) { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Derived(&quot; &lt;&lt; b &lt;&lt; &quot;)\\n&quot;; } };\nint main() { Derived d(1, 2); }</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Base(1)</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Derived(2)</p>","exam":"end","year":2018,"branch":"IT","src":"EndSem Papers.pdf pp.8-11 (IT203, Nov/Dec-2018"},{"p":30,"q":"3","marks":"9","unit":1,"topic":"Pointers versus references; user-defined age exception; macros and inline","qh":"<p>Q3. Answer the following with example: 3x3 a) Point out atleast 3 differences between pointers and references. b) Write a C++ program for user defined exception class for checking the age of student. That is if the student enters his age&gt;25 or less than 15 then the user defined exception should be raised and a warning should be printed- &quot;please check the age being entered&quot;. c) Discuss advantages and disadvantages of using Macros and inline functions.</p>","sh":"<p>a)</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Pointer                                    Reference</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Holds an address; can be NULL              Alias of an existing object; cannot be null Can be reassigned to point elsewhere       Bound once at initialisation; cannot be re-seated Needs * to dereference, → for members     Used like the object itself (no dereference syntax)</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Declared int* p;  may be uninitialised     Declared int&amp; r = x;  must be initialised Pointer arithmetic possible                No reference arithmetic</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Has its own address and size               Shares the address of the referred object</p><p>b)</p><p>Sample input:</p><pre>12\n#include &lt;exception&gt;\n#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass AgeException : public exception {\npublic:\n    const char* what() const noexcept override { return &quot;please check the age being entered&quot;; }\n};\nint main() {\n    int age;\n    cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Enter age: &quot;;\n    cin &gt;&gt; age;\n    try {\n        if (age &gt; 25 || age &lt; 15) throw AgeException();\n        cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Age &quot; &lt;&lt; age &lt;&lt; &quot; is valid\\n&quot;;\n    } catch (const AgeException&amp; e) {\n        cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Warning: &quot; &lt;&lt; e.what() &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n    }\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Enter age: Warning: please check the age being entered</p><p>c) Macros: advantages: no function-call overhead, work with any type, useful for constants and conditional compilation. Disadvantages: no type checking, arguments may be evaluated several times (SQ(i++)), precedence errors (SQ(a+b)), cannot be debugged, no scope, cannot be member functions. Inline functions: advantages: type safe, arguments evaluated once, respect scope, can be members, debuggable, same speed benefit as macros. Disadvantages: code growth (bloat) if used widely, only a request to the compiler (may be ignored), recursion/loops usually not inlined, changes force recompilation of callers.</p>","exam":"end","year":2018,"branch":"IT","src":"EndSem Papers.pdf pp.8-11 (IT203, Nov/Dec-2018"},{"p":30,"q":"4","marks":"9","unit":1,"topic":"Virtual and inline; overloading and overriding; specialization","qh":"<p>Q4. Answer the following with example: 3x3 a) Can virtual functions be inline? Explain b) Explain function overloading and overriding. c) What is specialization in case of function template overloading?</p>","sh":"<p>a) A virtual function may be declared inline (and a function defined inside the class body is implicitly inline), but when it is called through a base pointer/reference the call is resolved at run time via the vtable, so the compiler cannot inline it. It can be inlined only when the compiler knows the exact type at compile time (a call on an object, not through a pointer). So inline is syntactically allowed but ineffective for polymorphic calls.</p><p>b) Overloading: same name, different parameter lists, usually in one scope, resolved at compile time. Overriding: a derived class redefines a base virtual function with the same signature, resolved at run time.</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass A {\npublic:\n    void f(int x)    { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;f(int)\\n&quot;; }          // overloaded pair\n    void f(double x) { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;f(double)\\n&quot;; }\n    virtual void g() { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;A::g\\n&quot;; }\n    virtual ~A() {}\n};\nclass B : public A { public: void g() override { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;B::g (overridden)\\n&quot;; } };\nint main() { B b; A* p = &amp;b; b.f(1); b.f(1.5); p-&gt;g(); }</pre><p>Output:</p><pre>f(int)\nf(double)\nB::g (overridden)</pre><p>c) Template specialization gives a special implementation of a function template for a particular type (an explicit specialisation template &lt;&gt; ...), while the general template serves all other types. At a call the compiler prefers an exact non-template overload, then the specialisation, then the generic template.</p><pre>#include &lt;cstring&gt;\n#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\ntemplate &lt;class T&gt; T bigger(T a, T b) { return a &gt; b ? a : b; }                       // general\ntemplate &lt;&gt; const char* bigger&lt;const char*&gt;(const char* a, const char* b) {           // specialisation\n    return strcmp(a, b) &gt; 0 ? a : b;\n}\nint main() { cout &lt;&lt; bigger(3, 8) &lt;&lt; &#x27; &#x27; &lt;&lt; bigger(&quot;apple&quot;, &quot;pear&quot;) &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;; }</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">8 pear</p>","exam":"end","year":2018,"branch":"IT","src":"EndSem Papers.pdf pp.8-11 (IT203, Nov/Dec-2018"},{"p":30,"q":"5","marks":"8","unit":1,"topic":"Predict the output (four programs)","qh":"<p>Q5. Predict the output with the explanation 2x4 i) class Test { public: Test(); }; Test::Test() { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Constructor Called \\n&quot;; } int main() { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Start \\n&quot;; Test t1(); cout &lt;&lt; &quot;End \\n&quot;; return 0; } ii) class X { public: void foo() { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;X::f &quot;; } virtual void go() { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;X::g &quot;; } }; class Y : public X { public: virtual void foo() { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Y::f &quot;; } virtual void go() { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Y::g &quot;; } }; int main() { X x; Y y; X &amp;rx = y; rx.foo(); rx.go(); return 0; } iii) class gfg { ~gfg() { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;I love coding&quot;; } public: gfg() { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;I am at your service&quot;; } }; int main() { gfg mygfg; } iv) class Base { public: void f() { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Base\\n&quot;; } }; class Derived : public Base { public: void f() { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Derived\\n&quot;; } }; main() { Derived obj; obj.Base::f(); }</p>","sh":"<p>i) Output: Start then End. The statement Test t1(); is not an object definition: it declares a function t1 that takes no arguments and returns Test (the &quot;most vexing parse&quot;), so no object is created and the constructor is never called.</p><p class=\"ln i0\">(With Test t1; the output would be Start, Constructor Called, End.)</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Start</p><p class=\"ln i0\">End</p><p>ii) Output: X::f Y::g. rx is an X&amp; bound to a Y object. foo() is non-virtual in X (the virtual in Y does not change the already bound call through X), so it is resolved statically to X::foo. go() is virtual in X, so the call is resolved at run time to Y::go.</p><pre>X::f Y::g</pre><p>iii) Compile-time error: the destructor ~gfg() is in the private section (before public:). The object mygfg must be destroyed at the end of main, which needs an accessible destructor, so the compiler reports &quot;variable of type gfg has private destructor&quot;. (If the destructor were public the output would be I am at your serviceI love coding.)</p><p>iv) Output: Base. Derived::f hides Base::f, but obj.Base::f() uses the scope resolution operator to call the base-class version explicitly.</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Base</p>","exam":"end","year":2018,"branch":"IT","src":"EndSem Papers.pdf pp.8-11 (IT203, Nov/Dec-2018"},{"p":31,"q":"1","marks":"8","unit":0,"topic":"OOP features; Rational numbers; operators","qh":"<p>1. (a) Explain the important features of object oriented programming. 2 (b) Write a C++ program as follows to perform arithmetic operations addition and subtraction on Rational numbers of type x/y, where x and y are integers. Give a sample output. 4 (c) Operators which can&#x27;t be overloaded and which can&#x27;t be overloaded using friend function. 2</p>","sh":"<p>(a) Features: classes and objects; encapsulation (data and functions bound, hidden by access specifiers); abstraction; inheritance (reuse, hierarchy); polymorphism (overloading, virtual functions); dynamic binding; message passing.</p><p>(b) A Rational stores numerator and denominator; + and - use a common denominator and the result is reduced with the greatest common divisor.</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass Rational {\n    int x, y;                                   // x/y\n    static int gcd(int a, int b) { return b == 0 ? (a &lt; 0 ? -a : a) : gcd(b, a % b); }\n    void reduce() { int g = gcd(x, y); if (g) { x /= g; y /= g; } if (y &lt; 0) { x = -x; y = -y; } }\npublic:\n    Rational(int a = 0, int b = 1) : x(a), y(b) { reduce(); }\n    Rational operator+(const Rational&amp; r) const { return Rational(x * r.y + r.x * y, y * r.y); }\n    Rational operator-(const Rational&amp; r) const { return Rational(x * r.y - r.x * y, y * r.y); }\n    void show() const { cout &lt;&lt; x &lt;&lt; &#x27;/&#x27; &lt;&lt; y &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;; }\n};\nint main() {\n    Rational a(1, 2), b(1, 3);\n    cout &lt;&lt; &quot;a = &quot;; a.show();\n    cout &lt;&lt; &quot;b = &quot;; b.show();\n    cout &lt;&lt; &quot;a + b = &quot;; (a + b).show();\n    cout &lt;&lt; &quot;a - b = &quot;; (a - b).show();\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">a = 1/2</p><p class=\"ln i0\">b = 1/3</p><p class=\"ln i0\">a + b = 5/6</p><p class=\"ln i0\">a - b = 1/6</p><p>(c) Cannot be overloaded at all: ::, ., .*, ?:, sizeof (also typeid). Cannot be overloaded with a friend function: =, (), [], -&gt; (they must be member functions).</p>","exam":"end","year":2018,"branch":"CO","src":"EndSem Papers.pdf pp.1-2 (CO-203, Nov-2018"},{"p":31,"q":"2","marks":"8","unit":1,"topic":"MATRIX class; virtual class, virtual function, pure virtual","qh":"<p>2. (a) Write a C++ program to perform 2D matrix operations determinant and transpose using class MATRIX, use appropriate constructor(s). 4 (b) What are virtual class, virtual function and pure virtual function?. Explain the difference with a suitable examples. 4</p>","sh":"<p>(a) A 3 x 3 MATRIX: default constructor (zero matrix) and one that takes the 9 values; determinant() by the rule of Sarrus/cofactors, transpose() returns a new matrix.</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass MATRIX {\n    double a[3][3];\npublic:\n    MATRIX() { for (auto&amp; r : a) for (double&amp; v : r) v = 0; }\n    MATRIX(const double (&amp;v)[9]) { for (int i = 0; i &lt; 9; ++i) a[i / 3][i % 3] = v[i]; }\n    double determinant() const {\n        return a[0][0] * (a[1][1] * a[2][2] - a[1][2] * a[2][1])\n             - a[0][1] * (a[1][0] * a[2][2] - a[1][2] * a[2][0])\n             + a[0][2] * (a[1][0] * a[2][1] - a[1][1] * a[2][0]);\n    }\n    MATRIX transpose() const {\n        MATRIX t;\n        for (int i = 0; i &lt; 3; ++i) for (int j = 0; j &lt; 3; ++j) t.a[j][i] = a[i][j];\n        return t;\n    }\n    void show() const { for (auto&amp; r : a) { for (double v : r) cout &lt;&lt; v &lt;&lt; &#x27; &#x27;; cout &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;; } }\n};\nint main() {\n    double v[9] = {1, 2, 3, 0, 1, 4, 5, 6, 0};\n    MATRIX m(v);\n    cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Matrix:\\n&quot;; m.show();\n    cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Determinant = &quot; &lt;&lt; m.determinant() &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n    cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Transpose:\\n&quot;; m.transpose().show();\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0 h\">Matrix:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">1 2 3</p><p class=\"ln i0\">0 1 4</p><p class=\"ln i0\">5 6 0</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Determinant = 1</p><p class=\"ln i0 h\">Transpose:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">1 0 5</p><p class=\"ln i0\">2 1 6</p><p class=\"ln i0\">3 4 0</p><p>(b) A virtual class (virtual base class) is a base inherited with virtual so that a multipath hierarchy keeps one copy of</p><p>it. A virtual function is a base member function (with a body) that derived classes may override, bound at run time. A pure virtual function (= 0) has no body in the base, must be overridden, and makes the class abstract.</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass A { public: int x = 1; };\nclass B : virtual public A {};                  // virtual class (virtual base)\nclass C : virtual public A {};\nclass D : public B, public C {};                // one copy of A\nclass Shape {\npublic:\n    virtual void show() { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Shape (virtual function with body)\\n&quot;; }\n    virtual double area() = 0;                  // pure virtual function\n    virtual ~Shape() {}\n};\nclass Sq : public Shape { public: double area() override { return 4; } };\nint main() {\n    D d; d.x = 5; cout &lt;&lt; d.x &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n    Sq s; Shape* p = &amp;s; p-&gt;show(); cout &lt;&lt; p-&gt;area() &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">5</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Shape (virtual function with body)</p><p class=\"ln i0\">4</p>","exam":"end","year":2018,"branch":"CO","src":"EndSem Papers.pdf pp.1-2 (CO-203, Nov-2018"},{"p":31,"q":"3","marks":"8","unit":1,"topic":"Virtual inheritance X-A-B; multiple catch blocks","qh":"<p>3. (a) Write code to create and inherit class X into A and then A into B as virtual class, where X is having member x1 and A is having member a1 and both are having parameterised and other constructors. Create 3 objects of B, where B is having member functions adddata() and displaydata(). 4 (b) How can we restrict a function in throwing specific exceptions. Create multiple catch blocks (at least 3) in a program, one for int and one for all type of exceptions and show with the help of examples, that which catch block will be executed for int, float, char etc. 4</p>","sh":"<p>(a) A derives virtually from X, B derives from A; A&#x27;s constructor passes the value to X, and B&#x27;s constructor to A. adddata() stores values and displaydata() shows them.</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass X {\nprotected:\n    int x1;\npublic:\n    X() : x1(0) { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;X()\\n&quot;; }\n    X(int v) : x1(v) { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;X(&quot; &lt;&lt; v &lt;&lt; &quot;)\\n&quot;; }\n};\nclass A : virtual public X {\nprotected:\n    int a1;\npublic:\n    A() : a1(0) { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;A()\\n&quot;; }\n    A(int p, int q) : X(p), a1(q) { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;A(&quot; &lt;&lt; q &lt;&lt; &quot;)\\n&quot;; }\n};\nclass B : public A {\n    int b1;\npublic:\n    B() : b1(0) {}\n    B(int p, int q, int r) : X(p), A(p, q), b1(r) {}\n    void adddata(int p, int q, int r) { x1 = p; a1 = q; b1 = r; }\n    void displaydata() const { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;x1=&quot; &lt;&lt; x1 &lt;&lt; &quot; a1=&quot; &lt;&lt; a1 &lt;&lt; &quot; b1=&quot; &lt;&lt; b1 &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;; }\n};\nint main() {\n    B b1;                      // default constructors\n    B b2(1, 2, 3);             // parameterised constructors\n    B b3;\n    b3.adddata(7, 8, 9);\n    b1.displaydata(); b2.displaydata(); b3.displaydata();\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">X()</p><p class=\"ln i0\">A()</p><p class=\"ln i0\">X(1)</p><p class=\"ln i0\">A(2)</p><p class=\"ln i0\">X()</p><p class=\"ln i0\">A()</p><p class=\"ln i0\">x1=0 a1=0 b1=0</p><p class=\"ln i0\">x1=1 a1=2 b1=3</p><p class=\"ln i0\">x1=7 a1=8 b1=9</p><p>(b) A function can be restricted to throwing only certain types by an exception specification: void f() throw(int, char); (old C++98 dynamic exception specification: other types call unexpected(); deprecated in C++11 and removed in C++17, where noexcept means &quot;throws nothing&quot;). The call site handles the allowed types with several catch blocks; the first block whose type matches executes, catch (...) handles everything else.</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nvoid thrower(int k) {                       // in old C++ written: void thrower(int) throw(int, char, float)\n    if (k == 1) throw 5;                    // int\n    if (k == 2) throw 2.5f;                 // float\n    if (k == 3) throw &#x27;c&#x27;;                  // char\n    if (k == 4) throw &quot;text&quot;;               // other\n}\nint main() {\n    for (int k = 1; k &lt;= 4; ++k) {\n        try { thrower(k); }\n        catch (int e)   { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;catch(int): &quot; &lt;&lt; e &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;; }\n        catch (float e) { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;catch(float): &quot; &lt;&lt; e &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;; }\n        catch (char e)  { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;catch(char): &quot; &lt;&lt; e &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;; }\n        catch (...)     { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;catch(...): all other types\\n&quot;; }\n    }\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">catch(int): 5</p><p class=\"ln i0\">catch(float): 2.5</p><p class=\"ln i0\">catch(char): c</p><p class=\"ln i0\">catch(...): all other types</p>","exam":"end","year":2018,"branch":"CO","src":"EndSem Papers.pdf pp.1-2 (CO-203, Nov-2018"},{"p":31,"q":"4","marks":"8","unit":1,"topic":"Class-to-class conversion; pointer to member","qh":"<p>4. (a) Write a program to get the sum of all the members of a class into another class using class to class conversion. And using friend function. (if possible) 4 (b) Explain the use, syntax etc of pointer to members of a class. Is it different from pointer to object and pointer as member of a class, How? 4</p>","sh":"<p>(a) Class A holds three numbers; class B holds their sum. A conversion operator A::operator B() converts an A object into a B; a friend function sumFriend reads the private members directly.</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass B {\n    int total;\npublic:\n    B(int t = 0) : total(t) {}\n    void show() const { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Sum = &quot; &lt;&lt; total &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;; }\n};\nclass A {\n    int x, y, z;\npublic:\n    A(int a, int b, int c) : x(a), y(b), z(c) {}\n    operator B() const { return B(x + y + z); }          // class-to-class conversion\n    friend int sumFriend(const A&amp;);                      // friend function\n};\nint sumFriend(const A&amp; a) { return a.x + a.y + a.z; }\nint main() {\n    A a(10, 20, 30);\n    B b = a;                      // conversion operator\n    b.show();\n    cout &lt;&lt; &quot;By friend function: &quot; &lt;&lt; sumFriend(a) &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Sum = 60</p><p class=\"ln i0\">By friend function: 60</p><p>(b) A pointer to a member holds the offset (identity) of a class member, not an address of an object&#x27;s member: int A::*pd = &amp;A::x; (data) and void (A::*pf)() = &amp;A::show; (function). It is used with an object: obj.*pd, ptr-&gt;*pd, (obj.*pf)(). A pointer to an object holds the address of a whole object (A* p = &amp;obj;) and uses -&gt;. A pointer as a member of a class is an ordinary data member that holds an address (int* data; inside a class, e.g. a linked-list next), used with ./-&gt; like any member. Differences: pointer to member = type T C::*, needs an object to be used; pointer to object = address of an object; pointer member = member whose value is an address.</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass A {\npublic:\n    int x = 5;\n    A* next = nullptr;                 // pointer AS a member\n    void show() { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;x = &quot; &lt;&lt; x &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;; }\n};\nint main() {\n    A a, b;\n    a.next = &amp;b;\n    A* po = &amp;a;                        // pointer to object\n    int A::*pm = &amp;A::x;                // pointer to member\n    void (A::*pf)() = &amp;A::show;\n    po-&gt;*pm = 9;                       // -&gt;* with pointer to object\n    (a.*pf)();                         // .* with object\n    cout &lt;&lt; (a.next == &amp;b ? &quot;next points to b\\n&quot; : &quot;?\\n&quot;);\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">x = 9</p><p class=\"ln i0\">next points to b</p>","exam":"end","year":2018,"branch":"CO","src":"EndSem Papers.pdf pp.1-2 (CO-203, Nov-2018"},{"p":31,"q":"5","marks":"8","unit":0,"topic":"this and inline; getInstances","qh":"<p>5. (a) Explain the following (i) this pointer (ii) inline function 4 (b) Write a class which contains a method called getInstances. This method should return the number of instances of the class. Explain the technique applied. 4</p>","sh":"<p>(a) (i) this is the hidden pointer to the object that called a non-static member function; used to resolve name clashes (this-&gt;x = x), to return the object (*this) and to pass the object to other functions; unavailable in static functions.</p><p>(ii) An inline function is expanded at the call site (request to the compiler) to remove call overhead; defined with inline or inside the class body; suitable for small functions, not for recursion/large loops.</p><p>(b) Technique: a static data member count (one copy shared by the class) is incremented in every constructor (including the copy constructor) and decremented in the destructor; the static member function getInstances() returns it, and can be called without an object.</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass Widget {\n    static int count;\npublic:\n    Widget() { ++count; }\n    Widget(const Widget&amp;) { ++count; }\n    ~Widget() { --count; }\n    static int getInstances() { return count; }\n};\nint Widget::count = 0;\nint main() {\n    Widget a, b;\n    cout &lt;&lt; Widget::getInstances() &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n    { Widget c = a; cout &lt;&lt; Widget::getInstances() &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;; }\n    cout &lt;&lt; Widget::getInstances() &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">2</p><p class=\"ln i0\">3</p><p class=\"ln i0\">2</p>","exam":"end","year":2018,"branch":"CO","src":"EndSem Papers.pdf pp.1-2 (CO-203, Nov-2018"},{"p":31,"q":"6","marks":"8","unit":1,"topic":"Distinctions; overloading rules","qh":"<p>6. (a) Distinguish between the following (Give suitable examples) (i) static and dynamic binding. (ii) Java and C++.</p><p>(iii) overloaded function and function templates 6 (b) What are the rules of function overloading. 2</p>","sh":"<p>(a) (i) Static binding: resolved at compile time (normal, overloaded, non-virtual functions). Dynamic binding: resolved at run time through virtual functions: Base* p = new Derived; p-&gt;f(); calls Derived::f.</p><p>(ii)</p><p class=\"ln i0\">C++                                         Java</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Compiled to machine code, platform specific Compiled to bytecode, runs on the JVM (portable)</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Pointers, pointer arithmetic                No pointers (references only)</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Multiple inheritance of classes             Single inheritance; multiple through interfaces Operator overloading, templates             No operator overloading; generics</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Manual memory management (new/delete)       Automatic garbage collection</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Has global functions, struct, union, goto   Everything inside classes; no struct/union/goto Destructors                                 finalize()/try-with-resources</p><p>(iii) Overloaded functions: several separately written functions with the same name and different parameters (possibly different logic). Function template: one generic definition from which the compiler generates a function for each type.</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nvoid show(int x)    { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;int &quot; &lt;&lt; x &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;; }          // overloaded functions\nvoid show(double x) { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;double &quot; &lt;&lt; x &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;; }\ntemplate &lt;class T&gt; void display(T x) { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;template &quot; &lt;&lt; x &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;; }   // one template for all types\nint main() { show(1); show(2.5); display(1); display(2.5); display(&#x27;c&#x27;); }</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">int 1</p><p class=\"ln i0\">double 2.5</p><p class=\"ln i0\">template 1</p><p class=\"ln i0\">template 2.5</p><p class=\"ln i0\">template c</p><p>(b) Same name, different parameter list (number, types or order); the return type alone cannot differentiate; the functions must be in the same scope; avoid ambiguity from default arguments and implicit conversions; const/reference parameters can differ; resolved at compile time (exact match, promotion, standard conversion, user conversion).</p>","exam":"end","year":2018,"branch":"CO","src":"EndSem Papers.pdf pp.1-2 (CO-203, Nov-2018"},{"p":31,"q":"7","marks":"8","unit":1,"topic":"Short notes (any four)","qh":"<p>7. Write short notes (any four) 8 (a) Templates (b) JVM and JDK (c) Pointer to function (d) Rules for virtual function</p><p>(e) Array of pointers</p>","sh":"<p>(a) Templates: generic functions and classes (template &lt;class T&gt;); the compiler generates a version per type; reuse, type safety, basis of the STL.</p><p>(b) JVM: the virtual machine that loads, verifies and executes bytecode (class loader, runtime areas, interpreter/JIT, garbage collector); platform specific, makes Java portable. JDK: the development kit = JRE (JVM + libraries) + tools</p><p class=\"ln i0\">(javac, java, jar, javadoc, jdb).</p><p>(c) A pointer to function stores the address of a function: int (*fp)(int, int) = add; and is called as fp(2, 3). Uses: callbacks, tables of functions, passing a function to another function.</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nint add(int a, int b) { return a + b; }\nint mul(int a, int b) { return a * b; }\nint apply(int (*f)(int, int), int x, int y) { return f(x, y); }     // callback\nint main() {\n    int (*fp)(int, int) = add;\n    cout &lt;&lt; fp(2, 3) &lt;&lt; &#x27; &#x27; &lt;&lt; apply(mul, 4, 5) &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">5 20</p><p>(d) Rules for virtual functions: they must be members of a class, not static, and not friends (a friend of another class may be virtual); declared with virtual in the base class; the signature in derived classes must match; the call must be through a base pointer/reference for dynamic binding; a base class should have a virtual destructor; constructors cannot be virtual; they may be pure (= 0); if not overridden, the base version is used; the virtual keyword is not repeated outside the class.</p><p>(e) An array of pointers is an array whose elements are pointers: int* p[3]; or char* names[] = {&quot;Asha&quot;, &quot;Ravi&quot;};; it lets ragged data share storage and lets a base-pointer array hold different derived objects (Shape*</p><p class=\"ln i0\">s[3]).</p>","exam":"end","year":2018,"branch":"CO","src":"EndSem Papers.pdf pp.1-2 (CO-203, Nov-2018"},{"p":32,"q":"1","marks":"14","unit":0,"topic":"Parameterised constructor; type conversion basic to class","qh":"<p>Q1. (a) Explain parameterised constructor. Give suitable code to explain 7 (b) What is type conversion? Write suitable code for conversion from basic to class type. 7</p>","sh":"<p>(a) A parameterised constructor takes arguments so that each object is initialised with its own values when it is created: Rect r(3, 4); (explicit call) or Rect r = Rect(3, 4);. If any constructor is defined, the compiler does not supply the default one, so a default constructor must be added if Rect r; is needed.</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass Rect {\n    int l, b;\npublic:\n    Rect() : l(0), b(0) {}                               // default\n    Rect(int x, int y) : l(x), b(y) {}                   // parameterised\n    int area() const { return l * b; }\n};\nint main() {\n    Rect a;\n    Rect b(6, 7);\n    cout &lt;&lt; a.area() &lt;&lt; &#x27; &#x27; &lt;&lt; b.area() &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">0 42</p><p>(b) Type conversion converts a value from one type to another. Basic type to class type is done by a converting (one-argument) constructor; class to basic type by operator type(); class to class by either.</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass Meter {\n    double m;\npublic:\n    Meter(double v = 0) : m(v) {}          // basic (double) -&gt; class (Meter)\n    void show() const { cout &lt;&lt; m &lt;&lt; &quot; m\\n&quot;; }\n};\nint main() {\n    Meter a = 12.5;                        // implicit conversion calls Meter(double)\n    Meter b(3);                            // int -&gt; double -&gt; Meter\n    a.show(); b.show();\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">12.5 m</p><p class=\"ln i0\">3 m</p>","exam":"end","year":2018,"branch":"CO","src":"EndSem Papers.pdf pp.3-4 (CO/SE-201, B.Tech (CO/SE), Nov-2018, Max. 70 marks"},{"p":32,"q":"2","marks":"14","unit":0,"topic":"C++ keywords; types of inheritance","qh":"<p>Q2. (a) How many keywords are there in C++? Explain any 3 keywords of C++. 7 (b) What is the different type of inheritance? explain 7</p>","sh":"<p>(a) C++ (C++98) has 63 keywords (C has 32); modern standards have about 95 (C++11 added nullptr, constexpr, auto, decltype, noexcept, static_assert, ...). Three explained: class declares a user-defined type with data members and member functions and access control; virtual makes a member function dynamically bound (run-time polymorphism) or a base class shared (virtual inheritance); friend lets a non-member function or another class use the private members of a class.</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass Box {                                         // keyword: class\n    int v = 3;\npublic:\n    virtual void show() { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Box &quot; &lt;&lt; v &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;; }      // keyword: virtual\n    friend void peek(const Box&amp; b);                           // keyword: friend\n};\nvoid peek(const Box&amp; b) { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;peek &quot; &lt;&lt; b.v &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;; }\nint main() { Box b; b.show(); peek(b); }</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Box 3</p><p class=\"ln i0\">peek 3</p><p>(b) Inheritance lets a derived class reuse and extend a base class. Types: single (A -&gt; B), multiple (A, B -&gt; C), multilevel (A -&gt; B -&gt; C), hierarchical (A -&gt; B, A -&gt; C), hybrid (combination, often a diamond; use virtual base classes).</p><pre>Single   A -&gt; B        Multilevel  A -&gt; B -&gt; C        Multiple  A,B -&gt; C\nHierarchical  A -&gt; B, A -&gt; C        Hybrid  A -&gt; B, A -&gt; C, B,C -&gt; D\n#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass A { public: void a() { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;A &quot;; } };\nclass B : public A { public: void b() { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;B &quot;; } };            // single\nclass C : public B { public: void c() { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;C &quot;; } };            // multilevel\nclass P { public: void p() { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;P &quot;; } };\nclass Q : public A, public P { public: void q() { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Q &quot;; } };  // multiple\nint main() {\n    C c; c.a(); c.b(); c.c(); cout &lt;&lt; &quot;&lt;- multilevel\\n&quot;;\n    Q q; q.a(); q.p(); q.q(); cout &lt;&lt; &quot;&lt;- multiple\\n&quot;;\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">A B C &lt;- multilevel</p><p class=\"ln i0\">A P Q &lt;- multiple</p>","exam":"end","year":2018,"branch":"CO","src":"EndSem Papers.pdf pp.3-4 (CO/SE-201, B.Tech (CO/SE), Nov-2018, Max. 70 marks"},{"p":32,"q":"3","marks":"14","unit":1,"topic":"Inline function; function overloading for area","qh":"<p>Q3. (a) Explain the use of inline function. Give suitable example. 7 (b) What is function overloading? Write a program to overload function area using function overloading. 7</p>","sh":"<p>(a) An inline function (inline) is expanded at the point of call, removing the call/return overhead; used for small, frequently called functions (getters, max, square); it is a request, not a command; member functions defined inside the class are inline.</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\ninline int maxOf(int a, int b) { return a &gt; b ? a : b; }\nclass P { int v = 4; public: int get() const { return v; } };      // inline by default\nint main() { cout &lt;&lt; maxOf(3, 9) &lt;&lt; &#x27; &#x27; &lt;&lt; P().get() &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;; }</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">9 4</p><p>(b) Function overloading: functions with the same name but different parameter lists. area for a circle (one double), square (one int), rectangle (two arguments), triangle (three arguments).</p><pre>#include &lt;cmath&gt;\n#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\ndouble area(double r)              { return 3.14159 * r * r; }      // circle\nint    area(int s)                 { return s * s; }                // square\ndouble area(double l, double b)    { return l * b; }               // rectangle\ndouble area(double a, double b, double c) {                         // triangle (Heron)\n    double s = (a + b + c) / 2;\n    return sqrt(s * (s - a) * (s - b) * (s - c));\n}\nint main() {\n    cout &lt;&lt; area(2.0) &lt;&lt; &#x27; &#x27; &lt;&lt; area(5) &lt;&lt; &#x27; &#x27; &lt;&lt; area(3.0, 4.0) &lt;&lt; &#x27; &#x27; &lt;&lt; area(3.0, 4.0, 5.0) &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">12.5664 25 12 6</p>","exam":"end","year":2018,"branch":"CO","src":"EndSem Papers.pdf pp.3-4 (CO/SE-201, B.Tech (CO/SE), Nov-2018, Max. 70 marks"},{"p":32,"q":"4","marks":"14","unit":0,"topic":"Call by value/reference; emp and faculty","qh":"<p>Q4. (a) Explain call by value and call by reference. 7 (b) Write a program to add, show details of faculty using inheritance where class &quot;emp&quot; is inherited by class &quot;faculty&quot; 7</p>","sh":"<p>(a) Call by value: the argument is copied into the parameter; changes inside the function do not affect the caller (safe, but copying cost). Call by reference: the parameter is a reference (alias) or pointer to the caller&#x27;s variable; changes are visible to the caller; no copy.</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nvoid byValue(int a, int b)       { int t = a; a = b; b = t; }\nvoid byReference(int&amp; a, int&amp; b) { int t = a; a = b; b = t; }\nint main() {\n    int x = 1, y = 2, p = 1, q = 2;\n    byValue(x, y);       cout &lt;&lt; &quot;value:     &quot; &lt;&lt; x &lt;&lt; &#x27; &#x27; &lt;&lt; y &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n    byReference(p, q);   cout &lt;&lt; &quot;reference: &quot; &lt;&lt; p &lt;&lt; &#x27; &#x27; &lt;&lt; q &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">value:     1 2</p><p class=\"ln i0\">reference: 2 1</p><p>(b)</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n#include &lt;string&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass emp {\nprotected:\n    int id;\n    string name;\n    double salary;\npublic:\n    void add(int i, string n, double s) { id = i; name = n; salary = s; }\n    void show() const { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Id: &quot; &lt;&lt; id &lt;&lt; &quot;, Name: &quot; &lt;&lt; name &lt;&lt; &quot;, Salary: &quot; &lt;&lt; salary; }\n};\nclass faculty : public emp {\n    string dept, subject;\npublic:\n    void add(int i, string n, double s, string d, string sub) { emp::add(i, n, s); dept = d; subject = sub; }\n    void show() const { emp::show(); cout &lt;&lt; &quot;, Dept: &quot; &lt;&lt; dept &lt;&lt; &quot;, Subject: &quot; &lt;&lt; subject &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;; }\n};\nint main() {\n    faculty f;\n    f.add(7, &quot;Dr. Rao&quot;, 90000, &quot;CSE&quot;, &quot;OOP&quot;);\n    f.show();\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Id: 7, Name: Dr. Rao, Salary: 90000, Dept: CSE, Subject: OOP</p>","exam":"end","year":2018,"branch":"CO","src":"EndSem Papers.pdf pp.3-4 (CO/SE-201, B.Tech (CO/SE), Nov-2018, Max. 70 marks"},{"p":32,"q":"5","marks":"14","unit":0,"topic":"Abstraction/encapsulation/data hiding; visibility modes","qh":"<p>Q5. (a) Give the meaning of the following terms: i) Abstraction; ii) Encapsulation; iii) Data hiding. 9 (b) Explain visibility modes protected available in C++ along with their purpose. 5</p>","sh":"<p>(a) i) Abstraction: exposing only what an object does (its interface) and hiding how; achieved with classes and abstract classes. ii) Encapsulation: binding data and the functions that work on it into one unit (class). iii) Data hiding: making data private/protected so it is changed only through public member functions, protecting integrity. Example: a BankAccount keeps balance private (data hiding), bundles it with deposit()/withdraw() (encapsulation) and users see only those operations (abstraction).</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass BankAccount {\n    double balance = 0;                                  // hidden\npublic:\n    void deposit(double a) { if (a &gt; 0) balance += a; }\n    double get() const { return balance; }\n};\nint main() { BankAccount b; b.deposit(100); b.deposit(-50); cout &lt;&lt; b.get() &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;; }</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">100</p><p>(b) Visibility (access) modes: public (accessible everywhere: the interface), private (only the class and its friends; the default), protected (the class, its friends and derived classes; not outside). Purpose of protected: lets derived classes use base data directly while keeping it hidden from the outside world. In derivation, the mode (public, protected, private) limits how base members are seen in the derived class.</p>","exam":"end","year":2018,"branch":"CO","src":"EndSem Papers.pdf pp.3-4 (CO/SE-201, B.Tech (CO/SE), Nov-2018, Max. 70 marks"},{"p":32,"q":"6","marks":"14","unit":0,"topic":"Identifier rules; virtual functions; operators","qh":"<p>Q6. (a) What are the rules for naming identifier? 5 (b) How the virtual functions are declared in C++. 6 (c) List operators which can&#x27;t be overloaded 3</p>","sh":"<p>(a) An identifier (name of a variable, function, class...) may contain letters, digits and underscore only; must start with a letter or underscore (not a digit); is case sensitive (Sum and sum differ); cannot be a keyword; cannot contain spaces or special characters; has no length limit in standard C++ (compilers use at least 1024 significant characters); names starting with an underscore and a capital letter, or containing a double underscore, are reserved.</p><p>(b) A virtual function is declared by writing virtual before its declaration in the base class: virtual void show();. The derived class redefines it with the same signature. It must be non-static and is called through a base pointer/reference for run-time binding; it may be pure (= 0); virtual is not repeated in the out-of-class definition; constructors cannot be virtual, destructors can.</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass Base { public: virtual void show() { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Base\\n&quot;; } virtual ~Base() {} };\nclass Derived : public Base { public: void show() override { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Derived\\n&quot;; } };\nint main() { Base* p = new Derived; p-&gt;show(); delete p; }</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Derived</p><p>(c) ::, ., .*, ?:, sizeof (and typeid).</p>","exam":"end","year":2018,"branch":"CO","src":"EndSem Papers.pdf pp.3-4 (CO/SE-201, B.Tech (CO/SE), Nov-2018, Max. 70 marks"},{"p":32,"q":"7","marks":"14","unit":1,"topic":"Short notes (any two)","qh":"<p>Q7. Write short notes on any two :- (a) Friend function (b) Templates (c) Pure virtual function (d) Features of Java</p>","sh":"<p>(a) A friend function is a non-member function declared with friend in a class; it can access that class&#x27;s private and protected members. It is called like an ordinary function and has no this. Used for operators like &lt;&lt; and for functions that need the private data of two classes; it weakens encapsulation, so use sparingly.</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass Pt { int x = 3, y = 4; friend int sumXY(const Pt&amp;); };\nint sumXY(const Pt&amp; p) { return p.x + p.y; }\nint main() { Pt p; cout &lt;&lt; sumXY(p) &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;; }</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">7</p><p>(b) Templates: one definition for many types; function templates and class templates; instantiated per type by the compiler; give reuse and type safety.</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\ntemplate &lt;class T&gt; T sumOf(T a, T b) { return a + b; }\nint main() { cout &lt;&lt; sumOf(2, 3) &lt;&lt; &#x27; &#x27; &lt;&lt; sumOf(1.5, 2.5) &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;; }</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">5 4</p><p>(c) A pure virtual function (virtual void f() = 0;) has no definition in the base class; it makes the class abstract; derived classes must override it; it defines an interface for run-time polymorphism.</p><p>(d) Features of Java: simple, object-oriented, platform independent (bytecode/JVM), secure, robust, multithreaded, portable, high performance (JIT), distributed, dynamic.</p>","exam":"end","year":2018,"branch":"CO","src":"EndSem Papers.pdf pp.3-4 (CO/SE-201, B.Tech (CO/SE), Nov-2018, Max. 70 marks"},{"p":33,"q":"1","marks":"12","unit":0,"topic":"Six short questions","qh":"<p>Q.1 Answer all the following questions: [12] a) What is a pointer? Write its syntax. b) Explain how data is secured in C++? c) Define constructor and give example. d) What is an object and class? e) How can templates be used for generic programming? f) Write the significance of static data members in C++?</p>","sh":"<p>a) A pointer is a variable that stores the memory address of another variable. Syntax: datatype *name; e.g. int *p = &amp;x;; *p dereferences it and p-&gt;member accesses a member of an object it points to.</p><p>b) Data is secured by encapsulation and data hiding: members are made private or protected and are reachable only through public member functions (or friends); const members/functions prevent modification; inheritance modes restrict access of base members.</p><p>c) A constructor is a special member function with the class name and no return type, called automatically when an object is created, to initialise it.</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass Student {\n    int roll;\npublic:\n    Student(int r) : roll(r) { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;constructed &quot; &lt;&lt; roll &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;; }\n};\nint main() { Student s(21); }</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">constructed 21</p><p>d) A class is a user-defined type (blueprint) with data members and member functions; an object is an instance of a class with its own data (Student s;).</p><p>e) A template defines a type-independent function or class: template &lt;class T&gt; T maxOf(T a, T b); the compiler instantiates it for each type used, so the same code works for int, float, string..., giving reuse and type safety.</p><p>f) A static data member is shared by all objects (one copy per class); it keeps class-wide information (object count, totals), exists before any object is created, is defined outside the class and is accessed as Class::member.</p>","exam":"end","year":2018,"branch":"SE","src":"EndSem Papers.pdf pp.5-6 (SE203, B.Tech (CSE), Nov/Dec-2018"},{"p":33,"q":"2","marks":"7","unit":0,"topic":"Structure versus class; console I/O","qh":"<p>Q.2 a) Differentiate between Structures and class? Give an example of each? [3] b) Explain about the Formatted and Unformatted Console I/O Operations. [4]</p>","sh":"<p>a)</p><pre>Structure                                   Class\nDefault access public                       Default access private\nTypically plain data grouping               Data + functions + access control\n(C structs: no functions/inheritance)       Supports inheritance, constructors, virtual functions\nstruct Point { int x, y; };                 class Account { int bal; public: void dep(int); };</pre><p>b) Unformatted console I/O transfers characters/lines without any formatting: input cin.get(ch), cin.getline(s, n), cin.read(); output cout.put(ch), cout.write(s, n). Formatted I/O controls the appearance: width(),</p><pre>precision(), fill(), setf() and manipulators setw, setprecision, setfill, fixed, left, right, hex, endl.\n#include &lt;iomanip&gt;\n#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nint main() {\n    cout.put(&#x27;A&#x27;); cout.put(&#x27;\\n&#x27;);                      // unformatted output\n    cout.write(&quot;hello&quot;, 5); cout &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n    cout &lt;&lt; setw(8) &lt;&lt; setfill(&#x27;*&#x27;) &lt;&lt; 42 &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;      // formatted output\n    cout &lt;&lt; fixed &lt;&lt; setprecision(2) &lt;&lt; 3.14159 &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n    cout &lt;&lt; hex &lt;&lt; 255 &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">A</p><p class=\"ln i0\">hello</p><p class=\"ln i0\">******42</p><p class=\"ln i0\">3.14</p><p class=\"ln i0\">ff</p>","exam":"end","year":2018,"branch":"SE","src":"EndSem Papers.pdf pp.5-6 (SE203, B.Tech (CSE), Nov/Dec-2018"},{"p":33,"q":"3","marks":"7","unit":4,"topic":"File stream classes; friend pros and cons","qh":"<p>Q.3 a) Briefly explain classes for file stream operations in C++? [3] b) What is a friend function? Discuss its pros and cons with respective to normal member functions? [4]</p>","sh":"<p>a) The file stream classes of &lt;fstream&gt;: ifstream (input from a file, derived from istream), ofstream (output to a file, derived from ostream), fstream (both, derived from iostream), with the buffer class filebuf; common functions</p><p class=\"ln i0\">open(), close(), is_open(), eof(), get(), put(), read(), write(), seekg(), seekp(), tellg(), tellp().</p><p>b) A friend function is a non-member function with access to the private/protected members of the class that declares</p><p>it. Pros: needed for operators with a non-class left operand (cout &lt;&lt; obj), works on two classes at once, gives access without getters, symmetrical conversions for both operands. Cons: weakens encapsulation, not inherited, cannot be virtual (no run-time polymorphism), no this pointer, increases coupling.</p>","exam":"end","year":2018,"branch":"SE","src":"EndSem Papers.pdf pp.5-6 (SE203, B.Tech (CSE), Nov/Dec-2018"},{"p":33,"q":"4","marks":"7","unit":0,"topic":"Vehicle class; function overloading","qh":"<p>Q.4. a) Create a class with the following data members: Vehicle, Data members: name, model, company, Price, and variants. Member functions: putdetails() and getdetails() to set and display Vehicle details respectively? [4] b) Explain function overloading with a suitable program? [3]</p>","sh":"<p>a)</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n#include &lt;string&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass Vehicle {\n    string name, model, company, variants;\n    double price;\npublic:\n    void putdetails(string n, string m, string c, double p, string v) {     // set\n        name = n; model = m; company = c; price = p; variants = v;\n    }\n    void getdetails() const {                                                // display\n        cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Name: &quot; &lt;&lt; name &lt;&lt; &quot;\\nModel: &quot; &lt;&lt; model &lt;&lt; &quot;\\nCompany: &quot; &lt;&lt; company\n             &lt;&lt; &quot;\\nPrice: &quot; &lt;&lt; price &lt;&lt; &quot;\\nVariants: &quot; &lt;&lt; variants &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n    }\n};\nint main() {\n    Vehicle v;\n    v.putdetails(&quot;Swift&quot;, &quot;VXi&quot;, &quot;Maruti&quot;, 650000, &quot;Petrol, CNG&quot;);\n    v.getdetails();\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Name: Swift</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Model: VXi</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Company: Maruti</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Price: 650000</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Variants: Petrol, CNG</p><p>b) Function overloading: several functions with the same name but different parameter lists; chosen at compile time.</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nint    add(int a, int b)           { return a + b; }\ndouble add(double a, double b)     { return a + b; }\nint    add(int a, int b, int c)    { return a + b + c; }\nint main() { cout &lt;&lt; add(1, 2) &lt;&lt; &#x27; &#x27; &lt;&lt; add(1.5, 2.5) &lt;&lt; &#x27; &#x27; &lt;&lt; add(1, 2, 3) &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;; }</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">3 4 6</p>","exam":"end","year":2018,"branch":"SE","src":"EndSem Papers.pdf pp.5-6 (SE203, B.Tech (CSE), Nov/Dec-2018"},{"p":33,"q":"5","marks":"7","unit":1,"topic":"Dynamic initialization; operator overloading","qh":"<p>Q.5 a) Write a C++ Program for Dynamic Initialization using constructors. [4] b) Explain Operator overloading with one example. [3]</p>","sh":"<p>a) Dynamic initialization: the constructor arguments are values known only at run time (input or computed). Sample input:</p><pre>10000 5 8\n#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass FixedDeposit {\n    long principal; int years; float rate; double amount;\npublic:\n    FixedDeposit(long p, int y, float r) : principal(p), years(y), rate(r) {\n        amount = p;\n        for (int i = 0; i &lt; y; ++i) amount += amount * r / 100;\n    }\n    void show() const { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Maturity amount = &quot; &lt;&lt; amount &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;; }\n};\nint main() {\n    long p; int y; float r;\n    cin &gt;&gt; p &gt;&gt; y &gt;&gt; r;\n    FixedDeposit fd(p, y, r);          // initialised with run-time values\n    fd.show();\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Maturity amount = 14693.3</p><p>b) Operator overloading gives a built-in operator a meaning for a class by writing operator+ etc. (polymorphism at compile time).</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass Dist {\n    int cm;\npublic:\n    Dist(int c) : cm(c) {}\n    Dist operator+(const Dist&amp; d) const { return Dist(cm + d.cm); }\n    void show() const { cout &lt;&lt; cm &lt;&lt; &quot; cm\\n&quot;; }\n};\nint main() { Dist a(30), b(45); (a + b).show(); }</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">75 cm</p>","exam":"end","year":2018,"branch":"SE","src":"EndSem Papers.pdf pp.5-6 (SE203, B.Tech (CSE), Nov/Dec-2018"},{"p":33,"q":"6","marks":"7","unit":1,"topic":"Types of inheritance; virtual base class","qh":"<p>Q.6 a) Illustrate types of Inheritance. [4] b) What is virtual base class? [3]</p>","sh":"<p>a) Single: one base, one derived (A -&gt; B). Multiple: more than one base (A, B -&gt; C). Multilevel: chain (A -&gt; B -&gt; C). Hierarchical: one base, several derived (A -&gt; B, A -&gt; C). Hybrid: combination (often a diamond).</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass A { public: void a() { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;A &quot;; } };\nclass B : public A { public: void b() { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;B &quot;; } };            // single\nclass C : public B { public: void c() { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;C &quot;; } };            // multilevel\nclass P { public: void p() { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;P &quot;; } };\nclass M : public A, public P { };                                     // multiple\nclass H1 : public A { };  class H2 : public A { };                    // hierarchical\nint main() {\n    C c; c.a(); c.b(); c.c(); cout &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n    M m; m.a(); m.p(); cout &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n    H1 h1; H2 h2; h1.a(); h2.a(); cout &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">A B C</p><p class=\"ln i0\">A P</p><p class=\"ln i0\">A A</p><p>b) A virtual base class is a base inherited with virtual so that, in multipath (diamond) inheritance, the derived class holds a single copy of the base subobject, avoiding duplicate data and ambiguity. class B : virtual public A { };</p><pre>class C : virtual public A { }; class D : public B, public C { };</pre>","exam":"end","year":2018,"branch":"SE","src":"EndSem Papers.pdf pp.5-6 (SE203, B.Tech (CSE), Nov/Dec-2018"},{"p":33,"q":"7","marks":"7","unit":1,"topic":"Recursion with template; try-catch","qh":"<p>Q.7 a) Explain recursion with template function with suitable code segment. [4] b) Write a program containing a possible exception. Use a try block to throw it and a catch block to handle it properly. [3]</p>","sh":"<p>a) A function template may call itself, so one recursive algorithm works for every numeric type; the compiler instantiates a separate recursive function per type.</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\ntemplate &lt;class T&gt;\nT power(T base, int n) {                       // recursion inside a template function\n    if (n == 0) return 1;\n    return base * power(base, n - 1);\n}\ntemplate &lt;class T&gt;\nT sumTo(T n) { return n &lt;= 1 ? n : n + sumTo(n - 1); }\nint main() {\n    cout &lt;&lt; power(2, 10) &lt;&lt; &#x27; &#x27; &lt;&lt; power(1.5, 3) &lt;&lt; &#x27; &#x27; &lt;&lt; sumTo(5) &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">1024 3.375 15</p><p>b)</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nint main() {\n    int a = 10, b = 0;\n    try {\n        if (b == 0) throw &quot;Division by zero is not allowed&quot;;   // possible exception\n        cout &lt;&lt; a / b &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n    } catch (const char* msg) {\n        cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Exception caught: &quot; &lt;&lt; msg &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n    }\n    cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Program continues normally\\n&quot;;\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Exception caught: Division by zero is not allowed</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Program continues normally</p>","exam":"end","year":2018,"branch":"SE","src":"EndSem Papers.pdf pp.5-6 (SE203, B.Tech (CSE), Nov/Dec-2018"},{"p":34,"q":"1","marks":"10","unit":0,"topic":"Five short questions (2 each)","qh":"<p>Q1. Answer the following with the help of examples. 2x5 a) What are &#x27;new&#x27; and &#x27;delete&#x27; in C++? Explain their purpose with proper syntax. b) What is the role of &#x27;const&#x27; and &#x27;&amp;&#x27; symbol in case of copy constructor? c) Can a destructor be called explicitly? Justify your answer (Yes/No) with an example. d) What is a friend function? Can it be inherited? Why or why not? e) What is a class variable in C++? Give an example.</p>","sh":"<p>a) new allocates memory on the heap and returns its address (and calls the constructor); delete releases it (and calls</p><pre>the destructor). Syntax: T* p = new T(args);, delete p;, arrays new T[n], delete[] p;.\n#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nint main() {\n    int* p = new int(5);\n    int* a = new int[3]{1, 2, 3};\n    cout &lt;&lt; *p &lt;&lt; &#x27; &#x27; &lt;&lt; a[2] &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n    delete p;\n    delete[] a;\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">5 3</p><p>b) In X(const X&amp; o) the &amp; makes the parameter a reference; passing by value would need the copy constructor to copy the argument, recursing forever. const guarantees the source is not changed and allows temporaries/const objects to be copied.</p><p>c) Yes. A destructor can be called explicitly (obj.~Class();), but the object&#x27;s destructor runs again automatically when it goes out of scope, so it is dangerous (double destruction) except for objects created with placement new. It is rarely needed.</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nstruct T { ~T() { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;~T\\n&quot;; } };\nint main() {\n    T* p = new T;\n    p-&gt;~T();                      // explicit call: runs the destructor body only\n    operator delete(p);           // memory released separately (the object is already destroyed)\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">~T</p><p>d) A friend function is a non-member function with access to the private members of the class that grants friendship. It is not inherited: friendship is not transitive/inherited, because the base class granted it to one specific function, not to classes derived later, and a friend function is not a member so it is not part of the class that derived classes inherit.</p><p>e) A class variable is a static data member: one copy shared by all objects.</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass Counter { public: static int count; Counter() { ++count; } };\nint Counter::count = 0;\nint main() { Counter a, b, c; cout &lt;&lt; Counter::count &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;; }</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">3</p>","exam":"mid","year":2018,"branch":"IT","src":"OOPS MIDSEM ALL BRANCHES Compiled.pdf pp.16-17 (IT203, Sept-2018"},{"p":34,"q":"2","marks":"5","unit":1,"topic":"Non-overloadable operators; string concatenation with +","qh":"<p>Q2. a) Name the operator(s) which cannot be overloaded. Also name operator(s) which cannot be overloaded using friend function and the reason behind it. 2.5 b) Write a C++ program to overload the &#x27;+&#x27; operator to concatenate two strings (i.e. s3 = s1 + s2; where si is the object of a class). 2.5</p>","sh":"<p>a) Cannot be overloaded at all: :: (scope resolution), . (member access), .* (pointer to member), ?: (ternary), sizeof, typeid. Reason: these operators work on names/types/scopes or have special evaluation rules, so a user function cannot take their operands. Cannot be overloaded with a friend function: =, (), [], -&gt;; reason: the language requires them to be non-static member functions, so that they always act on an object (this) and an assignment/subscript/call/arrow cannot be hijacked by a non-member.</p><p>b)</p><pre>#include &lt;cstring&gt;\n#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass Str {\n    char* s;\npublic:\n    Str(const char* t = &quot;&quot;) { s = new char[strlen(t) + 1]; strcpy(s, t); }\n    Str(const Str&amp; o) { s = new char[strlen(o.s) + 1]; strcpy(s, o.s); }\n    Str&amp; operator=(const Str&amp; o) { if (this != &amp;o) { delete[] s; s = new char[strlen(o.s) + 1]; strcpy(s, o.s); } return *this; }\n    ~Str() { delete[] s; }\n    Str operator+(const Str&amp; o) const {\n        char* t = new char[strlen(s) + strlen(o.s) + 1];\n        strcpy(t, s); strcat(t, o.s);\n        Str r(t); delete[] t;\n        return r;\n    }\n    void show() const { cout &lt;&lt; s &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;; }\n};\nint main() {\n    Str s1(&quot;Hello &quot;), s2(&quot;World&quot;), s3;\n    s3 = s1 + s2;\n    s3.show();\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Hello World</p>","exam":"mid","year":2018,"branch":"IT","src":"OOPS MIDSEM ALL BRANCHES Compiled.pdf pp.16-17 (IT203, Sept-2018"},{"p":34,"q":"3","marks":"5","unit":1,"topic":"Virtual base class and abstract class","qh":"<p>Q3. What is the concept of a virtual base class and an abstract class? Where are they used? Explain in detail with the help of an example of each. [5]</p>","sh":"<p>Virtual base class: a base inherited with virtual so that in multipath (diamond) inheritance only one copy of the base exists in the most derived class; used to avoid duplicated data and ambiguity (I/O streams: ios is a virtual base of istream and ostream inside iostream). Abstract class: a class with at least one pure virtual function; it cannot be instantiated and defines an interface that derived classes must implement; used for frameworks and run-time polymorphism (Shape, Employee).</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass Person { public: int id = 1; };                       // virtual base\nclass Teacher : virtual public Person {};\nclass Student : virtual public Person {};\nclass TA : public Teacher, public Student {};\nclass Shape {                                              // abstract class\npublic:\n    virtual double area() const = 0;\n    virtual ~Shape() {}\n};\nclass Rect : public Shape { double l, b; public: Rect(double x, double y) : l(x), b(y) {} double area() const override { return l * b; } };\nint main() {\n    TA t; cout &lt;&lt; t.id &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;                            // single Person\n    Shape* s = new Rect(3, 4); cout &lt;&lt; s-&gt;area() &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;; delete s;\n    // Shape x;   // error: cannot instantiate an abstract class\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">1</p><p class=\"ln i0\">12</p>","exam":"mid","year":2018,"branch":"IT","src":"OOPS MIDSEM ALL BRANCHES Compiled.pdf pp.16-17 (IT203, Sept-2018"},{"p":34,"q":"4","marks":"10","unit":1,"topic":"Function template with multiplier; predict the output","qh":"<p>Q4. a) What are templates in C++? Write a program for defining a function template (taking &#x27;T&#x27; type argument(s) and an integer value) which calculates the max of two numbers and returns the maximum of the two after multiplying it with the integer value passed as argument. i.e. say max(2,7,5) should return 35 and max(1.1,5.6,2) should return 11.2. 5 b) Predict the output: 2.5x2 (i) #include &lt;cstdio&gt; using std::printf; #include&lt;iostream&gt; using namespace std; class X { public: int i,j; X(); X(int x); X(int x, int y); ~X(); }; X::X(int x):i(x),j(23) {printf(&quot;X::X(int)\\n&quot;);cout&lt;&lt;i&lt;&lt;endl;} X::X(int x, int y): X(x+y) { printf(&quot;X::X(int,int)\\n&quot;);} X::X():X(44,11) {printf(&quot;X::X()\\n&quot;);} X::~X() {printf(&quot;X::~X()\\n&quot;);} int main(void){ X x; } (ii) class A { int x; public: A() { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;A&#x27;s constructor called &quot; &lt;&lt; endl; } }; class B { static A a; public: B() { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;B&#x27;s constructor called &quot; &lt;&lt; endl; } static A getA() { return a; } }; A B::a; int main() { B b1, b2, b3; A a = b1.getA(); return 0; }</p>","sh":"<p>a) Templates are generic blueprints for functions and classes: a single definition works for any type, the compiler generates the code per type (compile-time polymorphism).</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\ntemplate &lt;class T&gt;\nT maxTimes(T a, T b, int k) { return (a &gt; b ? a : b) * k; }\nint main() {\n    cout &lt;&lt; maxTimes(2, 7, 5) &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n    cout &lt;&lt; maxTimes(1.1, 5.6, 2) &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">35</p><p class=\"ln i0\">11.2</p><p>b) (i) X x; calls the default constructor X(), which delegates (C++11) to X(44, 11), which delegates to X(55) (44 + 11). The innermost constructor runs first: it prints X::X(int) and then i, which is 55; then X(int,int) body runs; then X() body runs; at the end of main the destructor prints X::~X().</p><pre>X::X(int)\n55\nX::X(int,int)\nX::X()\nX::~X()</pre><p>(ii) B::a is a static member, defined once at namespace scope by A B::a;, so it is constructed before main starts, printing the A line first. The three B objects each print the B line; A a = b1.getA(); makes a copy with the implicit copy constructor, which prints nothing.</p><p class=\"ln i0\">A&#x27;s constructor called</p><p class=\"ln i0\">B&#x27;s constructor called</p><p class=\"ln i0\">B&#x27;s constructor called</p><p class=\"ln i0\">B&#x27;s constructor called</p>","exam":"mid","year":2018,"branch":"IT","src":"OOPS MIDSEM ALL BRANCHES Compiled.pdf pp.16-17 (IT203, Sept-2018"},{"p":35,"q":"1","marks":"3","unit":0,"topic":"Static data members","qh":"<p>Q 1. What is a static data member? Explain with suitable illustration. (3)</p>","sh":"<p>(The paper&#x27;s wording is &quot;What is a static data member? How they are used in static functions. Explain with suitable illustration.&quot;) A static data member is declared with static inside a class, has one copy shared by all objects, exists even if no object has been created, is defined outside the class (int A::count = 0;) and is accessed as A::count. A static member function can use only static members (no this) and is called as A::f().</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass Item {\n    static int count;                          // shared by all objects\npublic:\n    Item() { ++count; }\n    static void show() { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Items created: &quot; &lt;&lt; count &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;; }   // static function uses static data\n};\nint Item::count = 0;\nint main() { Item a, b; Item::show(); Item c; Item::show(); }</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Items created: 2</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Items created: 3</p>","exam":"mid","year":2018,"branch":"SE","src":"OOPS MIDSEM ALL BRANCHES Compiled.pdf p.19 (SE203, B.Tech (CSE), Sept-2018"},{"p":35,"q":"2","marks":"2","unit":0,"topic":"Namespace","qh":"<p>Q 2. What is a namespace? (2)</p>","sh":"<p>A namespace is a named scope that groups identifiers (classes, functions, variables) to prevent name conflicts between libraries. Declared with namespace N { ... }, used as N::name or using namespace N;. All standard library names are in std.</p>","exam":"mid","year":2018,"branch":"SE","src":"OOPS MIDSEM ALL BRANCHES Compiled.pdf p.19 (SE203, B.Tech (CSE), Sept-2018"},{"p":35,"q":"3","marks":"2","unit":1,"topic":"Operators that cannot be overloaded","qh":"<p>Q 3. List the operators that cannot be overloaded. (2)</p>","sh":"<p>:: (scope resolution), . (member access), .* (pointer to member), ?: (conditional), sizeof, typeid. (=, (), [], -&gt; can be overloaded only as member functions.)</p>","exam":"mid","year":2018,"branch":"SE","src":"OOPS MIDSEM ALL BRANCHES Compiled.pdf p.19 (SE203, B.Tech (CSE), Sept-2018"},{"p":35,"q":"4","marks":"3","unit":0,"topic":"Inline functions","qh":"<p>Q 4. What is inline function? Explain situations where inline expansion may not work. (3)</p>","sh":"<p>An inline function is expanded at the call site (request to the compiler) to avoid call overhead (inline int sq(int x) { return x*x; }). Inline expansion may not work when the function contains loops, switch or goto; is recursive; contains static variables; is too large; is virtual and called through a pointer; its address is taken; or the compiler decides it is not beneficial.</p>","exam":"mid","year":2018,"branch":"SE","src":"OOPS MIDSEM ALL BRANCHES Compiled.pdf p.19 (SE203, B.Tech (CSE), Sept-2018"},{"p":35,"q":"5","marks":"5","unit":0,"topic":"Object-oriented programming concepts","qh":"<p>Q 5. Explain the Object Oriented programming concepts. (5)</p>","sh":"<pre>Concept              Meaning\nObject               Instance with data and behaviour (Car c;)\nClass                Blueprint of objects (data members + member functions)\nEncapsulation        Data and functions bound into one unit, hidden by access specifiers\nData abstraction     Only essential features are exposed\nInheritance          A class acquires members of another (reuse, is-a)\nPolymorphism         One name, many forms (overloading at compile time, virtual functions at run time)\nDynamic binding      Function chosen at run time from the object&#x27;s actual type\nMessage passing      Objects communicate by calling each other&#x27;s member functions\n#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass Animal { public: virtual void speak() const { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;...\\n&quot;; } virtual ~Animal() {} };   // class, abstraction\nclass Dog : public Animal { public: void speak() const override { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Woof\\n&quot;; } };         // inheritance, polymorphism\nint main() { Animal* a = new Dog; a-&gt;speak(); delete a; }                                       // object, dynamic binding</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Woof</p>","exam":"mid","year":2018,"branch":"SE","src":"OOPS MIDSEM ALL BRANCHES Compiled.pdf p.19 (SE203, B.Tech (CSE), Sept-2018"},{"p":35,"q":"6","marks":"5","unit":0,"topic":"Parameterized constructor with default values","qh":"<p>Q 6. List the characteristics of a constructor. Write a C++ program to define a suitable parameterized constructor with default values for the class distance with data members feet and inches. (5)</p>","sh":"<p>Characteristics of a constructor: same name as the class; no return type (not even void); called automatically when an object is created; usually public; can take parameters and be overloaded; can have default arguments; cannot be inherited or virtual; cannot be called like a normal member; the compiler provides a default one if none is defined; base constructors run before derived ones.</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass distance_ {                                   // &quot;distance&quot; clashes with std::distance, so a different name is used\n    int feet, inches;\npublic:\n    distance_(int f = 0, int i = 0) : feet(f), inches(i) {}    // parameterized, with default values\n    void show() const { cout &lt;&lt; feet &lt;&lt; &quot; ft &quot; &lt;&lt; inches &lt;&lt; &quot; in\\n&quot;; }\n};\nint main() {\n    distance_ a;              // 0 ft 0 in\n    distance_ b(5);           // 5 ft 0 in\n    distance_ c(6, 3);        // 6 ft 3 in\n    a.show(); b.show(); c.show();\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">0 ft 0 in</p><p class=\"ln i0\">5 ft 0 in</p><p class=\"ln i0\">6 ft 3 in</p>","exam":"mid","year":2018,"branch":"SE","src":"OOPS MIDSEM ALL BRANCHES Compiled.pdf p.19 (SE203, B.Tech (CSE), Sept-2018"},{"p":35,"q":"7","marks":"4","unit":0,"topic":"Copy constructor","qh":"<p>Q 7. Explain copy constructor with suitable C++ coding. (4)</p>","sh":"<p>A copy constructor X(const X&amp;) creates a new object as a copy of an existing one; called for X b = a;, X b(a);, pass by value and return by value. The compiler supplies a member-wise (shallow) copy; for classes holding pointers a user-defined (deep) copy constructor is needed.</p><pre>#include &lt;cstring&gt;\n#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass Name {\n    char* s;\npublic:\n    Name(const char* t) { s = new char[strlen(t) + 1]; strcpy(s, t); }\n    Name(const Name&amp; o) { s = new char[strlen(o.s) + 1]; strcpy(s, o.s); cout &lt;&lt; &quot;deep copy made\\n&quot;; }\n    ~Name() { delete[] s; }\n    void change() { s[0] = &#x27;J&#x27;; }\n    void show() const { cout &lt;&lt; s &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;; }\n};\nint main() {\n    Name a(&quot;Rahul&quot;);\n    Name b = a;               // copy constructor\n    b.change();\n    a.show(); b.show();       // independent copies\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">deep copy made</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Rahul</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Jahul</p>","exam":"mid","year":2018,"branch":"SE","src":"OOPS MIDSEM ALL BRANCHES Compiled.pdf p.19 (SE203, B.Tech (CSE), Sept-2018"},{"p":35,"q":"8","marks":"6","unit":1,"topic":"STRING class with + and copy constructor","qh":"<p>Q 8. Write a C++ program to create a class called STRING and implement the following operations. Display the results after every operation by overloading the operator. STRING s1 = &quot;HELLO&quot;; STRING s2 = &quot;WORLD&quot;; STRING s3 = s1 + s2; (Use copy constructor) (6)</p>","sh":"<p>operator+ concatenates, the copy constructor makes s3 independent of the temporary, and operator&lt;&lt; displays the result after each operation.</p><pre>#include &lt;cstring&gt;\n#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass STRING {\n    char* p;\npublic:\n    STRING(const char* t = &quot;&quot;) { p = new char[strlen(t) + 1]; strcpy(p, t); }\n    STRING(const STRING&amp; o) { p = new char[strlen(o.p) + 1]; strcpy(p, o.p); }      // copy constructor\n    ~STRING() { delete[] p; }\n    STRING operator+(const STRING&amp; o) const {\n        char* t = new char[strlen(p) + strlen(o.p) + 1];\n        strcpy(t, p); strcat(t, o.p);\n        STRING r(t);\n        delete[] t;\n        return r;\n    }\n    friend ostream&amp; operator&lt;&lt;(ostream&amp; os, const STRING&amp; s) { return os &lt;&lt; s.p; }\n};\nint main() {\n    STRING s1 = &quot;HELLO&quot;;\n    cout &lt;&lt; &quot;s1 = &quot; &lt;&lt; s1 &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n    STRING s2 = &quot;WORLD&quot;;\n    cout &lt;&lt; &quot;s2 = &quot; &lt;&lt; s2 &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n    STRING s3 = s1 + s2;               // uses operator+ and the copy constructor\n    cout &lt;&lt; &quot;s3 = &quot; &lt;&lt; s3 &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">s1 = HELLO</p><p class=\"ln i0\">s2 = WORLD</p><p class=\"ln i0\">s3 = HELLOWORLD</p>","exam":"mid","year":2018,"branch":"SE","src":"OOPS MIDSEM ALL BRANCHES Compiled.pdf p.19 (SE203, B.Tech (CSE), Sept-2018"},{"p":36,"q":"1","marks":"8","unit":1,"topic":"Four distinctions (2 each)","qh":"<p>Q1. What is the difference between the following? Give minimum three points including examples. 2x4 a) Function Overloading and operator overloading b) Java application and applet c) Function template and class template d) class and structure</p>","sh":"<p>a)</p><pre>Function overloading                       Operator overloading\nSame function name, different parameters   Gives an operator a new meaning for class objects\nAny function name                          Only existing operators (no new ones)\nadd(int,int), add(double,double)           Complex operator+(const Complex&amp;)\nResolved by arguments at compile time      Resolved by operand types at compile time</pre><p>b) Java application: standalone, main() entry point, run with java, full system access. Java applet: embedded in a web page, no main(), life cycle methods, runs in a browser/appletviewer under sandbox restrictions, extends Applet.</p><p>c) A function template (template&lt;class T&gt; T max(T,T)) generates functions for each type, with the type deduced from the arguments; a class template (template&lt;class T&gt; class Stack) generates classes; the type must be given explicitly (Stack&lt;int&gt;) (until C++17); function templates can be overloaded, class templates are specialised.</p><p>d) Class: default private, data + functions, inheritance, constructors. Structure: default public (C: data only). A C++ struct differs from a class only in default access.</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nstruct S { int x = 1; };           // public by default\nclass C { int x = 1; public: int get() const { return x; } };   // private by default\ntemplate &lt;class T&gt; T twice(T v) { return v + v; }\ntemplate &lt;class T&gt; struct Wrap { T v; };\nint main() { S s; C c; Wrap&lt;int&gt; w{5}; cout &lt;&lt; s.x &lt;&lt; c.get() &lt;&lt; twice(2.5) &lt;&lt; w.v &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;; }</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">1155</p>","exam":"supp","year":2018,"branch":"IT","src":"EndSem Papers.pdf p.23 (IT-203, Feb-2018"},{"p":36,"q":"2","marks":"8","unit":1,"topic":"Operator overloading methods; friend function and class","qh":"<p>Q2. a) Explain the methods to overload operators. Why is it required? Overload the &lt;&lt; and &gt;&gt; operators. 4 b) What is a friend function? Explain the concept of friend function and friend class in detail with an example. Also discuss inheritance in case of a friend function giving reasons for your answer. 4</p>","sh":"<p>(Same question as Q2 of the IT Supplementary papers of Feb-2019 and Feb-2020.) (a) Two methods: a member function (left operand is the object) and a friend/non-member function (all operands are arguments; needed for cout &lt;&lt; obj and cin &gt;&gt; obj). Required so that user-defined types can use natural operator syntax.</p><p>Sample input:</p><pre>6 8\n#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass Vec {\n    int x, y;\npublic:\n    Vec(int a = 0, int b = 0) : x(a), y(b) {}\n    friend ostream&amp; operator&lt;&lt;(ostream&amp; os, const Vec&amp; v) { return os &lt;&lt; &quot;&lt;&quot; &lt;&lt; v.x &lt;&lt; &quot;, &quot; &lt;&lt; v.y &lt;&lt; &quot;&gt;&quot;; }\n    friend istream&amp; operator&gt;&gt;(istream&amp; is, Vec&amp; v) { return is &gt;&gt; v.x &gt;&gt; v.y; }\n};\nint main() { Vec v; cin &gt;&gt; v; cout &lt;&lt; v &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;; }</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">&lt;6, 8&gt;</p><p>(b) A friend function (declared with friend) is a non-member that can access private/protected members; a friend class gives all its member functions that access. Friendship is not inherited, not mutual and not transitive: a friend of a base class is not automatically a friend of its derived classes, since the base class cannot grant trust on behalf of classes written later.</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass Bank {\n    int vault = 1000;\n    friend void audit(const Bank&amp;);          // friend function\n    friend class Auditor;                    // friend class\n};\nvoid audit(const Bank&amp; b) { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;audit: &quot; &lt;&lt; b.vault &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;; }\nclass Auditor { public: int check(const Bank&amp; b) { return b.vault / 10; } };\nint main() { Bank b; audit(b); cout &lt;&lt; Auditor().check(b) &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;; }</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">audit: 1000</p><p class=\"ln i0\">100</p>","exam":"supp","year":2018,"branch":"IT","src":"EndSem Papers.pdf p.23 (IT-203, Feb-2018"},{"p":36,"q":"3","marks":"8","unit":1,"topic":"Binding; :: in inheritance; virtual base class; access specifiers","qh":"<p>Q3. Answer the following with examples a) What is dynamic and static binding in C++? b) What is the role of :: in inheritance? c) Write a program to show the use of virtual base class. d) Access specifiers in c++ with their roles.</p>","sh":"<p>a) Static binding: the function is chosen at compile time (normal, overloaded, non-virtual). Dynamic binding: chosen at run time from the object&#x27;s real type, using virtual functions (vtable).</p><p>b) :: selects a particular class&#x27;s member: Base::f() calls the base version from a derived function; resolves ambiguity between two bases; defines members outside the class.</p><p>c)</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass A { public: int v = 3; A() { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;A built once\\n&quot;; } };\nclass B : virtual public A {};\nclass C : virtual public A {};\nclass D : public B, public C {};\nint main() { D d; cout &lt;&lt; d.v &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;; }</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">A built once</p><p class=\"ln i0\">3</p><p>d) public: accessible from anywhere (the interface of the class). private: accessible only inside the class and its friends (default for classes; implements data hiding). protected: accessible inside the class, its friends and its derived classes, not from outside. In inheritance the access specifier before the base class (public, protected, private) caps how the base members are exposed in the derived class.</p>","exam":"supp","year":2018,"branch":"IT","src":"EndSem Papers.pdf p.23 (IT-203, Feb-2018"},{"p":36,"q":"4","marks":"8","unit":0,"topic":"Nested class; this; constructor overloading; run-time polymorphism","qh":"<p>Q4. Answer the following with examples. a) What is the role of a nested class? How to use it? b) Write a program to show the role of this keyword in C++. Explain. c) Write a program to show constructor overloading. d) How does runtime polymorphism occur in C++?</p>","sh":"<p>a) A nested class is declared inside another class to group a helper type that belongs to it; used as Outer::Inner x;.</p><p>b) this points to the object on which a member function was called; it separates a member from a same-named parameter and lets a function return the current object.</p><p>c) Constructor overloading: several constructors with different parameters.</p><p>d) Run-time polymorphism: a virtual base function, overridden in derived classes and called through a base pointer/reference; resolved at run time through the object&#x27;s vptr and the class&#x27;s vtable.</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass Outer { public: class Inner { public: void hi() { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;nested Inner\\n&quot;; } }; };\nclass Rect {\n    int l, b;\npublic:\n    Rect() : l(1), b(1) {}                                        // c) constructor overloading\n    Rect(int s) : l(s), b(s) {}\n    Rect(int x, int y) : l(x), b(y) {}\n    Rect&amp; scale(int k) { this-&gt;l *= k; this-&gt;b *= k; return *this; }   // b) this\n    int area() const { return l * b; }\n};\nclass Shape { public: virtual const char* name() const { return &quot;shape&quot;; } virtual ~Shape() {} };\nclass Tri : public Shape { public: const char* name() const override { return &quot;triangle&quot;; } };\nint main() {\n    Outer::Inner i; i.hi();\n    Rect a, b(3), c(2, 5);\n    cout &lt;&lt; a.area() &lt;&lt; &#x27; &#x27; &lt;&lt; b.area() &lt;&lt; &#x27; &#x27; &lt;&lt; c.scale(2).area() &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n    Shape* s = new Tri; cout &lt;&lt; s-&gt;name() &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;; delete s;     // d) dynamic dispatch\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">nested Inner</p><p class=\"ln i0\">1 9 40</p><p class=\"ln i0\">triangle</p>","exam":"supp","year":2018,"branch":"IT","src":"EndSem Papers.pdf p.23 (IT-203, Feb-2018"},{"p":36,"q":"5","marks":"8","unit":3,"topic":"C++ exceptions; Java applet","qh":"<p>Q5 a) Explain exception handling in c++. How is an exception rethrown? Also explain the concept of multiple catch and catch all. 4 b) What is java applet? Give its properties in detail. Give an example of the same by creating a basic applet which will print the string &quot;Hello User&quot; on the screen. 4</p>","sh":"<p>a) try encloses code that may fail, throw raises an exception object, catch handlers (checked in order) handle it; stack unwinding destroys locals. Multiple catch: several handlers for different types; catch-all: catch (...). Rethrow: throw; in a handler passes the same exception on.</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nvoid risky(int k) {\n    try { if (k == 1) throw 10; if (k == 2) throw &#x27;x&#x27;; throw 2.5; }\n    catch (int e) { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;risky: int &quot; &lt;&lt; e &lt;&lt; &quot;, rethrow\\n&quot;; throw; }\n    catch (char c) { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;risky: char &quot; &lt;&lt; c &lt;&lt; &quot; handled\\n&quot;; }\n}\nint main() {\n    for (int k = 1; k &lt;= 3; ++k)\n        try { risky(k); }\n        catch (int e) { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;main: int &quot; &lt;&lt; e &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;; }\n        catch (...) { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;main: catch-all\\n&quot;; }\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">risky: int 10, rethrow</p><p class=\"ln i0\">main: int 10</p><p class=\"ln i0\">risky: char x handled</p><p class=\"ln i0\">main: catch-all</p><p>b) A Java applet is a small program embedded in a web page and run by the browser&#x27;s JVM or appletviewer. Properties: extends Applet/JApplet; no main(); life cycle init, start, paint, stop, destroy; sandbox security; runs on the client; GUI by AWT/Swing; parameters by &lt;param&gt;.</p><pre>import java.applet.Applet;\nimport java.awt.Graphics;\npublic class HelloUser extends Applet {\n    public void paint(Graphics g) { g.drawString(&quot;Hello User&quot;, 40, 40); }\n}\n/* &lt;applet code=&quot;HelloUser.class&quot; width=&quot;250&quot; height=&quot;100&quot;&gt;&lt;/applet&gt; */</pre><p>Output: the applet window shows Hello User.</p>","exam":"supp","year":2018,"branch":"IT","src":"EndSem Papers.pdf p.23 (IT-203, Feb-2018"},{"p":36,"q":"6","marks":"8","unit":2,"topic":"Short notes: bytecode, destructor, array of objects, generic programming","qh":"<p>Q6. Write a short note on a) Bytecode b) Destructor c) array of objects d) generic programming</p>","sh":"<p>a) Bytecode is the platform-independent intermediate code (.class) produced by javac and executed by the JVM (interpreted/JIT); it gives portability and security.</p><p>b) A destructor ~X() has no arguments or return type; it is called automatically when an object is destroyed, to free resources; one per class; objects are destroyed in reverse order of construction.</p><p>c) An array of objects stores several objects of one class: Student s[3]; (needs a default constructor, or an initialiser list) accessed as s[i].show().</p><p>d) Generic programming writes algorithms and classes independent of data types: C++ templates (template&lt;class T&gt;), Java generics (ArrayList&lt;String&gt;); gives reuse and compile-time type safety.</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass Student {\n    int roll;\npublic:\n    Student(int r = 0) : roll(r) {}\n    ~Student() { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;~Student &quot; &lt;&lt; roll &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;; }\n    void show() const { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;roll &quot; &lt;&lt; roll &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;; }\n};\ntemplate &lt;class T&gt; T biggest(T a, T b) { return a &gt; b ? a : b; }\nint main() {\n    Student s[3] = { Student(1), Student(2), Student(3) };   // array of objects\n    for (int i = 0; i &lt; 3; ++i) s[i].show();\n    cout &lt;&lt; biggest(3, 7) &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">roll 1</p><p class=\"ln i0\">roll 2</p><p class=\"ln i0\">roll 3</p><p class=\"ln i0\">7</p><p class=\"ln i0\">~Student 3</p><p class=\"ln i0\">~Student 2</p><p class=\"ln i0\">~Student 1</p>","exam":"supp","year":2018,"branch":"IT","src":"EndSem Papers.pdf p.23 (IT-203, Feb-2018"},{"p":37,"q":"1","marks":"8","unit":0,"topic":"Data types; class versus object","qh":"<p>Q1. (a) Explain different data types in C++. Give suitable examples for each. 4 (b) Differentiate between class and object. How the memory to each member is allocated? 4</p>","sh":"<p>(a) Data types: fundamental (built-in): int, short, long, char, bool, float, double, void, wchar_t; derived: arrays, pointers, references, functions; user-defined: struct, union, enum, class, typedef.</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nstruct Point { int x, y; };                 // user-defined\nenum Colour { RED, GREEN, BLUE };           // user-defined\nint main() {\n    int i = 10; short s = 3; long l = 100000L; char c = &#x27;A&#x27;; bool b = true;\n    float f = 3.14f; double d = 2.718281828;\n    int arr[3] = {1, 2, 3}; int* p = &amp;i; int&amp; r = i;     // derived types\n    Point pt{1, 2}; Colour col = GREEN;\n    cout &lt;&lt; i &lt;&lt; &#x27; &#x27; &lt;&lt; s &lt;&lt; &#x27; &#x27; &lt;&lt; l &lt;&lt; &#x27; &#x27; &lt;&lt; c &lt;&lt; &#x27; &#x27; &lt;&lt; b &lt;&lt; &#x27; &#x27; &lt;&lt; f &lt;&lt; &#x27; &#x27; &lt;&lt; d &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n    cout &lt;&lt; arr[2] &lt;&lt; &#x27; &#x27; &lt;&lt; *p &lt;&lt; &#x27; &#x27; &lt;&lt; r &lt;&lt; &#x27; &#x27; &lt;&lt; pt.x &lt;&lt; pt.y &lt;&lt; &#x27; &#x27; &lt;&lt; col &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">10 3 100000 A 1 3.14 2.71828</p><p class=\"ln i0\">3 10 10 12 1</p><p>(b) A class is a user-defined type (blueprint, no data memory of its own); an object is an instance of the class that occupies memory. Memory: each object gets its own copy of every non-static data member (allocated when the object is created: stack, heap or static, depending on how it is declared); member functions are stored once, in the code segment, and shared by all objects; static data members have one copy in static storage shared by all objects.</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass C { int a; double b; static int s; public: void f() {} };\nint C::s = 0;\nint main() { C o1, o2; cout &lt;&lt; (sizeof(o1) == sizeof(o2) ? &quot;same size per object&quot; : &quot;?&quot;) &lt;&lt; &quot;, size &quot; &lt;&lt; sizeof(C) &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;; }</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">same size per object, size 16</p>","exam":"supp","year":2018,"branch":"CO","src":"EndSem Papers.pdf p.22 (CO/SE-203, B.Tech (CO/SE), Feb-2018"},{"p":37,"q":"2","marks":"8","unit":1,"topic":"Pointer to members of objects; function overloading for areas","qh":"<p>Q2. (a) How to create pointer to members of objects? Give examples. 4 (b) Write a program using function overloading to find area of different shapes. 4</p>","sh":"<p>(a) A pointer to an object: Rect* p = &amp;r; accesses members with p-&gt;length. A pointer to a data member: int</p><pre>Rect::*pm = &amp;Rect::length; used as r.*pm or p-&gt;*pm. A pointer to a member function: int (Rect::*pf)() const\n= &amp;Rect::area; called as (r.*pf)() or (p-&gt;*pf)().\n#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass Rect {\npublic:\n    int length = 4, breadth = 5;\n    int area() const { return length * breadth; }\n};\nint main() {\n    Rect r;\n    Rect* p = &amp;r;                              // pointer to object\n    int Rect::*pm = &amp;Rect::length;             // pointer to data member\n    int (Rect::*pf)() const = &amp;Rect::area;     // pointer to member function\n    cout &lt;&lt; p-&gt;breadth &lt;&lt; &#x27; &#x27; &lt;&lt; r.*pm &lt;&lt; &#x27; &#x27; &lt;&lt; (r.*pf)() &lt;&lt; &#x27; &#x27; &lt;&lt; (p-&gt;*pf)() &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">5 4 20 20</p><p>(b)</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\ndouble area(double r)               { return 3.14159 * r * r; }    // circle\nint    area(int side)               { return side * side; }        // square\ndouble area(double l, double b)     { return l * b; }              // rectangle\ndouble area(double b, double h, int) { return 0.5 * b * h; }       // triangle\nint main() { cout &lt;&lt; area(1.0) &lt;&lt; &#x27; &#x27; &lt;&lt; area(4) &lt;&lt; &#x27; &#x27; &lt;&lt; area(2.0, 3.5) &lt;&lt; &#x27; &#x27; &lt;&lt; area(6.0, 3.0, 0) &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;; }</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">3.14159 16 7 9</p>","exam":"supp","year":2018,"branch":"CO","src":"EndSem Papers.pdf p.22 (CO/SE-203, B.Tech (CO/SE), Feb-2018"},{"p":37,"q":"3","marks":"8","unit":1,"topic":"OOP features; operator overloading to add matrices","qh":"<p>Q3. (a) Explain the features of object oriented programming. 4 (b) What is operator overloading? Write a program to overload addition operator to add two matrices. 4</p>","sh":"<p>(a) Features: objects and classes; encapsulation (data + functions bound, data hidden); data abstraction; inheritance (reuse, hierarchy); polymorphism (compile-time overloading, run-time virtual functions); dynamic binding; message passing.</p><p>(b) Operator overloading assigns new meaning to an operator for class types (operator+). Matrix::operator+ adds corresponding elements.</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass Matrix {\n    int a[2][2];\npublic:\n    Matrix(int p, int q, int r, int s) { a[0][0] = p; a[0][1] = q; a[1][0] = r; a[1][1] = s; }\n    Matrix operator+(const Matrix&amp; m) const {\n        return Matrix(a[0][0] + m.a[0][0], a[0][1] + m.a[0][1], a[1][0] + m.a[1][0], a[1][1] + m.a[1][1]);\n    }\n    void show() const { for (auto&amp; row : a) cout &lt;&lt; row[0] &lt;&lt; &#x27; &#x27; &lt;&lt; row[1] &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;; }\n};\nint main() {\n    Matrix x(1, 2, 3, 4), y(5, 6, 7, 8);\n    (x + y).show();\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">6 8</p><p class=\"ln i0\">10 12</p>","exam":"supp","year":2018,"branch":"CO","src":"EndSem Papers.pdf p.22 (CO/SE-203, B.Tech (CO/SE), Feb-2018"},{"p":37,"q":"4","marks":"8","unit":0,"topic":"Keywords; exception handling steps","qh":"<p>Q4. (a) How many keywords are there in C++. Explain keywords - class, virtual, protected. 4 (b) Explain the steps involved in exception handling. 4</p>","sh":"<p>(a) C++98 has 63 keywords (C has 32; later standards have about 95). class declares a class (members private by default). virtual makes a function dynamically bound (run-time polymorphism) or a base class shared (virtual inheritance). protected is an access specifier: members are accessible in the class and its derived classes but not outside.</p><p>(b) Steps: (1) find the problem (hazard: bad input, divide by zero); (2) inform that it has happened: throw an exception object; (3) receive the exception: catch handler with a matching type, found by unwinding the stack from the try block; (4) take corrective action in the handler; execution continues after the handlers. If no handler matches, terminate() ends the program.</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\ndouble divide(double a, double b) {\n    if (b == 0) throw &quot;Division by zero&quot;;          // step 2: throw\n    return a / b;\n}\nint main() {\n    try {                                          // step 1: try block with the risky code\n        cout &lt;&lt; divide(10, 4) &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n        cout &lt;&lt; divide(1, 0) &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;\n    } catch (const char* msg) {                    // step 3: catch\n        cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Handled: &quot; &lt;&lt; msg &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;        // step 4: corrective action\n    }\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">2.5</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Handled: Division by zero</p>","exam":"supp","year":2018,"branch":"CO","src":"EndSem Papers.pdf p.22 (CO/SE-203, B.Tech (CO/SE), Feb-2018"},{"p":37,"q":"5","marks":"8","unit":1,"topic":"Friend function addition; inheritance types and modes","qh":"<p>Q5. (a) What is a friend function? Write a program to add to numbers using a friendly function. 4 (b) Explain inheritance, their types, modes, visibility with examples? 4</p>","sh":"<p>(a) A friend function is a non-member function granted access to a class&#x27;s private members. It adds the private numbers of two objects:</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass Num {\n    int v;\npublic:\n    Num(int x) : v(x) {}\n    friend int addNums(const Num&amp;, const Num&amp;);\n};\nint addNums(const Num&amp; a, const Num&amp; b) { return a.v + b.v; }\nint main() { Num a(14), b(28); cout &lt;&lt; addNums(a, b) &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;; }</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">42</p><p>(b) Inheritance: a derived class acquires the members of a base class. Types: single, multiple, multilevel, hierarchical, hybrid. Modes: public, protected, private. Visibility:</p><pre>Base member      public mode      protected mode    private mode\npublic           public           protected         private\nprotected        protected        protected         private\nprivate          not accessible   not accessible    not accessible\n#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass Base { public: int a = 1; protected: int b = 2; private: int c = 3; public: int getC() const { return c; } };\nclass D1 : public Base    { public: int sum() const { return a + b + getC(); } };\nclass D2 : private Base   { public: int sum() const { return a + b + getC(); } };\nint main() {\n    D1 x; D2 y;\n    cout &lt;&lt; x.a &lt;&lt; &#x27; &#x27; &lt;&lt; x.sum() &lt;&lt; &#x27; &#x27; &lt;&lt; y.sum() &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;;      // y.a is private in D2: not accessible\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">1 6 6</p>","exam":"supp","year":2018,"branch":"CO","src":"EndSem Papers.pdf p.22 (CO/SE-203, B.Tech (CO/SE), Feb-2018"},{"p":37,"q":"6","marks":"8","unit":0,"topic":"Identifier rules; vehicle inheritance","qh":"<p>Q6. (a) What are the rules for naming identifier? 3 (b) Write a program to add, show details of two wheeler and four wheeler using inheritance where class &quot;vehicle&quot; is inherited by two different classes &quot;two_wheeler&quot; and &quot;four_wheeler&quot; 5</p>","sh":"<p>(a) An identifier is a name for a variable, function, class etc. Rules: letters, digits and underscore only; must not start with a digit; case sensitive; must not be a keyword; no spaces or special characters; any length (compilers distinguish at least the first 1024 characters); avoid names beginning with an underscore followed by a capital letter or containing __.</p><p>(b)</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n#include &lt;string&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass vehicle {\nprotected:\n    string company, model;\n    double price;\npublic:\n    void add(string c, string m, double p) { company = c; model = m; price = p; }\n    void show() const { cout &lt;&lt; company &lt;&lt; &#x27; &#x27; &lt;&lt; model &lt;&lt; &quot;, Rs &quot; &lt;&lt; price; }\n};\nclass two_wheeler : public vehicle {\n    int engineCC;\npublic:\n    void add(string c, string m, double p, int cc) { vehicle::add(c, m, p); engineCC = cc; }\n    void show() const { vehicle::show(); cout &lt;&lt; &quot;, &quot; &lt;&lt; engineCC &lt;&lt; &quot; cc (two wheeler)\\n&quot;; }\n};\nclass four_wheeler : public vehicle {\n    int seats;\npublic:\n    void add(string c, string m, double p, int s) { vehicle::add(c, m, p); seats = s; }\n    void show() const { vehicle::show(); cout &lt;&lt; &quot;, &quot; &lt;&lt; seats &lt;&lt; &quot; seats (four wheeler)\\n&quot;; }\n};\nint main() {\n    two_wheeler t; four_wheeler f;\n    t.add(&quot;Honda&quot;, &quot;Activa&quot;, 80000, 110);\n    f.add(&quot;Maruti&quot;, &quot;Swift&quot;, 650000, 5);\n    t.show();\n    f.show();\n}</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Honda Activa, Rs 80000, 110 cc (two wheeler)</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Maruti Swift, Rs 650000, 5 seats (four wheeler)</p>","exam":"supp","year":2018,"branch":"CO","src":"EndSem Papers.pdf p.22 (CO/SE-203, B.Tech (CO/SE), Feb-2018"},{"p":37,"q":"7","marks":"8","unit":1,"topic":"Short notes (any two)","qh":"<p>Q7. Write short notes on any two :- (a) Virtual function (b) Templates (c) Call by value and call by reference (d) Features of Java</p>","sh":"<p>(a) A virtual function is a base-class member function declared with virtual; derived classes may override it; a call through a base pointer/reference is bound at run time to the override of the real object (run-time polymorphism). Implemented with a vtable/vptr. A base class with virtual functions should have a virtual destructor.</p><p>(b) Templates define generic functions (template &lt;class T&gt; T maxOf(T a, T b)) and classes (template &lt;class T&gt; class Stack); the compiler instantiates them for each type; reuse, type safety, STL.</p><pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nclass Base { public: virtual void who() { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Base\\n&quot;; } virtual ~Base() {} };\nclass Derived : public Base { public: void who() override { cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Derived\\n&quot;; } };\ntemplate &lt;class T&gt; T maxOf(T a, T b) { return a &gt; b ? a : b; }\nint main() { Base* p = new Derived; p-&gt;who(); delete p; cout &lt;&lt; maxOf(3, 8) &lt;&lt; &#x27; &#x27; &lt;&lt; maxOf(2.5, 1.5) &lt;&lt; &#x27;\\n&#x27;; }</pre><p>Output:</p><p class=\"ln i0\">Derived</p><p class=\"ln i0\">8 2.5</p><p>(c) Call by value passes a copy (caller unchanged); call by reference passes an alias/address (callee changes the caller&#x27;s variable, no copy).</p><p>(d) Features of Java: simple, object-oriented, platform independent (bytecode/JVM), secure, robust (exceptions, garbage collection), multithreaded, portable, high performance (JIT), distributed, dynamic.</p>","exam":"supp","year":2018,"branch":"CO","src":"EndSem Papers.pdf p.22 (CO/SE-203, B.Tech (CO/SE), Feb-2018"}];
